// Content for Us, After Kids (node g61, Parenthood, Chapter 8): NEW v2 build to GDD 61 (mechanic-embodying),
// OPENING Chapter 8 (Parenthood) with the relationship parenting strains most: the couple's own, and the self
// within it. A baby changes everything, including you two. 84 researched, India-grounded scenarios across
// the-big-shift → talk-through-tired → share-dont-resent → reconnecting → you-still-matter → tools-and-help, each
// encoded as one of the shared v2 play actions (branch / strike-rewrite / role-play / sort / reflect / match /
// spot): no binary "tap the right card". Lead: branch (your move), strike-rewrite (bust the myth), role-play (say
// it). Six modes: the big shift (a baby reshapes the couple and each self, normalised; ~2 in 3 couples feel the
// dip and it isn't a verdict; thrivers use learnable skills, not luck), talk through the tired (communication &
// repair under exhaustion: name the tiredness, not each other; appreciation; assume good intent; busts "the
// romance is just over"), share don't resent (fair division is the strongest relationship protector; a partner's
// involvement is practical AND symbolic ("this is ours, not yours"; engages FATHERS directly) a father isn't
// "babysitting" his own child; links Equal Parents g62), reconnecting (rebuild emotional & physical intimacy at
// the pace of BOTH partners' readiness: no deadline, no obligation; recovery is individual; postpartum discomfort
// routes to a clinician), you still matter (protect each partner's identity & wellbeing: you're still a whole
// person, not only a parent; the "I used to be someone else" shift is common; persistent low mood routes to
// Looking After You g63), tools & help. Even-handed and warm: engages fathers as equal parents, offers partnership
// not pity, applies NO pressure on intimacy timing, frank but respectful; NOT medical advice. Builds on Choosing &
// Building (g53) and Real Relationships (g46); links Equal Parents (g62), Looking After You (g63), The Family Map
// (g57) and Respect at Home (g56). India: a new baby often arrives within a joint family (help, but also pressure
// & less couple privacy), and postpartum women face a dual burden, role conflict and guilt; strain that turns to
// control or abuse routes to Respect at Home (g56) and help. gameId "us-after-kids".
import type { V2GameConfig, Scenario } from "./v2-schema";

const SCENARIOS: Scenario[] = [
  {"id":"uk-001","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If our relationship struggles after the baby, it means we're failing.\"","myth":{"un":"If our relationship struggles after the baby, it means we're failing.","re":"Around two in three couples find their satisfaction dips after a first child; the strain is common, not a verdict on your love.","why":"Knowing it's normal takes the panic out of it."},"relearn":"Most couples feel the strain after a baby; it's common and normal, not a sign you're failing.","persona":"Divya","source":"common not failing"},
  {"id":"uk-002","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"The couples who stay happy after kids just got lucky with an easy baby.\"","myth":{"un":"The couples who stay happy after kids just got lucky with an easy baby.","re":"The couples who thrive aren't luckier; they use specific skills, sharing the load, communicating and appreciating each other.","why":"It's skills you can learn, not luck."},"relearn":"Couples who thrive after kids use learnable skills, not luck or an easier baby.","persona":"Vikram","source":"skills not luck"},
  {"id":"uk-003","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a normal part of the new-baby shift, or a sign something's truly wrong?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Feeling tired and short on couple time"},{"id":"b","text":"Being controlled or frightened by your partner"},{"id":"c","text":"More friction while you adjust"},{"id":"d","text":"Threats or being put down constantly"},{"id":"e","text":"Snapping then saying sorry"},{"id":"f","text":"Being cut off from your own family"}],"bins":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal adjustment","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wrong","label":"Truly wrong, get help","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"normal","b":"wrong","c":"normal","d":"wrong","e":"normal","f":"wrong"},"relearn":"Tiredness and more friction are normal adjustment; control, threats and constant put-downs are not, and need help.","persona":"any","source":"normal vs wrong"},
  {"id":"uk-004","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"name the phase","hook":"Since the baby, you and Arjun feel more like exhausted teammates than lovers, and it scares you.","steps":[{"prompt":"It is late and neither of you has said it aloud. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name it as a normal phase you can work on together","then":"Arjun exhales. \"I felt it too. I thought it was only me.\"","best":true},{"text":"Assume it's over and quietly pull away from Arjun","then":"You both grow quieter, and the distance starts to feel real."},{"text":"Tell a friend first, and wait before telling Arjun","then":"Your friend says it's normal, but Arjun has no idea you are struggling."},{"text":"Joke that you're just roommates now, and leave it","then":"Arjun laughs, but the fear under the joke stays unspoken."}],"why":"Naming the shift as a normal phase turns a scary feeling into a shared project."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Arjun says he misses you but is too tired to plan anything. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll make time for each other once the baby sleeps through","then":"The wait has no end date, and the evenings stay parallel."},{"text":"Say \"That's just life with a baby now\" and pick up your phone","then":"Arjun goes quiet, and the gap between you stays where it was."},{"text":"Say you miss him too, and try ten minutes phone-free","then":"Arjun smiles. \"Ten minutes I can do.\" You sit close on the sofa.","best":true},{"text":"Say he should plan something, since he's the one who misses it","then":"Arjun feels stuck with the whole job, and nothing gets planned."}],"why":"One small, doable step beats waiting for energy that isn't coming."},{"prompt":"A month on, a friend asks how you two are doing. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's a hard stretch, and a normal one you can work on","then":"Your friend nods. \"Us too. Nobody says it out loud.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say everything is perfect and you're loving every minute","then":"Your friend smiles, and the conversation stays on the surface."},{"text":"Say it's probably over and there's no point trying","then":"Your friend frowns and says it's too early to call it. The verdict sits heavy on you."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not talk about it with anyone at all","then":"Your friend backs off, and you keep the worry to yourself."}],"why":"Saying it's hard and workable is honest, and it makes it easier for others to be honest too."}],"debrief":"Treat the post-baby dip as a normal phase to work through together, not proof the relationship is over.","relearn":"Name the post-baby shift as a normal phase to work on together, not a crisis."},
  {"id":"uk-005","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of the big shift to the reassuring truth.","pairs":[{"left":"Less time and energy for each other","right":"Common, and it eases as you adapt"},{"left":"You both feel changed","right":"New parents do change; that's normal"},{"left":"More little conflicts","right":"Friction while adjusting isn't the end"},{"left":"You miss who you used to be","right":"Grieving the old self is valid, not ingratitude"},{"left":"The dip feels endless","right":"It's steepest early, then it eases"}],"relearn":"The dip in time, the sense of change and the extra friction are all normal parts of becoming parents.","persona":"any","source":"reassuring truths"},
  {"id":"uk-006","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Becoming a parent shouldn't change who I am at all.\"","myth":{"un":"Becoming a parent shouldn't change who I am at all.","re":"Parenthood does shift your identity, and feeling 'I used to be someone else' is common; you can hold both selves.","why":"Acknowledging the change helps you adapt."},"relearn":"Parenthood shifts your identity; feeling changed is normal, and you can hold both who you were and who you're becoming.","persona":"Divya","source":"identity shifts"},
  {"id":"uk-007","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a baby reshapes you two and each of you; knowing that's normal is the first relief. Land okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands okay","That's a relief","Good to know"],"affirm":"Knowing the post-baby reshaping of the couple and the self is normal removes a lot of needless fear.","relearn":"The post-baby reshaping of you two, and of each of you, is normal; knowing that is the first relief.","persona":"any","source":"first relief"},
  {"id":"uk-008","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two thoughts that turn a normal hard day into doom.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Expecting an adjustment and teaming up","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"One bad night means we're failing\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Being patient with each other and yourselves","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"The romance is gone for good now\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Naming the tiredness instead of blaming","trick":false}],"why":"\"One bad night means failing\" and \"romance is gone for good\" are catastrophising; a hard day isn't a verdict.","relearn":"A hard day isn't a verdict; catch the doom thoughts that turn one rough night into a story about failing.","persona":"Vikram","source":"meet the shift"},
  {"id":"uk-009","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"no highlight reel","hook":"You catch yourself comparing your tired, messy days with glossy \"perfect new parents\" online.","steps":[{"prompt":"Another perfect-looking family post comes up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel you're failing because your home doesn't look like that","then":"The post stays on your mind, and the shame settles in."},{"text":"Post one polished photo of your own to keep up","then":"A few likes arrive, and the pressure to keep posting grows."},{"text":"Copy their whole routine, down to the schedule","then":"The routine doesn't fit your baby, and you feel even more behind."},{"text":"Remember it's a highlight reel and judge by your own progress","then":"You put the phone down and notice the baby's small smile.","best":true}],"why":"Seeing the feed as a highlight reel frees you to judge by your own real progress."},{"prompt":"That night Arjun catches you frowning at another glossy post and asks what's wrong. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing is wrong and put the phone away","then":"Arjun lets it go, and the worry stays with you."},{"text":"Say what the posts make you feel, and ask how he feels","then":"Arjun puts his phone down. \"Honestly, I feel the same. Let's stop looking.\"","best":true},{"text":"Show him the post and say you should be like them","then":"Arjun looks at it and feels judged too, so he says nothing."},{"text":"Say he doesn't do enough to look like that","then":"Arjun bristles, and the talk becomes about him."}],"why":"Sharing the feeling turns a private comparison into something you can face together."},{"prompt":"Your new-parents group chat is full of \"we're thriving!\" messages. What do you post?","options":[{"text":"Match the cheer with \"All great here!\"","then":"Everyone thinks it's just them, and the chat stays glossy."},{"text":"Say nothing and keep reading the others' posts","then":"The messages keep piling up, and you feel more alone."},{"text":"Post a long complaint about everyone's fake posts","then":"The chat goes quiet, and you feel awkward afterwards."},{"text":"A kind, honest note: \"Rough week here. Anyone else?\"","then":"Three replies arrive within minutes. \"Same. Thank you for saying it.\"","best":true}],"why":"One honest, kind message lets other parents admit they are struggling too."}],"debrief":"Don't measure your real new-parent life against curated online perfection; judge by your own progress.","relearn":"Ignore the 'perfect parents' highlight reel; your real, messy progress is the only fair measure."},
  {"id":"uk-010","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you're not a worse couple now, you're a couple under new load. Big difference. Feel it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Feel it","Under load","Not worse"],"affirm":"You're not a worse couple after a baby; you're a couple carrying new load, which is workable.","relearn":"You're not a worse couple now, just one under new load, and that's something you can work with.","persona":"any","source":"under load"},
  {"id":"uk-011","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once you have kids, the romance and real talking are just over.\"","myth":{"un":"Once you have kids, the romance and real talking are just over.","re":"Connection changes shape but isn't over; small, intentional communication and appreciation keep the bond alive.","why":"The bond is maintained, not finished."},"relearn":"Romance and real talking aren't over after kids; small, intentional connection keeps the bond alive.","persona":"Vikram","source":"not over"},
  {"id":"uk-012","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"When we're both exhausted, snapping at each other is just unavoidable.\"","myth":{"un":"When we're both exhausted, snapping at each other is just unavoidable.","re":"Exhaustion is the enemy, not your partner; naming the tiredness and pausing hard talks for calmer moments protects you both.","why":"Aim the frustration at the problem, not the person."},"relearn":"Snapping isn't unavoidable; name the exhaustion, not each other, and hold hard talks for calmer moments.","persona":"Divya","source":"name the tired"},
  {"id":"uk-013","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"pause the spiral","hook":"A small thing flares into a fight with Neeta, but really you're both just running on no sleep.","steps":[{"prompt":"She snaps that you left a wet towel on the bed. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Insist you're right about the towel, point by point","then":"She counters point by point. It's 2am, and the argument now has a scoreboard."},{"text":"Apologise fast and quietly put up with the tone","then":"You take the towel away. You feel resentful, and she's still stiff."},{"text":"Say she's being unreasonable and walk out of the room","then":"The door clicks. She sits with the baby, angrier and alone."},{"text":"Say you're both exhausted and revisit it rested","then":"She lets out a slow breath. \"Yes. Tomorrow.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the tiredness pauses the fight, so you can solve it rested."},{"prompt":"In the morning, Neeta says she barely remembers what she was angry about. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say she was out of line last night","then":"Her face tightens. The morning goes cold."},{"text":"Say let's forget it and drop it entirely","then":"She nods. Nothing about sleep or towels changes."},{"text":"Ask her to promise not to snap like that again","then":"She tenses. \"I can't promise that on four hours of sleep.\""},{"text":"Laugh gently and say sorry for your part too","then":"She smiles. \"Me too. That was the tiredness talking.\"","best":true}],"why":"A quick, mutual sorry mends it faster than deciding who was right."},{"prompt":"A few nights later, the baby wakes and you both feel the old snap coming. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Bite your tongue and stay silent all night","then":"The snap doesn't come. The silence sits between you."},{"text":"Tell her to stop being dramatic and go back to sleep","then":"She flinches. \"Dramatic?\""},{"text":"Say you'll sort it out tomorrow and go back to bed","then":"She's left holding the baby. Tomorrow you both forget."},{"text":"Say you're both wiped, then split the night","then":"She laughs, tired. \"Deal. I'll take the next one.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the tiredness lightly, then acting on it, keeps you a team."},{"prompt":"A week later, Neeta says she wants a way to catch this before it becomes a fight. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll try to be less snappy yourself","then":"She nods. It's a promise about one of you, not both."},{"text":"Say you'll talk it through whenever you're both free","then":"She agrees. The two of you are never both free."},{"text":"Say it's just a phase and it will pass by itself","then":"She half nods. Nothing changes for when it happens next."},{"text":"Agree a signal, like 'tired talking', to pause things","then":"She laughs. \"Tired talking. I like it.\" You try it that night.","best":true}],"why":"A shared signal gives you both a quick way to pause before things escalate."}],"debrief":"When a fight is really exhaustion talking, pause and revisit it rested rather than pushing to win.","relearn":"Pause depleted fights and revisit rested; don't try to win an argument that's really just tiredness."},
  {"id":"uk-014","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each communication habit to what it protects after a baby.","pairs":[{"left":"Naming tiredness, not blaming","right":"Fewer needless fights"},{"left":"Repairing quickly after a snap","right":"Trust through the hard weeks"},{"left":"Small daily appreciation","right":"The warmth the load erodes"},{"left":"Pausing hard talks till calm","right":"Words you won't regret"},{"left":"Assuming good intent","right":"Spirals that never start"}],"relearn":"Naming tiredness, repairing fast, appreciating, pausing and assuming good intent each protect a part of the bond under load.","persona":"any","source":"protective habits"},
  {"id":"uk-015","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Saying thank you for ordinary parenting stuff is unnecessary.\"","myth":{"un":"Saying thank you for ordinary parenting stuff is unnecessary.","re":"Expressing appreciation, even small and imperfect, is one of the strongest things that keeps couples close through this phase.","why":"Feeling seen sustains the bond."},"relearn":"Appreciation, even for ordinary parenting work, is one of the strongest ways couples stay close after kids.","persona":"Divya","source":"appreciation matters"},
  {"id":"uk-016","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"make them seen","hook":"Your partner Meera has been quietly carrying a lot, and you want her to feel seen.","setup":"She is folding baby clothes late at night, looking drained.","steps":[{"prompt":"Meera says: \"Sorry the house is such a mess. I'm not managing anything.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I see all you're doing. We're in this together.\"","then":"She sets down the shirt she was folding. \"Thank you. I didn't think anyone noticed.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"This is just what parents do. No need to fuss.\"","then":"She goes quiet. \"Right. Of course. I'll keep going.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Leave it and I'll sort it tomorrow.\"","then":"She nods, but her eyes stay on the pile. \"Okay.\""},{"text":"\"You could ask me for help, you know. I'm right here.\"","then":"Her jaw tightens. \"I'm too tired to even think what to ask for.\""}],"why":"Naming what she does, and that you are in it together, makes her feel seen."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Meera says: \"I don't think you know how much I keep track of.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I know. I do plenty too, you know. It's not easy.\"","then":"She nods slowly, and the list stays inside her."},{"text":"\"I'm sure it's not as much as it feels like.\"","then":"Her eyes drop. \"Okay. Never mind.\""},{"text":"\"Tell me. I want to know what I'm not seeing.\"","then":"She counts on her fingers: appointments, vaccine dates, the nappy stock.","best":true},{"text":"\"Just tell me what to do and I'll do it right away.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Then I'm still the one who has to think of everything.\""}],"why":"Asking to see the unseen work makes it visible, and shows you want to share it."},{"prompt":"On another night, Meera says: \"Sorry, I'm crying over nothing.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's not nothing. How are you really doing? I've got this.\"","then":"She lets the tears come. \"Not great. I needed someone to ask.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're just tired. Go and get some sleep now.\"","then":"She wipes her eyes. \"It's not only tired.\""},{"text":"\"Please don't cry. It really upsets me too.\"","then":"She stops, quickly, and looks away."},{"text":"\"Everyone cries with a newborn. It passes fast.\"","then":"She nods. It doesn't feel like comfort."}],"why":"Asking how she really is tells her she is seen. If low mood lasts, a clinician or Tele-MANAS 14416 can help."},{"prompt":"Later that week, Meera says: \"What can we do so I don't feel alone in this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll help whenever you tell me what needs doing.\"","then":"She nods, but she's still the manager of everything."},{"text":"\"Maybe your mum could come to stay for a while.\"","then":"She nods. \"My mum's help would be lovely. But I asked what we can do.\""},{"text":"\"Let's each own some jobs fully, plus a daily thanks.\"","then":"She smiles, tired but real. \"I'd like that. I'll start tonight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It'll get easier soon. You just have to wait.\"","then":"She goes quiet, and the waiting feels long."}],"why":"Owning jobs fully, plus a daily thanks, makes the load shared and seen."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Meera asks: \"How will I know you see what I do?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll thank you often. And tell me what I miss.\"","then":"She laughs. \"Deal. Starting with the laundry.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You'll just know. I'm not one for saying it.\"","then":"She frowns. \"Then how would I ever know?\""},{"text":"\"Remind me when you need a thank-you, I suppose.\"","then":"She sighs. \"So I have to ask for that too.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry so much. I'm busy too, you know.\"","then":"She nods. \"I know you are.\" The warmth thins."}],"why":"Thanking her often, and asking what you miss, keeps her work seen and the load shared."}],"relearn":"Naming what they do and that you're in it together makes a depleted partner feel seen and held."},
  {"id":"uk-017","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that make a hard conversation under exhaustion worse, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Picking a calmer moment to talk","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Launching a serious talk at 3am, depleted","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Leading with 'I' and a soft start","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Opening with 'you never' accusations","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Asking 'is now an okay time?'","trick":false}],"why":"A depleted 3am launch and a 'you never' opener wreck a hard talk; a calm moment, a soft start and checking in help.","relearn":"A depleted 3am launch and a 'you never' opener wreck a hard talk; pick a calm moment and a soft start.","persona":"any","source":"help the talk"},
  {"id":"uk-018","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"clear the air","hook":"You're hurt by something Vivek said while frazzled, and resentment is building.","steps":[{"prompt":"It's been two days since he said you do nothing all day, and it still stings. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep a mental list of moments like this for later","then":"The list grows. You're ready for a fight he doesn't know is coming."},{"text":"Tell your friend everything, and skip telling him","then":"Your friend is supportive. Vivek doesn't know what he did."},{"text":"Say nothing and be extra polite until he notices","then":"He notices the politeness, not the reason. \"You okay?\" \"Yes.\""},{"text":"Wait for a calm moment and raise it gently","then":"You breathe out and pick your words. The sting stays, but now you have a plan.","best":true}],"why":"Raising it gently when calm, assuming good intent, lets him hear you."},{"prompt":"That evening, Vivek says he's worn out and wants an early night. The sting is still there. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Insist you talk tonight or you'll explode","then":"He rubs his face. The talk starts at 11pm and goes badly."},{"text":"Say fine and turn away, hurt","then":"He sees the turn. Neither of you says a word."},{"text":"Say he never has time for anything that matters to you","then":"He goes still. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"Say okay, and ask for ten minutes tomorrow","then":"He nods, relieved. \"Tomorrow after breakfast. I promise.\"","best":true}],"why":"Choosing a rested time keeps the talk about the hurt, not the tiredness."},{"prompt":"The next morning, you begin gently, and Vivek says he didn't mean it like that. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say a comment like that shows what he really thinks","then":"He gets defensive. \"That's not what I think.\""},{"text":"Say it's fine now and change the subject","then":"He looks relieved. The sting stays with you."},{"text":"Say he should watch his words more carefully","then":"He nods stiffly. It feels like a rule, not a repair."},{"text":"Say you believe him, then explain how it landed","then":"He listens. \"I'm sorry. I was tired and I said it badly.\"","best":true}],"why":"Assuming good intent while saying how it landed lets him repair it."},{"prompt":"Later, Vivek says something sharp about the untidy house. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap back with something just as sharp as his","then":"The house rings with two raised voices, and the baby starts crying."},{"text":"Leave the room and say nothing about it at all","then":"He hears the door close. He isn't sure what he did."},{"text":"Start keeping a list of things he's said","then":"You keep score. The list never gets discussed."},{"text":"Name the sting gently, and ask what's behind it","then":"He exhales. \"Sorry. I'm scared of missing things at work.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the sting gently, then asking what's behind it, keeps the repair going."}],"debrief":"Raise hurts gently when calm and assume good intent; don't store them up as evidence.","relearn":"Raise hurts calmly, assuming good intent; storing them as a case against your partner corrodes the team."},
  {"id":"uk-019","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: under sleep deprivation, assume your partner is tired, not against you. Worth holding onto?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Worth it","Tired not against me","I'll hold it"],"affirm":"Assuming good intent, that your partner is tired rather than against you, prevents most exhausted conflicts.","relearn":"Under exhaustion, assume your partner is tired, not against you; it defuses most conflicts.","persona":"any","source":"assume good intent"},
  {"id":"uk-020","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match a frazzled moment to a kinder response.","pairs":[{"left":"Partner snaps over something small","right":"\"We're both wiped, let's pause.\""},{"left":"You feel unseen","right":"\"I need a little appreciation too.\""},{"left":"A serious issue keeps colliding with chaos","right":"\"Let's set ten calm minutes for this.\""},{"left":"You said something harsh","right":"\"Sorry, that was the tiredness.\""},{"left":"Their silence worries you","right":"\"You okay? Rough day?\""}],"relearn":"Each frazzled moment has a kinder response, pause, appreciate, schedule, repair or check in, that protects the couple.","persona":"any","source":"kinder responses"},
  {"id":"uk-021","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"The mother is the natural default parent; the father just helps out.\"","myth":{"un":"The mother is the natural default parent; the father just helps out.","re":"Both parents are real parents; sharing the load fairly, not 'helping', is one of the strongest protectors of the relationship.","why":"Co-parenting is shared ownership, not assistance."},"relearn":"Both are real parents; fairly sharing the load, not 'helping out', strongly protects the relationship.","persona":"Vikram","source":"both real parents"},
  {"id":"uk-022","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A father looking after his own child is 'babysitting'.\"","myth":{"un":"A father looking after his own child is 'babysitting'.","re":"It's just parenting; a father caring for his child is doing his own job, not doing anyone a favour.","why":"You don't babysit your own kid."},"relearn":"A father caring for his child is parenting, not babysitting; it's his own job, not a favour.","persona":"Vikram","source":"not babysitting"},
  {"id":"uk-023","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: shared parenting that protects the couple, or a lopsided load that breeds resentment?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Both tracking and doing the baby's needs"},{"id":"b","text":"One parent silently carrying it all"},{"id":"c","text":"Splitting nights and tasks fairly"},{"id":"d","text":"One parent 'helping' only when asked"},{"id":"e","text":"Both holding the week's plan in mind"},{"id":"f","text":"Calling your own parenting 'babysitting'"}],"bins":[{"id":"shared","label":"Protective sharing","valence":"pos"},{"id":"lopsided","label":"Resentment-breeding","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"shared","b":"lopsided","c":"shared","d":"lopsided","e":"shared","f":"lopsided"},"relearn":"Tracking, fair splits and holding the plan protect you; solo, 'helping' and 'babysitting' breed resentment.","persona":"Divya","source":"shared vs lopsided"},
  {"id":"uk-024","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"renegotiate the load","hook":"You realise you're carrying almost all the baby load with Ashwin, and resentment is creeping in.","steps":[{"prompt":"It's 11pm and you're still sterilising bottles while Ashwin watches cricket. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep going and say nothing, since he's had a long day","then":"He watches the match. You scrub harder than you need to."},{"text":"Sigh loudly and clatter the bottles as a hint","then":"He glances over. \"Something wrong?\" \"No,\" you say."},{"text":"Ask him to finish the bottles, just for tonight","then":"He finishes them cheerfully. Nothing else about the load comes up."},{"text":"Say you're tired and ask to go over the whole load","then":"He mutes the TV. \"Okay. I probably don't see half of it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Making the whole load visible fixes the pattern, not just tonight's bottles."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Ashwin sees how tired you look and says, \"Just tell me what to do.\" What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept, and start assigning him tasks by text message","then":"Your list grows. You still hold the whole plan in your head."},{"text":"Say you'd rather he noticed what needs doing himself","then":"He frowns. \"I try. I just don't know what to notice.\""},{"text":"Say it doesn't matter and you'll just manage it yourself","then":"He looks relieved. You end the day with more resentment."},{"text":"Say you don't want to be the manager and list jobs together","then":"He pulls out his phone. \"Fine. Type them in. All of them.\"","best":true}],"why":"Listing the jobs together shows the whole load, not just the tasks you assign."},{"prompt":"That weekend, Ashwin asks how you're feeling about things at home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you're fine, so you don't start a fight tonight","then":"He nods. The resentment stays private."},{"text":"Tell him everything he's done wrong in the past month","then":"He gets defensive. The talk turns into a defence, not a plan."},{"text":"Say you'll be less tired soon, so this can all wait","then":"He agrees. Neither of you sets a date to change anything."},{"text":"Say the load feels lopsided and suggest a fairer split","then":"He listens. \"Let's redo it. What would fair look like?\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the lopsidedness calmly turns resentment into a plan."},{"prompt":"A month on, Ashwin has taken on a few jobs, and you're tempted to redo them your way. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Redo his work quietly after he's gone to bed","then":"He finds the fresh folding. He stops trying."},{"text":"Correct him every time he does it differently","then":"He sighs. \"Then do it yourself.\""},{"text":"Take the job back, since it's quicker","then":"The load is back on you by Friday."},{"text":"Let him do it his way, and give feedback if asked","then":"He finishes the laundry. The shirts are folded oddly, and he's proud.","best":true}],"why":"Letting him own the job in his own way is what makes the split stick."}],"debrief":"Surface the full baby load and renegotiate a fair split before resentment builds and bursts.","relearn":"Make the load visible and share it fairly; silent resentment is what eventually explodes."},
  {"id":"uk-025","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each kind of post-baby work to the fair approach.","pairs":[{"left":"Night feeds and wake-ups","right":"Shared or alternated, not one person's"},{"left":"The mental load of tracking needs","right":"Both notice and plan, not one manager"},{"left":"Chores and baby care","right":"Owned in fair shares, not 'helped' with"},{"left":"Doctor visits and appointments","right":"Either parent books and takes them"},{"left":"Daytime care on a workday","right":"Real parenting, never 'babysitting'"}],"relearn":"Nights, the mental load, chores, appointments and daytime care are fairer owned together.","persona":"any","source":"fair approach"},
  {"id":"uk-026","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Co-parenting and load-sharing are really mothers' topics.\"","myth":{"un":"Co-parenting and load-sharing are really mothers' topics.","re":"Co-parenting is for both parents directly; a father's involvement in the load is a top predictor of a happy couple.","why":"This is a both-parents conversation."},"relearn":"Co-parenting isn't a mothers-only topic; a father's real involvement strongly predicts a happy couple.","persona":"Vikram","source":"both-parent topic"},
  {"id":"uk-027","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: sharing the load isn't just practical, it says 'this is ours, not yours'. Feel the difference?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Feel it","Ours not yours","It matters"],"affirm":"Sharing the load is practical and symbolic: it tells your partner the children are 'ours', not their sole burden.","relearn":"Sharing the load says 'this is ours, not yours', which protects the couple as much as the practical help does.","persona":"Vikram","source":"ours not yours"},
  {"id":"uk-028","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that don't genuinely share the parenting load.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Owning whole tasks without being asked","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Waiting to be told each step","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Both holding the mental load","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Calling your own childcare 'babysitting'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Tracking the baby's needs yourself too","trick":false}],"why":"Waiting to be told and calling your own childcare 'babysitting' leave the managing on one parent; the rest genuinely share it.","relearn":"Waiting to be told and calling your own childcare 'babysitting' aren't sharing; owning tasks and the mental load is.","persona":"Vikram","source":"genuine sharing"},
  {"id":"uk-029","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"equal standard","hook":"At a family lunch, relatives praise Manoj lavishly for doing the basic parenting you do daily unremarked.","steps":[{"prompt":"An uncle says Manoj is a wonderful father for feeding the baby today. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree that he's a rare and wonderful kind of father","then":"The uncle beams. The praise doubles, and you feel smaller."},{"text":"Stay quiet and slip off to the kitchen to help serve","then":"You busy yourself with rotis. No one notices."},{"text":"Say sharply that nobody ever praises you like that","then":"The table goes silent. Manoj looks embarrassed, and someone whispers."},{"text":"Smile and say it's ordinary parenting you both do","then":"The uncle laughs, caught off guard. Manoj glances at you, thoughtful.","best":true}],"why":"A kind, plain reframe shows this is ordinary parenting you both do."},{"prompt":"Later, Manoj admits he enjoyed the praise but felt a bit awkward. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's fine and that you didn't mind the praise at all","then":"He relaxes. The pattern stays as it is."},{"text":"Tell him he should turn down praise like that next time","then":"He bristles. \"I'm not going to be rude to my uncle.\""},{"text":"Say you're hurt he didn't share the credit","then":"He gets defensive. \"I didn't ask for it.\""},{"text":"Say you'd like family to see you as equal parents","then":"He nods. \"Next time I'll say it too.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking him to share the frame makes it teamwork, not blame."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, a neighbour says you're lucky to have such a helpful husband. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Laugh and agree that you feel very lucky indeed","then":"The neighbour smiles. The idea sticks around."},{"text":"List everything he does to prove how much he helps","then":"You list his tasks. The neighbour nods, and it's a favour again."},{"text":"Say it's really about how tired you are these days","then":"She says all mothers are tired. The talk drifts to your fatigue, not the split."},{"text":"Say you both parent the baby and share the load","then":"She blinks, then nods. \"That's how it should be.\"","best":true}],"why":"Said calmly each time, the same reframe slowly changes the standard."}],"debrief":"Push back kindly on the double standard that praises a father for the parenting a mother does unremarked.","relearn":"Reset the gendered double standard kindly; the same parenting deserves the same."},
  {"id":"uk-030","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the fairer the load, the warmer the couple; this is the strongest protector there is. Worth prioritising?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","Fair load, warm couple","Prioritising it"],"affirm":"A fairly shared load is one of the strongest protectors of post-baby relationship satisfaction.","relearn":"The fairer the load, the warmer the couple; sharing it is the strongest protector there is.","persona":"any","source":"fair protects"},
  {"id":"uk-031","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"After a baby, you owe each other sex again by a set date.\"","myth":{"un":"After a baby, you owe each other sex again by a set date.","re":"There's no deadline; intimacy returns at the pace of both partners' physical and emotional readiness, never on a schedule or by obligation.","why":"Readiness leads, never a clock."},"relearn":"Intimacy after a baby has no deadline; it returns at both partners' physical and emotional readiness, never by obligation.","persona":"any","source":"no deadline"},
  {"id":"uk-032","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Wanting to wait for intimacy after birth means something is wrong with you.\"","myth":{"un":"Wanting to wait for intimacy after birth means something is wrong with you.","re":"Recovery is individual and bodies and feelings differ; needing time, often weeks or more, is normal and healthy, not a problem.","why":"Healing has its own timeline."},"relearn":"Needing time for intimacy after birth is normal and healthy; recovery is individual, not a problem with you.","persona":"Divya","source":"waiting is normal"},
  {"id":"uk-033","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a healthy way to rebuild closeness after kids, or pressure to avoid?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Going at a pace you both choose"},{"id":"b","text":"Pressuring a partner who isn't ready"},{"id":"c","text":"Reconnecting emotionally first"},{"id":"d","text":"Treating intimacy as an obligation owed"},{"id":"e","text":"Asking warmly how they're feeling"},{"id":"f","text":"Sulking until they give in"}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy reconnecting","valence":"pos"},{"id":"pressure","label":"Pressure to avoid","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"pressure","c":"healthy","d":"pressure","e":"healthy","f":"pressure"},"relearn":"A shared pace, emotional reconnection and warm asking are healthy; pressuring, obligation and sulking are not.","persona":"any","source":"healthy vs pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-034","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"patient closeness","hook":"You miss physical closeness, but Neha isn't ready yet after the birth.","steps":[{"prompt":"She squeezes your hand and says she still needs time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Mention it's been a while, though you're trying to be patient","then":"She goes quiet and says she'll try. Her shoulders tighten."},{"text":"Say you miss her and ask what closeness feels good now","then":"Neha smiles, relieved. \"A hug at night. Just that, for now.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and keep your distance until she brings it up","then":"You both tiptoe around each other, and the gap grows unspoken."},{"text":"Suggest picking a night so you both know when","then":"She nods slowly, but the date starts to loom over her week."}],"why":"Asking what feels okay rebuilds closeness without a clock."},{"prompt":"A few evenings later, she rests her head on your shoulder while the baby sleeps. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Turn it into a kiss to see if she's ready now","then":"She stiffens and pulls back. \"I'm just tired tonight.\""},{"text":"Say this feels like progress, so maybe soon","then":"She goes still. \"Please don't count this as a step.\""},{"text":"Stay there, hold her, and keep it simple","then":"You sit for a while. She exhales, and the room feels easier.","best":true},{"text":"Move away so you don't get your hopes up","then":"She notices you leave. \"Did I do something?\" she asks softly."}],"why":"Comfort with no agenda tells her closeness is safe, not a step toward something."},{"prompt":"Neha asks if you're okay with how slow this is. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you're fine, so she won't worry","then":"She half believes you. Neither of you names the ache you carry."},{"text":"Say it's okay and you're just glad it's getting closer","then":"Her smile flickers. \"Closer to what, exactly?\""},{"text":"Say honestly you miss her, and you're fine to wait","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Thank you for saying both.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say it's hard, and ask when she thinks she'll be ready","then":"She goes quiet. \"I wish I knew,\" she says, and the room feels smaller."}],"why":"Honest warmth without a deadline lets her answer without guilt."},{"prompt":"Some weeks later, Neha says she's feeling a bit more like herself. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask if that means this weekend could be your night","then":"Her smile fades. \"That's not what I meant.\""},{"text":"Suggest booking a hotel for a night away","then":"She says maybe. The plan hangs over her like an exam."},{"text":"Say you knew she'd get there eventually","then":"She frowns. \"Eventually?\""},{"text":"Say you're glad, and let her lead on what's next","then":"She smiles. \"I'll tell you when. Dinner first.\"","best":true}],"why":"Letting her lead keeps closeness safe as she feels more like herself."}],"debrief":"Rebuild closeness without pressure, letting intimacy return when both are ready.","relearn":"Reconnect gently and patiently; pushing an unready partner harms the very closeness you want."},
  {"id":"uk-035","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each small reconnection to what it rebuilds.","pairs":[{"left":"A short walk or chat together","right":"Everyday emotional closeness"},{"left":"A genuine hug, no agenda","right":"Safe physical warmth"},{"left":"Ten minutes of real attention","right":"Feeling like partners again"},{"left":"Laughing at an old shared joke","right":"The lightness the load buried"},{"left":"Tea together once baby sleeps","right":"A small daily ritual that's yours"}],"relearn":"Small reconnections, a walk, a hug, real attention, a laugh, shared tea, rebuild the closeness new-parent life erodes.","persona":"any","source":"small reconnections"},
  {"id":"uk-036","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Reconnecting means jumping straight back to how sex was before.\"","myth":{"un":"Reconnecting means jumping straight back to how sex was before.","re":"Intimacy after kids often rebuilds gradually and may look different; communication and patience matter more than going 'back to before'.","why":"New normal, not a rewind."},"relearn":"Intimacy after kids rebuilds gradually and may look different; patience and talking matter more than rushing back to before.","persona":"any","source":"new normal"},
  {"id":"uk-037","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"talk reconnect","hook":"You want to talk with Neha about reconnecting, without either of you feeling pressured.","setup":"Tonight, after the baby is asleep, you sit beside her.","steps":[{"prompt":"Neha looks up and asks: \"You wanted to talk?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's been long enough now, hasn't it? Aren't we due?\"","then":"She stiffens. \"Long enough for what?\""},{"text":"\"I've been thinking it's time we got back to normal, you know.\"","then":"\"Normal?\" she says. \"What do you mean by that?\""},{"text":"\"No pressure. I miss us. Can we find small ways to feel close?\"","then":"Her face eases. \"I miss us too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Never mind. It's nothing important. Forget I said anything.\"","then":"She pauses. \"It isn't nothing. Is this about us being close?\""}],"why":"A no-pressure 'I miss us' opener invites closeness instead of demanding it."},{"prompt":"Neha says: \"I do want to feel close. I just don't know what I'm ready for.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe we should just set a date and try it then.\"","then":"Her voice tightens. \"A date? Like a deadline?\""},{"text":"\"I'll wait as long as it takes. I just hope it's soon.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Please don't hope it's soon.\""},{"text":"\"Take your time. Just tell me roughly how long it'll be.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"I can't put a number on it.\""},{"text":"\"That's okay. What feels good right now? We'll start there.\"","then":"She thinks. \"Holding hands. Sitting close. That I can do.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking what feels good lets her set the pace."},{"prompt":"Neha asks quietly: \"What if it takes me a long time?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It won't. You'll feel better soon. I'm sure of it.\"","then":"She looks away. \"You can't know that.\""},{"text":"\"I'll try to be patient, but I'm only human too.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"I know. I'm sorry.\""},{"text":"\"Then it takes time. We'll stay close in other ways.\"","then":"She lets out a breath and leans into you. \"I needed to hear that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's not worry about it right now.\"","then":"She nods, but the worry stays in her eyes."}],"why":"Promising no deadline, and staying close, takes the pressure away."},{"prompt":"Neha says: \"Can we do this again in a few weeks? Just talking, like tonight.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sure. But maybe next time we should set a plan.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"A plan for what?\""},{"text":"\"Only if you don't mind me asking about progress.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Progress. Right.\""},{"text":"\"I'd rather wait until you bring it up.\"","then":"She nods. Weeks pass, and neither of you brings it up."},{"text":"\"Yes. Let's make it regular, whenever you like.\"","then":"She smiles. \"I'd like that. Sundays, maybe.\"","best":true}],"why":"A regular, open check-in keeps the door open without turning it into a review."}],"relearn":"A no-pressure, 'I miss us', ready-when-you-are opener invites closeness instead of demanding it."},
  {"id":"uk-038","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: closeness rebuilds best on safety and zero pressure; emotional first, the rest follows. Make sense?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Makes sense","Safety first","No pressure"],"affirm":"Closeness rebuilds best with emotional safety and no pressure; emotional reconnection tends to come first.","relearn":"Closeness rebuilds on safety and no pressure, with emotional reconnection usually leading the way.","persona":"any","source":"safety first"},
  {"id":"uk-039","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"get care","hook":"Being close since the birth has been painful, and you don't know if that's normal.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rohan has noticed you wince and asks gently if you're okay. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's just tiredness and change the subject","then":"He lets it go. The pain is still there, now unspoken between you."},{"text":"Tell him it hurts, but say you'll wait it out at home","then":"He nods, glad to know. Weeks pass with no answers, and the worry sits with you both."},{"text":"Tell him it hurts, and say you'll ask a clinician","then":"He listens. \"Okay. I'll come with you if you want.\"","best":true},{"text":"Search online forums and follow whatever they suggest","then":"You find ten different answers that contradict each other, and feel more anxious."}],"why":"Discomfort is common and treatable. A clinician can help, and you never have to push through pain."},{"prompt":"On a call, your aunt says everyone goes through it and you just bear it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Nod and decide you may be making too much of it","then":"You end the call doubting yourself. The pain doesn't change."},{"text":"Ask her to tell you what she did","then":"She gives you a home remedy and a long story. It doesn't answer whether this is normal."},{"text":"Snap that she has no idea what you're going through","then":"The call turns cold. She says she only meant to help."},{"text":"Thank her, and say you'll still see a clinician","then":"She sighs. \"Fine, beta. Ask the doctor, then.\" The call moves on.","best":true}],"why":"Advice to bear it isn't medical care. A clinician's check-up is."},{"prompt":"That evening, Rohan sees you looking up the clinic's number. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it isn't that serious and he shouldn't worry","then":"He studies your face. \"You wince when you think I'm not looking.\" You book it weeks later."},{"text":"Say you'll wait until the baby is older and there's time","then":"Months blur by in feeds and naps before you finally call the clinic."},{"text":"Book a slot now, and note what you want to ask","then":"He sits beside you as you pick a time. \"Write your questions down,\" he says.","best":true},{"text":"Ask him to drop it, since it makes you feel ashamed","then":"He backs off, worried. It's another month before you book it."}],"why":"Booking now gets you real care instead of worry, and it isn't shameful."},{"prompt":"At your check-up, the clinician gives you a plan that may take some weeks. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Follow the plan, but keep doing all the baby care yourself","then":"By the second week you're worn thin, and the plan starts to slip."},{"text":"Stop the plan once it feels a bit better","then":"Two weeks later, the discomfort returns."},{"text":"Skip the follow-up because the baby needs so much","then":"The appointment date passes while you're busy with feeds."},{"text":"Follow the plan, and let Rohan share the load while you heal","then":"He nods. \"I'll take the night feeds. Tell me what helps.\"","best":true}],"why":"Following through, with his support, keeps care on track and gives you room to heal."}],"debrief":"Postpartum discomfort is common and treatable; see a clinician rather than pushing through pain or staying silent.","relearn":"See a clinician about postpartum discomfort; it's common and helpable, not to be endured."},
  {"id":"uk-040","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: this is frank but yours to pace; nothing here is owed, and care is there if you need it. Feel respected?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Respected","Mine to pace","Good"],"affirm":"Intimacy after kids is yours to pace, with nothing owed and real care available; that's the respectful frame.","relearn":"Intimacy after kids is yours to pace, nothing owed, with care available if you need it.","persona":"any","source":"yours to pace"},
  {"id":"uk-041","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good parent disappears completely into their children.\"","myth":{"un":"A good parent disappears completely into their children.","re":"You're still a whole person, not only a parent; keeping some of your own self makes you a steadier, happier parent.","why":"A self isn't selfish; it sustains you."},"relearn":"Good parents don't vanish into their kids; keeping some of your own self makes you steadier and happier.","persona":"Divya","source":"still a person"},
  {"id":"uk-042","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Feeling like 'I used to be someone else' after a baby is shameful.\"","myth":{"un":"Feeling like 'I used to be someone else' after a baby is shameful.","re":"This identity shift is extremely common; naming it, and reclaiming small pieces of your old self, is healthy, not shameful.","why":"Naming it is the start of holding both."},"relearn":"Feeling 'I used to be someone else' after a baby is very common and nothing to be ashamed of.","persona":"Divya","source":"identity is common"},
  {"id":"uk-043","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: protects a parent's sense of self, or quietly erases it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Keeping one small thing that's just yours"},{"id":"b","text":"Zero time or identity outside the baby"},{"id":"c","text":"Partners covering so each gets a break"},{"id":"d","text":"Guilt for any need of your own"},{"id":"e","text":"Ten honest minutes with an old friend"},{"id":"f","text":"Believing wanting anything is selfish"}],"bins":[{"id":"protects","label":"Protects the self","valence":"pos"},{"id":"erases","label":"Erases it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"protects","b":"erases","c":"protects","d":"erases","e":"protects","f":"erases"},"relearn":"A small thing of your own, breaks and a friend protect the self; zero identity, guilt and 'wanting is selfish' erase it.","persona":"any","source":"protect or erase"},
  {"id":"uk-044","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"make space","hook":"Your partner is fading into being \"just a parent\" and looks lost in it.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner hasn't touched their sketchbook in months. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Assume giving things up is just what parents do","then":"They keep dropping small things, and nobody remarks on it."},{"text":"Buy a new sketchbook and say nothing more","then":"The sketchbook stays wrapped, with no time to open it."},{"text":"Offer a free Saturday morning for their sketching","then":"They blink, then smile. \"Really? Saturday? Yes.\"","best":true},{"text":"Tell them to make time when the baby sleeps","then":"The baby's naps go to laundry, and the sketchbook stays shut."}],"why":"Giving actual time protects the person you fell for."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, your partner says they miss who they were before the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say every parent gives things up","then":"They nod slowly, and the missing goes quiet again."},{"text":"Suggest they wait until the baby is older","then":"Years feel long, and they say nothing more."},{"text":"Offer a big weekend away to make up for it","then":"It's a lovely gift, but everyday life stays exactly the same."},{"text":"Ask what they miss, and make weekly time for it","then":"They talk about sketching and old friends, then smile. \"Weekly? Deal.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking what they miss, then making regular time for it, beats a one-off gift."},{"prompt":"Your mother visits and says a parent's time belongs to the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay silent to keep the peace","then":"Your partner hears it, and quietly stops asking for time."},{"text":"Argue that your mother is old-fashioned","then":"Your mother is hurt, and your partner feels caught in the middle."},{"text":"Say kindly that their own time helps the whole family","then":"Your mother frowns, then nods. \"Well, if it helps the baby.\"","best":true},{"text":"Agree for now, and promise to raise it again later","then":"Later never comes, and your partner says nothing."}],"why":"Kindly standing up for their own time in front of family shows it truly matters."}],"debrief":"Help your partner keep a piece of their own self; total self-erasure isn't what good parenting requires.","relearn":"Make space for your partner's own self; self-erasure isn't the price of good parenting."},
  {"id":"uk-045","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each parent's need to why it matters.","pairs":[{"left":"A little time that's their own","right":"Refuels them for the kids"},{"left":"Being asked how they are","right":"Counters the invisible-parent feeling"},{"left":"Support for an old interest","right":"Keeps their identity alive"},{"left":"Permission to rest without guilt","right":"Stops burnout before it builds"},{"left":"Being told they're doing well","right":"Quiets the 'I'm failing' fear"}],"relearn":"Own-time, being asked, support for an interest, guilt-free rest and reassurance all keep a parent whole.","persona":"Divya","source":"needs that matter"},
  {"id":"uk-046","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Asking how the parent is, not just the baby, is unnecessary.\"","myth":{"un":"Asking how the parent is, not just the baby, is unnecessary.","re":"New parents, especially the one recovering, often feel unseen; asking 'how are you?' is small and powerful.","why":"Being asked matters more than it seems."},"relearn":"Asking how the parent is, not only the baby, matters; new parents often feel unseen, and the question helps.","persona":"Divya","source":"ask how they are"},
  {"id":"uk-047","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can pour into your child and still keep a self; in fact, keeping one helps you parent. Sit right?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits right","Keep a self","It helps"],"affirm":"Keeping some of your own identity isn't at odds with good parenting; it sustains the parent you want to be.","relearn":"You can love your child fully and keep a self; in fact, keeping one makes you a steadier parent.","persona":"any","source":"keep a self"},
  {"id":"uk-048","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that fail a new parent's wellbeing. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Regularly checking how they're really doing","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Expecting them to run on empty","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Sharing care so they can rest","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"A good parent never needs a break\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Asking what would take a load off them","trick":false}],"why":"'Run on empty' and 'a good parent never needs a break' both deny a tired parent's real needs, the red flags.","relearn":"Expecting a parent to run on empty, or saying they should never need a break, fails their wellbeing.","persona":"Vikram","source":"support wellbeing"},
  {"id":"uk-049","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"postpartum support","hook":"Your partner has seemed low, tearful or detached for weeks after the birth.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says \"I'm fine\" again, though they look drained. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell them to just cheer up and get on with it","then":"They nod, and the sadness goes further out of sight."},{"text":"Give them space until they bring it up themselves","then":"Weeks pass, and they still haven't said a word."},{"text":"Say you've noticed, and gently suggest getting support","then":"Their eyes fill. \"I didn't know how to say it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask a relative to talk to them for you","then":"They feel talked about, and withdraw further."}],"why":"Gently naming what you've seen opens the door, and it is never their fault."},{"prompt":"They say they don't want to be a burden and should be happy. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree that they have a lot to be happy about","then":"Guilt grows on top of the low mood."},{"text":"Suggest reading a positive-thinking book together","then":"The book sits on the shelf, and the flatness stays."},{"text":"Change the subject to something lighter","then":"The moment passes, and they go quiet again."},{"text":"Say it's common, real and nothing to be ashamed of","then":"They exhale. \"Really? Everyone always says it's the happiest time.\"","best":true}],"why":"Normalising it lifts the shame that makes people hide low mood."},{"prompt":"Weeks on, their mood still hasn't lifted. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep waiting and hope it lifts by itself","then":"Another fortnight passes, and they are quieter than before."},{"text":"Make the call for them without telling them","then":"They find out, and feel their choice was taken away."},{"text":"Tell them they must see a doctor this week","then":"They feel pushed, and say they'll think about it."},{"text":"Help them reach a doctor or Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"They squeeze your hand. \"Okay. But you'll stay with me?\"","best":true}],"why":"Helping them reach support at their pace is care, not pressure."}],"debrief":"Persistent low mood after birth is real and helpable; encourage support (see Looking After You).","relearn":"Take post-birth low mood seriously; encourage support."},
  {"id":"uk-050","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"daily check-in","hook":"You and Anjali love each other, but the days blur past in chaos and you feel yourselves drifting.","steps":[{"prompt":"You want a simple way to stay connected. What do you set up?","options":[{"text":"A weekly date night, once the baby sleeps through the night","then":"Months on, the baby still wakes at night, and no date has happened."},{"text":"A long Sunday talk covering everything at once","then":"Sunday arrives and you're both too tired, so it slips."},{"text":"Nothing yet. Wait for a calmer stretch first","then":"The calm week doesn't come, and weeks pass with only logistics."},{"text":"A daily check-in: how you are, what you need, one thanks","then":"Anjali smiles. \"Three questions I can manage.\" You try it tonight.","best":true}],"why":"A tiny daily habit connects you even when the days are chaotic."},{"prompt":"One evening you and Anjali sit together for a few minutes, and she seems flat. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Jump straight to solutions for whatever she tells you","then":"Anjali says she just wanted to be heard, and goes quiet."},{"text":"Ask \"How are you, really?\" and listen without fixing","then":"Anjali's shoulders drop. \"Thank you for asking. I'm worn out.\"","best":true},{"text":"Move on to the logistics list for tomorrow","then":"The list gets done, and the flat mood is never touched."},{"text":"Say \"You seem off\" and wait to be told more","then":"Anjali says she's fine, and the moment slides by."}],"why":"Asking how she really is, and listening, is the point of a check-in."},{"prompt":"Anjali wonders whether a few minutes a day could really keep you close. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll plan a big weekend away to make up for it","then":"The weekend is exciting to plan and gets pushed to next month."},{"text":"Say it might not be worth it if she isn't keen","then":"Anjali feels the habit was hers to carry, and it fades."},{"text":"Say you'll drop it until things settle down","then":"Things don't settle, and the daily link goes quiet."},{"text":"Say small and steady beats big and rare, so do it daily","then":"Anjali nods. \"Okay. Tonight then.\"","best":true}],"why":"A few minutes each day is what keeps a couple connected, so small and steady counts."}],"debrief":"A short daily check-in with a question, a need and one thank-you keeps a couple connected through chaos.","relearn":"Build a tiny daily check-in; don't wait for a calm week that never arrives."},
  {"id":"uk-051","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"the toolkit","relearn":"The check-in, protected couple time, the help routes, fast repair and appreciation are the core tools for a couple after kids.","hook":"Match each tool to the job it does after kids.","pairs":[{"left":"The daily check-in","right":"Stay connected through the load"},{"left":"Protected couple time","right":"Keep the partnership alive"},{"left":"The help routes","right":"When strain needs more than the two of you"},{"left":"Fast repair after a snap","right":"Stop a tired moment becoming a grudge"},{"left":"A specific thank-you","right":"Make your partner feel truly seen"}]},
  {"id":"uk-052","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"protect couple time","hook":"In a busy joint household, you want to protect a little couple time without offending anyone.","setup":"Your mother finds you in the kitchen after dinner.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"You two look tired. Is something wrong?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's nothing, Ma. Please don't worry about us.\"","then":"She nods, and the tiredness goes unspoken again."},{"text":"\"We never get a moment alone in this house.\"","then":"Her smile stiffens. \"I only wanted to help.\""},{"text":"\"We love the help, and we'd treasure a short walk.\"","then":"Her face softens. \"A walk? Of course. Why didn't you say?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Could you ask Papa to give us some space, Ma?\"","then":"She looks unsure. \"Tell him yourself, beta. What space?\""}],"why":"Thanking her first, then asking for something small, keeps it warm."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your mother says: \"Dinner is at eight. Everyone sits together, as always.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Of course, Ma. We'll eat with everyone.\"","then":"She smiles, and any time for just the two of you slips away again."},{"text":"\"We can't always eat together. It's too much.\"","then":"She frowns. \"Too much? This is a family.\""},{"text":"\"We'll be there most nights. Tonight, a short walk first.\"","then":"She thinks about it. \"Then be back by eight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll skip dinner tonight. No need to wait for us.\"","then":"She says nothing, and the empty places at the table are noticed."}],"why":"Honouring the family meal while asking for one small slot keeps both."},{"prompt":"A week on, your mother sighs: \"Couples today want outings alone. In my time we never needed that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Times are different now, Ma. You really wouldn't understand.\"","then":"She turns away, hurt, and the talk ends there."},{"text":"\"You're right, Ma. We don't really need it either.\"","then":"She nods, satisfied, and you both stay in again."},{"text":"\"Honestly, Ma, it's my partner who wants time alone.\"","then":"Your partner is put on the spot, and your mother's look turns to them."},{"text":"\"We'd like a little, Ma. Your help frees up twenty minutes.\"","then":"She laughs softly. \"Twenty minutes. Fine, but take a shawl.\"","best":true}],"why":"Crediting her help while still asking for time shows it's a thank-you, not a complaint."}],"relearn":"A warm 'we love the help, and we'd treasure some couple time' protects the bond without a family rift."},
  {"id":"uk-053","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"support vs help","relearn":"Check-ins, shared load and small reconnects are healthy support; control, threats or put-downs need outside help.","hook":"Sort: a healthy support for the couple after kids, or strain that needs outside help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A weekly appreciation and check-in"},{"id":"b","text":"A partner controlling or frightening you"},{"id":"c","text":"Sharing the load and small reconnects"},{"id":"d","text":"Strain turning into put-downs or threats"},{"id":"e","text":"A fast repair after a tired snap"},{"id":"f","text":"Being isolated from your friends and family"}],"bins":[{"id":"support","label":"Healthy support","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Needs outside help","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"support","b":"help","c":"support","d":"help","e":"support","f":"help"}},
  {"id":"uk-054","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If things get hard after the baby, there's nothing to do but endure it.\"","myth":{"un":"If things get hard after the baby, there's nothing to do but endure it.","re":"There's a lot you can do, share the load, communicate, reconnect, and reach for help for postpartum wellbeing or serious strain.","why":"It's workable, not just endurable."},"relearn":"Hard times after a baby are workable: share the load, communicate, reconnect, and reach for help when needed.","persona":"any","source":"workable not endure"},
  {"id":"uk-055","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: protect the team, share the load, reconnect gently, and get help when it's beyond you two. Equipped?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Equipped","I've got this","Ready"],"affirm":"Protecting the team, sharing the load, reconnecting gently and reaching for help equip a couple for life after kids.","relearn":"You hold the kit: protect the team, share the load, reconnect gently, and get help when it's beyond the two of you.","persona":"any","source":"equipped"},
  {"id":"uk-056","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"reach out","hook":"Since the baby, you have felt more weighed down than tiredness explains.","steps":[{"prompt":"You are not sure if it needs more than time. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wait a few more weeks and see if it passes","then":"The weeks pass, and the heaviness stays."},{"text":"Ask a relative whether it is normal","then":"She says every new parent feels like this, and you feel dismissed."},{"text":"Tell Rohan, and book a clinician chat to check","then":"Rohan says he'd noticed too, and is relieved you said it.","best":true},{"text":"Tell Rohan you're worried, then leave it there","then":"Rohan nods. Nothing gets booked, and the worry keeps sitting."}],"why":"You don't have to be sure to reach out. Early help makes it easier."},{"prompt":"Soon after, Rohan sees you're struggling and asks what would help. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Name two things: some sleep cover, and someone to talk to","then":"Rohan writes both down. \"I'll take Thursday and Saturday nights.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"Nothing at all. I really don't want to be a burden.\"","then":"Rohan looks helpless, and the load stays where it was."},{"text":"Say \"Just act normal and don't fuss.\"","then":"Rohan acts normal, and you feel more alone."},{"text":"Say Rohan should ask your mother what to do about it","then":"Your mother means well but can't give the sleep or the listening."}],"why":"Asking for specific help is not being a burden. It lets people actually help."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, the heavy feeling hasn't lifted. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide this is simply how you are now","then":"The feeling settles in, and the days get harder to face."},{"text":"Push through it, because it must only be baby blues","then":"Weeks of pushing leave you emptier, and the baby feels far away."},{"text":"Tell your clinician, or call Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"The clinician listens, and you have a plan. You don't feel so alone.","best":true},{"text":"Stop mentioning it so nobody worries","then":"Silence makes the days longer, and Rohan senses the distance."}],"why":"Low mood that lingers is real and treatable. It is never your fault."}],"debrief":"You can reach out about a post-baby struggle while unsure; help for wellbeing or the couple is there early.","relearn":"Reach out early about a post-baby struggle; you don't have to be certain."},
  {"id":"uk-057","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"where it routes","relearn":"Postpartum wellbeing, fair load, any turn to control, recovery discomfort and ordinary strain each route to a clear place.","hook":"Match a post-baby need to where it routes.","pairs":[{"left":"Persistent low mood after birth","right":"Wellbeing support, Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"Sharing the load fairly","right":"The equal-parenting node"},{"left":"Strain turning to control or fear","right":"Respect at Home and 181"},{"left":"Lingering discomfort after birth","right":"A clinician, not endurance"},{"left":"Snappy, out of sync, exhausted","right":"The couple toolkit and repair"}]},
  {"id":"uk-058","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"sustainable couple","relearn":"Small regular connection, fair sharing and early help sustain a couple; a one-big-fix holiday and 'just endure it' don't.","hook":"Lensy: three keep a couple strong. Spot the two that secretly won't work.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Small, regular connection and fair sharing","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"One big holiday to 'fix' everything","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Reaching for help early when needed","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Deciding to just silently endure it","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A specific thank-you most days","trick":false}],"why":"A one-big-fix holiday and 'silently endure it' don't sustain a couple; small connection, fair sharing and early help do."},
  {"id":"uk-059","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"set the tone","hook":"Three weeks of broken nights with the new baby have worn you both thin.","setup":"Late one night, you both slump on the sofa.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"How are we going to get through the next few months?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll just survive it somehow and hope she sleeps soon.\"","then":"They laugh weakly. \"Somehow. Right.\" The worry stays."},{"text":"\"As a team. We share it, check in daily, stay kind, and ask for help.\"","then":"They breathe out. \"Okay. That sounds like a plan.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just tell me what needs doing and I'll do it.\"","then":"They nod, then realise they will be the one tracking everything."},{"text":"\"Once she sleeps through, it will all sort itself out.\"","then":"They nod, but nobody knows when that day will come."}],"why":"A shared plan turns survival into something you carry together."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I'm so tired I can barely think. How do we stay kind to each other?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll just try not to fight.\"","then":"They nod, but neither of you knows how."},{"text":"\"Let's skip the hard talks until she sleeps better.\"","then":"The unsaid things pile up for months."},{"text":"\"When we're wrecked, let's say so and not blame each other.\"","then":"They squeeze your hand. \"Deal. It's the tiredness, not us.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're the more patient one, so I'll follow your lead.\"","then":"They smile thinly, now carrying the mood of the whole house."}],"why":"Naming the tiredness aims the blame at exhaustion, not at each other."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"Your mother offered to help all next week. Should we say yes?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, we should manage on our own.\"","then":"They look relieved, then tired, thinking of the week ahead."},{"text":"\"Yes. Let's tell her exactly what would help.\"","then":"They smile. \"Meals and one afternoon nap. I'll ask her.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes, and let her run everything.\"","then":"Your mother happily takes over, and you both feel like guests at home."},{"text":"\"You decide. I'll go along with anything.\"","then":"They decide alone and carry the guilt of whichever way it goes."}],"why":"Accepting help with a clear ask keeps you both rested and in charge."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I think we're doing okay. Are we?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're doing badly. It's just not working for us.\"","then":"They deflate, and everything you have tried feels pointless."},{"text":"\"We're under load, not broken. Let's keep going.\"","then":"They smile. \"Under load. Not broken. Yes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We're fine. Don't overthink it.\"","then":"They nod, but the doubt stays."},{"text":"\"Only if we do exactly what the books say.\"","then":"They fetch the book, and every choice becomes homework."}],"why":"Under load is not broken, and saying so keeps you steady as a team."}],"relearn":"Setting a shared, kind, help-friendly tone turns survival into a plan you carry together."},
  {"id":"uk-060","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"reassure friend","hook":"Your friend Anita, a new mum, has gone quiet about her marriage lately.","setup":"You are having tea while her baby naps.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anita says: \"I think we're doomed. It's just so hard.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yeah. Kids do ruin relationships, sadly. It happens.\"","then":"She goes pale. \"So there's no hope?\""},{"text":"\"Don't think like that. Just be grateful you have each other.\"","then":"She nods, and swallows the fear."},{"text":"\"Maybe you two were never that strong to begin with.\"","then":"She stares at you, hurt."},{"text":"\"Lots of couples feel this. It's the load, not love.\"","then":"Her eyes fill. \"Really? Everyone I see looks fine.\"","best":true}],"why":"Telling her the strain is common, and about load, replaces dread with hope."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Anita says: \"I snapped at him last night and I feel awful.\"","options":[{"text":"\"He probably deserved it. Don't feel bad at all.\"","then":"She hesitates. The guilt stays, and so does the snap."},{"text":"\"That's exhaustion. Say sorry and name the tiredness.\"","then":"She breathes out. \"I can do that. He'll understand.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Never say sorry first. It weakens you in a marriage.\"","then":"She frowns. The snap stays between them."},{"text":"\"Ignore it and hope he forgets it by morning.\"","then":"She nods. It sits there unmended."}],"why":"A quick sorry that names the tiredness repairs a snap, and it's a skill anyone can use."},{"prompt":"Anita asks: \"How do I know it's normal and not something worse?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Honestly? If you're asking, something's probably wrong.\"","then":"She goes pale. The worry deepens."},{"text":"\"Just push through it. It always passes on its own if you ignore it.\"","then":"She nods, and hides how heavy things feel."},{"text":"\"Ask your mother-in-law. She will tell you what's normal.\"","then":"She winces. She doesn't want to ask her."},{"text":"\"Snappy is normal. If low mood lasts, see a clinician or call Tele-MANAS 14416.\"","then":"She nods. \"Okay. I'll watch for that, and I won't feel ashamed.\"","best":true}],"why":"Snappiness is normal. Lingering low mood is real, treatable, and never her fault."},{"prompt":"A week later, Anita says: \"Thank you for listening. Do you think we'll be okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I can't say. Some couples don't make it, you know.\"","then":"She sinks. The hope goes out of her."},{"text":"\"I do. It's the load, not your love, and skills help.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Skills. I can learn skills.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Of course. It's all fine. Nothing to worry about.\"","then":"She smiles, but she doesn't believe it."},{"text":"\"Only if you both stop complaining about every little thing.\"","then":"She stiffens. She wasn't complaining. She was hurting."}],"why":"Hope rests on the load being shared, and on skills you can learn together."}],"relearn":"Telling her the strain is common and about load, not love, replaces dread with hope she can act on."},
  {"id":"uk-061","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a fair expectation of early parenthood, or a setup for disappointment?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Less sleep and less couple time for a while"},{"id":"b","text":"Everything staying exactly as before"},{"id":"c","text":"Needing to adapt as a team"},{"id":"d","text":"Feeling like perfect parents instantly"},{"id":"e","text":"Some rough patches you work through"},{"id":"f","text":"Never disagreeing once baby arrives"}],"bins":[{"id":"fair","label":"Fair expectation","valence":"pos"},{"id":"setup","label":"Setup to hurt","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"fair","b":"setup","c":"fair","d":"setup","e":"fair","f":"setup"},"relearn":"Expecting less sleep and needing to adapt is fair; expecting nothing to change or instant perfection sets you up to hurt.","persona":"any","source":"fair expectations"},
  {"id":"uk-062","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: within the normal post-baby adjustment, or a reason to get support?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Occasional tears from sheer exhaustion"},{"id":"b","text":"Weeks of feeling numb or hopeless"},{"id":"c","text":"Bickering more while sleep-starved"},{"id":"d","text":"Frightening thoughts that worry you"},{"id":"e","text":"Feeling out of sync for a few weeks"},{"id":"f","text":"No joy in anything for a long stretch"}],"bins":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal adjustment","valence":"pos"},{"id":"support","label":"Get support","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"normal","b":"support","c":"normal","d":"support","e":"normal","f":"support"},"relearn":"Exhausted tears and more bickering are normal; weeks of numbness or frightening thoughts are reasons to get support.","persona":"Divya","source":"adjust vs support"},
  {"id":"uk-063","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the goal isn't to skip the hard part, it's to go through it as a team. Sit right?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits right","Through it together","As a team"],"affirm":"The aim isn't to avoid the hard transition but to move through it together, which keeps the couple whole.","relearn":"The goal isn't skipping the hard part, but going through it as a team.","persona":"any","source":"through it together"},
  {"id":"uk-064","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"quick repair","hook":"You snapped at your partner Sheetal over nothing on no sleep, and you want to repair.","setup":"The kitchen is quiet, and she stirs tea with her back to you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sheetal says: \"You didn't have to speak to me like that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, you started it, if we're being honest.\"","then":"Her mouth tightens. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"\"I'm sorry I snapped. That was exhaustion, not you.\"","then":"She turns. \"Thank you for saying it. That stung.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It was nothing. Why are you making it a big deal?\"","then":"Her voice drops. \"It wasn't nothing to me.\""},{"text":"\"I'm just so tired. Anyone would have snapped.\"","then":"She nods, but the sorry never came."}],"why":"A quick, honest sorry that names the exhaustion mends a snap before it hardens."},{"prompt":"A few hours later, Sheetal says: \"I'm still thinking about earlier.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I've said my piece. What else do you want from me?\"","then":"She stiffens. \"Never mind.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about it and just move on.\"","then":"She nods, and the sore spot stays."},{"text":"\"I'll make it up to you with something nice.\"","then":"She smiles thinly. \"That's not what I need.\""},{"text":"\"That makes sense. What would help you feel better?\"","then":"She thinks. \"Just knowing you'll try to pause next time.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking what would help shows you take her hurt seriously, and lets her say what she needs."},{"prompt":"Two nights later, Sheetal snaps: \"Where's the bottle? I asked twice!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Now you know how it feels to be snapped at.\"","then":"Her eyes flash. \"Really?\""},{"text":"\"Tiredness talking. Here it is. Sit for a second.\"","then":"She breathes out. \"Sorry. I'm frazzled.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't take that tone with me. Not tonight.\"","then":"She snaps back louder. The bottle is forgotten."},{"text":"\"Fine. Do it yourself then. I'm done for tonight.\"","then":"She grabs the bottle herself. The evening sinks."}],"why":"Assuming a snap is tiredness lets you both cool down."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Sheetal says: \"Can we agree a faster way to repair?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sure. Just don't be the one who snaps first.\"","then":"She frowns. \"That's not a plan.\""},{"text":"\"I'll say sorry if you say it too, at once.\"","then":"She hesitates. The deal sounds like a trade."},{"text":"\"We don't need a rule. We'll just know when it happens.\"","then":"She looks doubtful. \"Knowing isn't the same as doing it.\""},{"text":"\"Yes. Whoever snaps says sorry within the hour.\"","then":"She smiles. \"And names the tiredness. Deal.\"","best":true}],"why":"A simple shared rule makes repair quick, whoever snaps."}],"relearn":"A quick, honest repair, naming the exhaustion, mends a snap before it hardens into distance."},
  {"id":"uk-065","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a soft start to a hard talk, or a harsh one that backfires?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm struggling and need your help with...\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You never do anything around here.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Can we find a calm minute to sort this?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"What is wrong with you?\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"I felt a bit alone today.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"This mess is entirely your fault.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"soft","label":"Soft start","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh start","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"soft","b":"harsh","c":"soft","d":"harsh","e":"soft","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"'I'm struggling', 'a calm minute' and 'I felt alone' are soft starts; 'you never', 'what's wrong with you' and 'your fault' backfire.","persona":"any","source":"soft vs harsh"},
  {"id":"uk-066","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each exhausted-couple trap to the skill that defuses it.","pairs":[{"left":"Snapping over nothing","right":"Pause and name the tiredness"},{"left":"Keeping score of who did more","right":"Appreciate instead of tally"},{"left":"Letting hurts pile up","right":"Repair quickly and gently"},{"left":"Reading silence as contempt","right":"Assume good intent and ask"},{"left":"Fighting at 3am","right":"Park it for a rested moment"}],"relearn":"Snapping, scorekeeping, piling hurts, misreading silence and 3am fights are each defused by a specific tired-couple skill.","persona":"any","source":"defusing traps"},
  {"id":"uk-067","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"break the loop","hook":"You and Meghna keep having the same fight in circles, both too tired to resolve it.","steps":[{"prompt":"It's midnight, and the same fight starts again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep going, since you're already in it","then":"The same points repeat. It ends at one, unresolved."},{"text":"Say you're too tired and go to sleep angry","then":"You sleep badly. The fight is still there in the morning."},{"text":"Text each other your sides and stop talking","then":"You trade messages at 1am. Each one sounds colder than the last."},{"text":"Agree to park it and book ten calm minutes","then":"She nods. \"Tomorrow evening. After the baby's down.\"","best":true}],"why":"Parking a circular fight for a rested moment stops it feeding on tiredness."},{"prompt":"The next evening, you both have a few quiet minutes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Restart with what she did last night","then":"She defends herself. You're back in the loop."},{"text":"Use the time to catch up on chores instead","then":"The chores get done. The fight is still waiting."},{"text":"Ask her to go first, then correct her","then":"Her sentence gets interrupted three times."},{"text":"Start with what you both want before the old points","then":"She says she wants sleep. So do you. The fight looks smaller.","best":true}],"why":"Starting from shared goals breaks the loop."},{"prompt":"Meghna suggests a small fix that isn't what you had in mind. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it won't work and explain why","then":"Her face falls. The loop begins again in a new form."},{"text":"Agree just to end the talk, then ignore it","then":"You say yes. By Friday, nothing has changed."},{"text":"Say you'll try it if she also tries yours","then":"She tenses. \"It's not a trade.\""},{"text":"Try her fix for a week, then review it together","then":"She smiles. \"A week. Then we compare notes.\"","best":true}],"why":"Testing a fix for a week, then reviewing it, keeps you both invested."}],"debrief":"Break a circular tired fight by parking it for a calm, rested ten minutes, not re-running it depleted.","relearn":"Park circular tired fights for a rested moment; midnight re-runs never resolve them."},
  {"id":"uk-068","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"own my half","hook":"You want to be an equal parent, not a helper, and you tell Jaya how you see it.","setup":"After dinner, you sit down with her while the baby sleeps.","steps":[{"prompt":"Jaya says: \"You've been helping a lot lately. Thank you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just tell me what you want me to do.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I'd rather not have to decide it all.\""},{"text":"\"No problem. Happy to help when I can.\"","then":"She nods. The word 'help' stays in the room."},{"text":"\"It's the least I can do after all you've done.\"","then":"Her smile is polite. \"It's not a debt.\""},{"text":"\"These jobs are ours, not yours. I'll own my half.\"","then":"She smiles, surprised. \"Your half. What does that look like?\"","best":true}],"why":"Calling the work yours as well, not help, makes you a co-parent."},{"prompt":"Jaya asks: \"Which parts do you want to take?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Whatever's left over that you don't want to do.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow. \"The leftovers. Is that really a half?\""},{"text":"\"I'll do a bit of everything as it comes.\"","then":"She frowns. \"A bit of everything is what I do now.\""},{"text":"\"Anything, as long as you remind me.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"Then I'm still the reminder.\""},{"text":"\"Bath, bedtime and doctor visits, start to finish.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Doctor visits too? Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"Taking whole jobs, start to finish, lifts the managing off her."},{"prompt":"Jaya asks: \"What happens when you're tired and don't feel like it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then you can take it, since you're used to it.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"Used to it. That's the problem.\""},{"text":"\"I won't be tired. I'll manage.\"","then":"She half smiles. \"You will be. We all are.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not plan for that. We'll see.\"","then":"She nods, unconvinced. She knows how 'we'll see' goes."},{"text":"\"I'll still do it, and tell you if I hit my limit.\"","then":"She relaxes. \"That I can work with. Same for me.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning it includes saying honestly when you hit your limit."}],"relearn":"Claiming the work as shared and owning it unasked turns a 'helper' into a true co-parent."},
  {"id":"uk-069","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a co-parenting move that shares the load, or one that quietly dumps it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tracking the baby's needs yourself too"},{"id":"b","text":"Waiting to be handed a task list"},{"id":"c","text":"Taking whole nights in turn"},{"id":"d","text":"Calling your own parenting 'helping out'"},{"id":"e","text":"Booking the check-ups off your own bat"},{"id":"f","text":"Stepping in only for the fun bits"}],"bins":[{"id":"share","label":"Shares the load","valence":"pos"},{"id":"dump","label":"Dumps it on one","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"share","b":"dump","c":"share","d":"dump","e":"share","f":"dump"},"relearn":"Tracking needs, taking whole nights and booking check-ups share the load; waiting for a list, 'helping out' and cherry-picking dump it on one.","persona":"Vikram","source":"share or dump"},
  {"id":"uk-070","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Meena","source":"own a domain","hook":"Your partner Rahul keeps managing everything and won't hand any of it over, but you want to take a real share.","setup":"He's already reaching for the baby's towel.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rahul says: \"It's fine. I'll do bedtime. I know how she likes it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tell me if you need anything.\"","then":"He says he will. He doesn't, and he does bedtime again."},{"text":"\"Okay, you're better at it anyway.\"","then":"He nods, pleased. You've handed it back."},{"text":"\"You always take over. Why don't you trust me?\"","then":"He stiffens. \"That's not fair. I'm just being efficient.\""},{"text":"\"Let me own bath and bedtime, start to finish.\"","then":"He hesitates. \"Everything? The way she likes it?\"","best":true}],"why":"Offering to own a whole job lifts the managing off him too."},{"prompt":"Rahul says: \"I'm worn out, honestly. But she only settles one way.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine, do it your way. I'll stay out of it.\"","then":"He nods, then hesitates. \"Though I could really use a night off.\""},{"text":"\"She'll survive one bad night.\"","then":"He frowns. \"That's not funny. I'm the one up with her.\""},{"text":"\"Write it all down and I'll follow it exactly.\"","then":"He spends an hour on instructions. You're following his plan, not owning yours."},{"text":"\"Teach me your routine once, then I'll adapt.\"","then":"He sighs, then smiles. \"Okay. Sit down. Step one.\"","best":true}],"why":"Learning his routine once, then adjusting it, lets you truly take over."},{"prompt":"Rahul asks: \"And what if you forget something she needs?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then you can just remind me when it comes up.\"","then":"He nods. He's still the reminder."},{"text":"\"I won't forget. Honestly, don't worry so much about it.\"","then":"He purses his lips. \"You will, sometimes.\""},{"text":"\"Then maybe it's just easier if you keep doing it.\"","then":"He sighs and takes the baby. You're back where you started."},{"text":"\"Then I'll fix it myself. That's how I'll learn.\"","then":"He exhales. \"Fine. I'll sit in the other room.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning it includes owning small mistakes, so he can let go."}],"relearn":"Offering to own a whole domain end-to-end lifts the mental load, not just the task, off your partner."},
  {"id":"uk-071","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each parent's contribution to the respect it deserves.","pairs":[{"left":"Night feeds and soothing","right":"Real, tiring work, equally valued"},{"left":"Tracking jabs, food, supplies","right":"The mental load, shared not solo"},{"left":"Daytime care while the other works","right":"Parenting, not 'babysitting'"},{"left":"Planning the week's logistics","right":"Invisible labour that truly counts"},{"left":"Cleaning up after every meal","right":"A shared chore, not one person's job"}],"relearn":"Night care, the mental load, daytime care, planning and cleaning are all real parenting work that deserves equal respect.","persona":"any","source":"respect the work"},
  {"id":"uk-072","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"zero pressure","hook":"You want your recovering partner Anjali to know there's zero pressure about intimacy.","setup":"Tonight she starts to apologise again, looking down at her hands.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anjali says: \"I'm sorry I'm not ready. I know it's hard for you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Don't be sorry. Just tell me when you are.\"","then":"She nods, but 'tell me when' sounds like something she owes."},{"text":"\"I'm managing. It's fine.\"","then":"She studies you. \"You don't sound fine.\""},{"text":"\"There's no clock here. We go at your pace.\"","then":"Her shoulders drop. \"Really? You're not counting?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"So when do you think things will go back to normal?\"","then":"She goes still. \"I don't know. I wish I did.\""}],"why":"Saying there's no clock lifts the guilt and makes closeness feel safe."},{"prompt":"Anjali asks: \"Do you miss it a lot?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, a lot. But I'm not complaining about it at all.\"","then":"She winces. \"You are, a little.\""},{"text":"\"Not at all. I never think about it.\"","then":"She narrows her eyes. \"Really?\""},{"text":"\"Let's just say I'll be ready whenever you are.\"","then":"Her jaw tightens. \"That sounds like a countdown.\""},{"text":"\"I miss you. Whatever closeness works is enough.\"","then":"She half smiles. \"I miss you too. I just don't know how to start.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying you miss her, not the act, keeps closeness pressure-free."},{"prompt":"Anjali says: \"Could we just hold each other tonight?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sure. Maybe we'll see where it goes from there?\"","then":"She stiffens. \"Only holding. Please.\""},{"text":"\"Of course. I think this is a really good start.\"","then":"Her smile flickers. \"Start toward what?\""},{"text":"\"Yes, thanks. I was starting to worry a little.\"","then":"She looks stricken. \"I didn't know you were worried.\""},{"text":"\"Yes. Just that, and nothing more unless you ask.\"","then":"She curls into you. \"Thank you for making it easy.\"","best":true}],"why":"Agreeing to just hold each other, with nothing expected, builds trust."}],"relearn":"A clear 'no clock, your pace' reassurance removes pressure and makes safe closeness possible."},
  {"id":"uk-073","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: rebuilds closeness safely, or pressures a recovering partner?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Letting readiness lead, both ways"},{"id":"b","text":"Hinting they're overdue"},{"id":"c","text":"Cuddles and talk with no agenda"},{"id":"d","text":"Sulking when they're not ready"},{"id":"e","text":"Saying there's truly no rush"},{"id":"f","text":"Comparing to other couples"}],"bins":[{"id":"safe","label":"Safe rebuilding","valence":"pos"},{"id":"pressure","label":"Pressures them","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"safe","b":"pressure","c":"safe","d":"pressure","e":"safe","f":"pressure"},"relearn":"Readiness, agenda-free affection and 'no rush' rebuild safely; hinting overdue, sulking or comparing is pressure.","persona":"any","source":"safe vs pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-074","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: feeling close again often starts with a laugh or a hug, long before anything else. Reassuring?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Reassuring","Starts small","No rush"],"affirm":"Reconnection usually starts with small emotional closeness, a laugh or a hug, well before physical intimacy.","relearn":"Feeling close again often starts with a laugh or a hug, long before anything else.","persona":"Divya","source":"starts small"},
  {"id":"uk-075","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that pressure a recovering partner.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"We go at your pace, truly.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"It's been long enough now, hasn't it?\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I just like being near you.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Other couples are back to normal by now.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Tell me whenever you feel ready.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'It's been long enough' and 'other couples are back to normal' both pressure; the rest follow her readiness.","relearn":"Lines that invoke a deadline or compare you to other couples are pressure; readiness-led lines are not.","persona":"any","source":"pressure-free rebuild"},
  {"id":"uk-076","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"give a break","hook":"Your partner hasn't had a moment to themselves in weeks, and you want to give them a real break.","setup":"They pace the room with the baby on their shoulder.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I'd love a shower that lasts more than three minutes.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You can rest once everything's done.\"","then":"They laugh tiredly. \"Everything is never done.\""},{"text":"\"Go take two hours that are just yours. I've got the baby.\"","then":"They stare. \"Two hours? Really?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Sure. Just finish the bottles first, then go.\"","then":"They finish the bottles, and by then the break has gone."},{"text":"\"Take an hour, but text me if she cries.\"","then":"They spend the hour half listening to their phone."}],"why":"A no-strings break says \"you matter too\" and gives them real rest."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner asks: \"If I took a proper break, would you really not mind?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's fine. Just don't make it a habit.\"","then":"They wince, and the warmth drains from the offer."},{"text":"\"Honestly, yes, but I'll cope.\"","then":"They hear the sigh and take much less than they need."},{"text":"\"No trouble. Tell me what to do and I'll do it.\"","then":"They start listing instructions, and the break shrinks to a briefing."},{"text":"\"I'm not doing you a favour. She's my child too.\"","then":"Their eyes soften. \"Okay. Then I'll go.\"","best":true}],"why":"Parenting your own child is not a favour, so the break has no debt attached."},{"prompt":"On Saturday, your partner comes back from a short break and asks: \"Was she okay? Was she fussy?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She was a nightmare. Don't make me do that again.\"","then":"Their glow fades, and they dread the next time."},{"text":"\"I did everything perfectly, obviously.\"","then":"They smile, but your boast lands where their rest was."},{"text":"\"She was fine. Tell me about your break.\"","then":"They sit down and tell you about the tea and the quiet.","best":true},{"text":"\"Never mind that. Look at the state of the house.\"","then":"The house steals their rest."}],"why":"Turning to their break, not your effort, keeps the rest theirs."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says: \"Next time I want to use my break to nap. Is that lame?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe do something more useful with it?\"","then":"They sigh, and go to fold laundry."},{"text":"\"Sure, but tidy up before you do.\"","then":"They sit down to tidy, and the nap never comes."},{"text":"\"Make it a proper outing. A nap is a waste.\"","then":"They agree, and feel guilty about napping at all."},{"text":"\"Not at all. Your break, your choice.\"","then":"They smile and lie down. The house is silent.","best":true}],"why":"Letting them choose is what makes a break truly theirs."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"Do you want a break of your own next?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, I'm fine. You need it more than I do.\"","then":"They nod, and your own rest never gets planned."},{"text":"\"Only if you've done everything at home first.\"","then":"The condition sours the offer."},{"text":"\"Let me see whether I'm too busy.\"","then":"Busy wins, and your own break slips away."},{"text":"\"Yes. I'd like Thursday, if that works.\"","then":"They smile. \"Thursday. It's yours.\"","best":true}],"why":"When both parents get rest, no one runs on empty."}],"relearn":"A real, no-strings break that says 'you matter too' refuels your partner and protects the person they are."},
  {"id":"uk-077","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"ask about the parent","hook":"A relative keeps asking only about the baby, never how new mother Divya is doing.","setup":"Your aunt bustles in and peers into the cot.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Look at that face. Is she feeding well? Sleeping through?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes, baby's fine. That's all that matters.\"","then":"Your aunt beams, and Divya, nearby, looks down at her hands."},{"text":"\"Divya's exhausted, but she'd never say so.\"","then":"Divya flushes, wishing you'd let her answer."},{"text":"\"She's great. And how is Divya? She's been amazing.\"","then":"Your aunt blinks. Divya looks up, surprised. \"Tired, honestly.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ask her yourself. I really don't know.\"","then":"Your aunt shrugs, and Divya stays unasked."}],"why":"Turning the question toward the parent shows her she counts too."},{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Divya is looking a bit thin. Is she eating properly?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's fine. She's just being modest.\"","then":"Divya nods and goes back to her tea."},{"text":"\"Let's ask her. Divya, how are you feeling today?\"","then":"Divya smiles slowly. \"Honestly? Tired, but glad you asked.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"She's wasting away. Feed her more, Aunty.\"","then":"Divya winces. It felt like being discussed, not asked."},{"text":"\"Don't worry about her. Look at the baby.\"","then":"Your aunt turns back to the cot, and Divya is unseen again."}],"why":"Asking the person directly makes her a participant, not a topic."},{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"New mothers today talk of tiredness. In my day, we had no time for it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Times have changed, Aunty. You wouldn't understand.\"","then":"Your aunt bristles, and Divya's rest is forgotten."},{"text":"\"You're right. Divya just needs to toughen up.\"","then":"Divya's smile freezes."},{"text":"\"That sounds hard. Divya could use rest and a listener.\"","then":"Your aunt softens. \"Hm. I'll hold the baby. Go, beta.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone is tired. It's just part of it.\"","then":"Your aunt nods, satisfied, and nothing shifts."}],"why":"Naming the need for rest, kindly, gets the help you want."},{"prompt":"At the next family lunch, your aunt asks Divya: \"Why so quiet, beta? Aren't you happy?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's just shy today, Aunty. Ignore her.\"","then":"Divya's voice stays unheard."},{"text":"\"Cheer up, Divya! Just look how lucky you are.\"","then":"Divya's smile tightens."},{"text":"\"Aunty, she's fine. Don't be so nosy.\"","then":"Your aunt huffs off, and Divya feels responsible for the tension."},{"text":"\"Happy and tired can go together, Aunty. Divya?\"","then":"Divya breathes out. \"Both, honestly.\" Your aunt nods. \"Fair enough.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying both can be true, then letting Divya answer, lifts the pressure to perform happiness."}],"relearn":"Redirecting attention to how the parent is counters the invisible-parent feeling that wears new parents down."},
  {"id":"uk-078","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: keeps a parent whole, or erases them into the role?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Holding onto one small personal thing"},{"id":"b","text":"Guilt over any time for yourself"},{"id":"c","text":"Partners trading time off"},{"id":"d","text":"Believing needs make you a bad parent"},{"id":"e","text":"Keeping a name beyond 'baby's mum/dad'"},{"id":"f","text":"Dropping every friendship you had"}],"bins":[{"id":"whole","label":"Keeps them whole","valence":"pos"},{"id":"erase","label":"Erases them","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"whole","b":"erase","c":"whole","d":"erase","e":"whole","f":"erase"},"relearn":"A personal thing, trading time off and a name of your own keep a parent whole; guilt, 'needs are bad' and dropping all friends erase them.","persona":"Meena","source":"whole vs erased"},
  {"id":"uk-079","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each sign to what a depleted parent really needs.","pairs":[{"left":"Snappy and tearful from no rest","right":"A genuine break and support"},{"left":"Feeling invisible behind the baby","right":"To be seen and asked about"},{"left":"Losing touch with who they are","right":"Space to reclaim a little self"},{"left":"Low and flat for weeks on end","right":"Support like Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"Cut off from every old friend","right":"One reconnection, gently made"}],"relearn":"A depleted parent needs rest, to be seen, space to keep some self, help if low for weeks, and a friend back.","persona":"Divya","source":"what they need"},
  {"id":"uk-080","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt-free break","hook":"You feel guilty for wanting an hour to yourself away from the baby.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner offers to take the baby for an hour. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say no thanks, and stay to show your devotion","then":"The hour passes, and you feel more tired than before."},{"text":"Take the hour but spend it on chores","then":"The hour disappears into laundry, and you rest no more."},{"text":"Take the hour and do something just for you","then":"You sit in the park alone, and you return calmer.","best":true},{"text":"Take it, but check on the baby every ten minutes","then":"You barely enjoy the walk, and come back tense."}],"why":"An hour that is truly yours refuels you, and that's good for everyone."},{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Back in my day, we never left our babies for a second.\" What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Feel ashamed and cancel your next hour","then":"The hour disappears, and the guilt gets bigger."},{"text":"Snap that times have changed and she should stay out of it","then":"Your aunt goes cold, and the room turns tense."},{"text":"Say kindly that a rested you is better for the baby","then":"Your aunt raises her brows, then sighs. \"Hm. Maybe.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll only go when the baby is asleep","then":"You end up going nowhere, since the baby is rarely asleep for long."}],"why":"Explaining calmly that rest helps the baby answers the guilt without a fight."},{"prompt":"A pang of guilt hits as you get ready to take an hour for yourself. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Skip it this week, just to be safe","then":"The week passes, and you are more tired than ever."},{"text":"Go, but promise to make it up with extra chores","then":"You return and pay the debt in hours you don't have."},{"text":"Keep asking your partner if they mind","then":"They say fine each time, and the asking wears on you both."},{"text":"Remind yourself the hour refuels you, and go","then":"You come back with energy, and the baby hasn't noticed a thing.","best":true}],"why":"Treating the hour as refuelling, not a favour, keeps it going."}],"debrief":"Taking some time for yourself isn't a failure of devotion; a refuelled parent parents better.","relearn":"Take the break without guilt. Self-care refuels the parent and doesn't betray the child."},
  {"id":"uk-081","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"nightly ritual","hook":"You want to start a tiny nightly ritual so the two of you stay connected.","setup":"Tonight you both finally sit down after the baby settles.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"We barely talk anymore, do we?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll talk properly once life calms down a little.\"","then":"They nod, though the calm feels very far away."},{"text":"\"Two minutes each night? One hard thing, one good thing.\"","then":"They think. \"Two minutes I can do. You go first tonight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We talk plenty. It's just mostly about the baby now.\"","then":"They go quiet. \"That's not the same thing.\""},{"text":"\"Let's plan a big weekend away as soon as we can manage it.\"","then":"They smile at the idea, but nothing changes tonight."}],"why":"A tiny, doable ritual works now, without waiting for a calm that may never come."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner is half asleep by nine and says: \"I'm too tired to talk about anything tonight.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then let's drop it until the baby sleeps through.\"","then":"They mumble goodnight, and the talking waits another night."},{"text":"\"Then just one good thing from me, and you sleep.\"","then":"They smile into the pillow. \"Go on. Mine is this pillow.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're always too tired for us these days, aren't you.\"","then":"They flinch. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. I'll tell you about my whole day anyway then.\"","then":"You talk while they nod off, and neither of you feels any closer."}],"why":"Shrinking it to one good thing keeps the habit alive on the worst nights, without asking for energy."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"Honestly, a nightly check-in feels a bit forced to me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe it just isn't for us. Let's forget the idea.\"","then":"They shrug, and the little bridge never gets built."},{"text":"\"It only feels forced because you never really try.\"","then":"They stiffen. \"I'm trying. I'm just tired.\""},{"text":"\"Then let's do a proper one, with ten deep questions.\"","then":"The ritual grows to twenty minutes, and by Friday nobody has the energy."},{"text":"\"Awkward at first, maybe. Two minutes, and it's ours.\"","then":"They laugh. \"Okay. Awkward and ours.\"","best":true}],"why":"Small awkward habits become natural with repetition, so keep it light and keep going."}],"relearn":"A tiny, doable nightly ritual keeps the couple connected now, instead of waiting for a calm that never comes."},
  {"id":"uk-082","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Meena","source":"open the door","hook":"Your partner seems to be struggling with more than tiredness, and you want to help.","setup":"You find them alone in the dark nursery.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says quietly: \"I don't know why I feel so flat lately.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You'll be fine. Everyone copes in their own way.\"","then":"They nod slowly and stop mentioning it."},{"text":"\"Maybe you just need a few proper nights' sleep.\"","then":"They agree, though sleep has never touched the flatness."},{"text":"\"I'm worried how heavy this feels. Shall we talk to someone?\"","then":"They look up, teary. \"Together? Yes. I didn't know how to ask.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Try to think of everything you have to be happy about.\"","then":"They look down. \"I know. That's the problem.\""}],"why":"A gentle, worried opening invites help without shame or dismissal."},{"prompt":"Days later, your partner says: \"I don't want to be a burden. Other people manage.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then let's not make a big thing of it, and just carry on.\"","then":"They relax for a moment, and the subject closes again."},{"text":"\"I'll sort it all out and just tell you what to do next.\"","then":"They feel handled, not asked, and the door half closes."},{"text":"\"You're not a burden. Needing help is normal. Together, okay?\"","then":"Their shoulders drop. \"Together. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's wait another month or so and see if it passes.\"","then":"A month passes, and the flatness is still there."}],"why":"Saying it's normal and shared lifts the shame that keeps people from asking."},{"prompt":"A week on, your partner asks: \"If it is something bad, who would we even talk to?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's ask my aunt. She raised four kids and will know what to do.\"","then":"They wince at the thought of the whole family knowing."},{"text":"\"Let's search online and work it out by ourselves at home.\"","then":"Hours of forums leave them more worried and no closer to help."},{"text":"\"It's nothing bad. I'm sure you are just overthinking it all.\"","then":"They nod, and the fear goes back inside."},{"text":"\"Our doctor, or Tele-MANAS 14416. It's treatable.\"","then":"They breathe out. \"Okay. Let's call together tomorrow.\"","best":true}],"why":"A doctor or Tele-MANAS 14416 is the right door, and low mood is never their fault."}],"relearn":"A gentle, worried, 'shall we talk to someone together' opens the door to help without shame or dismissal."},
  {"id":"uk-083","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"keep or change","relearn":"Check-ins, appreciation and protected couple time are habits to keep; bottling up, a resentment ledger and stonewalling need changing.","hook":"Sort: a green-flag couple habit after kids, or a pattern to change?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Daily check-ins and appreciation"},{"id":"b","text":"Bottling everything up"},{"id":"c","text":"Protecting a little couple time"},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping a silent resentment ledger"},{"id":"e","text":"A quick repair after a snap"},{"id":"f","text":"Stonewalling and refusing to talk"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Green-flag habit","valence":"pos"},{"id":"change","label":"Pattern to change","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"change","c":"keep","d":"change","e":"keep","f":"change"}},
  {"id":"uk-084","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: small habits, said out loud, beat grand gestures you never have energy for. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","Small and steady","Out loud"],"affirm":"Small, consistent, spoken habits sustain a couple after kids better than grand gestures you can't sustain.","relearn":"Small habits done consistently beat grand gestures you never have the energy for.","persona":"any","source":"small beats grand"},
  {"id":"uk-900","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In a joint family, there's no room to reconnect as a couple, so don't bother.\"","myth":{"un":"In a joint family, there's no room to reconnect, so don't bother.","re":"Even in a full house, small private moments can be carved out, a late tea, a walk, and they keep the couple alive.","why":"Privacy is made, not just found."},"relearn":"A joint family doesn't end couple closeness; small carved-out moments keep you connected even in a full house.","persona":"any","source":"room in a full house"},
  {"id":"uk-901","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If desire hasn't come back yet, the love must be fading.\"","myth":{"un":"If desire hasn't come back yet, the love must be fading.","re":"Low desire after a baby is usually exhaustion and recovery, not lost love; it returns as rest and closeness return.","why":"Tiredness dims desire, not the love."},"relearn":"Low desire after a baby is usually tiredness and recovery, not fading love; it lifts as rest and closeness return.","persona":"Divya","source":"desire not love"},
  {"id":"uk-902","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Talking about intimacy out loud just makes it awkward, better to stay silent.\"","myth":{"un":"Talking about intimacy out loud just makes it awkward, better to stay silent.","re":"Gentle, honest talk about what each of you wants and isn't ready for removes guesswork and lowers the pressure.","why":"Naming it beats guessing in silence."},"relearn":"Talking gently about intimacy lowers pressure and removes guesswork; silence leaves you both guessing.","persona":"any","source":"talk not silence"},
  {"id":"uk-903","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Reconnecting has to mean a grand romantic gesture, or it doesn't count.\"","myth":{"un":"Reconnecting has to mean a grand romantic gesture, or it doesn't count.","re":"Tiny everyday closeness, a held hand, a shared cup of chai, rebuilds the bond more reliably than a one-off grand night.","why":"Consistent small beats rare grand."},"relearn":"Small everyday closeness rebuilds the bond better than a rare grand gesture; little reconnections count.","persona":"any","source":"small counts"},
  {"id":"uk-904","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once you've had a baby, your body should just bounce back to how it was for intimacy.\"","myth":{"un":"Once you've had a baby, your body should just bounce back for intimacy.","re":"Physical recovery after childbirth is individual with no fixed timeline; needing time is healing, not falling behind.","why":"Healing runs on its own clock."},"relearn":"Recovery after childbirth is individual with no set timeline; needing time is healing, not falling behind.","persona":"Divya","source":"individual recovery"},
  {"id":"uk-905","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Emotional closeness can wait, physical intimacy is what really fixes a couple.\"","myth":{"un":"Emotional closeness can wait, physical intimacy is what really fixes a couple.","re":"Emotional reconnection usually leads; feeling safe and seen first is what makes physical closeness welcome, not forced.","why":"Safety opens the door to the rest."},"relearn":"Emotional reconnection usually comes first; feeling safe and seen is what makes physical closeness welcome.","persona":"any","source":"emotional leads"},
  {"id":"uk-906","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good partner would just want intimacy by now to please me.\"","myth":{"un":"A good partner would just want intimacy by now to please me.","re":"Intimacy given to please you, not from real readiness, isn't closeness; it's pressure, and it erodes trust over time.","why":"Obligation isn't intimacy."},"relearn":"Intimacy given to please you rather than from readiness is pressure, not closeness, and it erodes trust.","persona":"Vikram","source":"not to please"},
  {"id":"uk-907","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Bringing up that intimacy hurts will just embarrass us both, so I'll endure it.\"","myth":{"un":"Bringing up that intimacy hurts will just embarrass us both, so I'll endure it.","re":"Postpartum discomfort is common and treatable; a clinician can help, and enduring pain in silence helps no one.","why":"Pain is a cue to seek care."},"relearn":"Postpartum discomfort is common and treatable; tell a clinician rather than enduring pain in silence.","persona":"Divya","source":"don't endure pain"},
  {"id":"uk-908","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If we don't reconnect fast, the window closes and we'll drift apart forever.\"","myth":{"un":"If we don't reconnect fast, the window closes and we'll drift apart forever.","re":"There's no closing window; couples reconnect at all sorts of paces, and patient, steady effort works far better than panic.","why":"Steady effort beats a deadline."},"relearn":"There's no closing window for reconnecting; patient, steady effort works better than rushing in panic.","persona":"any","source":"no closing window"},
  {"id":"uk-909","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Reconnecting is the recovering partner's job to initiate once they're 'better'.\"","myth":{"un":"Reconnecting is the recovering partner's job to initiate once they're 'better'.","re":"Reconnection is shared work; the other partner offering warmth, patience and no-pressure closeness carries half of it.","why":"Both build the bridge back."},"relearn":"Reconnecting is shared work; offering warmth and patient, no-pressure closeness is half the job.","persona":"Vikram","source":"shared job"},
  {"id":"uk-910","cat":"reconnecting","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Wanting closeness without it leading to sex would confuse my partner, so I won't ask.\"","myth":{"un":"Wanting closeness without it leading to sex would confuse my partner, so I won't ask.","re":"Asking for a hug or cuddle for its own sake is clear and kind; closeness needn't be a step toward anything else.","why":"Affection can stand alone."},"relearn":"Asking for affection for its own sake is clear and kind; closeness doesn't have to lead anywhere.","persona":"any","source":"affection stands alone"},
  {"id":"uk-911","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: lets readiness lead, or quietly applies a deadline?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Whenever you feel ready, no rush.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"It's been months already.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I just like being close to you.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Everyone says by now things are back.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Tell me what feels okay for you.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Are we ever going to be normal again?\""}],"bins":[{"id":"led","label":"Readiness leads","valence":"pos"},{"id":"clock","label":"Applies a clock","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"led","b":"clock","c":"led","d":"clock","e":"led","f":"clock"},"relearn":"Lines that wait on readiness keep closeness safe; lines that count months or invoke 'normal' apply a deadline.","persona":"any","source":"readiness vs clock"},
  {"id":"uk-912","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: emotional reconnection, physical closeness, or a help route?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A long honest talk after baby sleeps"},{"id":"b","text":"Holding hands on an evening walk"},{"id":"c","text":"Painful intimacy that keeps recurring"},{"id":"d","text":"Sharing how your day really felt"},{"id":"e","text":"A warm, unhurried hug"},{"id":"f","text":"Low mood that won't lift for weeks"}],"bins":[{"id":"emot","label":"Emotional reconnect","valence":"pos"},{"id":"phys","label":"Physical closeness","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Reach for help","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"emot","b":"phys","c":"help","d":"emot","e":"phys","f":"help"},"relearn":"Talk and sharing rebuild emotional closeness; hugs and walks rebuild physical warmth; recurring pain or lasting low mood need help.","persona":"any","source":"emotional physical help"},
  {"id":"uk-913","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: invites closeness, or pushes it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Cooking a quiet dinner just for two"},{"id":"b","text":"Keeping a tally of 'how long it's been'"},{"id":"c","text":"Saying 'I've missed just talking to you'"},{"id":"d","text":"Going cold until they initiate"},{"id":"e","text":"Planning a small outing you'd both enjoy"},{"id":"f","text":"Comparing your sex life to before the baby"}],"bins":[{"id":"invite","label":"Invites closeness","valence":"pos"},{"id":"push","label":"Pushes it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"invite","b":"push","c":"invite","d":"push","e":"invite","f":"push"},"relearn":"Quiet shared time and honest warmth invite closeness; tallies, cold withdrawal and comparisons push it away.","persona":"any","source":"invite vs push"},
  {"id":"uk-914","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a normal part of reconnecting after a baby, or a sign to seek care?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Desire taking time to return"},{"id":"b","text":"Pain during intimacy each time"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling shy reconnecting at first"},{"id":"d","text":"Bleeding or symptoms that worry you"},{"id":"e","text":"Preferring cuddles over more for now"},{"id":"f","text":"Dread or numbness that won't lift"}],"bins":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal reconnecting","valence":"pos"},{"id":"care","label":"See a clinician","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"normal","b":"care","c":"normal","d":"care","e":"normal","f":"care"},"relearn":"Slow desire, shyness and preferring cuddles are normal; recurring pain, worrying symptoms or lasting dread need a clinician.","persona":"any","source":"normal vs care"},
  {"id":"uk-915","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each opener: gentle and pressure-free, or loaded with expectation?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I miss us; no agenda at all.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Don't you find me attractive anymore?\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Can we just spend time together?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'm trying really hard to be patient here.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Whatever feels right for you works.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Other husbands wouldn't wait this long.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"gentle","label":"Gentle, no pressure","valence":"pos"},{"id":"loaded","label":"Loaded with pressure","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"gentle","b":"loaded","c":"gentle","d":"loaded","e":"gentle","f":"loaded"},"relearn":"Openers that miss 'us' and follow their pace are gentle; guilt, impatience and comparisons load on pressure.","persona":"any","source":"gentle vs loaded"},
  {"id":"uk-916","cat":"reconnecting","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each act: rebuilds the friendship under the romance, or skips it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking about their day and listening"},{"id":"b","text":"Jumping straight to 'so, tonight?'"},{"id":"c","text":"Sharing a worry honestly"},{"id":"d","text":"Treating closeness as a box to tick"},{"id":"e","text":"Doing a small chore so they can rest"},{"id":"f","text":"Keeping score of who tried last"}],"bins":[{"id":"build","label":"Builds friendship","valence":"pos"},{"id":"skip","label":"Skips the bond","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"build","b":"skip","c":"build","d":"skip","e":"build","f":"skip"},"relearn":"Listening, honesty and easing their load rebuild the friendship; rushing, ticking boxes and scorekeeping skip it.","persona":"any","source":"friendship first"},
  {"id":"uk-917","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"protect your pace","hook":"Your in-laws keep hinting at a second baby, while you and Karan are still finding your way back to each other.","steps":[{"prompt":"At Sunday lunch, your mother-in-law smiles and says the house needs another little one. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll think about it and give them an answer soon","then":"She beams. \"Don't take too long, beta.\" Karan stares at his plate."},{"text":"Smile and say you two will decide in your own time","then":"She pauses, then passes the dal. Karan glances at you, relieved.","best":true},{"text":"Laugh along and say maybe soon, just to keep the peace","then":"The table cheers, and the hints get louder from then on."},{"text":"Tell her sharply it's none of her business","then":"The room goes silent. Your father-in-law changes the subject, and the air stays stiff."}],"why":"A warm, clear line keeps the pace with you two."},{"prompt":"Later, Karan says his mother's hints have been getting to him too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say maybe it's time you both stopped resisting","then":"He looks at you carefully. \"Only if you truly want to,\" he says, unsure."},{"text":"Tell him not to take it seriously. It's just talk","then":"He shrugs, but the next hint still lands hard on both of you."},{"text":"Agree on one line you'll both use when family asks","then":"He nods. \"'We'll tell you when there's news.'\" You both feel lighter.","best":true},{"text":"Ask him to handle his mother, and you'll handle yours","then":"He sighs. \"She listens to you more,\" and you're carrying it again."}],"why":"A shared line keeps you united and the pace in your hands."},{"prompt":"At a family function, a cousin whispers that a second baby would bring you two closer. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask about her second baby, to move the talk along","then":"She launches into a long story. You nod through it, and the hint hangs there."},{"text":"Say you'll think about it, since it might help","then":"She squeezes your arm and tells two aunts before dessert."},{"text":"Tell Karan aloud that his family is pushy","then":"He freezes. Several relatives look over, and the evening turns awkward."},{"text":"Smile and say you're rebuilding closeness, no rush","then":"She blinks. \"Of course,\" she says, and drifts to another group.","best":true}],"why":"Closeness comes from time together, not from a plan someone else sets."}],"debrief":"Reconnection is the couple's call. Set aside outside hints, and don't let them rush you.","relearn":"Keep reconnection at your own pace; set aside in-laws' hints instead of rushing."},
  {"id":"uk-918","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"quiet counts","hook":"A rare evening alone arrives, and you and Kabir are both drained.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kabir drops onto the sofa beside you and yawns. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you should make the most of it, and plan a proper date night","then":"He tries to smile. \"Sure. Give me five minutes to wake up.\""},{"text":"Suggest napping in separate rooms to catch up on sleep","then":"You each sleep two hours. You're rested, but haven't really seen each other."},{"text":"Lean on him, put on something easy, and let that be the evening","then":"He puts an arm around you. Ten minutes in, you're both half asleep and smiling.","best":true},{"text":"Do the chores you've been putting off","then":"The sink is finally clear. Kabir dries dishes beside you, but neither of you talks."}],"why":"Sitting close with no plan still counts as time together."},{"prompt":"Later, Kabir says quietly that he wishes things felt more romantic. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say the evening has been long and change the subject","then":"He nods and lets it drop. What he wished for stays unsaid."},{"text":"Point out how much you've been doing lately","then":"He goes defensive. \"I know. I wasn't blaming you.\""},{"text":"Agree you should both try harder next time","then":"He agrees, but 'try harder' hangs over you like homework."},{"text":"Ask what he misses, and say quiet time together counts","then":"He laughs softly. \"Mostly you. And it does count, doesn't it? Even just sitting close.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking what he misses and naming what still feels good keeps you close."},{"prompt":"The next week, another rare evening opens up. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Plan a big evening to make up for the last one","then":"You book a table and buy something new. By the time it comes, you're worn out."},{"text":"Wait to see whether he suggests something","then":"Neither of you does. The evening slips by with the TV on."},{"text":"Ask what he'd like, and offer quiet or something small","then":"He picks tea on the balcony. You make it, and it becomes a small habit.","best":true},{"text":"Use the hours for errands you can't do with the baby","then":"The errands get done. You both feel lighter and still a bit distant."}],"why":"Asking what you can both manage keeps it easy, not a performance."}],"debrief":"A rare evening can be quiet closeness, not a performance; sitting together counts.","relearn":"Closeness can be just sitting together; you needn't force romance to make it 'count'."},
  {"id":"uk-919","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"name what's okay","hook":"Aman reaches for a hug, but you tense up, still sore and unsure, and he notices.","steps":[{"prompt":"He drops his arms and asks if he did something wrong. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing is wrong and turn to the baby","then":"He nods, unconvinced, and picks up the baby's blanket. The moment closes."},{"text":"Think he should have known, and let him guess","then":"He steps back, unsure, and keeps his distance that night."},{"text":"Say a slow hug is fine and you're just still sore","then":"His shoulders relax. \"Slow is fine. Tell me how.\"","best":true},{"text":"Apologise for being difficult and offer to try anyway","then":"He shakes his head, unsure. Now you're both tense."}],"why":"Saying what feels okay keeps him close without pushing you."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Aman asks how your body is feeling. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it's complicated and you'll explain another time","then":"He says of course. Another time doesn't come, and the tension stays."},{"text":"Tell him he shouldn't have to ask because it's obvious","then":"He winces. \"I really can't tell, though.\""},{"text":"Say some parts still hurt, and what touch feels okay","then":"He listens without fixing. \"Thanks for telling me. That helps.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you're fine, so he doesn't worry","then":"He says good, but watches you carefully across the kitchen."}],"why":"Naming what's okay lets him respond to you instead of guessing."},{"prompt":"A week on, Aman puts an arm around you as you feed the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Apologise that you're not more fun these days","then":"He frowns. \"You don't owe me fun.\" The moment turns heavy."},{"text":"Joke that he's getting clingy","then":"He laughs, a bit stiffly. He isn't sure if you're joking."},{"text":"Tell yourself you ought to want more than this by now","then":"The thought crowds out the moment. You barely feel his arm."},{"text":"Lean in a little and say this part feels good","then":"He smiles. \"Then let's keep doing this part.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the touch you like builds closeness at your pace, with no 'by now'."}],"debrief":"Naming what feels okay keeps you connected; silent withdrawal leaves a partner guessing.","relearn":"Tell your partner gently what does and doesn't feel okay; silence leaves them confused."},
  {"id":"uk-920","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"tiny ritual","hook":"You realise weeks have gone by with no real moment together with Ishaan, just baby logistics.","steps":[{"prompt":"Ishaan is washing bottles, and you have ten spare minutes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wait and see if things feel close again on their own","then":"The week runs the same. You pass the baby, and pass each other."},{"text":"Plan a weekend away for when the baby is older","then":"He likes the idea. The weekend is months off, and nothing changes tonight."},{"text":"Suggest ten minutes each night, just the two of you","then":"He dries his hands. \"Yes. Even ten minutes.\" You set a time.","best":true},{"text":"Send him a long message about how distant it feels","then":"He reads it mid-feed at 3am and replies with a heart. There's still no talk."}],"why":"A small daily moment beats waiting or a plan for someday."},{"prompt":"A few days later, the baby cries just as you and Ishaan sit down for a few minutes together. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Skip it tonight, and try again when things calm down","then":"Things don't calm down. Tomorrow the same thing happens."},{"text":"Hand the baby to him and take the time alone","then":"He soothes her, and you get a quiet shower. The moment together is gone."},{"text":"Settle the baby, then come back and pick up again","then":"You return with fresh tea. He grins. \"Where were we?\"","best":true},{"text":"Sigh that this never works","then":"He nods with a tired laugh. Time together starts to feel like a chore."}],"why":"Coming back after an interruption keeps your time together alive."},{"prompt":"Ishaan admits he doesn't know what to talk about when you do get a moment. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest you both just review the baby's schedule","then":"You settle the feeds in five minutes. It's useful, but it isn't the point."},{"text":"Ask him one real question about his day","then":"He thinks. \"Honestly, the walk to work.\" You laugh, and it goes from there.","best":true},{"text":"Fill it with news from the family group","then":"You read out messages. He nods, and nobody says anything about themselves."},{"text":"Say maybe this isn't going to work","then":"He looks crushed. \"I didn't mean give up,\" he says quietly."}],"why":"One real question moves your time together from logistics to the two of you."}],"debrief":"Don't wait for closeness to return alone; a tiny protected daily ritual rebuilds it.","relearn":"Build a small daily moment together; closeness rarely returns on its own while you wait."},
  {"id":"uk-921","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"tune out timelines","hook":"A friend brags she was 'back to normal in no time', and you and Sameer feel behind.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sameer looks a little deflated after hearing it too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask what she did, so you can catch up","then":"You get a long list of things. Sameer looks even more tired."},{"text":"Agree you're both just slower than most","then":"Sameer nods glumly. The word 'slower' sticks to you both."},{"text":"Decide to push a little, to get to normal sooner","then":"You both try to act ready. It feels like effort, and neither of you says so."},{"text":"Remind each other that recovery has no set timeline","then":"He exhales. \"Right. She's not us.\" The comparison loosens its grip.","best":true}],"why":"Recovery is individual, so no one else's timeline is yours."},{"prompt":"At a family gathering, an aunt asks whether you two are back to normal yet. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say yes, almost there, to end the questions","then":"She's satisfied. You feel like you've told a small lie."},{"text":"Change the subject to the baby's feeding","then":"She's off on feeding tips within seconds, and the question stays hanging."},{"text":"Say you two are going at your own pace","then":"She nods. \"Good, good.\" Sameer squeezes your hand under the table.","best":true},{"text":"Say it's taking longer than you expected","then":"She tuts sympathetically and shares her own timeline, which is different again."}],"why":"A calm line about your own pace shuts the comparison down kindly."},{"prompt":"A month on, Sameer mentions an article about when couples should feel ready. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Read it closely and compare your progress","then":"You tick off boxes. You come away feeling behind again."},{"text":"Ask him to keep that kind of reading to himself","then":"He shrinks a little. \"Okay. I just wanted to help.\""},{"text":"Take it as a plan and set a target date","then":"You pick a date. It sits on the calendar like an exam."},{"text":"Set it aside and check in on how each of you feels","then":"He nods. \"Fair. I'd rather know how you're doing.\" You talk for twenty minutes.","best":true}],"why":"Your own check-in tells you more than any article's timeline."}],"debrief":"Recovery and reconnection are individual; tune out others' timelines rather than chasing them.","relearn":"Ignore others' 'back to normal' timelines; recovery is individual and yours to pace."},
  {"id":"uk-922","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"readiness both ways","hook":"You and Maya have been close again, but tonight she's clearly exhausted.","steps":[{"prompt":"Maya says she's happy to be intimate tonight, if you'd like. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept, since she offered","then":"Her smile is thin. Later she turns to the wall without a word."},{"text":"Say you'd love to, and it might help her unwind","then":"She hesitates, then apologises that she's too tired after all."},{"text":"Say not tonight, but that tomorrow you'd really like to","then":"She nods, but her face tightens. Tomorrow now has a weight."},{"text":"Say you'd rather rest and cuddle tonight, and mean it","then":"She sags with relief. \"Thank you.\" She's asleep against you within minutes.","best":true}],"why":"Choosing rest keeps her yes real and her no safe."},{"prompt":"The next evening, Maya admits she sometimes offers so you won't feel rejected. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say a no would sting a little, but you'd get over it soon","then":"She nods. Now your feelings are one more thing she has to manage."},{"text":"Joke that you'd never turn down an offer","then":"She laughs a little too late. \"That's what I was worried about.\""},{"text":"Say it's fine, then go quiet for the evening","then":"You eat in silence. She tries twice to start a conversation."},{"text":"Say a tired no isn't rejection, and you'd want to hear it","then":"She smiles. \"Then I'll stop offering on tired nights.\"","best":true}],"why":"Hearing that a tired no isn't rejection, and that you'd want to hear it, keeps her yes free."},{"prompt":"Maya asks how she can say she's too tired without it becoming a big deal. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say she can always tell you, and leave it there","then":"She nods. It's kind, but the words still feel hard to find at night."},{"text":"Promise you'll notice on your own from now on","then":"You try. Twice you miss it, and she doesn't say so."},{"text":"Say she should tell you before bed, not at the last minute","then":"She feels the rule land on her. \"I'll try.\""},{"text":"Suggest a simple code phrase she can use, no explanation","then":"She laughs with relief. \"'Sleepy night'?\" You agree, and it's easy from then on.","best":true}],"why":"A simple agreed signal makes saying no easy and keeps closeness safe."}],"debrief":"Readiness goes both ways; declining an exhausted offer keeps closeness safe.","relearn":"If a partner offers intimacy from exhaustion, kindly say 'just rest'; readiness goes both ways."},
  {"id":"uk-923","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"mood first","hook":"Reconnecting with Arjun feels stuck, and you suspect your own low mood, not the relationship, is in the way.","steps":[{"prompt":"Arjun asks why you've seemed flat lately. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you feel ungrateful for feeling this low with a healthy baby","then":"He says he doesn't think you're ungrateful. Neither of you knows what happens next."},{"text":"Say you're fine and plan a romantic evening to prove it to him","then":"The evening is polite and empty. You go to bed feeling worse for pretending."},{"text":"Tell him it's lasted weeks and you'll call Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"He takes your hand. \"Thank you for telling me. Let's call together.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say it's just baby tiredness and it will pass","then":"He accepts it. Weeks later you still feel numb and haven't said so."}],"why":"Low mood that lingers is real and treatable, and never your fault."},{"prompt":"Arjun's mother says new mothers just have to push through the blues. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree with her and try to push through the weeks","then":"You smile through the week. The flatness comes along."},{"text":"Say nothing and start folding the baby's clothes","then":"She keeps talking about pushing through. You feel more alone."},{"text":"Ask her to talk to Arjun about it instead of you","then":"She turns to Arjun, worried. He's caught off guard, and you're left out."},{"text":"Say it's lasted too long and you're getting support","then":"She goes quiet. \"Then get the help, beta,\" she says, softer than you expected.","best":true}],"why":"Saying it has lasted, and getting support, moves it past 'just the blues'."},{"prompt":"Arjun asks what he can do for you this week. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say there's nothing much he can do about how you feel","then":"He nods, helpless, and hovers around the kitchen unsure what to do."},{"text":"Ask him to plan more romantic time to cheer you up","then":"He tries hard. You smile through it, and feel more tired than before."},{"text":"Say you'll handle it alone so he can focus on work","then":"He hesitates, then agrees. You carry all of it again."},{"text":"Ask for some nights off and his help getting support","then":"He blocks the nights on his phone. \"Tuesday and Friday are mine. We'll call together.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking for practical relief and company makes reaching out easier."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, on a slightly better day, Arjun suggests a walk. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go, and decide you don't need any help after all","then":"The walk is lovely. Three weeks later the flatness creeps back."},{"text":"Go, but hide that the flatness still comes and goes","then":"You smile the whole way. He thinks you're better, and you feel alone with it."},{"text":"Go, and plan a big weekend to make up for lost time","then":"You plan a lot. You're worn out by Saturday."},{"text":"Go, and say you'll get or keep support on better days too","then":"He takes your hand. \"I'll keep making the time.\" You talk about the baby, and about you.","best":true}],"why":"Support on lighter days, new or continued, helps them last."}],"debrief":"When low mood blocks reconnecting, the right move is support, not forcing it.","relearn":"If low mood blocks closeness, tend to the mood first; Tele-MANAS 14416 helps."},
  {"id":"uk-924","cat":"reconnecting","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"not rejection","hook":"Nisha says she's just not feeling desire yet, and a voice in you wants to take it personally.","steps":[{"prompt":"She looks worried about how you'll react. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's fine, then sigh heavily and turn away to your side","then":"She sees the sigh. She apologises, and you both lie awake."},{"text":"Ask her whether it's something you did to cause this","then":"She says no, but now she's reassuring you, which she has no energy for."},{"text":"Thank her for telling you and say you'll wait with her","then":"Her face softens. \"I was scared you'd be upset.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you've heard other couples manage it by this point","then":"Her eyes fill. \"I'm doing what I can.\""}],"why":"Thanking her makes it safe for her to keep being honest."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she turns away at bedtime and you feel a sting. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Go and sleep in the other room, to give her space","then":"She wakes alone and wonders if she caused this."},{"text":"Ask her when she thinks things will change back","then":"She says she doesn't know, and the question sits heavy."},{"text":"Notice the sting, remember it's recovery, and stay warm","then":"You say goodnight and squeeze her hand. She squeezes back.","best":true},{"text":"Go cold and stop starting conversations with her for a while","then":"She notices the distance. \"Are we okay?\" she asks in the morning."}],"why":"Seeing it as recovery, not rejection, lets you stay warm."},{"prompt":"Nisha asks what you've been thinking about all this. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing bothers you and you're totally fine","then":"She doesn't quite believe you, and the topic closes."},{"text":"Say you've wondered whether she still loves you","then":"She's shocked. She says of course, and cries from the pressure of reassuring you."},{"text":"Say you're trying to be patient and hope it won't be long","then":"Her smile fades. 'Won't be long' sounds like a deadline."},{"text":"Say it stung at times, but you know it's her recovery","then":"She reaches over. \"Thank you for not hiding it. That helps me stay close.\"","best":true}],"why":"Honesty without blame keeps you both feeling safe."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Nisha says she's starting to feel desire again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll clear the whole weekend for it","then":"She tenses. \"Please don't plan it.\""},{"text":"Say you'd almost given up hope by now","then":"Her eyes fill. \"Almost given up?\""},{"text":"Suggest going straight back to how things were","then":"She looks unsure. \"I'm not the same as before.\""},{"text":"Say you're glad, and let her set the pace","then":"She smiles. \"Thank you for not rushing me.\"","best":true}],"why":"Staying warm and letting her set the pace keeps it her choice."}],"debrief":"Low desire after a baby is recovery and tiredness, not rejection; stay warm.","relearn":"A partner's low desire is recovery, not rejection; stay warm rather than wounded."},
  {"id":"uk-925","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each reconnecting move to the worry it gently answers.","pairs":[{"left":"\"No clock, your pace\"","right":"Fear of being pressured"},{"left":"A hug with no next step","right":"Worry that closeness means more"},{"left":"Asking how they truly feel","right":"Feeling unseen in recovery"},{"left":"Seeing a clinician about pain","right":"Suffering discomfort silently"},{"left":"A small daily check-in","right":"Drifting into just logistics"}],"relearn":"Each gentle move, no clock, a no-step hug, asking, getting care, a check-in, answers a real reconnecting worry.","persona":"any","source":"moves to worries"},
  {"id":"uk-926","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each phase of reconnecting to what it actually looks like.","pairs":[{"left":"Emotional reconnection","right":"Talking, laughing, feeling seen"},{"left":"Gentle physical closeness","right":"Hugs, hand-holding, sitting near"},{"left":"Readiness for more","right":"Both wanting it, no obligation"},{"left":"Handling discomfort","right":"A clinician, not silent enduring"},{"left":"Protecting the pace","right":"No deadline from anyone"}],"relearn":"Reconnecting moves through emotional closeness, gentle touch, mutual readiness, care for discomfort, and a protected pace.","persona":"any","source":"phases of reconnecting"},
  {"id":"uk-927","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each pressuring line to the kinder thing you could say instead.","pairs":[{"left":"\"It's been long enough.\"","right":"\"There's truly no rush.\""},{"left":"\"Don't you want me?\"","right":"\"I just like being near you.\""},{"left":"\"Everyone else is back to normal.\"","right":"\"Our pace is the only one that matters.\""},{"left":"\"You owe me this.\"","right":"\"Only when you genuinely want to.\""},{"left":"\"Why are you so cold?\"","right":"\"How are you really doing?\""}],"relearn":"Every pressuring line has a kinder version that follows readiness instead of demanding it.","persona":"any","source":"kinder swap"},
  {"id":"uk-928","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each small shared moment to the closeness it quietly grows.","pairs":[{"left":"Making chai for two at dawn","right":"A ritual that's just yours"},{"left":"Watching baby sleep together","right":"Shared tenderness as a team"},{"left":"A quick message during the day","right":"Feeling thought of"},{"left":"Thanking them for one real thing","right":"Feeling seen and valued"},{"left":"A slow hug at the door","right":"Warmth with no demand"}],"relearn":"Tiny shared moments, dawn chai, a watched-over baby, a message, a thank-you, a hug, grow real closeness.","persona":"any","source":"moments grow closeness"},
  {"id":"uk-929","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each reconnecting truth to why it takes the pressure off.","pairs":[{"left":"Recovery is individual","right":"There's no timeline to be late for"},{"left":"Emotional closeness leads","right":"You can start with a talk, not more"},{"left":"Waiting isn't rejection","right":"Needing time means nothing's wrong"},{"left":"Closeness changes shape","right":"It doesn't have to be like before"},{"left":"Discomfort is treatable","right":"Pain is a cue to ask, not endure"}],"relearn":"Each truth, individual recovery, emotional first, waiting is fine, new shape, treatable discomfort, removes pressure.","persona":"any","source":"truths ease pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-930","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"waiting with you","hook":"Anita is still recovering from the birth, and you've been holding back so you don't pressure her.","setup":"You find her sitting quietly on the bed after the baby's feed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Anita asks: \"You've gone so quiet lately. Have you lost interest in me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I figured you'd come around eventually, so I stopped trying.\"","then":"She stares at you. \"Stopped trying. I thought so.\""},{"text":"\"Of course not. Why would you even think that?\"","then":"She looks down. \"Because you never say anything.\""},{"text":"\"I'm not pulling away. I'm waiting with you, and I miss you.\"","then":"Her eyes fill. \"I thought you'd given up on me.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I just don't want to pressure you. That's all.\"","then":"She half nods. \"But what do you want?\""}],"why":"Saying you're waiting with her shows you haven't pulled away."},{"prompt":"Anita says: \"I hate feeling like I'm letting you down.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's okay. It's just hard sometimes. That's all.\"","then":"She flinches. \"So it is hard for you.\""},{"text":"\"You could try a little, maybe? Just to see.\"","then":"She tenses. \"Try what, exactly?\""},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about this anymore tonight, okay?\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"If we stop now, I'll lie awake. Please.\""},{"text":"\"You're not. You're recovering, and that's no failure.\"","then":"She breathes out slowly. \"I needed someone to say that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming recovery, not failure, lifts her guilt."},{"prompt":"Anita asks: \"So what can we do, in the meantime?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You tell me. I don't want to guess wrong.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I don't know either. It'd help if you suggested something.\""},{"text":"\"We could watch a film once the baby sleeps and see how it goes.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"See how what goes?\""},{"text":"\"I'll just keep waiting. There's nothing else to do.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"That makes me feel like a chore.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever suits you. Tea together, a walk, or just sitting close.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Tea on the terrace tomorrow. Just us.\"","best":true}],"why":"Offering small, easy options invites closeness with no pressure."},{"prompt":"Anita says: \"I feel like I owe you an answer at some point.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, some idea would help me plan.\"","then":"She looks away. \"Plan.\""},{"text":"\"No rush. I've just been waiting for a sign.\"","then":"She feels the weight of the sign she hasn't given."},{"text":"\"Maybe by the holidays?\"","then":"She stiffens. \"The holidays. So there's a date.\""},{"text":"\"You don't. I'm here when you have one.\"","then":"She lets out a shaky breath. \"Thank you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Telling her she owes no answer leaves the timing entirely with her."}],"relearn":"Saying 'I'm waiting with you, close however you're comfortable' reassures a partner who fears you've lost interest."},
  {"id":"uk-931","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"home date","hook":"You want to suggest a small date at home to Imran, without it sounding like a demand.","setup":"He's scrolling on the sofa as the baby's lullaby fades.","steps":[{"prompt":"Imran glances up and asks: \"What's up?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're finally doing a proper date night tonight. No excuses this time.\"","then":"He blinks. \"Excuses? I've been up since five.\""},{"text":"\"You never plan anything for us anymore, do you?\"","then":"He puts down his phone. \"That's not fair, and you know it.\""},{"text":"\"Maybe we could do something sometime, if you feel like it.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"Sure, sometime.\" The idea drifts off."},{"text":"\"Once she's down, can we have dinner just us? No plan beyond that.\"","then":"He smiles, surprised. \"Just us? When did we last do that?\"","best":true}],"why":"A warm, no-strings invitation makes it easy to say yes."},{"prompt":"Imran says: \"I want time with you too. I'm just wiped, honestly.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. We'll do it when you have more energy.\"","then":"He nods, relieved. \"Another night, then.\" No night gets named."},{"text":"\"I'm tired too. Let's not make it a big deal.\"","then":"He agrees, and reaches for the remote as usual."},{"text":"\"Keep it easy, then. Dal-chawal on the sofa.\"","then":"He grins. \"Dal-chawal, phones away. Deal.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nobody's asking for much. Just try, okay?\"","then":"His face tightens. \"I am trying.\""}],"why":"Lowering the bar keeps the plan doable for two tired people."},{"prompt":"The baby cries just as you both sit down to eat. Imran sighs: \"Of course.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Never mind then. We'll try another night.\"","then":"He shrugs and clears the plates. Another night doesn't come."},{"text":"\"This always happens. We can't even eat.\"","then":"His shoulders sag. \"I know. I'm sorry.\""},{"text":"\"You take her. I've done enough today.\"","then":"He gets up wearily. The dinner is cold when he returns."},{"text":"\"I'll settle her. Keep my plate warm.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Deal.\" When you return, he's saved you the best piece.","best":true}],"why":"Pausing and returning keeps the evening yours, with the interruption shared."}],"relearn":"A warm, no-strings invitation to time together rebuilds closeness where a 'no excuses' demand backfires."},
  {"id":"uk-932","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"safe to tell","hook":"Smita has seemed tense since you were last intimate, and she needs to know it's safe to tell you.","setup":"Later, over tea, she avoids your eyes.","steps":[{"prompt":"Smita says quietly: \"Last time was not great. I didn't say anything.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You'd tell me if something was wrong, right? I'm sure it's fine.\"","then":"She looks away, then back. \"It's not fine, though. It hurt.\""},{"text":"\"I didn't notice anything. Are you sure it was that bad?\"","then":"Her voice shrinks. \"Maybe I'm overreacting.\""},{"text":"\"I feel awful. Please don't be upset with me.\"","then":"She rushes to comfort you, and her own hurt slips away."},{"text":"\"Thank you for telling me. If it hurts, I'd rather stop.\"","then":"Her shoulders loosen. \"I was scared to say it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Thanking her and putting stopping first makes it safe to tell you. It's never her fault."},{"prompt":"Smita asks: \"What if it hurts again? I don't want to spoil the mood.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just tell me quickly if it does, so I can adjust.\"","then":"She nods, but worries about saying it quickly enough."},{"text":"\"Let's just be careful, and carry on like before.\"","then":"She nods, unsure. She still doesn't know if she could say stop."},{"text":"\"It probably won't. The worst is likely over.\"","then":"She wants to believe you. She isn't sure it's yours to say."},{"text":"\"You'd never spoil it. Say stop and we stop. No questions.\"","then":"She lets out a shaky laugh. \"No questions. Okay.\"","best":true}],"why":"A stop that needs no reason makes honesty easy in the moment."},{"prompt":"Smita asks: \"Should I see the doctor about it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"If you think you need to. Whatever you decide is fine by me.\"","then":"She shrugs. \"I don't know what I need.\""},{"text":"\"Let's give it a couple of weeks and see.\"","then":"She agrees. The weeks pass, and the discomfort doesn't."},{"text":"\"Doctors just say to relax. We'll figure it out together.\"","then":"She relaxes for a second, then tenses. \"But what if we don't?\""},{"text":"\"Yes, please. I'll come along, or wait outside if you like.\"","then":"She smiles, relieved. \"Come with me. I'll book it tomorrow.\"","best":true}],"why":"Backing her care and letting her choose how you help puts her comfort first."}],"relearn":"Telling a partner you'd always rather stop than have them push through makes it safe to name discomfort."},
  {"id":"uk-933","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"want it slowly","hook":"You're the one recovering, and you want closeness with Sanjay, just not everything yet.","setup":"You sit beside him on the sofa after dinner.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sanjay puts an arm around you and asks: \"Is this okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Forget it. It's easier if I say I'm not interested.\"","then":"His arm drops. \"Oh. Okay. I didn't know.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Do whatever you want.\"","then":"He hesitates. \"That's not really an answer.\""},{"text":"\"Let's just watch something instead.\"","then":"He nods and lets go. You both watch the screen, and the closeness goes unspoken."},{"text":"\"I do want to feel close. Just slowly. Cuddles for now?\"","then":"He relaxes. \"Thank you for telling me. Cuddles it is.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming what you do want keeps closeness open at your pace."},{"prompt":"Sanjay says: \"I don't want to push you. How will I know if you want more?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe you could guess from how I act?\"","then":"He frowns. \"I'm bad at guessing. I'd rather hear it.\""},{"text":"\"I'll make it up to you soon. I promise.\"","then":"He goes still. \"Make it up? It isn't a debt.\""},{"text":"\"You're not pushing. I'll tell you if I want more.\"","then":"He nods. \"Then I'll wait for you to say so.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's keep things separate for a while, then.\"","then":"He nods and moves to his side of the sofa. You miss his arm at once."}],"why":"Promising to say so gives him a clear signal and takes away his guessing."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Sanjay says: \"I've liked how things are between us lately. I'm happy at your pace.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You've been patient. Maybe I owe you more by now.\"","then":"His smile fades. \"You don't owe me anything. I only want what you want.\""},{"text":"\"Ha. I'm sure you can tell what I like.\"","then":"He laughs, unsure. \"I really can't. I'd rather hear it.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. I can put up with a bit of discomfort.\"","then":"He stops smiling. \"Hurting isn't something to put up with. A doctor could help.\""},{"text":"\"Thanks. Nothing big yet. Longer hugs, and hearing about your day.\"","then":"He smiles. \"I can do that. Starting tonight.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming small wants keeps closeness warm at your own pace. Nothing is owed."}],"relearn":"Naming what you do want, 'close, slowly, cuddles for now', invites closeness better than shutting the door."},
  {"id":"uk-934","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"no guilt","hook":"Since the birth, Leela hasn't felt ready for closeness yet, and she seems to blame herself.","setup":"Tonight she's folding the baby's clothes beside you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Leela says: \"I'm sorry I'm taking so long to feel ready.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's okay. I've been patient, though it has been a while.\"","then":"She winces. \"I know. I'm sorry.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry. Everyone gets there in the end.\"","then":"She nods, but 'in the end' sounds like a deadline to her."},{"text":"\"Nothing to apologise for. You're not on a clock with me.\"","then":"Her shoulders drop. \"Are you sure?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't be sorry. I just want you to be happy.\"","then":"She swallows. \"I'm trying to be.\""}],"why":"Saying there's no clock lifts the guilt. Adding a 'but' would quietly reload it."},{"prompt":"Leela says: \"My cousin told me most husbands don't wait this long.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Some do get impatient, but I'm doing my best.\"","then":"She looks down. \"I know you are.\""},{"text":"\"Your cousin should mind her own business.\"","then":"She half laughs, but her worry stays."},{"text":"\"Let's not compare ourselves to anyone.\"","then":"She nods. The worry is still there, unspoken."},{"text":"\"That's her story. Ours has no deadline.\"","then":"Her eyes soften. \"Say that again?\" You do.","best":true}],"why":"Refusing the comparison and naming your own story keeps the pressure off."},{"prompt":"Leela asks: \"Do you ever feel I owe you something?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You don't owe me, but it's nice when you try.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"So I should try.\""},{"text":"\"No. But I'd be lying if I said I don't think about it.\"","then":"She goes quiet. \"Now I'll be thinking about it too.\""},{"text":"\"Never. You owe me nothing. Not a date, not a favour.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"I didn't know how much I needed to hear that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Of course not. Why are we even talking about this?\"","then":"She stops, hurt. \"Because I needed to know.\""}],"why":"Saying she owes you nothing removes the debt she fears."}],"relearn":"'Nothing to apologise for, no clock with me' lifts the guilt; a 'but it has been a while' quietly reloads it."},
  {"id":"uk-935","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"miss your company","hook":"You miss Dev's company more than anything physical, and you want him to know that's what you mean.","setup":"He's stacking bottles in the drying rack, looking worn out.","steps":[{"prompt":"Dev looks up from the bottles and asks: \"You seem down. What's going on?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I miss us, you know. All of it, if you catch my drift.\"","then":"He frowns. \"Your drift? Is this about sex?\""},{"text":"\"Nothing. You wouldn't understand anyway.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"Okay.\" He finishes the bottles in silence."},{"text":"\"You're always busy with the baby. I barely exist to you.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"That's not true. I'm exhausted.\""},{"text":"\"Honestly, I miss your company. Can we just talk and be us?\"","then":"He puts the bottle down. \"I miss that too.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying plainly that it's his company you miss invites closeness with no hint of pressure."},{"prompt":"Dev sits down beside you and says: \"I'm so tired I can barely string a sentence together.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then when will you have energy? Tell me a day.\"","then":"He rubs his eyes. \"I can't promise a day.\""},{"text":"\"I'm tired too. Never mind. Forget it.\"","then":"He nods, relieved and guilty. The gap stays."},{"text":"\"Maybe we should fix our sleep first, then talk.\"","then":"You spend twenty minutes on a sleep schedule and feel no closer."},{"text":"\"Then don't talk. Just sit with me a bit.\"","then":"He leans back beside you. Neither of you says a word, and it's enough.","best":true}],"why":"Sitting together with no talking needed keeps closeness within reach."},{"prompt":"After a while, Dev asks: \"What do you actually want to talk about?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I don't know. Just something meaningful.\"","then":"He looks blank. \"Meaningful is a lot at nine at night.\""},{"text":"\"Our future. Where we'll be in five years.\"","then":"He blinks. \"That's huge. I can barely plan tomorrow.\""},{"text":"\"How we split the chores. That's been bothering me.\"","then":"He groans. \"Not tonight. Please.\""},{"text":"\"Anything but the baby. Your day, or something silly.\"","then":"He grins. \"There was a dog at the bus stop today.\" You both laugh.","best":true}],"why":"Light, ordinary talk is a doorway back to each other."}],"relearn":"Saying clearly it's their company you miss invites real closeness without the hint of pressure a wink carries."},
  {"id":"uk-936","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"open check-in","hook":"You want to check in on how reconnecting feels for Mira, leaving every door open for her honesty.","setup":"You choose a calm moment while the baby naps.","steps":[{"prompt":"Mira looks up from her phone and says: \"You wanted to ask me something?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We're good now though, right? Back on track?\"","then":"She nods quickly. \"Yes. Of course.\" She doesn't look at you."},{"text":"\"Are you still upset about how things are between us?\"","then":"She stiffens. \"Upset? I never said I was upset.\""},{"text":"\"I just want to check you're happy with how it's going.\"","then":"\"Sure, I'm happy,\" she says. It comes out too quickly."},{"text":"\"How's all this feeling for you, really? Any answer is fine.\"","then":"She puts her phone down. \"Can I think out loud?\"","best":true}],"why":"An open question with any answer welcome invites honesty a leading one shuts down."},{"prompt":"Mira says slowly: \"Some of it feels good. Some of it feels like too much.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Too much? I thought we were going slowly.\"","then":"She shrinks. \"We are. I didn't mean it as a complaint.\""},{"text":"\"Okay, so I'll just do less of everything.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"That's not what I meant either.\""},{"text":"\"I'll try to guess, and back off whenever I can tell.\"","then":"She frowns. \"What if you can't tell?\""},{"text":"\"Thank you for saying. Which parts feel like too much?\"","then":"She thinks. \"Evenings, mostly, when I'm already worn out.\"","best":true}],"why":"Thanking her and asking which parts helps you respond to her, not to a guess."},{"prompt":"Mira says: \"I don't want you to think it's about you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I don't, but honestly it's hard not to sometimes.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"I'm sorry.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. Let's not overthink this. We're both tired.\"","then":"She nods, but the talk ends there."},{"text":"\"Good, then we can just carry on as we were before.\"","then":"She freezes. \"As before?\""},{"text":"\"I don't. Can we check in like this whenever you like?\"","then":"She smiles. \"I'd like that. Maybe Sundays.\"","best":true}],"why":"A regular, open check-in keeps honesty easy on both sides."}],"relearn":"An open 'how's this feeling, really, whatever you say is okay' invites honesty a leading question shuts down."},
  {"id":"uk-937","cat":"reconnecting","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"back their care","hook":"Riya has been seeing a clinician about postpartum discomfort, and you want to back her fully.","setup":"At dinner, she sets the clinic's notes beside her plate.","steps":[{"prompt":"Riya says: \"The doctor suggested a plan. It'll take a few weeks.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, hopefully that sorts it quickly so things can get back to normal.\"","then":"Her face closes. \"So there's a deadline now.\""},{"text":"\"Weeks? That's a long time to wait.\"","then":"She looks down. \"I know. I'm sorry.\""},{"text":"\"Is it really necessary? Maybe it'll settle by itself.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"The doctor said it wouldn't.\""},{"text":"\"I'm glad you went. Let's do whatever the clinician says.\"","then":"She exhales. \"I was scared you'd be impatient.\"","best":true}],"why":"Backing her care fully, with no rush from you, supports healing."},{"prompt":"Riya says: \"Part of it means we can't be close for a while.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I understand. Just tell me when it's over.\"","then":"She nods, but 'when it's over' sounds like a countdown."},{"text":"\"Okay. Did the doctor mention any exceptions?\"","then":"She flushes. \"I didn't ask. I don't want to.\""},{"text":"\"Sure. I've waited this long.\"","then":"She flinches. \"Please don't say it like that.\""},{"text":"\"Then we don't. No rush from me. Rest and heal.\"","then":"She smiles, eyes wet. \"Thank you for making this easy.\"","best":true}],"why":"A plain 'no rush from me' makes healing the only goal."},{"prompt":"Riya asks: \"Will you come to the next appointment with me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You'll manage. I'll be at work anyway that day.\"","then":"She nods, hurt. She goes alone."},{"text":"\"Sure, if my meeting ends on time.\"","then":"She hides her disappointment. \"Okay. Maybe.\""},{"text":"\"Do you want me to? I don't want to intrude.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"I don't know. It would help if you just came.\""},{"text":"\"Yes. I'll sort the baby's care so you can focus.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Thank you. That takes a weight off.\"","best":true}],"why":"Showing up and taking care of the practical bits turns support into action."}],"relearn":"Backing a partner's care with 'no rush from me' supports healing; tying it to 'back to normal' adds pressure."},
  {"id":"uk-938","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: reconnecting isn't a finish line you race to, it's a slow walk back toward each other. Sit okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits okay","A slow walk","No race"],"affirm":"Reconnecting is a slow walk back toward each other, not a finish line to race; there's no clock on it.","relearn":"Reconnecting is a slow walk back toward each other, not a race to a finish line.","persona":"any","source":"slow walk"},
  {"id":"uk-939","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: feeling like partners again can start with one honest, ten-minute talk, before anything else. Reassuring?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Reassuring","Starts with talk","Good to know"],"affirm":"Feeling like partners again often starts with one honest talk; emotional closeness leads, the rest can follow.","relearn":"Feeling like partners again can start with one honest talk; emotional closeness leads the way.","persona":"any","source":"starts with talk"},
  {"id":"uk-940","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'not yet' is a complete sentence, and a loving partner hears it without a tally. Land okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands okay","Not yet is enough","Heard"],"affirm":"'Not yet' is a complete answer; a loving partner hears it without keeping a tally or asking for a reason.","relearn":"'Not yet' is a full answer; it deserves to be heard without a tally or a demand for reasons.","persona":"any","source":"not yet is enough"},
  {"id":"uk-941","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: closeness you build at your own pace tends to last; closeness rushed by a clock rarely does. Feel it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Feel it","Pace lasts","Makes sense"],"affirm":"Closeness built at your own pace tends to last; closeness rushed to beat a clock rarely holds.","relearn":"Closeness built at your own pace lasts; closeness rushed against a clock rarely holds.","persona":"any","source":"pace that lasts"},
  {"id":"uk-942","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: needing time after a baby isn't a problem to fix, it's a body and heart doing exactly their job. Okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Okay","Doing its job","Not a problem"],"affirm":"Needing time after a baby isn't a problem to fix; it's your body and heart healing on their own schedule.","relearn":"Needing time after a baby isn't a fault to fix; it's healing happening on its own schedule.","persona":"Divya","source":"healing not fault"},
  {"id":"uk-943","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the warmest reconnecting often hides in dull moments, folding clothes together, a shared yawn. Notice it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Notice it","In the small stuff","Sweet"],"affirm":"A lot of real reconnecting lives in ordinary moments together; closeness grows in the dull bits, not just date nights.","relearn":"Real reconnecting often lives in ordinary shared moments, not only in grand or planned ones.","persona":"any","source":"in the small stuff"},
  {"id":"uk-944","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'I miss you' can simply mean 'I miss you', no hidden ask underneath. Restful to hear?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Restful","No hidden ask","Good"],"affirm":"'I miss you' can mean exactly that, with no hidden demand; closeness named honestly carries no pressure.","relearn":"'I miss you' can mean just that, with no hidden ask; honest closeness carries no demand.","persona":"any","source":"no hidden ask"},
  {"id":"uk-945","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: reconnecting goes two ways, your readiness matters as much as theirs. Worth holding onto?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth it","Both ways","Mine too"],"affirm":"Reconnecting respects both partners' readiness; your own 'not yet' or 'yes' counts every bit as much as theirs.","relearn":"Reconnecting respects both partners' readiness; your own pace counts as much as your partner's.","persona":"any","source":"both readiness"},
  {"id":"uk-946","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can be exhausted, out of sync, and still deeply on the same team. One doesn't cancel the other. See it?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["See it","Still a team","True"],"affirm":"Being tired and out of sync on closeness doesn't cancel being on the same team; both can be true at once.","relearn":"You can be exhausted and out of sync on intimacy and still be firmly on the same team.","persona":"any","source":"still a team"},
  {"id":"uk-947","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: there's no medal for being 'back to normal' fastest, only a couple that feels safe with each other. Agree?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Agree","No medal","Safe matters"],"affirm":"There's no prize for reconnecting fastest; a couple that feels safe and unhurried with each other is the real win.","relearn":"There's no reward for reconnecting fastest; feeling safe and unhurried together is what matters.","persona":"any","source":"no medal"},
  {"id":"uk-948","cat":"reconnecting","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: telling a clinician something hurts isn't unromantic, it's how you protect the closeness you want. Lands?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands","Protects it","Makes sense"],"affirm":"Asking a clinician about postpartum discomfort isn't unromantic; it protects the closeness you're rebuilding.","relearn":"Getting care for postpartum discomfort protects the closeness you want; it isn't unromantic.","persona":"Divya","source":"care protects closeness"},
  {"id":"uk-949","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that pressure a partner who isn't ready.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Whenever you're ready, I'm here.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're making me wait an awfully long time.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Let's just cuddle and watch something.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"A normal couple would be fine by now.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"No agenda, I just like being near you.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Making me wait' and 'a normal couple would be fine' both pressure; the rest follow the partner's readiness.","relearn":"Lines that frame waiting as a fault or invoke 'a normal couple' are pressure; readiness-led lines are not.","persona":"any","source":"spot the pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-950","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that turn a partner's discomfort into something to push past.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"If it hurts, we stop, no question.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"It'll be fine if you just relax.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Want to ask a clinician about it?\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Push through, it's normal at first.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Tell me the second it's uncomfortable.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Just relax' and 'push through' dismiss real discomfort; the rest take pain seriously and offer care.","relearn":"Lines that tell a partner to 'relax' or 'push through' pain dismiss real discomfort that may need care.","persona":"any","source":"spot dismiss pain"},
  {"id":"uk-951","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses that read a partner's 'not yet' as rejection.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Okay, thanks for telling me.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"So you just don't want me anymore.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"No problem, I'm glad you're honest.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Fine, I'll stop trying then.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"We'll find our way, no rush.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'You don't want me' and 'I'll stop trying' read recovery as rejection; the others accept 'not yet' warmly.","relearn":"Treating a partner's 'not yet' as rejection or sulking is the trap; the warm move is simply to accept it.","persona":"any","source":"spot rejection read"},
  {"id":"uk-952","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'reconnecting' moves that actually skip the closeness they claim to want.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Asking how their week truly felt","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Scheduling intimacy like a chore","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A long, unhurried hug","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping score of who initiates","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Sharing a quiet cup of tea","trick":false}],"why":"Scheduling it like a chore and scorekeeping skip real closeness; the rest build the bond they claim to want.","relearn":"Treating closeness as a scheduled chore or a scoreboard skips the very connection it pretends to seek.","persona":"any","source":"spot fake closeness"},
  {"id":"uk-953","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two things that pile family pressure onto a couple's private pace.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"In-laws giving you a free evening out","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Relatives hinting at a second baby soon","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A grandparent watching the baby so you rest","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Aunts asking why you're 'still not normal'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Family making space for couple time","trick":false}],"why":"Hinting at another baby and asking why you're 'still not normal' pressure your pace; the rest make room for it.","relearn":"Family pressure (a 'second baby' push, 'why still not normal') intrudes on a couple's pace; real help makes room.","persona":"any","source":"spot family pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-954","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that quietly make intimacy feel owed.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Only if you genuinely want to.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"After all I do, the least you could do...\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Your comfort matters more to me.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"We're married, so it's kind of expected.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"There's never any pressure from me.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'The least you could do' and 'it's kind of expected' frame intimacy as owed; the rest leave it freely chosen.","relearn":"Lines that trade favours or claim marriage 'expects' intimacy make it owed; closeness is only ever freely given.","persona":"any","source":"spot owed framing"},
  {"id":"uk-955","cat":"reconnecting","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that wait for a grand gesture instead of rebuilding now.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A two-minute hug before work","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Saving all effort for a far-off trip","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A kind text in the afternoon","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Putting closeness off till 'life calms down'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Thanking them for one real thing","trick":false}],"why":"Saving it all for a far-off trip and waiting for 'life to calm down' delay closeness; small moves rebuild it now.","relearn":"Waiting for a grand trip or for 'life to calm down' postpones closeness; small daily moves rebuild it now.","persona":"any","source":"spot grand-gesture wait"},
  {"id":"uk-956","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each quiet bid for closeness to the warm response it's hoping for.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Sit with me a minute?\"","right":"Putting the phone down and joining"},{"left":"\"Rough day, honestly.\"","right":"Listening before fixing anything"},{"left":"Reaching for your hand","right":"Holding it without making it more"},{"left":"\"I miss talking to you.\"","right":"Making real time soon, not 'later'"},{"left":"A tired, hopeful smile","right":"A smile and a 'come here' back"}],"relearn":"Small bids for closeness, a 'sit with me', a reach, a smile, just want a warm, unhurried response.","persona":"any","source":"bids and responses"},
  {"id":"uk-957","cat":"reconnecting","type":"match","hook":"Match each reason intimacy may be slow to the reassurance that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"Still healing physically","right":"Bodies recover on their own clock"},{"left":"Bone-tired from night feeds","right":"Desire returns as rest does"},{"left":"Feeling unlike your old self","right":"That shift is common, not a fault"},{"left":"Discomfort during intimacy","right":"A clinician can help with this"},{"left":"Just needing more time","right":"Time isn't rejection or a problem"}],"relearn":"Slow intimacy after a baby, healing, tiredness, a changed self, discomfort, needing time, each has a calm answer.","persona":"any","source":"reasons and reassurance"},
  {"id":"uk-990","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I earn for the family, so the baby work is fairly hers.\"","myth":{"un":"I earn for the family, so the baby work is fairly hers.","re":"Earning is real, but it doesn't buy you out of care and managing; both are parents off the clock too.","why":"Provision is not your half of the parenting."},"relearn":"Earning doesn't cancel your share of care; both parents are still parents at home.","persona":"Vikram","source":"earning isn't your half"},
  {"id":"uk-991","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I do plenty at home, so the load between us is already fair.\"","myth":{"un":"I do plenty at home, so the load between us is already fair.","re":"Doing tasks isn't the same as carrying the planning and worry; check who still holds the mental load.","why":"Effort can be real and the load still lopsided."},"relearn":"Helping with tasks isn't fairness if one of you still carries all the planning and worry.","persona":"Vikram","source":"tasks aren't the load"},
  {"id":"uk-992","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A real fix means a strict 50/50 tally of every chore.\"","myth":{"un":"A real fix means a strict 50/50 tally of every chore.","re":"Fair means renegotiating to genuinely balanced, not a stopwatch; lopsided is the problem, not the exact split.","why":"Balance, not an exact tally, is the goal."},"relearn":"Fair isn't a stopwatched 50/50; it's renegotiating until neither of you is buried.","persona":"any","source":"balance not tally"},
  {"id":"uk-993","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My in-laws say the wife runs the home; who am I to upset that?\"","myth":{"un":"My in-laws say the wife runs the home; who am I to upset that?","re":"You two get to decide how you parent; an old default isn't a rule you must keep.","why":"Your home, your shared call."},"relearn":"An elder's old 'wife runs the home' default isn't binding; you two choose your own split.","persona":"Vikram","source":"old default not a rule"},
  {"id":"uk-994","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If she just tells me each task, that's me doing my share.\"","myth":{"un":"If she just tells me each task, that's me doing my share.","re":"Waiting to be told leaves her as the manager of it all; owning a domain unasked lifts that load.","why":"Being assigned tasks isn't sharing the managing."},"relearn":"Being told each task still leaves her managing everything; owning a domain unasked is the real share.","persona":"Vikram","source":"told vs owning"},
  {"id":"uk-995","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Remembering jabs and supplies isn't really work; she just does it.\"","myth":{"un":"Remembering jabs and supplies isn't really work; she just does it.","re":"The invisible load, like planning, remembering and worrying, is real work, even when no one sees it.","why":"Unseen managing is still labour."},"relearn":"Remembering, planning and worrying is real, tiring work even though no one sees it happen.","persona":"Divya","source":"invisible load is work"},
  {"id":"uk-996","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Scolding myself to 'step up, dad' is how I'll finally change.\"","myth":{"un":"Scolding myself to 'step up, dad' is how I'll finally change.","re":"Shame tends to make you tune out; concrete actions and respect for effort move you further.","why":"Shame stalls; clear next steps move you."},"relearn":"Shame and 'step up, dad' stall you; concrete actions and credit for effort actually change things.","persona":"Vikram","source":"action not shame"},
  {"id":"uk-997","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"How we split things now won't really matter to the kids.\"","myth":{"un":"How we split things now won't really matter to the kids.","re":"Children learn fairness or its absence by watching you two; how you share is a lesson they absorb.","why":"Your split is a model they copy."},"relearn":"Kids learn fairness, or its absence, by watching how their parents share the load.","persona":"Vikram","source":"kids learn by watching"},
  {"id":"uk-998","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Sharing the load is just about getting chores done faster.\"","myth":{"un":"Sharing the load is just about getting chores done faster.","re":"It's practical and symbolic: a fair load tells your partner the children are 'ours', not their burden alone.","why":"It says 'ours, not yours'."},"relearn":"A shared load isn't only efficient; it tells your partner the kids are 'ours', not theirs alone.","persona":"Vikram","source":"ours not yours"},
  {"id":"uk-999","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"I take the fun bits, like play and outings, and that's my fair share.\"","myth":{"un":"I take the fun bits, like play and outings, and that's my fair share.","re":"Cherry-picking the nice parts leaves the grind to one person; share the dull and hard bits too.","why":"Fair means the boring tasks as well."},"relearn":"Taking only the fun parts isn't sharing; the dull, hard tasks need owning too.","persona":"Vikram","source":"share the grind"},
  {"id":"uk-1000","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once she's back at her job, then I'll start pulling my weight.\"","myth":{"un":"Once she's back at her job, then I'll start pulling my weight.","re":"The load is heavy now; sharing shouldn't wait for her to earn again to count as fair.","why":"Fair care isn't conditional on her income."},"relearn":"Sharing the load now, not only when she's earning again, is what fairness means.","persona":"Vikram","source":"don't wait to share"},
  {"id":"uk-1001","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: owning a whole domain end-to-end, or just waiting to be handed tasks?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Running bath and bedtime start to finish"},{"id":"b","text":"Asking 'what do you need me to do?' each evening"},{"id":"c","text":"Booking and taking the baby to every check-up"},{"id":"d","text":"Doing dishes only once she points them out"},{"id":"e","text":"Tracking the nappy stock and reordering yourself"},{"id":"f","text":"Standing by until she delegates a step"}],"bins":[{"id":"own","label":"Owns a domain","valence":"pos"},{"id":"wait","label":"Waits to be told","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"own","b":"wait","c":"own","d":"wait","e":"own","f":"wait"},"relearn":"Owning bath, check-ups and supplies end-to-end shares the managing; waiting to be told leaves it all on her.","persona":"Vikram","source":"own vs wait"},
  {"id":"uk-1002","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: invisible mental-load work, or a hands-on visible task?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Remembering the next vaccination is due"},{"id":"b","text":"Wiping and changing the baby"},{"id":"c","text":"Noticing the formula tin is nearly empty"},{"id":"d","text":"Pushing the pram on a walk"},{"id":"e","text":"Planning who minds the baby on Monday"},{"id":"f","text":"Folding the washed baby clothes"}],"bins":[{"id":"mental","label":"Invisible load","valence":"uhoh"},{"id":"hands","label":"Visible task","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"mental","b":"hands","c":"mental","d":"hands","e":"mental","f":"hands"},"relearn":"Remembering, noticing and planning are the invisible load; changing, walking and folding are the visible tasks.","persona":"any","source":"mental vs visible"},
  {"id":"uk-1003","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a phrase that treats dad as a real parent, or one that treats him as a helper?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I've got the baby tonight, you rest.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"I'm babysitting my own kid today.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Bedtime's mine, start to finish.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"I helped out with the baby a bit.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"I'll handle the doctor visit myself.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Tell me if you need a hand.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"parent","label":"Real parent","valence":"pos"},{"id":"helper","label":"Just a helper","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"parent","b":"helper","c":"parent","d":"helper","e":"parent","f":"helper"},"relearn":"'I've got tonight', 'bedtime's mine', 'I'll handle it' own it; 'babysitting', 'helped out', 'need a hand' make dad a helper.","persona":"Vikram","source":"parent vs helper phrasing"},
  {"id":"uk-1004","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a move that renegotiates the load fairly, or one that just dumps it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Listing every baby job together, then dividing it"},{"id":"b","text":"Assuming she'll keep doing what she always has"},{"id":"c","text":"Swapping who does nights each week"},{"id":"d","text":"Saying 'you're just better at this' and stepping back"},{"id":"e","text":"Checking in monthly on whether the split still feels fair"},{"id":"f","text":"Taking only the tasks you happen to enjoy"}],"bins":[{"id":"share","label":"Renegotiates fairly","valence":"pos"},{"id":"dump","label":"Dumps the load","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"share","b":"dump","c":"share","d":"dump","e":"share","f":"dump"},"relearn":"Mapping and re-checking shares fairly; assuming and cherry-picking dump the load.","persona":"any","source":"renegotiate vs dump"},
  {"id":"uk-1005","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: respecting his effort while naming the gap, or just shaming him?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You've done the nights this week. Thank you. Can we split days too?\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You never do anything around here.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"You're great at bedtime; let's add the morning rush.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Any other dad would've stepped up by now.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"That helped a lot today; here's the next bit I'm carrying.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Why do I have to spell out being a parent to you?\""}],"bins":[{"id":"respect","label":"Respect + ask","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shame","label":"Shaming","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"respect","b":"shame","c":"respect","d":"shame","e":"respect","f":"shame"},"relearn":"Thank the effort, then name the gap; shaming shuts him down.","persona":"Divya","source":"respect vs shame"},
  {"id":"uk-1006","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a sign the load is shared, or that one person carries it all?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Both of you know when the next jab is due"},{"id":"b","text":"Only one of you ever notices supplies running low"},{"id":"c","text":"Either parent can run the bedtime routine alone"},{"id":"d","text":"One partner is the 'default' the baby's cries go to"},{"id":"e","text":"You both hold the week's childcare plan in your heads"},{"id":"f","text":"One of you needs a handover list to manage a day"}],"bins":[{"id":"shared","label":"Truly shared","valence":"pos"},{"id":"solo","label":"One carries it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"shared","b":"solo","c":"shared","d":"solo","e":"shared","f":"solo"},"relearn":"Both knowing and planning means shared; one 'default' parent means solo.","persona":"any","source":"shared vs solo signs"},
  {"id":"uk-1007","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: what a fairly shared load protects, or what a lopsided one slowly erodes?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Warmth and goodwill between you two"},{"id":"b","text":"A quiet, growing resentment in one partner"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling like teammates, not rivals"},{"id":"d","text":"One parent's sense of being unseen"},{"id":"e","text":"Trust that the other will carry their bit"},{"id":"f","text":"A scorecard of who did what"}],"bins":[{"id":"protects","label":"Sharing protects","valence":"pos"},{"id":"erodes","label":"Lopsided erodes","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"protects","b":"erodes","c":"protects","d":"erodes","e":"protects","f":"erodes"},"relearn":"A fair load protects warmth, teamwork and trust; a lopsided one breeds resentment, feeling unseen and scorekeeping.","persona":"any","source":"protects vs erodes"},
  {"id":"uk-1008","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: which baby jobs at night truly share the load, or quietly leave it on one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Taking alternate wake-ups so each of you sleeps"},{"id":"b","text":"Sleeping through and assuming she'll get up"},{"id":"c","text":"Doing the night feed so she rests the morning"},{"id":"d","text":"Waiting for her to nudge you awake each time"},{"id":"e","text":"Owning the early shift so she has a lie-in"},{"id":"f","text":"Calling getting up 'her thing' since she's home"}],"bins":[{"id":"share","label":"Shares nights","valence":"pos"},{"id":"leave","label":"Leaves it on her","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"share","b":"leave","c":"share","d":"leave","e":"share","f":"leave"},"relearn":"Alternating, feeding and owning a shift share nights; sleeping through doesn't.","persona":"Vikram","source":"share nights"},
  {"id":"uk-1009","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"reframe babysitting","hook":"Your wife Sunita is out tonight, and you tell a friend you're babysitting your daughter.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your friend Ravi laughs and says, \"Brave man!\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Shrug it off, since it's just a figure of speech","then":"Ravi grins. The word stays in your vocabulary."},{"text":"Joke that you deserve a medal for the night","then":"Ravi laughs and offers a beer. You feel smug for a moment, then a bit off."},{"text":"Say you're doing Sunita a favour so she can relax","then":"Ravi says she's lucky. The favour frame settles in."},{"text":"Say you're not babysitting but parenting tonight","then":"Ravi pauses. \"Fair. My wife says the same thing.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming it parenting, not babysitting, treats it as your job, not a favour."},{"prompt":"At bedtime, your daughter cries and asks for her mother. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Call Sunita right away to come back","then":"She answers, worried, and cuts her evening short. You feel useless."},{"text":"Put on a cartoon to calm her down","then":"She quiets in front of the screen. The crying starts again when it ends."},{"text":"Tell her firmly to stop, because Mummy's back soon","then":"She cries louder. You feel out of your depth."},{"text":"Stay with her, hold her, and say you're here","then":"She sobs, then hiccups. Ten minutes later she's asleep on your chest.","best":true}],"why":"Staying and learning to soothe her is part of owning the job."},{"prompt":"Sunita comes home and asks how it went. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say it was fine, and you were glad to give her a night off","then":"Her smile is thin. \"Give me? She's your daughter too.\""},{"text":"Say it was hard work and that she owes you one now","then":"She raises an eyebrow. \"Owes you?\""},{"text":"Say it was fine and hand her the baby monitor back","then":"She takes it and checks on the baby anyway. You still don't know where the night-light is."},{"text":"Tell her the whole night, including what you'd change","then":"She listens. \"Next time I'll do bath and you do bedtime,\" she says, smiling.","best":true}],"why":"Owning the evening, and learning from it, is what makes it parenting."}],"debrief":"Rename 'babysitting my own kid' as parenting. It's your job.","relearn":"Your own child is never babysitting; it's parenting."},
  {"id":"uk-1010","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"own the managing","hook":"Your partner Preeti seems overwhelmed by the unseen managing of the baby's life, and you can't see what it is.","steps":[{"prompt":"Preeti snaps that you have no idea what she's carrying. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it can't be that much and she's exaggerating","then":"Her eyes flash. \"Then you do it for a week.\""},{"text":"Tell her to hand you tasks whenever she needs them","then":"She stares. \"So I'd still be the one planning everything.\""},{"text":"Apologise and offer to do the dishes tonight","then":"She nods. The dishes are clean, and the rest stays unseen."},{"text":"Ask her to list what she's managing, and listen","then":"She takes a breath. \"Okay. Get comfortable. It's long.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking to see the whole list shows you the managing you couldn't see."},{"prompt":"The next evening, Preeti mentions the baby's next vaccine visit is due. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask what time to be there and what to carry","then":"She lists everything. She's still managing, with you as an extra pair of hands."},{"text":"Say you'll go along if she reminds you","then":"She sighs. \"So I remember, and you show up.\""},{"text":"Suggest she books it and you'll take the day off","then":"She books it. You take the day off, and the remembering stays hers."},{"text":"Say you'll own vaccines and check-ups from now on","then":"She blinks. \"All of it? Dates, forms, the doctor?\" \"All of it,\" you say.","best":true}],"why":"Owning one whole domain, like vaccines, takes the remembering off her."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, you spot another job Preeti is still carrying in her head. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Mention it and wait for her to assign it","then":"She says, \"Fine, do this.\" You're back to being her assistant."},{"text":"Leave it, since you've already done your bit","then":"She keeps it, and notices you stopped short."},{"text":"Ask her to just remind you when it's due","then":"She agrees, but she's still the one watching the calendar."},{"text":"Take it on fully without waiting to be handed it","then":"You set reminders and tell her it's yours. She lets out a breath.","best":true}],"why":"Taking a job on fully is the fix, not waiting to be assigned."},{"prompt":"A month on, Preeti still double-checks the baby jobs that are meant to be yours. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap that she should trust you by now","then":"She flinches. \"It's hard to stop.\""},{"text":"Tell her to stop worrying about it","then":"She nods. Now she worries about the worrying."},{"text":"Give up and let her check everything","then":"She checks. The managing is back in her head."},{"text":"Show her how you track those jobs, and what's done","then":"She looks it over. \"Okay. I can let go of this one.\"","best":true}],"why":"Showing her your system lets her hand over the worry, not just the task."}],"debrief":"Make the unseen managing visible and own a whole chunk, not stray tasks.","relearn":"Own a whole chunk of the managing; being handed tasks leaves it on her."},
  {"id":"uk-1011","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"map into domains","hook":"You and Faisal keep clashing over who does what, and you want a fix, not a scorecard.","steps":[{"prompt":"Another clash starts over the laundry pile. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Start a tally of who did what this week","then":"You tally it on paper. He starts one too, and the numbers never agree."},{"text":"Agree to both try harder and leave it vague","then":"You both nod. By Thursday the pile is back."},{"text":"Point out that he never notices what needs doing","then":"He fires back with his own list of things you missed."},{"text":"Suggest listing every job and dividing by domain","then":"Faisal nods slowly. \"Every job? Okay. I'll bring a pen.\"","best":true}],"why":"Mapping every job into domains you each own replaces keeping score."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you both find ten minutes to talk about the split. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"List only the big jobs, like night feeds and doctor visits","then":"The list is short and tidy. The daily invisible bits stay with you."},{"text":"List only the jobs you do, so he can see them","then":"He says it looks one-sided. You both stare at the page."},{"text":"Let him write his own list of what he does","then":"His list is short, yours would be longer, and now it's a contest."},{"text":"List every job, including the small, invisible ones","then":"The list runs over two pages. Faisal whistles softly.","best":true}],"why":"Including the invisible jobs shows the real load."},{"prompt":"Faisal asks how you'll decide who takes what. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Split it by count, one for you and one for him","then":"He gets the easy ones and you get the hard ones. It still feels off."},{"text":"Keep what you're better at, and he takes the rest","then":"He ends up with the leftovers, and neither of you feels good."},{"text":"Let him choose first, and you take what's left","then":"He picks the quick ones. You get the daily grind again."},{"text":"Pick whole domains to own, and revisit later","then":"He nods. \"I'll take laundry and doctor visits.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owned domains you revisit are fairer than a tally or a leftover pile."}],"debrief":"Map the jobs into owned domains rather than keeping score or staying vague.","relearn":"Divide the work into owned domains; a scorecard or 'try harder' won't."},
  {"id":"uk-1012","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"reset double standard","hook":"At a family function, relatives praise you lavishly for the ordinary parenting Nandini does unremarked.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says you're a hero for changing one diaper. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Soak up the praise and say nothing","then":"The aunt tells the others. You feel good, then a bit uneasy."},{"text":"Wave it off modestly and say it's nothing","then":"The aunt says you're too humble and praises you again."},{"text":"Point out loudly that Nandini never gets thanked","then":"The room stiffens. Nandini looks embarrassed to be pulled in."},{"text":"Smile and say you both do this, Nandini all day too","then":"The aunt laughs, surprised. \"Of course. She does, doesn't she.\"","best":true}],"why":"A light reset gives her the credit without making a scene."},{"prompt":"Later, Nandini says the praise made her feel invisible. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say they meant well, so don't take it personally","then":"She stays quiet. The feeling stays too."},{"text":"Say she should tell them herself if it bothers her","then":"She sighs. \"Why is it always mine to fix?\""},{"text":"Say you did pitch in, which is more than most dads","then":"She stares. \"More than most dads.\""},{"text":"Say she's right, and you'll speak up next time","then":"She nods. \"Thank you. It matters.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning your part of the double standard shows her you see it."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, another relative says you're wonderful for taking the baby out. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Smile and say you're just trying your best","then":"The praise settles on you again, and Nandini hears it from the kitchen."},{"text":"Say you'd rather not discuss parenting at parties","then":"The relative looks puzzled. The frame stays as it was."},{"text":"Joke that Nandini has trained you well","then":"There's laughter. The joke keeps the idea that you're helping."},{"text":"Say Nandini does it daily too. It's just parenting","then":"The relative pauses. \"Well, yes, of course she does too.\"","best":true}],"why":"A calm, repeated reset shifts the standard without shame."}],"debrief":"Reset the double standard kindly when praised for what she does unseen.","relearn":"Reset the double standard; the same parenting deserves the same credit."},
  {"id":"uk-1013","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"earning isn't the share","hook":"You're back at work while Tanvi is on leave, and you assume the baby load is now all hers.","steps":[{"prompt":"You get home tired, and the baby is fussing in Tanvi's arms. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say your work pays for the family, so you need rest first","then":"Tanvi's jaw tightens. \"Mine is also work.\""},{"text":"Say you'll take over once she's back at work","then":"She stares. \"So I do it all until then.\""},{"text":"Offer to help on Saturday when you're free","then":"She says thanks, but Saturday is days away and tonight is now."},{"text":"Take the baby and say evenings and mornings are yours too","then":"She hands her over slowly. \"Really? Every day?\"","best":true}],"why":"Leave is not a free pass. You own real care on weekdays too."},{"prompt":"A few nights later, Tanvi says she hasn't slept properly in a week. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest she naps when the baby does","then":"She laughs tiredly. \"That's when I wash bottles and do everything else.\""},{"text":"Say you'd help but you have an early meeting","then":"She nods. The early meeting is every day."},{"text":"Suggest she asks her mother to stay","then":"Her mother comes. The load moves around, but it isn't shared."},{"text":"Take tonight's wake-up and say you'll alternate","then":"She sinks into the pillow. \"Wake me if she needs feeding.\"","best":true}],"why":"Alternating nights shares the tiredness even when one of you is at work."},{"prompt":"Tanvi asks what part of the baby's care you'll own. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll help wherever she asks","then":"She sighs. \"Then I'm still the one asking.\""},{"text":"Say your salary is your share","then":"Her voice goes quiet. \"That's not what I asked.\""},{"text":"Say you'll figure it out after the next work deadline","then":"She nods. The deadline moves, and so does the answer."},{"text":"Say you'll own bath and bedtime, start to finish","then":"She smiles. \"Then I can stop thinking about it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning a domain end to end is fairer than helping whenever asked."},{"prompt":"A month on, Tanvi is preparing to go back to work. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say things can go back to how they were","then":"She stares. \"How they were? I did most of it.\""},{"text":"Say you'll manage while she settles in","then":"She smiles, then asks: \"Managing what, exactly? Which days?\""},{"text":"Suggest hiring help and leave it there","then":"She agrees. The care work is still unshared."},{"text":"Ask her to plan the new split with you first","then":"She pulls out a notebook. \"Good. Let's do it tonight.\"","best":true}],"why":"Re-planning the split before she returns keeps care equal when you both work."}],"debrief":"Earning doesn't buy you out; own real care even when one's on leave.","relearn":"Earning isn't your half; share care on unequal schedules."},
  {"id":"uk-1014","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Meena","source":"skill by doing","hook":"Your partner Kunal says you're just better with the baby, so he'll do less.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kunal hands you the baby again after a fussy feed. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Accept the compliment and keep doing more of it yourself","then":"He beams. You do the rest of the evening alone."},{"text":"Agree with him, but ask him to help out on weekends","then":"He nods. Weekends come, and it's still mostly you."},{"text":"Say he's just being lazy and using this as an excuse","then":"He goes defensive. \"I'm not lazy. I just get it wrong.\""},{"text":"Say skill comes from doing, and ask him to start","then":"He blinks. \"Okay. But you'll have to show me.\"","best":true}],"why":"Skill comes from doing, so name the trap and ask him to start."},{"prompt":"The next weekend, Kunal offers to try bath time, and it ends with a crying baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take over, since it's quicker for you to do it","then":"He steps back, relieved. He learns nothing."},{"text":"Tell him what he did wrong, step by step","then":"He gets defensive. \"You could have shown me before.\""},{"text":"Laugh and say this is why you do it","then":"He laughs too, and calls you the expert again."},{"text":"Stay calm, show him one tip, and let him finish","then":"He tries again, slower. The baby stops crying halfway through.","best":true}],"why":"Staying calm and letting him finish is how skill grows."},{"prompt":"A month on, Kunal says he wants a bigger part in the baby's care. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say maybe later, when he's more confident","then":"He nods. Later stretches, and he stays on the edges."},{"text":"Give him the easy ones and keep the hard ones","then":"He takes the easy ones. The hard ones stay yours."},{"text":"Say you'd need to check his work afterwards","then":"He sighs. \"Then it's still your job.\""},{"text":"Say yes and hand over the whole night routine","then":"He takes a breath. \"Okay. Teach me the lullaby.\"","best":true}],"why":"Handing over a whole job, and trusting him with it, completes the shift."}],"debrief":"'You're better at it' cedes the load; skill comes from owning it.","relearn":"'You're better at it' is a trap; you improve by owning a domain."},
  {"id":"uk-1015","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"model it now","hook":"You realise your kids only ever see Asha cook, feed and worry.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your daughter is playing house, and the father doll only reads the newspaper. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell yourself she's too young to notice","then":"She keeps playing. The game repeats every day."},{"text":"Tell her fathers do things too, and move on","then":"She nods. Words don't compete with what she sees each night."},{"text":"Ask Asha to include you more in the routine","then":"Asha sighs. \"Include you? It's your routine too.\""},{"text":"Take on visible care starting this week","then":"You pick up the spoon that evening. Your daughter watches, wide-eyed.","best":true}],"why":"Kids learn from what they watch, so visible action beats words."},{"prompt":"Asha is cooking dinner, and your son asks why Papa never cooks. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say Papa works all day and Mama does the cooking","then":"He nods. He's learned a rule about who does what."},{"text":"Say you'll cook someday when you have time","then":"He waits. Someday doesn't arrive."},{"text":"Laugh it off and offer to order in tonight instead","then":"Dinner arrives in boxes. The question stays unanswered."},{"text":"Say you'll cook together on Sundays, starting now","then":"He grins. Sunday comes, and he stands on a stool stirring the rice.","best":true}],"why":"A specific, visible plan beats a vague promise or a rule about roles."},{"prompt":"A week on, Asha is up at night with a sick child while you sleep. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stay asleep, since she's already up and coping fine","then":"Morning comes. Your son says Mama stayed up, not Papa."},{"text":"Get up and ask her what you should do to help","then":"She sighs. \"Please just pick something.\""},{"text":"Take over, and tell Asha you're doing her a favour","then":"Asha thanks you stiffly. \"A favour? They're your kids too.\""},{"text":"Get up, take a turn, and let the kids see you there","then":"You carry your son to the sofa. He clutches your shirt, and Asha sits beside you.","best":true}],"why":"Kids learn by seeing both parents share the hard moments too."},{"prompt":"Months later, your daughter asks at dinner whether Papa can cook tomorrow. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you're too tired these days","then":"She looks let down. \"You always say that.\""},{"text":"Say Mama will do it, since she's quicker","then":"Asha sighs. Your daughter learns who cooks in this house."},{"text":"Laugh and say she's teasing you","then":"Her face falls. She meant it."},{"text":"Say yes, and ask her to be your helper","then":"She grins. The next evening she drags a stool to the stove.","best":true}],"why":"Saying yes where they can see it shows them what sharing looks like."}],"debrief":"Kids learn sharing by watching. Own care visibly now, rather than waiting.","relearn":"Kids copy the split they watch; share care visibly now."},
  {"id":"uk-1016","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"surface and renegotiate","hook":"Resentment is building because you silently carry almost everything for the baby with Naveen.","steps":[{"prompt":"Naveen asks why you seem cold this week. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's nothing and turn away to face the wall","then":"He shrugs. The chill stays."},{"text":"Drop a hint about how busy you've been all week","then":"He says he's busy too, and the hint is lost."},{"text":"Let it simmer until you can't hold it in any more","then":"Two days later a dropped bottle sets off a row about everything."},{"text":"Calmly say you carry most of it, and ask to talk","then":"He looks surprised but puts his phone away. \"Okay. Talk me through it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the imbalance calmly lets you fix it before it becomes a row."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Naveen says the house feels tense and asks what he's missing. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's too long to explain","then":"He shrugs and lets it go. The days stay the same."},{"text":"Say he'd understand if he did a week of it","then":"He bristles. \"That's not helpful.\""},{"text":"Send him a long list of complaints at 2am","then":"He reads it in the morning. He feels attacked, not informed."},{"text":"Walk him through a day, hour by hour, calmly","then":"He's quiet after. \"I've been missing most of that.\"","best":true}],"why":"A calm, full picture helps him understand instead of defend."},{"prompt":"At the weekend, Naveen asks straight out what would make things fairer. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say he should just do more, and you won't say what","then":"He agrees, but he doesn't know what more means."},{"text":"Say he owes you, so he takes the hard ones for a month","then":"He agrees stiffly. It feels like a penalty, not a partnership."},{"text":"Say things are fine now, since you talked","then":"He looks relieved. The load stays exactly as it was."},{"text":"Propose you each own jobs, and review in two weeks","then":"He nods. \"I'll take mornings. Let's check in on the fifteenth.\"","best":true}],"why":"Concrete jobs and a review turn a good talk into a fairer week."}],"debrief":"Surface the full load and renegotiate before resentment bursts.","relearn":"Surface the load and renegotiate; silent resentment and hints don't fix it."},
  {"id":"uk-1017","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'helper' phrase to the equal-parent reframe of it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I'm babysitting the baby tonight\"","right":"\"I'm parenting tonight. She's mine too\""},{"left":"\"Just tell me what to do\"","right":"\"I'll own bath and bedtime end to end\""},{"left":"\"I helped out a bit today\"","right":"\"I did my share of the work today\""},{"left":"\"You're better at this than me\"","right":"\"I'll learn it by doing my turn\""},{"left":"\"I earn, so that's my contribution\"","right":"\"I earn and I still share the care\""}],"relearn":"Owning, sharing and learning are the equal-parent reframes of the helper lines.","persona":"Vikram","source":"reframe helper lines"},
  {"id":"uk-1018","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each piece of the invisible mental load to what owning it actually looks like.","pairs":[{"left":"Remembering vaccinations","right":"Keeping the jab calendar yourself"},{"left":"Watching supplies run low","right":"Reordering nappies before they're out"},{"left":"Planning the week's childcare","right":"Holding Monday's minder plan in your head"},{"left":"Tracking what the baby has eaten","right":"Knowing the last feed without asking"},{"left":"Worrying over a new symptom","right":"Booking the check-up off your own bat"}],"relearn":"Owning the mental load means holding the calendar, plan and worry yourself.","persona":"any","source":"owning the mental load"},
  {"id":"uk-1019","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each post-baby job to the respect it actually deserves.","pairs":[{"left":"Night feeds and soothing","right":"Real, tiring work, equally valued"},{"left":"Remembering jabs and supplies","right":"The mental load, shared not solo"},{"left":"Daytime care while the other works","right":"Parenting, not 'babysitting'"},{"left":"Planning the week's logistics","right":"Invisible labour that counts"},{"left":"Cleaning up after every meal","right":"A shared chore, not one person's"}],"relearn":"Nights, remembering, daytime care, planning and cleaning are all real work deserving equal respect and a fair share.","persona":"any","source":"respect each job"},
  {"id":"uk-1020","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each lopsided-load complaint to the fairer move that answers it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I'm the only one who notices anything\"","right":"Both of you track the baby's needs"},{"left":"\"I always have to ask you to help\"","right":"You own a domain without being asked"},{"left":"\"You only do the fun parts\"","right":"You take the dull, hard tasks too"},{"left":"\"It's all on me at night\"","right":"You alternate the wake-ups fairly"},{"left":"\"You never plan anything\"","right":"You hold part of the week's plan"}],"relearn":"Each complaint has a fairer move: both track, own unasked, take the grind, alternate nights, hold the plan.","persona":"Divya","source":"complaint to fix"},
  {"id":"uk-1021","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each shaming line to the respectful version that actually lands.","pairs":[{"left":"\"You never lift a finger\"","right":"\"Can we split the evenings this week?\""},{"left":"\"Any real dad would do more\"","right":"\"You're great at bedtime. Let's add mornings\""},{"left":"\"Why must I spell out parenting?\"","right":"\"Here's the managing I'm holding. Take a piece\""},{"left":"\"You're useless with the baby\"","right":"\"Want to own bath time and learn it with me?\""},{"left":"\"I do everything around here\"","right":"\"Let's map who does what and rebalance\""}],"relearn":"Swap shaming for a clear ask, credit plus a next step.","persona":"Meena","source":"shame to respect"},
  {"id":"uk-1022","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each kind of fairness move to what it protects in your bond.","pairs":[{"left":"Owning a whole domain end to end","right":"Lifts the managing, not just the task"},{"left":"Thanking your partner's effort","right":"Keeps goodwill instead of scorekeeping"},{"left":"Checking monthly if the split still feels fair","right":"Catches resentment before it builds"},{"left":"Sharing the dull tasks too","right":"Stops one person carrying the grind"},{"left":"Modelling the split for your kids","right":"Teaches them fairness by example"}],"relearn":"Owning domains, thanking, re-checking, sharing the grind and modelling protect the bond.","persona":"any","source":"move to what it protects"},
  {"id":"uk-1023","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'I earn, so...' excuse to the truth that answers it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"My earning is my half of parenting\"","right":"Provision doesn't buy out of care"},{"left":"\"I'm too tired from work to do nights\"","right":"Her days are work too; share the rest"},{"left":"\"I'll pitch in once she earns again\"","right":"The load is heavy now, not later"},{"left":"\"Money matters more than nappies\"","right":"Both keep the child alive and loved"},{"left":"\"Providing is the manly part\"","right":"Caring is a parent's part, not a gender's"}],"relearn":"Earning is real but never your half; care and nights are shared now.","persona":"Vikram","source":"earning excuse to truth"},
  {"id":"uk-1024","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'waiting to be told' habit to the 'owning it' upgrade.","pairs":[{"left":"Asking 'what do you need?' each night","right":"Owning bedtime so it's never asked"},{"left":"Doing dishes once she points them out","right":"Clearing up after meals on your own"},{"left":"Standing by until you're delegated a step","right":"Picking up a whole domain yourself"},{"left":"Helping with the baby on request","right":"Taking set shifts she can count on"},{"left":"Waiting for the shopping list","right":"Noticing what's low and buying it"}],"relearn":"Each 'wait to be told' habit upgrades to owning a shift or domain.","persona":"Vikram","source":"wait to own upgrade"},
  {"id":"uk-1025","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of one baby job your partner always does that you've never even thought about. Got it?","prompt":"What came to mind?","options":["Got one","More than one","Hadn't noticed"],"affirm":"Naming the jobs you never think about is the first step to seeing the invisible load, and then taking a share of it.","relearn":"Spotting a job you never think about reveals the invisible load you can start to share.","persona":"Vikram","source":"name the unseen job"},
  {"id":"uk-1026","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'this is ours, not yours'. Say it about the baby work in your head. How does it land?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Lands true","Feels new","Worth saying"],"affirm":"A fairly shared load is practical and symbolic: it tells your partner the children are 'ours', not their burden alone.","relearn":"Holding the baby work as 'ours, not yours' is what makes sharing protect the couple, not just the schedule.","persona":"Vikram","source":"ours not yours"},
  {"id":"uk-1027","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: imagine your kid grown up, splitting a home. They'd copy what they saw you two do. Sit with that.","prompt":"What do you feel?","options":["Sitting with it","Want to model better","It matters"],"affirm":"Children learn equality, or its absence, by watching how their parents share. How you split things now is a quiet lesson.","relearn":"Your kids learn fairness from the split they watch, so how you share now is already teaching them.","persona":"any","source":"what they'll copy"},
  {"id":"uk-1028","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the fairer the load, the warmer you two tend to be. Worth putting near the top of the list?","prompt":"Your call?","options":["Worth it","Near the top","Fair keeps us warm"],"affirm":"A fairly shared load is one of the strongest protectors of how a couple feels after a baby. Prioritising it is prioritising the bond.","relearn":"The fairer the load, the warmer the couple; sharing it is one of the strongest protectors there is.","persona":"any","source":"fair keeps us warm"},
  {"id":"uk-1029","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: be honest, when you 'help', who's still the manager keeping it all in their head?","prompt":"What's the honest answer?","options":["Still her","Mostly her","Time to change that"],"affirm":"Helping with tasks while one person holds all the planning isn't sharing the load. Owning a domain end to end is.","relearn":"If you 'help' but she still manages it all, the load isn't shared; owning a domain is what shares it.","persona":"Vikram","source":"who's the manager"},
  {"id":"uk-1030","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: notice the difference between being thanked for a favour and being a true partner. Which do you want?","prompt":"What sits right?","options":["True partner","Not a favour","Both clearer now"],"affirm":"Being praised as a helpful 'good dad' is nice, but being a true equal parent is what your partner and child actually need.","relearn":"Aim to be an equal parent, not a helper thanked for favours; the bar is partnership, not praise.","persona":"Vikram","source":"partner not favour"},
  {"id":"uk-1031","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'fair' isn't a stopwatch. It's neither of you feeling buried. When did you last check in on that?","prompt":"What comes up?","options":["A while ago","Never really","Time to ask"],"affirm":"Fair isn't a precise tally; it's renegotiating until neither partner feels buried. Checking in keeps the split honest.","relearn":"Fairness is neither of you feeling buried, not an exact tally; a regular check-in keeps it honest.","persona":"any","source":"check the balance"},
  {"id":"uk-1032","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: think of effort your partner makes that you rarely thank out loud. Picture saying it today.","prompt":"What do you notice?","options":["Have one","Should say it","Often skip it"],"affirm":"Naming and thanking effort, on both sides, keeps goodwill flowing and beats keeping a silent scorecard of who did what.","relearn":"Thanking the effort you usually leave unsaid keeps goodwill alive and crowds out resentment.","persona":"Divya","source":"thank the effort"},
  {"id":"uk-1033","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that frame dad as a helper, not an equal parent.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'll own bath and bedtime tonight.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I'm babysitting my own kid this evening.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Both of us track the baby's jabs.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Just tell me what you need me to do.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"I noticed we're low on nappies and reordered.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Babysitting my own kid' and 'just tell me what to do' frame dad as a helper waiting on instructions; the rest own the parenting.","relearn":"'Babysitting' and 'tell me what to do' are helper lines; owning isn't.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot the helper lines"},
  {"id":"uk-1034","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that quietly dump the load on one parent.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Mapping every baby job and dividing it together","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Assuming she'll just keep doing what she always has","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Alternating who does the night wake-ups","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Saying 'you're better at this' and stepping back","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Checking monthly if the split still feels fair","trick":false}],"why":"Assuming she'll carry on and 'you're better at this' both offload the work onto one parent; the others share it deliberately.","relearn":"Assuming and 'you're better' dump the load; mapping and check-ins share it.","persona":"any","source":"spot the dump"},
  {"id":"uk-1035","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that shame instead of inviting a fairer share.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You're great at bedtime. Can we add mornings?\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Any real dad would've stepped up by now.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Here's the managing I hold. Take a piece.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Why must I spell out being a parent to you?\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Thanks for the nights. Let's split the days too.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Any real dad' and 'why must I spell it out' shame and make a partner tune out; the rest credit effort and ask clearly.","relearn":"'Any real dad' and 'why spell it out' shame; a clear, crediting ask invites sharing.","persona":"Divya","source":"spot the shaming"},
  {"id":"uk-1036","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two beliefs that let the earner opt out of real care.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Earning and sharing care can both be on me.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"I provide, so the baby work is fairly hers.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"My tiredness from work isn't more than hers.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'll start pitching in once she earns again.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Care isn't a gender's job; it's a parent's.\"","trick":false}],"why":"'I provide, so it's hers' and 'I'll pitch in once she earns' use earning to dodge care; the others keep care shared now.","relearn":"'It's hers' and 'once she earns' dodge care; earning never cancels your share.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot the opt-out"},
  {"id":"uk-1037","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two signs the mental load still sits on one person.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Both of you know when the next jab is due","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Only she ever notices the formula is low","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Either parent can plan a full day solo","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"He needs a handover list to manage an afternoon","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"You both hold the week's childcare plan","trick":false}],"why":"Only she noticing supplies, and him needing a list to cope, show the managing is still solo; the others show it's shared.","relearn":"Only one noticing supplies, or needing a handover list, means a solo mental load; both knowing means it's shared.","persona":"any","source":"spot the solo load"},
  {"id":"uk-1038","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two 'sharing' moves that are really just cherry-picking the nice bits.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Taking whole nights in turn, hard ones included","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Only ever doing the fun play and outings","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Owning the dull clean-up after meals too","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Stepping in just for cuddles, never the mess","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Handling the tiring early-morning shift","trick":false}],"why":"Only doing play, and only cuddles never mess, cherry-pick the nice parts; real sharing takes the dull and hard bits too.","relearn":"Only play or cuddles cherry-picks; real sharing takes the nights and mess too.","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot the cherry-pick"},
  {"id":"uk-1039","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two reactions that turn a fairness talk into a fight instead of a fix.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Mapping the jobs together into owned domains","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Pulling out a tally of who did what to win","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Thanking the effort before naming the gap","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Snapping 'fine, I do nothing then' and shutting down","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Agreeing a monthly check-in on the split","trick":false}],"why":"A point-scoring tally and a 'fine, I do nothing' shutdown make it a fight; mapping, thanking and check-ins keep it a fix.","relearn":"A tally or a shutdown makes it a fight; mapping and check-ins keep it a fix.","persona":"any","source":"spot the fight"},
  {"id":"uk-1040","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"not babysitting","hook":"Aisha is off to a friend's party tonight, leaving you at home with your own kids.","setup":"She's putting on her coat at the door.","steps":[{"prompt":"Aisha says: \"Thanks for babysitting tonight. I owe you one.\"","options":[{"text":"\"No problem at all. Happy to cover for you tonight.\"","then":"She smiles and leaves. The words 'cover for you' go with her."},{"text":"\"You do owe me one. This is a lot of work for one evening.\"","then":"She hesitates at the door. \"It's your work too, no?\""},{"text":"\"Anytime. Just tell me when you need me to step in.\"","then":"She nods. She's still the one who decides when."},{"text":"\"It's not babysitting when they're mine. I'm just parenting.\"","then":"She pauses, then laughs softly. \"Right. I keep saying that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming it parenting, not babysitting, takes apart the helper frame."},{"prompt":"Aisha texts: \"Did they eat? Are they asleep? Don't forget the medicine.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Can you remind me what time the medicine is?\"","then":"She calls and explains it all. The party pauses for her too."},{"text":"\"Stop checking on me. I'm not an idiot.\"","then":"She replies with one word, sorry. Her evening dims."},{"text":"\"Everything's fine. Don't worry. Just relax.\"","then":"She reads it, half reassured. She keeps checking her phone."},{"text":"\"Fed, bathed, medicine done. Enjoy your evening.\"","then":"She sends a heart. Her next text is a photo of a smiling table.","best":true}],"why":"Knowing the details yourself means she can actually switch off."},{"prompt":"Back home, Aisha says: \"Honestly, I still feel I should have stayed.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then don't go out next time if it stresses you.\"","then":"She looks stung. \"That's not what I meant.\""},{"text":"\"Don't worry. Next time will be easier for you.\"","then":"She smiles tiredly. She's still the one worrying."},{"text":"\"See? I did the whole evening without a single problem.\"","then":"She laughs politely. \"Yes. Thank you.\" The evening loses its shine."},{"text":"\"You needed it. Next time I'll plan it before you ask.\"","then":"Her shoulders drop. \"That would mean a lot.\"","best":true}],"why":"Planning her breaks ahead shows it's your job too, not a favour."}],"relearn":"Naming your own caregiving as parenting, not a favour, dismantles the 'helper' frame at the root."},
  {"id":"uk-1041","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Meena","source":"own a domain","hook":"Your partner Sohail manages everything and won't hand any of it over, and you want to truly take a domain.","setup":"Tonight he's already laying out the baby's pyjamas.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sohail says: \"You don't need to do the evening routine. I have a system.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Tell me if you ever need a hand with anything.\"","then":"He says he will. Tonight he does it all again."},{"text":"\"Okay, if you don't mind doing it.\"","then":"He shrugs, hiding relief. The system stays his."},{"text":"\"Your system is too fussy. Let me do it my way.\"","then":"He looks hurt. \"Fussy? It works.\""},{"text":"\"Let me own bath and bedtime fully, so you can stop.\"","then":"He blinks. \"Fully? As in, I don't check?\"","best":true}],"why":"Offering to own a whole domain lifts the mental load, not just the task."},{"prompt":"Sohail says: \"You're tired from work. It's easier if I just do it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe just for this week, and then we'll see how it goes.\"","then":"He nods. The week ends, and 'we'll see' never comes."},{"text":"\"Then I'd rather do a few small pieces of it instead.\"","then":"He hands you the towel. He still runs the rest."},{"text":"\"Fine. But you'll regret not sharing it.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"That sounded like a threat.\""},{"text":"\"You're tired too. I'll do it tonight, and you rest.\"","then":"He looks at you. \"You're serious.\" \"Completely,\" you say.","best":true}],"why":"Meeting his worry with 'you're tired too' turns the job into a shared one."},{"prompt":"Sohail says quietly: \"If I stopped managing it, I wouldn't know what to do with myself.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then just keep an eye on me while I do it all.\"","then":"He hovers in the doorway. Twice he corrects you."},{"text":"\"Just relax. It'll all be fine, so don't worry so much.\"","then":"He tries. He keeps checking the monitor."},{"text":"\"You'll figure it out. That's really not my problem.\"","then":"He looks stung. \"Right. Of course.\""},{"text":"\"Rest, or go for a walk. I'll text if anything's wrong.\"","then":"He exhales. \"Okay. I'll go for a walk.\"","best":true}],"why":"Giving him a real way to step back makes handing over easier."}],"relearn":"Offering to own a whole domain end to end lifts the mental load, not just the task, off your partner."},
  {"id":"uk-1042","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"reset the praise","hook":"At a family visit, relatives keep treating your care for your daughter as a favour to Sana.","setup":"Your uncle beams at you across the tea table.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle says: \"You help so much with the baby. What a wonderful husband!\"","options":[{"text":"\"Ha, I try to pitch in when I can.\"","then":"He nods approvingly. 'Pitch in' stays on the table."},{"text":"\"It's easy. Sana does the hard part anyway.\"","then":"He chuckles. Sana looks up, unsure whether to feel touched."},{"text":"\"Please don't call it helping. That's rude to her.\"","then":"The table goes quiet. He mumbles that he meant well."},{"text":"\"Thank you, Uncle. She's my child too. I'm parenting.\"","then":"He pauses, then laughs. \"Times have changed, haven't they?\"","best":true}],"why":"A kind, plain correction keeps the standard equal without a scene."},{"prompt":"Your uncle adds: \"Of course, the mother is the real parent.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sure, but I'm still learning how to do it all.\"","then":"He nods. The idea that mothers are the default stays."},{"text":"\"That was your time, Uncle. Things are different now.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"My time raised you fine.\""},{"text":"\"Let's not argue about it, Uncle. Have some more tea.\"","then":"He shrugs and sips. He'll say it again next visit."},{"text":"\"We're both real parents, Uncle. We each do our share.\"","then":"He tilts his head. \"Hm. I suppose that's fair.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying you're both real parents corrects the myth without insulting him."},{"prompt":"Your uncle says quietly: \"Your father never changed a diaper, you know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, he had his reasons back then, of course.\"","then":"He nods. The subject closes."},{"text":"\"That's not really something to be proud of, Uncle.\"","then":"He looks hurt. \"He was a good man.\""},{"text":"\"Then I suppose I must be more special than him.\"","then":"He laughs, pleased. The praise circles back to you."},{"text":"\"I know. I want it different for my daughter.\"","then":"He is quiet for a moment. \"She's lucky, then.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning your choice warmly models a new normal without dismissing his generation."}],"relearn":"Gently correcting 'helping' to 'parenting' keeps the standard equal and stops the favour frame sticking."},
  {"id":"uk-1043","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"open without blame","hook":"You want to start the fairness talk with Zain without it turning into blame.","setup":"You've picked a quiet Sunday afternoon.","steps":[{"prompt":"Zain looks up and says: \"You wanted to talk about something?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. Let's count up who actually does more around here.\"","then":"His face closes. \"So this is a trial.\""},{"text":"\"I'm exhausted, and I think you don't see why.\"","then":"He looks hurt. \"Of course I see it.\""},{"text":"\"It's just that things feel a bit uneven lately.\"","then":"He shrugs. \"They feel fine to me.\""},{"text":"\"I don't want to keep score. I want us to map every job.\"","then":"He sets his phone down. \"Okay. That I can do.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying you want a map, not a scoreboard, keeps you on the same team."},{"prompt":"Zain says: \"Okay. But I think I do a lot already.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You do some. Honestly, I do most of it though.\"","then":"He bristles. \"Most? Show me.\""},{"text":"\"Fine. We can compare our lists and see who's right.\"","then":"He nods, but now you're two opponents with paper."},{"text":"\"Let's drop it. I'm sorry I even brought it up.\"","then":"He relaxes. The full list of jobs stays in your head."},{"text":"\"I know you do. Let's write it all down together.\"","then":"He finds a pen. \"Start with the night feeds.\"","best":true}],"why":"Crediting what he does, then listing everything, makes it about the work, not the person."},{"prompt":"A week later, Zain says: \"I've been thinking. Where do we start?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You tell me what you want to take.\"","then":"He picks the easy ones. You keep the rest."},{"text":"\"Just do more, and I'll say if it's not enough.\"","then":"He tries, but doesn't know what more means."},{"text":"\"Start with the night feeds. Those are the worst.\"","then":"He looks dismayed. \"That's all I get?\""},{"text":"\"Let's each own whole jobs, then review in two weeks.\"","then":"He nods. \"Deal. I'll take laundry and pharmacy.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owned jobs and a check-in turn good intent into a fair split."}],"relearn":"Framing it as mapping-and-sharing, not scorekeeping, keeps you teammates instead of rivals."},
  {"id":"uk-1044","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"share the nights","hook":"You're back at work, and your partner Reema has quietly taken every night feed since.","setup":"It's bedtime, and she's already warming the milk.","steps":[{"prompt":"Reema says: \"You have work tomorrow. I'll do the nights.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, if you're sure you don't mind doing them.\"","then":"She smiles thinly. \"I don't mind,\" she says, and you both know it's not quite true."},{"text":"\"You'll manage. I really need to sleep tonight.\"","then":"She nods. Her eyes look heavy."},{"text":"\"I'll take the weekend nights and you keep the rest.\"","then":"She agrees. Five nights a week are still hers."},{"text":"\"Your days are work too. Let's alternate wake-ups.\"","then":"She hesitates, then smiles. \"Really? Every other night?\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming her days as work too makes alternating nights the fair start."},{"prompt":"Reema says: \"Your work pays the bills. I don't want you tired in meetings.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fair point. Let's keep it as it was for now.\"","then":"She sighs. The nights stay hers."},{"text":"\"I'll take nights once this work deadline eases.\"","then":"She nods. The deadline never quite eases."},{"text":"\"Don't argue. I've already decided how this goes.\"","then":"She goes quiet, hurt. \"I thought we were deciding this together.\""},{"text":"\"I'd rather be tired and fair. Let's alternate.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Okay. Let's try for a week.\"","best":true}],"why":"Choosing fair over rested shows her load matters as much as your work."},{"prompt":"A week later, Reema admits: \"Part of me thinks the nights are my job alone.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You don't have to feel that. I'll try to do more.\"","then":"She nods. 'Try' leaves it uncertain."},{"text":"\"Then keep them, if that feels better to you.\"","then":"She looks relieved and guilty. The nights stay hers."},{"text":"\"It's okay. I'll do some when I can.\"","then":"She counts the nights you can't. Most nights she's up."},{"text":"\"They're our nights, not your job. Tonight's mine.\"","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"Then I'm sleeping.\"","best":true}],"why":"Calling the nights shared, and taking one, removes the asking and the guilt."},{"prompt":"A month on, Reema asks: \"What happens to the nights when things get harder?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll see how it goes.\"","then":"She nods, unsure. 'We'll see' sounds like an exit."},{"text":"\"If it gets too hard, the nights fall back to you.\"","then":"She sighs. \"So they were always mine to fall back on.\""},{"text":"\"I'll be fine. Let's not overthink it.\"","then":"She looks at you carefully. There's no room in that plan to say you're not."},{"text":"\"We share them, and rework the plan together.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Together. That's all I wanted.\"","best":true}],"why":"Sharing the nights and reworking the plan together keeps them fair when it gets harder."}],"relearn":"Naming her days as work too and alternating nights keeps the load shared on unequal schedules."},
  {"id":"uk-1045","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"credit then ask","hook":"You want to thank Amit for his effort and still ask for a fairer share, without shaming him.","setup":"He's just finished the night feed, hair messy.","steps":[{"prompt":"Amit says: \"I know I'm not perfect at this, but I'm trying.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You could honestly do a bit more to make this fair.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"I'm doing what I can.\""},{"text":"\"I know you're trying. I just wish it showed more.\"","then":"He looks crushed. \"Show more how?\""},{"text":"\"You're doing great. Let's just leave it there for now.\"","then":"He smiles, relieved. The split stays lopsided."},{"text":"\"Thank you for the nights. Could you own bath time too?\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Bath time. Okay, I can try that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Crediting the effort before a specific ask makes it easy for him to say yes."},{"prompt":"Amit says: \"I want to do more. I'm just scared I'll get things wrong.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then maybe I'll just keep doing the rest myself.\"","then":"He looks relieved and ashamed. Nothing changes."},{"text":"\"Just don't ask me unless it's really serious.\"","then":"He nods. He's alone with it, and you didn't share the how."},{"text":"\"You always say that. You'll be fine. Trust me.\"","then":"He winces. \"Do I? I didn't realise.\""},{"text":"\"You'll learn by doing. I'll show you once first.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Okay. Show me tonight.\"","best":true}],"why":"Showing him once, then handing it over, builds his skill and trust."},{"prompt":"Amit asks: \"How do I know if I'm doing enough?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You'll know. It'll feel obvious when it's fair.\"","then":"He nods, unsure. It doesn't feel obvious."},{"text":"\"I'll tell you if you're falling short of it.\"","then":"He tenses. Every night becomes a test."},{"text":"\"Just do as much as you can manage each day.\"","then":"He tries. By Thursday he asks, \"Was that enough?\" and you can't say."},{"text":"\"Let's check in on Sundays and share what's hard.\"","then":"He grins. \"Sundays. I'll bring chai.\"","best":true}],"why":"A weekly check-in gives you both a way to keep it fair without blame."}],"relearn":"Crediting the effort before a specific ask lands far better than blame, and moves the split."},
  {"id":"uk-1046","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"learn by owning","hook":"Since the birth, Ritu has done nearly all the baby's care, and you want to start learning.","setup":"The baby is fussing, and Ritu reaches for her, as usual.","steps":[{"prompt":"Ritu says: \"Here, I'll take her. You always say I'm better at this.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're just so much better at this than I'll ever be.\"","then":"She sighs and takes the baby again. The pattern holds."},{"text":"\"Okay, thanks. I'll get on with the dishes.\"","then":"She nods. She's back on baby duty, and you're on dishes."},{"text":"\"Don't be like that. I do plenty around here.\"","then":"Her eyes narrow. \"Name three things.\""},{"text":"\"I'll only improve by doing it. Give me bath time.\"","then":"She holds the baby a little tighter. \"Bath time? Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning a job to learn it beats saying she's better, which just hands it back."},{"prompt":"Ritu says: \"Honestly, I'm scared to let anyone else take care of her.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're being unfair. I'm not clumsy.\"","then":"She tightens her arms around the baby. \"I didn't say clumsy.\" Days later, she lets you try bath time alone."},{"text":"\"Fair enough. You keep doing it, then.\"","then":"She sighs. \"That's how it's been, hasn't it?\" Days later, she lets you try bath time anyway."},{"text":"\"Fine. I'll do it when you're not watching.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"Then who checks the water?\""},{"text":"\"Show me once. Then bath time is mine.\"","then":"She softens. \"Okay. Warm water, elbow test. Come here.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking to be shown once, then doing it alone, is how skill starts."},{"prompt":"A few days later, you do bath time on your own. Ritu says: \"It went okay. But you forgot the towel.\"","options":[{"text":"\"See? I told you I'm not as good at this.\"","then":"She looks tired. \"That's not the point.\""},{"text":"\"Sorry. Maybe it's better if you handle it.\"","then":"She shakes her head. \"One towel isn't a reason to stop.\""},{"text":"\"You could have handed it to me.\"","then":"Her face closes. \"I shouldn't have to.\""},{"text":"\"Noted. I'll set it out first. Bedtime too?\"","then":"She smiles, surprised. \"Yes. Bedtime too.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning the slip and asking for more shows you're learning, not opting out."},{"prompt":"A month on, Ritu asks: \"Which part of her care do you want to learn next?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe just the parts you don't like.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow. \"The worst bits. Right.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever you think I can handle.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I don't want to decide that for you.\""},{"text":"\"I think one job is enough for now.\"","then":"She nods, disappointed. The old pattern quietly returns."},{"text":"\"The morning routine, start to finish, from Monday.\"","then":"She laughs. \"Monday. I'll hold you to it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Taking another whole job, on a date, keeps you moving from helper to co-parent."}],"relearn":"Owning a domain to learn it beats 'you're better at it', which only cedes the load for good."},
  {"id":"uk-1047","cat":"share-dont-resent","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"model for the kids","hook":"You want your kids to grow up seeing both parents share, and you tell Diya why you're stepping into more care.","setup":"Diya is packing the kids' bags for tomorrow, tired.","steps":[{"prompt":"Diya asks: \"Why the sudden interest in doing more?\"","options":[{"text":"\"They're too little to notice who does what anyway.\"","then":"She stares. \"They notice everything.\""},{"text":"\"My friends said I should do more. So I'm trying.\"","then":"She half smiles. \"So it's about your friends.\""},{"text":"\"I just felt guilty, that's all.\"","then":"She nods. Guilt doesn't keep a routine going."},{"text":"\"I want them to see us both cook, feed and plan.\"","then":"Her face softens. \"I'd like that too.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying what you want your kids to see gives the change a reason that lasts."},{"prompt":"Diya says: \"Which chunks? I don't want another few weeks of enthusiasm.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Whatever you want to give me. I'll follow along.\"","then":"She sighs. \"That's what I have now.\""},{"text":"\"I'll try a few things and see what sticks.\"","then":"She nods, unconvinced. \"And who keeps track?\""},{"text":"\"Give me a week to think about it.\"","then":"She nods. A week passes without a plan."},{"text":"\"Breakfasts and bedtime stories, daily, from tomorrow.\"","then":"She raises an eyebrow, smiling. \"Every day. I'll hold you to it.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming real chunks and a start date shows this isn't a passing mood."},{"prompt":"Your son asks in front of you both: \"Why is Papa making the rotis?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Because Mama needs a break today.\"","then":"He nods. He learns cooking is Mama's job, and Papa's is a favour."},{"text":"\"Because I felt like it. Don't ask questions.\"","then":"He shrugs. The chance to explain is lost."},{"text":"\"Ask Mama. It was her idea.\"","then":"Diya raises her eyebrows. The reason is now hers."},{"text":"\"Because Papa cooks too. We both look after you.\"","then":"He grins. \"Can I roll one?\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying plainly that you both look after them teaches equality by example."}],"relearn":"Visibly taking real care now models equality for your kids, who learn the split by watching."},
  {"id":"uk-1080","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If we loved each other enough, we wouldn't be snapping like this.\"","myth":{"un":"If we loved each other enough, we wouldn't be snapping like this.","re":"Snapping in this phase is usually sleep debt talking, not a love problem; name the tiredness and it loosens its grip.","why":"The fuse is short because you're depleted, not because the love shrank."},"relearn":"Snapping after a baby is usually exhaustion, not a love problem; name the tiredness instead of doubting each other.","persona":"Divya","source":"sleep debt not love"},
  {"id":"uk-1081","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A real apology means it never happens again, so saying sorry is pointless.\"","myth":{"un":"A real apology means it never happens again, so saying sorry is pointless.","re":"In this phase you'll both snap again; a quick, honest repair each time is what keeps a snap from hardening into distance.","why":"Repairing fast matters more than never slipping."},"relearn":"You'll both snap again while this tired; quick repair each time matters more than a perfect record.","persona":"Vikram","source":"repair beats perfect"},
  {"id":"uk-1082","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"He came home from work, so it's fair he gets to switch off while I keep going.\"","myth":{"un":"Whoever earned outside all day has earned the right to switch off at home.","re":"Both of you are running on empty; trading 'who's more tired' rarely helps. Naming it together beats keeping a tiredness scoreboard.","why":"Comparing exhaustion fuels resentment, not rest."},"relearn":"Both partners are depleted; comparing whose tiredness counts more fuels resentment, naming it together eases it.","persona":"any","source":"no tiredness scoreboard"},
  {"id":"uk-1083","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"His sharp tone at dinner means he's losing respect for me.\"","myth":{"un":"A tired partner's sharp tone means they're losing respect for you.","re":"Assume good intent: a frazzled partner's edge is usually exhaustion, not contempt. Ask before you conclude.","why":"Reading tiredness as contempt starts fights that aren't really there."},"relearn":"A tired partner's sharp tone is usually exhaustion, not contempt; assume good intent and ask before concluding.","persona":"Divya","source":"assume good intent"},
  {"id":"uk-1084","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Whoever brings up the problem first wins, so I should get my version out fast.\"","myth":{"un":"In a tired-couple argument, whoever states their case first wins.","re":"The goal isn't winning; it's repair. Making your partner feel heard ends the spiral faster than landing your point.","why":"A 'win' that leaves your partner unheard costs you both."},"relearn":"The aim under exhaustion is repair, not winning; making your partner feel heard ends the spiral.","persona":"any","source":"repair not winning"},
  {"id":"uk-1085","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"We're too tired to talk properly, so we may as well not talk at all.\"","myth":{"un":"When you're both this tired, there's no point talking at all.","re":"You don't need long talks; a few honest minutes, or a 'let's park this till morning', keeps the line open.","why":"Going fully silent lets distance grow in the gap."},"relearn":"You don't need long talks when tired; a few honest minutes or a gentle 'let's park this' keeps the line open.","persona":"Vikram","source":"short over silent"},
  {"id":"uk-1086","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Bringing up that he forgot the doctor's appointment will just start a fight, so I'll bottle it.\"","myth":{"un":"Raising a hurt will only start a fight, so it's safer to bottle it.","re":"Bottled hurts pile into resentment; raised gently when you're both calmer, with a soft start, they clear instead.","why":"A gentle airing beats a silent stockpile."},"relearn":"Bottled hurts become resentment; raise them gently when calm, with a soft start, and they clear.","persona":"Divya","source":"air don't stockpile"},
  {"id":"uk-1087","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Thanking each other for normal baby chores is fake and a bit much.\"","myth":{"un":"Thanking each other for ordinary baby chores is fake and over the top.","re":"Specific, small thanks, 'you got up so I could sleep', isn't fake; feeling noticed is what carries a couple through the hard weeks.","why":"Being seen is fuel, not flattery."},"relearn":"Small specific thanks isn't fake; feeling noticed for the daily baby work carries a couple through hard weeks.","persona":"Vikram","source":"specific thanks"},
  {"id":"uk-1088","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I admit I'm barely coping, he'll think I'm weak or a bad mother.\"","myth":{"un":"Admitting you're barely coping makes you look weak or like a bad parent.","re":"Saying 'I'm running on empty, I need a hand' is honest, not weak; it lets your partner actually help instead of guessing.","why":"Naming the limit invites support; hiding it invites a blow-up."},"relearn":"Saying 'I'm running on empty, I need a hand' is honest, not weak; it lets your partner help instead of guess.","persona":"Divya","source":"naming the limit"},
  {"id":"uk-1089","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"3am is when the feelings come up, so 3am is when we should sort them out.\"","myth":{"un":"If the big feelings surface at 3am, that's when you should sort them out.","re":"Timing is a skill, not avoidance: 'this matters, can we talk rested in the morning?' lands far better than a depleted 3am showdown.","why":"Hard talks at your most depleted go worst."},"relearn":"Pausing a hard talk for a rested moment is timing, not avoidance; a 3am showdown goes worst.","persona":"any","source":"timing is a skill"},
  {"id":"uk-1090","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once we say something cruel in a tired fight, it's permanent damage.\"","myth":{"un":"A cruel thing said in a tired fight is permanent damage you can't undo.","re":"A fast, sincere 'I'm sorry, that was the exhaustion, not what I believe' repairs most of it; couples mend, they don't have to be flawless.","why":"Repair, not flawlessness, holds a couple together."},"relearn":"A fast sincere repair, naming the exhaustion, mends most of a harsh word; couples mend, they aren't flawless.","persona":"Vikram","source":"repairable"},
  {"id":"uk-1091","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"defer the 2am tally","hook":"At 2am, the baby has finally settled, and Poonam mutters that you never get up first.","steps":[{"prompt":"She turns over with her arms folded. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Fire back with every night you did get up","then":"She sits up. \"You're counting?\" Both of you are wide awake now."},{"text":"Roll over and ignore her","then":"You turn away and say nothing. She lies awake, still angry."},{"text":"Apologise and promise to do every wake-up from now on","then":"She's too tired to argue. You can't keep that promise for long."},{"text":"Say it can wait, and you'll wake first tomorrow","then":"She exhales. \"Okay. Tomorrow.\"","best":true}],"why":"Deferring a 2am tally to a rested moment protects your sleep and the talk."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Poonam looks worn out and says nothing about last night. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Act as though nothing happened","then":"You chat. She answers in short sentences."},{"text":"Bring it up straight away over breakfast","then":"She's exhausted. The talk begins badly."},{"text":"Say you'll wake first from tomorrow and leave it there","then":"She nods. It's a start, but she still wants to be heard."},{"text":"Bring her tea and ask when she'd like to talk","then":"She takes it. \"After lunch, when the baby naps.\"","best":true}],"why":"Setting a time for the talk respects her tiredness and shows you'll follow through."},{"prompt":"Later that day, Poonam says she feels she carries the nights alone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"List the nights you did get up","then":"She sighs. \"That's not what I said.\""},{"text":"Say your job makes nights harder for you","then":"She stares. \"And mine?\""},{"text":"Agree with everything and offer to take all the nights","then":"It sounds generous. Within days you're spent, and the promise wobbles."},{"text":"Ask what it's like for her, listen, then agree a rota","then":"She talks for ten minutes. You write down the rota together.","best":true}],"why":"Listening first, then agreeing a rota, makes it sustainable."},{"prompt":"Some nights later, it's 3am and Poonam is up with the baby again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Point out that you already did your share of nights","then":"She says nothing. The nights start to feel like a contract."},{"text":"Stay in bed and hope she manages on her own","then":"She manages. She also notices."},{"text":"Blame the baby's schedule for all of it","then":"She half laughs. It's not the baby she's upset with."},{"text":"Take the baby, and say you'll go over nights after breakfast","then":"She hands over the baby. \"Thanks. Let's go over it after breakfast.\"","best":true}],"why":"Stepping in now, then going over nights in daylight, keeps it fair, not a fight."}],"debrief":"At 2am, defer the talk to a rested moment instead of trading tallies in the dark.","relearn":"Defer a 2am grievance to a rested moment; trading tallies in the dark only deepens the hurt."},
  {"id":"uk-1092","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"private not public","hook":"Yash snapped at you in front of the in-laws over the baby's feed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law goes quiet, and Yash looks embarrassed. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap back right there in front of everyone","then":"Voices rise. The in-laws stare, and the baby starts to cry."},{"text":"Go quiet and decide not to bring it up","then":"You get through lunch. Yash has no idea how you feel."},{"text":"Turn to your mother-in-law and complain about him","then":"She listens. Now the family has picked sides."},{"text":"Stay calm, finish the feed, and talk to him later","then":"You feed the baby. Yash won't meet your eyes for the rest of lunch.","best":true}],"why":"Calm now, a private talk later, avoids a public fight and lets him hear you."},{"prompt":"Later that night, Yash tries to joke about it. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Laugh along with him, so things stay light tonight","then":"He relaxes. The sting stays."},{"text":"Say he owes you an apology in front of everyone too","then":"He bristles. \"In front of everyone? No.\""},{"text":"Say his mother always makes everything worse anyway","then":"He goes defensive. The talk becomes about his mother."},{"text":"Say it stung, and ask him not to snap in front of family","then":"He stops smiling. \"I know. I was stressed. I'm sorry.\"","best":true}],"why":"'That stung' is honest without blaming, so he can apologise."},{"prompt":"Yash says his mother criticises how he handles the baby too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's his problem to fix with his own mother","then":"He nods stiffly. You've left him alone with it."},{"text":"Say she's only trying to help you both, really","then":"He sighs. \"Even you.\""},{"text":"Say you'd rather not talk about her tonight at all","then":"He nods. The pressure stays in the house."},{"text":"Listen, then agree to back each other up","then":"He smiles for the first time today. \"Deal.\"","best":true}],"why":"Agreeing to back each other up turns family strain into teamwork."},{"prompt":"A week later, the in-laws comment on the feeding routine again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Explain and defend every choice in detail","then":"The explaining takes twenty minutes. No one changes their view."},{"text":"Leave the reply to Yash since it's his family","then":"Yash looks caught out. The family looks at him."},{"text":"Nod along and quietly do it your own way anyway","then":"It works, but you feel unseen. Yash doesn't know why."},{"text":"Catch Yash's eye, and say you two have it covered","then":"His shoulders lower. Your mother-in-law nods and passes the roti.","best":true}],"why":"Speaking as a team in front of family takes the pressure off you both."}],"debrief":"Raise a public snap privately and calmly later, not with a counter-snap.","relearn":"Raise a public snap privately and calmly later; a counter-snap in front of family settles nothing."},
  {"id":"uk-1093","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"name not slam","hook":"Three times tonight you start a sentence to Kabir, and the baby cries before you finish.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your frustration is building. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"I keep getting cut off. Ten quiet minutes later?\"","then":"Kabir nods, a little relieved. \"Yes. I want to hear it too.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"I keep getting cut off. We'll talk tomorrow.\"","then":"Kabir agrees, but tomorrow comes and goes with no time set."},{"text":"Go quiet and slam the cupboards","then":"Kabir braces at every bang and can't tell what he did."},{"text":"Say \"You never let me finish anything.\"","then":"Kabir snaps that the baby isn't his fault, and the evening turns sour."}],"why":"Naming the frustration and asking for a quiet slot aims it at the situation, not at Kabir."},{"prompt":"A few nights later Kabir starts a sentence and the baby cries again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Sigh loudly and roll your eyes","then":"Kabir stops mid-word and doesn't try again tonight."},{"text":"Say \"It's fine. Forget it.\"","then":"Kabir shrugs, but his sentence stays unsaid and you never hear it."},{"text":"Say \"Hold that thought. I want to hear it properly later.\"","then":"Kabir smiles tiredly and types one word into his phone to remember.","best":true},{"text":"Talk over the crying anyway","then":"Neither of you hears the other, and the baby cries harder."}],"why":"Promising to come back to it keeps the person feeling heard while the baby needs you."},{"prompt":"Kabir says \"We never get to finish a conversation.\" What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree, then fix a time tonight with phones down","then":"Kabir relaxes. \"Ten minutes once the baby sleeps. Deal.\"","best":true},{"text":"Suggest texting each other everything instead","then":"Texts pile up, but you still haven't talked face to face."},{"text":"Say the baby is the problem and nothing can change","then":"Kabir goes quiet, and the feeling that it's hopeless settles in."},{"text":"Say he could talk more during the day","then":"Kabir bristles. He has been at work, and the point is lost."}],"why":"Agreeing and setting a real time turns a complaint into a plan you both keep."}],"debrief":"Name the frustration at the situation and ask for a quiet window, not cold slamming.","relearn":"Name the frustration at the situation and ask for a quiet window, not cold slamming."},
  {"id":"uk-1094","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"own your snap","hook":"You snapped \"just deal with it\" when Neha asked for help with the night feed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Neha went quiet and turned away. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Sorry, that was harsh. Can we split nights?\"","then":"Neha turns back and her shoulders drop. \"Yes. That would help.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry, then add that she was nagging you","then":"Neha hears the sorry, then the excuse. The quiet stays."},{"text":"Tell yourself she will get over it by morning","then":"By morning Neha is polite and distant, and neither of you mentions it."},{"text":"Do the feed in silence and say nothing at all","then":"Neha sees the effort, but the snap still hangs between you."}],"why":"A quick sorry plus a real fix turns the snap into teamwork."},{"prompt":"The next night the feed rota comes up again. What do you suggest?","options":[{"text":"Take a feed only when Neha asks you to","then":"Neha ends up asking every time, and starts to feel like your manager."},{"text":"Say you'll see how you feel each night","then":"By 2am you're both guessing, and Neha wakes anyway."},{"text":"A fixed split, each owning set hours","then":"Neha smiles. \"Then I can actually sleep.\" You write it on the fridge.","best":true},{"text":"Let Neha work out the whole schedule alone","then":"Neha builds it alone and is tired before it even starts."}],"why":"A fixed split means neither of you has to ask or guess."},{"prompt":"A week later you snap again over something small. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry within the hour and name the tiredness","then":"Neha nods. \"Me too. I was short with you yesterday.\"","best":true},{"text":"Wait for Neha to raise it before you do","then":"Neha doesn't. The small hurt sits there another day."},{"text":"Explain to Neha why you were right to be annoyed","then":"Neha listens, then says she doesn't feel any better."},{"text":"Make a joke of it and move straight on","then":"Neha gives a thin smile, and the hurt goes unsaid."}],"why":"Repairing fast, and owning your own snap, stops small hurts hardening."}],"debrief":"Circle back fast on your own snap and offer a fix, not hope it's forgotten.","relearn":"Circle back fast on your own snap and offer a fix; an un-repaired snap quietly hardens."},
  {"id":"uk-1095","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"schedule the fix","hook":"The same chore fight has looped all week, and tonight it flares again over the laundry.","steps":[{"prompt":"Voices are rising and you are both fried. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Fine, we'll talk about it sometime soon.\"","then":"Meera agrees, but no day is named and the fight stays open."},{"text":"Push to settle it now, however late it gets","then":"You go until midnight. Nothing sticks, and the fight is ready by morning."},{"text":"Walk out to the balcony and stay out there","then":"The door clicks. Meera is left with the laundry and the argument."},{"text":"Say \"We're circling this tired. Ten calm minutes Sunday?\"","then":"Meera pauses, then nods. \"Sunday. I'd rather do it rested.\"","best":true}],"why":"Booking a rested time for a looping fight beats forcing a fix while depleted."},{"prompt":"A few days on, you both have a rare quiet half hour. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Open with a list of everything Meera has missed","then":"Meera answers with her own list. The old loop is back."},{"text":"Start with who does what now, not who to blame","then":"Meera counts on her fingers and finds two jobs you never knew she held.","best":true},{"text":"Say the fight is behind you and skip the plan","then":"It feels lighter for a day, and then the laundry sits there again."},{"text":"Ask Meera what she wants and agree to all of it","then":"Meera is surprised, then wary. You haven't said what you can carry."}],"why":"Laying out who does what now gives you facts to fix, instead of blame to trade."},{"prompt":"A week later the chore fight starts again over dishes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give in on the dishes just to end it quickly","then":"The dishes are done, and Meera senses the peace was bought."},{"text":"Point out that you warned this would happen","then":"Meera says that doesn't help. The volume rises."},{"text":"Do the dishes alone and stay silent about it","then":"Meera watches, unsure what the silence means."},{"text":"Say \"Our loop again. Let's stop and pick a time.\"","then":"Meera exhales. \"Yes. Thursday, after she sleeps?\"","best":true}],"why":"Spotting the loop and pausing it stops the same fight from running again."}],"debrief":"Give a looping tired fight a scheduled calm slot, not a depleted resolution.","relearn":"Give a looping tired fight a scheduled calm slot; forcing it late just reopens the loop."},
  {"id":"uk-1096","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"ask what helps","hook":"After a brutal night, Arjun sits at the table looking flattened, and last night's argument is still fresh.","steps":[{"prompt":"You are tired too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take the baby for an hour without asking him first","then":"Arjun is glad of the break, but wonders if you wanted to talk."},{"text":"Bring up last night's argument straight away, while it's fresh","then":"Arjun's face closes. He hears one more demand."},{"text":"Ask \"Want to talk, or should I take the baby for an hour?\"","then":"Arjun lets out a breath. \"Baby first. Then maybe talk.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"At least you got some sleep last night.\"","then":"Arjun stiffens. He wasn't counting, and now feels he has to."}],"why":"Offering a choice lets a worn-out partner pick what helps right now."},{"prompt":"Later, Arjun says he feels like he is failing at all of it. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Tell him what you've seen him do, and that it counts","then":"Arjun looks up. \"You noticed the 4am feeds?\" His eyes are wet.","best":true},{"text":"Say everyone feels like that, so don't dwell on it","then":"Arjun nods, but feels a little more alone."},{"text":"List a few ways he could do better tomorrow","then":"Arjun goes quiet. He asked to be heard, and got a to-do list."},{"text":"Say you feel like you're failing too, just as much","then":"Arjun listens, but now he is holding your worry as well."}],"why":"Naming what you have seen him do makes him feel seen instead of judged or fixed."},{"prompt":"A few nights later Arjun looks flattened again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Assume he wants space and keep well away","then":"Arjun sits alone, wondering if you have noticed."},{"text":"Keep asking him to talk after he says not now","then":"Arjun snaps. \"I said later.\" The evening turns tense."},{"text":"Ask whether he wants to talk or rest","then":"Arjun thinks, then says \"Talk.\" You both sit down with chai.","best":true},{"text":"Take over everything so he never has to ask","then":"Arjun rests, but feels sidelined from his own baby."}],"why":"Asking, and letting him choose, keeps support from turning into guessing."}],"debrief":"Offer a choice between talking and practical relief, not a forced problem talk.","relearn":"Offer a flattened partner a choice between talking and practical relief, not an immediate problem talk."},
  {"id":"uk-1097","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"clear the fake truce","hook":"You apologised for snapping, and Tara says \"it's fine\" in a flat voice.","steps":[{"prompt":"It clearly isn't fine. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"It doesn't sound fine. That's okay. Let's clear it.\"","then":"Tara's face softens. \"I was hurt. I didn't want to say.\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask \"Are you sure?\" once, then drop it once she nods","then":"Tara says yes again, and the hurt stays exactly where it was."},{"text":"Take \"it's fine\" at face value, relieved it's all over","then":"Tara turns to the baby monitor, and the hurt goes unsaid."},{"text":"Say \"Good, because I already said sorry.\"","then":"Tara's jaw tightens. The apology now sounds like a demand."}],"why":"Gently reopening a hollow \"it's fine\" makes room for the real feeling."},{"prompt":"A day later Tara says the snap stung more than she let on. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Explain how tired you were that night, in detail","then":"Tara says she knows, but still doesn't feel heard."},{"text":"Say she should have told you at the time","then":"Tara goes quiet. Telling you now suddenly feels risky."},{"text":"\"I hear you. That hurt. I'll pause before I snap.\"","then":"Tara nods slowly. \"Thank you. That's all I wanted.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say sorry again and again, until she says stop","then":"Tara ends up comforting you, and her own hurt slips away."}],"why":"Hearing the hurt and naming one change repairs more than explaining or over-apologising."},{"prompt":"A week on, after a small argument, Tara says \"it's fine\" flatly again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Take your time. I'm here when you want to say it.\"","then":"Tara nods. An hour later she says what bothered her.","best":true},{"text":"Ask again right away, over and over, until she tells you","then":"Tara says \"Stop pushing,\" and the tension spikes."},{"text":"Let it go and hope it fades on its own","then":"The flat \"fine\" hangs over dinner, and nothing changes."},{"text":"Decide she is punishing you and go cold","then":"The silence spreads across the flat, and neither of you names it."}],"why":"Leaving the door open, without forcing it, lets a real answer come when she is ready."}],"debrief":"Gently reopen a hollow 'it's fine' so the real hurt clears, not a fake truce.","relearn":"Gently reopen a hollow 'it's fine'; a fake truce just lets the hurt resurface bigger later."},
  {"id":"uk-1098","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"check the bandwidth","hook":"You are desperate to vent about your day, but Sameer walks in equally shattered.","steps":[{"prompt":"He drops onto the sofa. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wait for him to ask, and say nothing until then","then":"He doesn't ask. You swallow your day and feel invisible."},{"text":"Unload the moment he sits down and closes his eyes","then":"Sameer snaps that his day was worse. Now you are arguing."},{"text":"Say \"Rough day\" and hope he asks for more","then":"Sameer nods, too tired to follow up, and you both go quiet."},{"text":"Say \"I need to offload. Got ten minutes, or should I wait?\"","then":"Sameer rubs his eyes. \"Ten minutes, then a shower. Go.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking for his bandwidth first lets him say yes, or say later."},{"prompt":"Another evening, Sameer arrives even more wiped and starts on his boss. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you can't deal with any of it tonight","then":"Sameer stops mid-sentence and goes to the balcony alone."},{"text":"Say \"I'm tired too. Ten minutes for you, then me.\"","then":"Sameer laughs weakly. \"Deal. Set a timer.\"","best":true},{"text":"Listen for an hour and keep your own day inside","then":"You nod along, and your own day sits heavier by bedtime."},{"text":"Cut in with your own day before he finishes","then":"Sameer says he wasn't done. Two people talk at once."}],"why":"Owning your limit and sharing the time keeps both of you heard."},{"prompt":"Weeks on, busy evenings keep crowding out talk about your days. What do you suggest?","options":[{"text":"Stop sharing hard days altogether, to keep the peace","then":"The evenings go calm and empty, and you know less about each other."},{"text":"Vent to friends only, and skip Sameer","then":"Friends help a bit, but Sameer feels left outside your days."},{"text":"Take turns by whose day was worse","then":"Ranking days starts a small contest, and neither of you wins."},{"text":"A nightly turn, five minutes each, then switch","then":"Sameer nods. \"Five and five. I like that.\"","best":true}],"why":"A short, shared turn each night gives both of you a real hearing."}],"debrief":"Check your partner has bandwidth before unloading, not dump the moment they sit.","relearn":"Check your partner has bandwidth before you offload; dumping on empty sparks a clash, not relief."},
  {"id":"uk-1099","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"name the real cause","hook":"Mid-argument over the dishes, you notice you are both just exhausted, not really angry.","steps":[{"prompt":"The voices are still raised. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep going until the dishes point is settled","then":"The argument stretches on, and you both feel worse."},{"text":"Say \"I'm done\" and leave the kitchen without more","then":"Ishaan is left holding the sponge, confused about what just ended."},{"text":"Say \"This isn't about dishes. We're shattered. Let's stop.\"","then":"Ishaan blinks, then lets out a breath. \"Yeah. I'm running on nothing.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"We're being ridiculous. Look at us.\"","then":"Ishaan feels labelled, and the edge in his voice comes back."}],"why":"Naming the real cause, tiredness, lets you both step back."},{"prompt":"Later that evening the dishes are still in the sink. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wash them together and say thanks","then":"You each wash a stack, and it feels like being on one team.","best":true},{"text":"Bring the argument up again to finish it","then":"The old tension returns, over a sink of plates."},{"text":"Do them and list all the times he hasn't","then":"Ishaan hears the tally, and the peace ends before it started."},{"text":"Leave them and wait for him to notice","then":"The plates sit through the night, with a small chill in the room."}],"why":"Small joint action after a stopped fight rebuilds the team feeling."},{"prompt":"A week on, another snappy evening starts building. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Hold your line, since the dishes really do matter","then":"The argument grows, and the tiredness never gets named."},{"text":"Pretend nothing is happening and stay silent","then":"The edge builds anyway, and you both feel it."},{"text":"Ask \"Is this the dishes, or are we just tired?\"","then":"Ishaan almost laughs. \"Tired. Definitely tired.\"","best":true},{"text":"Tell Ishaan to calm down and stop overreacting","then":"He feels dismissed and turns colder, not calmer."}],"why":"A quick check on whether it is really tiredness can stop a small fight early."}],"debrief":"Name out loud when a fight is really exhaustion, not the surface issue.","relearn":"Name out loud when a fight is really exhaustion, not the dishes, so you can both step back."},
  {"id":"uk-1100","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"thanks over correction","hook":"Aman put the baby to bed differently from you tonight, and you feel the criticism rising.","steps":[{"prompt":"The baby is asleep. What do you say to Aman?","options":[{"text":"Point out each step he did differently","then":"Aman's smile fades. He takes it as \"you failed the test.\""},{"text":"Say \"Thanks for bedtime. That gave me a real break.\"","then":"Aman looks pleased and relieved. \"I wasn't sure I'd done it right.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"Thanks, but next time do it my way\"","then":"Aman nods flatly. The thanks got lost behind the correction."},{"text":"Say nothing, then redo the routine quietly later","then":"Aman hears the rustling and feels he wasn't trusted."}],"why":"Thanking him first tells him his way of doing bedtime counts too."},{"prompt":"A few days later Aman says he'll take bedtime again. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say yes, then hover in the doorway watching","then":"Aman feels watched and rushes through it, tense."},{"text":"Say no. It's quicker if you do it yourself","then":"Aman shrugs and lets you take over. A week later he offers bedtime again anyway."},{"text":"Say yes, but text him a long list of steps","then":"Aman reads it and feels handed a manual, not trusted."},{"text":"Say yes, and let him do it his own way","then":"Aman does bedtime with a different song. The baby sleeps fine.","best":true}],"why":"Letting him do it his way shows trust, so he keeps taking bedtime."},{"prompt":"A week on, Aman is doing bedtime again, and one small part of it really matters to you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give him the whole list of differences","then":"Aman's shoulders drop. \"Then I'll never get it right.\""},{"text":"Name that one thing, and ask him kindly","then":"Aman nods. \"Easy. That one I can do.\"","best":true},{"text":"Let it go, to avoid a fight","then":"Bedtime stays calm, but the one thing that matters stays unsaid."},{"text":"Tell him he should have worked it out by now","then":"Aman stiffens. \"How would I know if you never said?\""}],"why":"One kind, clear ask about what matters beats a long list or silence."}],"debrief":"Lead with appreciation when a partner takes a task, not correction.","relearn":"Lead with thanks when a partner takes a task; nit-picking every difference makes them hand it back."},
  {"id":"uk-1101","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"soft reset","hook":"You are both on fumes, and a tense silence has settled over the flat all evening.","steps":[{"prompt":"It is nearly bedtime and nobody has spoken. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"We're both wrecked. No fight needed. Chai and bed?\"","then":"Rhea gives a tired smile. \"Chai. Yes please.\"","best":true},{"text":"Wait it out to see who speaks first tonight","then":"Neither of you speaks, and the silence is still there at lights out."},{"text":"Make chai and leave it beside her without a word","then":"Rhea sips it, touched, but the silence stays unspoken."},{"text":"Ask \"What did I do?\" in a stiff, wary voice","then":"Rhea says \"Nothing,\" and the room gets colder."}],"why":"A soft reset that names the tiredness ends a standoff without a winner."},{"prompt":"The next evening the silence starts again after a small snap. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say sorry, but only if she says it too","then":"Rhea hears the condition, and neither of you says sorry."},{"text":"Stay quiet, sure it will pass by itself","then":"It passes slowly, and you both feel a little lonelier."},{"text":"Say \"Reset? I'm tired, and sorry for my tone.\"","then":"Rhea exhales. \"Reset. Me too.\" The room warms.","best":true},{"text":"Keep count, and remind her she snapped first","then":"Rhea says that isn't the point, and the tension climbs."}],"why":"Owning your tone and inviting a reset breaks the cold quickly."},{"prompt":"Rhea asks what you two can do when silences start. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Pick a simple signal, like \"reset\", that either can say","then":"Rhea grins. \"Reset. Easy to remember.\"","best":true},{"text":"Promise never to go quiet on each other again","then":"You both know it isn't a promise anyone can keep."},{"text":"Say silences just happen and there's no fixing them","then":"Rhea's face falls. The next silence feels inevitable."},{"text":"Say whoever is more tired should just go to bed","then":"You go to bed apart, and the standoff stays unspoken."}],"why":"A shared signal makes it easy for either of you to break a tired standoff."}],"debrief":"Break a tired standoff with a soft reset, not a waiting game.","relearn":"Break a tired standoff with a soft reset; a who-caves-first wait just stretches the distance."},
  {"id":"uk-1102","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"i see the nights","hook":"Your partner Farah has been up three nights with a teething baby and is close to tears.","setup":"It is early evening, and she is pacing with the baby.","steps":[{"prompt":"Farah says: \"I can't do another night like that. I'm falling apart.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Everyone's tired. You're not the only one here.\"","then":"Her face closes. \"I know. That's not what I meant.\""},{"text":"\"You've carried these nights. Go sleep. I've got her.\"","then":"Her shoulders drop. \"Really? All night? Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Try to nap whenever the baby naps, you know.\"","then":"She stares. \"That's your advice?\""},{"text":"\"I'll help tonight if you write down what to do.\"","then":"She blinks. The list is one more task on her."}],"why":"Naming her load and taking a whole night shift shows real support."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Farah says: \"I feel guilty that I can't cope better.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You just need more rest. That's all it is.\"","then":"She nods, but the guilt stays."},{"text":"\"Don't feel guilty. That's a silly thing to feel.\"","then":"She looks down. The guilt just goes quiet."},{"text":"\"I'm tired too. Let's not compare our nights.\"","then":"She stiffens. Now she feels she complained."},{"text":"\"You're not failing. Three broken nights wreck anyone.\"","then":"She lets out a shaky laugh. \"Thank you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying she isn't failing lifts the guilt, and it's the load that is heavy."},{"prompt":"That evening, Farah says: \"I don't want to be a burden. Can we just manage?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, but tell me if it gets bad.\"","then":"She nods, and won't say when it does."},{"text":"\"You're not a burden. We'll split nights this week.\"","then":"She wipes her eyes. \"Okay. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes. We can manage. Let's just get on with it.\"","then":"She gives a tired shrug. The nights stay hers."},{"text":"\"I'll ask my mother to come and take over.\"","then":"She flinches. It feels like being replaced."}],"why":"Splitting nights fairly means she is never a burden, and you both get rest."},{"prompt":"A few nights on, Farah says: \"She finally slept. I'm awake anyway, wired.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then use the time to tidy up the flat a bit.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I don't want to tidy.\""},{"text":"\"Scroll on your phone for a bit. It helps me.\"","then":"She scrolls for an hour. \"Now I'm even more awake.\""},{"text":"\"Try harder to sleep. You really need it now.\"","then":"She goes rigid. Trying harder never works."},{"text":"\"That happens. Sit with me ten minutes, then sleep.\"","then":"She leans on your shoulder. \"Ten minutes. Yes.\"","best":true}],"why":"Sitting together first eases the wired-tired feeling and keeps you a team."}],"relearn":"Naming what they've carried and taking a real shift over the nights makes a wrecked partner feel seen."},
  {"id":"uk-1103","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"own the week","hook":"You realise you have been short and cold with your partner Rohit all week without meaning to be.","setup":"He is at the sink, quiet, and glances over at you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rohit says: \"Everything okay? You've seemed far away.\"","options":[{"text":"\"If I've been off, it's because you've been difficult.\"","then":"His face falls. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"\"I've been snappy all week. It's tiredness, not you.\"","then":"He lets out a breath. \"I thought I'd done something.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm fine. Just tired. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"He nods, unconvinced, and goes back to the sink."},{"text":"\"Oh, so now you notice. That's new.\"","then":"He blinks. \"I'm trying to ask.\""}],"why":"Owning the week and naming the tiredness opens a real repair."},{"prompt":"That evening, Rohit says: \"I was worried I'd upset you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, you never ask me how I am anyway.\"","then":"He looks confused. \"I just did.\""},{"text":"\"You worry too much. It isn't about you.\"","then":"He shrugs, and the worry has nowhere to go."},{"text":"\"You always think it's about you, don't you?\"","then":"He goes quiet. The talk is over."},{"text":"\"You didn't. Thank you for not snapping back.\"","then":"He smiles. \"I wanted to. I just didn't.\"","best":true}],"why":"Thanking him for staying calm shows you value how he handled it."},{"prompt":"Later, Rohit says: \"What can I do when you're this tired?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nothing. You can't fix it. Nobody can.\"","then":"He looks helpless. \"There must be something.\""},{"text":"\"Ask me 'tired or upset?' and give me ten minutes.\"","then":"He nods. \"Tired or upset. Ten minutes. Got it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You should know by now what I need.\"","then":"He sighs. \"I really don't.\""},{"text":"\"Take the baby all night and stay out of my way.\"","then":"He nods, wary, unsure how to help."}],"why":"Giving him one simple, clear thing to do lets him actually help."},{"prompt":"Days later, Rohit asks: \"Can we agree a signal for when you're too tired to talk?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We don't need a signal. Just be patient with me.\"","then":"He hesitates. \"I'll try. It's hard to read you.\""},{"text":"\"Sure. But don't push me to talk about it after.\"","then":"He nods, but wonders when the talk will happen."},{"text":"\"Okay. And you use one too, if you snap.\"","then":"He frowns. It sounds like a tally."},{"text":"\"Yes. I'll say 'tired, not you' and we talk after.\"","then":"He grins. \"Signal received.\"","best":true}],"why":"A simple signal plus a break lets you rest, and keeps talking on track."}],"relearn":"Owning a week of snappiness and naming the exhaustion, not blaming back, opens a real repair."},
  {"id":"uk-1104","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"soft start","hook":"You need to raise that you feel unseen, but every past attempt turned into a blame match.","setup":"Tonight the baby is asleep and Mihir is on the sofa.","steps":[{"prompt":"Mihir says: \"You've been quiet all evening. What is it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm struggling. I need to feel seen. Can we talk?\"","then":"He sets the remote down. \"Okay. Yes. Tell me what seen would look like.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You never notice anything I do around here.\"","then":"He stiffens. \"That's not true. I do plenty.\""},{"text":"\"Nothing. Forget it. It's not worth the fight.\"","then":"He shrugs and goes back to the screen. The quiet stays."},{"text":"\"I feel bad about how things are. Can you fix it?\"","then":"He looks lost. \"Fix what? Tell me what you need.\""}],"why":"A soft start names your need without blame, so he can listen."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Mihir says: \"I can tell you're unhappy. I just don't know what you need.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then you're not trying hard enough, are you?\"","then":"He stiffens. \"I am trying.\""},{"text":"\"Forget it. It's not worth trying to explain.\"","then":"He nods, and stays in the dark about what you need."},{"text":"\"A thank-you for the small things would help a lot.\"","then":"He nods. \"That I can do. Starting now.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"If you really loved me, you would just know.\"","then":"He looks stung. \"I can't read your mind.\""}],"why":"A specific, small ask is easier to meet than a hint or a test."},{"prompt":"That weekend, Mihir says: \"I said thanks for dinner and you barely reacted.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Sorry, it did land. I was distracted. Keep going.\"","then":"He grins. \"Okay. I'll say it louder.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It took you this long to say it, though.\"","then":"His smile fades. \"I'm trying here.\""},{"text":"\"Thanks for noticing that I didn't react.\"","then":"He frowns. \"Was that sarcasm?\""},{"text":"\"Well, one thank-you doesn't fix a whole month.\"","then":"He shrugs, and wonders whether it's worth it."}],"why":"Owning that you missed it, and asking him to keep going, keeps the thanks coming."},{"prompt":"Later, Mihir asks: \"Is there anything else I should know?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. You've done enough. Let's leave it there.\"","then":"He nods, relieved, and what you still need goes unsaid."},{"text":"\"Yes. A list of everything you've missed so far.\"","then":"He goes still. The list is long, and he feels small."},{"text":"\"Yes. I'd like one hour a week just for us two.\"","then":"He smiles. \"Saturday morning, then. It's ours.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't want to talk about it anymore, honestly.\"","then":"He nods. The topic closes, and the need stays."}],"why":"Making one shared, protected hour builds on the talk without turning it into blame."}],"relearn":"A soft start like 'I'm struggling and need to feel seen' lands where a harsh 'you never' backfires."},
  {"id":"uk-1105","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"de-escalate","hook":"Worn out, your partner Tanvi lashes out at you, and you want to snap right back.","setup":"It is 3am, and the baby is crying in the next room.","steps":[{"prompt":"Tanvi says: \"You're useless at night. I do everything!\"","options":[{"text":"\"That landed hard. We're both fried. Pause and try later?\"","then":"Her shoulders drop. \"Okay. Yes. Sorry. Baby first.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Useless? Look in the mirror before you say that.\"","then":"She flares. \"Oh, so it's my fault?\""},{"text":"\"Fine. You do it all then. See how you like it.\"","then":"She grabs the baby. The night gets colder."},{"text":"\"You're right. I'm useless. I can't do anything right.\"","then":"She looks unsettled. Now she has to comfort you."}],"why":"Naming the hurt and the shared exhaustion, then pausing, calms a fight."},{"prompt":"The next morning, Tanvi says: \"I've been thinking about last night.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. You should be sorry about what you said.\"","then":"Her face hardens. \"I was about to say sorry.\""},{"text":"\"Let's just forget it happened. It was nothing.\"","then":"She nods, and the hurt stays."},{"text":"\"Me too. Tell me what you need from me at night.\"","then":"She thinks. \"Just take the first waking. I'll take the second.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I did do things at night. You just didn't see them.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"So I'm the one who's wrong?\""}],"why":"Asking what she needs gives you something to do, instead of a point to win."},{"prompt":"That evening, Tanvi says: \"I need you to wake when the baby cries, not after.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay. Monitor on my side. The first cry is mine.\"","then":"She nods, relieved. \"Then I'll take the next one.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm exhausted too. Can't you be understanding?\"","then":"She frowns. \"I've been understanding all week.\""},{"text":"\"I'll try. I can't promise anything though.\"","then":"She nods, unconvinced. Nothing is planned."},{"text":"\"Then set a stricter alarm for me. Your call.\"","then":"She sighs. It puts the whole job on her."}],"why":"A clear, concrete plan for the night turns a complaint into something you both own."}],"relearn":"Naming the hurt and the shared exhaustion, then pausing, de-escalates where a counter-attack escalates."},
  {"id":"uk-1106","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"that counted","hook":"Your partner Ira did the 4am feed so you could sleep, and you want her to know it counted.","setup":"In the morning she is making tea, looking wrecked.","steps":[{"prompt":"Ira says: \"Morning. Did you sleep okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thanks for last night. So, what's the plan today?\"","then":"She nods. \"Sure.\" The thanks slips straight past her."},{"text":"\"Yes, fine. Is there any tea going for me?\"","then":"She hands you a cup, and doesn't say more."},{"text":"\"Fine. I'm sorry you had to do the feed alone.\"","then":"She shrugs. \"It's okay.\" It sounds like it isn't."},{"text":"\"I slept through 4am and woke up human. Thank you.\"","then":"Her face softens. \"That's exactly what I needed to hear.\"","best":true}],"why":"Specific, small appreciation tells her it counted."},{"prompt":"That afternoon, Ira says: \"I did the feed, then the laundry, then the bottles.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. That's what needs doing around here.\"","then":"She goes quiet. Nothing seems to land."},{"text":"\"I see all of that. Which one can I take tonight?\"","then":"She thinks. \"The bottles. Then I can breathe.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I did a lot today too. It wasn't easy either.\"","then":"She nods. \"I know.\" But she doesn't feel seen."},{"text":"\"You should rest more instead of doing it all.\"","then":"She sighs. \"I would, if it wasn't all there.\""}],"why":"Seeing the whole list and offering a real job shows you counted it."},{"prompt":"In the evening, Ira asks: \"How can we both sleep enough?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You handle the nights. I'll handle the earning.\"","then":"She frowns. \"That's not sharing. That's a split of who suffers.\""},{"text":"\"We'll cope. Everyone does with a newborn around.\"","then":"She nods, but nothing has changed."},{"text":"\"You should sleep in the day when it's quiet.\"","then":"She sighs. \"When is that, exactly?\""},{"text":"\"Swap the 4am each night, and a thanks each morning.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Okay. Both. I like that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Swapping the night shift and saying thanks shares both the load and the credit."},{"prompt":"Days later, Ira says: \"I did the 4am again, and it didn't feel like a chore this time.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. Then it's easy, and you can keep doing it.\"","then":"She blinks. \"I'd like a turn off, too.\""},{"text":"\"I'm glad. Tomorrow I'll take it, and you sleep.\"","then":"She beams. \"Deal. Then we're even on sleep.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's just a feed. It's really not a big deal.\"","then":"She goes quiet, and the warmth cools."},{"text":"\"I'll say thanks once a week. Is that enough?\"","then":"She smiles politely. It doesn't feel like enough."}],"why":"Answering her good moment by taking your turn keeps the night shift shared."}],"relearn":"Specific, small appreciation, like 'you let me sleep and I woke up human', sustains the bond under load."},
  {"id":"uk-1107","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"same side","hook":"You both kept score all week of who did more, and you want to break the tally without blaming.","setup":"At the end of a long Sunday, Jaya starts it up again.","steps":[{"prompt":"Jaya says: \"I did the last three night feeds. You did two.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. Let's count properly and see who did more.\"","then":"She pulls out her phone. The evening becomes an audit."},{"text":"\"I'll do four feeds tomorrow to even it up, okay?\"","then":"She nods, but the count is still running."},{"text":"\"You always turn everything into a competition.\"","then":"She flares. \"Me? You're the one counting.\""},{"text":"\"This tally is making us enemies. Can we be a team?\"","then":"She pauses, then sighs. \"Yes. I hate this too.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming the scorekeeping and choosing the same side breaks the tally."},{"prompt":"The next evening, Jaya says: \"I feel like nobody sees what I do.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You keep count of everything, though. That's not seeing.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"So I'm the problem?\""},{"text":"\"I see it. Can we swap thanks instead of tallies?\"","then":"She almost smiles. \"Thanks would be nice.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nobody sees what I do either. It's the same for me.\"","then":"She looks away. Now it's a contest of who is more unseen."},{"text":"\"Write it all down if it matters that much to you.\"","then":"She nods, stung. The list is what remains."}],"why":"Seeing her, and offering thanks in place of counting, makes room for both of you."},{"prompt":"Days later, Jaya says: \"I caught myself counting again today.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then just stop. It's not that hard, really.\"","then":"She looks away. \"Easy for you to say.\""},{"text":"\"Keep a chart so it stays fair for both of us.\"","then":"She sighs. \"That's just counting with a chart.\""},{"text":"\"Let's just avoid talking about chores altogether.\"","then":"She nods, and the chores stay unspoken and unshared."},{"text":"\"Same. When we catch it, we say 'same side' and stop.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Same side. I can do that.\"","best":true}],"why":"A shared phrase for catching the tally makes it easier to stop together."},{"prompt":"On Sunday, Jaya says: \"So who did more this week, really?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I did, obviously. You can see it as well as I can.\"","then":"She stiffens, and the old fight returns."},{"text":"\"Let's skip the count and look at who owns what.\"","then":"She nods slowly. \"Okay. Let's list the jobs.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You did. I give up on counting. You win this one.\"","then":"She frowns. \"I don't want to win.\""},{"text":"\"Let's do a proper chart and settle it for good.\"","then":"She shrugs, and the chart becomes another scoreboard."}],"why":"Looking at who owns what shifts the question from blame to a fair plan."}],"relearn":"Naming the scorekeeping and choosing the same side beats settling the tally, which keeps you opponents."},
  {"id":"uk-1108","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"protect the topic","hook":"A hard topic, money and the baby's costs, keeps colliding with chaos and ending in a snap.","setup":"It is nine at night, and the baby has just started fussing.","steps":[{"prompt":"Reena says: \"We need to talk about the bills right now.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Too big for now. Ten calm minutes when it's quiet?\"","then":"Reena hesitates, then nods. \"Ten minutes, rested. Yes. I'd rather that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll hash it out now, however late it gets.\"","then":"You go round in circles until midnight. It ends in a snap."},{"text":"\"Not now. Let's just skip it for this whole month.\"","then":"Reena nods, but the bills stay unopened and her worry grows."},{"text":"\"Let's talk tomorrow sometime. Whenever we can.\"","then":"Reena agrees, but no time is set and tomorrow slips by."}],"why":"Booking a calm slot for a heavy topic protects it from the chaos."},{"prompt":"Later, Reena says: \"I'm scared about the costs. I do sums in my head at 3am.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Don't worry. It will all work out in the end.\"","then":"She nods, but the sums keep going at 3am."},{"text":"\"I'm handling the earning. That's my part of it.\"","then":"She goes quiet. \"And I'm handling the worrying, then.\""},{"text":"\"I didn't know that. Let's put the numbers together.\"","then":"Reena's shoulders drop. \"Thank you. I've been carrying it alone.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Stop doing sums. It only makes you more tired.\"","then":"She looks away. The sums are still there."}],"why":"Learning what she carries, and sharing the numbers, lets you worry together."},{"prompt":"When you finally sit down with the bills, Reena asks: \"Where do we start?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Facts first: what comes in and what's fixed.\"","then":"She opens a notebook. \"Okay. Numbers, then choices.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"With everything you spent on the baby things.\"","then":"She flinches. The talk turns into a trial."},{"text":"\"You decide, and I'll follow whatever you say.\"","then":"She sighs. It's the whole load on her again."},{"text":"\"With a huge saving target, starting right now.\"","then":"She stares. The target looks impossible, and she freezes."}],"why":"Starting with facts, together, keeps it about the numbers and not about blame."},{"prompt":"A week later, Reena says: \"Money talk still knots my stomach. How do we keep it calm?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Don't worry. I'll handle all of the money myself.\"","then":"She nods, relieved, but now she is shut out of her own budget."},{"text":"\"Let's just never bring it up unless we have to.\"","then":"She hesitates. Both of you know it won't disappear."},{"text":"\"Let's book ten calm minutes and stop if we get tense.\"","then":"She smiles. \"With a stop signal? That helps.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We'll wing it as it comes. It usually works out.\"","then":"She looks doubtful, and the worry stays where it was."}],"why":"A short calm slot with an easy exit makes the hard topic doable."}],"relearn":"Booking a calm slot for a heavy topic, instead of forcing it in the chaos, is timing, not avoidance."},
  {"id":"uk-1109","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each tired-moment trigger to the repair line that defuses it.","pairs":[{"left":"You snapped over nothing","right":"\"Sorry, that was the tiredness, not you.\""},{"left":"A hard talk keeps colliding with chaos","right":"\"Let's give this ten calm minutes later.\""},{"left":"Your partner seems flat and far away","right":"\"How are YOU doing, really?\""},{"left":"You feel unnoticed","right":"\"I could use a little appreciation too.\""},{"left":"You're both circling a fight","right":"\"We're both fried; let's pause this.\""}],"relearn":"Each tired-moment trigger has a repair line that defuses it.","persona":"any","source":"trigger to repair"},
  {"id":"uk-1110","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each soft, gentle opener to the harsh version it replaces.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I'm struggling, can you help me here?\"","right":"\"You never lift a finger.\""},{"left":"\"Can we find a calm minute for this?\"","right":"\"We're talking about this RIGHT now.\""},{"left":"\"I felt a bit alone today.\"","right":"\"You don't care about me at all.\""},{"left":"\"I'd love a hand with bedtime.\"","right":"\"Why is it always me doing bedtime?\""},{"left":"\"That stung; can we redo it?\"","right":"\"Forget it, you always do this.\""}],"relearn":"Every harsh opener has a soft-start version that says the same need without the defensiveness.","persona":"any","source":"soft vs harsh openers"},
  {"id":"uk-1111","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each repair tool to the bond it protects after a baby.","pairs":[{"left":"Naming tiredness, not blaming","right":"Fewer needless tired fights"},{"left":"Quick 'sorry' after a snap","right":"Trust through the hardest weeks"},{"left":"Small specific appreciation","right":"The warmth the load erodes"},{"left":"Pausing hard talks till calm","right":"Words you won't regret"},{"left":"Assuming good intent","right":"Fights that never had to start"}],"relearn":"Naming tiredness, repairing fast, appreciating, pausing and assuming good intent each protect a different part of the bond.","persona":"any","source":"tools to bonds"},
  {"id":"uk-1112","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each tired thought to the reframe that keeps you on the same team.","pairs":[{"left":"\"He's being cold on purpose.\"","right":"\"He's probably just wrecked.\""},{"left":"\"I have to win this point.\"","right":"\"I just want us to feel okay.\""},{"left":"\"She doesn't appreciate me.\"","right":"\"She's running on empty too.\""},{"left":"\"We have to fix this tonight.\"","right":"\"This can wait till we're rested.\""},{"left":"\"Saying sorry first means I lose.\"","right":"\"Saying sorry first ends the cold.\""}],"relearn":"Each tired, suspicious thought has a reframe, assume good intent, aim for repair, that keeps you on the same team.","persona":"any","source":"thought to reframe"},
  {"id":"uk-1113","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each moment to the right next move under exhaustion.","pairs":[{"left":"Baby just went down; you're both spent","right":"Park the heavy talk for tomorrow"},{"left":"You just said something sharp","right":"Repair it before it sets"},{"left":"Partner did a thankless 3am shift","right":"Notice it out loud"},{"left":"You feel a complaint rising","right":"Soften it into a request"},{"left":"You're misreading their silence","right":"Ask instead of assume"}],"relearn":"Park heavy talks when spent, repair fast, notice the unseen shifts, soften complaints and ask before assuming.","persona":"any","source":"moment to move"},
  {"id":"uk-1114","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that make a tired talk worse, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"We're both exhausted; can we pause this?\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You ALWAYS do this, every single time.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Sorry, that came out harsh, I'm wiped.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Fine. Whatever. Forget I said anything.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Can we talk about this once we've slept?\"","trick":false}],"why":"'You ALWAYS' and a stonewalling 'forget it' escalate or shut down; the others name tiredness, repair or pause.","relearn":"'You always' and a stonewalling 'forget it' worsen tired talks; naming tiredness, repairing and pausing help.","persona":"any","source":"catch the worse lines"},
  {"id":"uk-1115","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two moves that turn a snap into a wound, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Saying sorry soon after you snap","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Storing the snap as evidence for later","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Naming it was the exhaustion talking","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Bringing up every past snap to even the score","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Checking in once you're both calmer","trick":false}],"why":"Stockpiling a snap and dragging up old ones to even the score harden it; repairing, naming and checking in heal it.","relearn":"Stockpiling a snap or dragging up old ones turns it into a wound; repair fast, name it, and check in calmly.","persona":"Vikram","source":"catch snap-to-wound"},
  {"id":"uk-1116","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that assume contempt instead of tiredness, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You're snappy, are you running on empty?\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"That tone means you've stopped respecting me.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"You seem far away; rough night?\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"You're cold because you don't care anymore.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Hard day? You look wiped.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Reading a tired tone as lost respect or not caring assumes contempt; the others assume good intent and ask.","relearn":"Reading a tired partner's tone as contempt assumes the worst; assume good intent and ask if they're wiped.","persona":"Divya","source":"catch the contempt-read"},
  {"id":"uk-1117","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two bad-timing moves for a hard talk, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Raising it after a decent night's sleep","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Opening it at 3am mid-feed, depleted","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Choosing a calm ten minutes once she's down","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Starting it as you both rush out the door","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Asking 'is now an okay time to talk?'","trick":false}],"why":"A depleted 3am or a rushed doorway start dooms a hard talk; a rested, calm, agreed moment lets it land.","relearn":"A depleted 3am or rushed-doorway start dooms a hard talk; pick a rested, calm, agreed moment instead.","persona":"any","source":"catch bad timing"},
  {"id":"uk-1118","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two responses that shut your partner down, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Tell me more, I'm listening.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're overreacting, it's not a big deal.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"That sounds really hard, I get it.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Scrolling your phone while they talk","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"What would actually help right now?\"","trick":false}],"why":"Calling them an overreaction or scrolling mid-vent shuts them down; listening and asking what helps opens them up.","relearn":"Dismissing them as overreacting or scrolling mid-vent shuts a partner down; listen and ask what would help.","persona":"any","source":"catch the shutdowns"},
  {"id":"uk-1119","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two appreciation-killers, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Thanks for doing the night feed.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You only did it because I asked.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I noticed you tidied up, that helped.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"That's literally your job as a parent.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"You handled bedtime so well tonight.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Adding 'only because I asked' or 'that's your job' cancels the thanks; the clean, specific thank-yous land.","relearn":"Tacking 'only because I asked' or 'that's your job' onto thanks kills it; keep appreciation clean and specific.","persona":"any","source":"catch appreciation-killers"},
  {"id":"uk-1120","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that aim the frustration at the person, the red flags to catch.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm exhausted and I'm struggling tonight.\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You are the reason I'm this miserable.\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"The lack of sleep is wrecking me.\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"If you were a better partner I'd be fine.\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"This phase is brutal on both of us.\"","trick":false}],"why":"Blaming your partner for your misery aims at the person; naming exhaustion aims at the real enemy.","relearn":"Blaming your partner aims at the person; name the exhaustion, the real enemy is the tiredness.","persona":"any","source":"catch person-blame"},
  {"id":"uk-1121","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: tonight, the enemy isn't your partner, it's the tiredness you're both carrying. Sit with that?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["I'll hold it","Tiredness is the enemy","We're on one side"],"affirm":"Aiming the frustration at the exhaustion, not each other, is the move that keeps a couple a team under load.","relearn":"The enemy under load is the exhaustion, not your partner; aiming there keeps you a team.","persona":"any","source":"enemy is tiredness"},
  {"id":"uk-1122","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a fast 'sorry, that was the tiredness' mends more than never snapping ever could. Worth keeping?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Worth keeping","Repair over perfect","I'll try it"],"affirm":"Quick repair after a snap protects a couple far more than chasing a flawless, never-snap record.","relearn":"Quick repair after a snap protects you more than chasing a flawless never-snap record.","persona":"any","source":"repair over perfect"},
  {"id":"uk-1123","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: noticing one small thing your partner did today, out loud, is quiet glue. Land okay?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Lands okay","I'll notice one thing","Small glue"],"affirm":"Small, specific appreciation, said out loud, is one of the strongest things keeping a couple close right now.","relearn":"Noticing one small thing out loud is quiet glue; small specific appreciation keeps you close.","persona":"any","source":"quiet glue"},
  {"id":"uk-1124","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: not every hard talk has to happen tonight; some are kinder in the morning. Feel that?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Feel it","Some wait till morning","Timing matters"],"affirm":"Choosing the right moment for a hard talk is a skill, not avoidance; a rested conversation lands better.","relearn":"Choosing the moment for a hard talk is a skill, not avoidance; rested conversations land better.","persona":"any","source":"some wait"},
  {"id":"uk-1125","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: when their tone is sharp tonight, try reading 'tired', not 'against me'. Worth trying?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth trying","Tired not against me","I'll assume good intent"],"affirm":"Assuming a sharp tone is exhaustion, not contempt, defuses most of the conflicts this phase throws up.","relearn":"Read a sharp tone as tired, not against you; assuming good intent defuses most tired conflicts.","persona":"any","source":"read tired"},
  {"id":"uk-1126","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the romance isn't over, it just changed shape; a tiny effort still keeps it alive. Land?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands","Changed not ended","Tiny effort counts"],"affirm":"Connection after a baby changes shape rather than ending; small, intentional effort keeps the bond alive.","relearn":"Connection changes shape, it doesn't end; a small intentional effort keeps the romance alive.","persona":"any","source":"changed not ended"},
  {"id":"uk-1127","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: in a tired fight, ask 'do I want to win, or do I want us okay?' Worth holding?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Worth holding","I want us okay","Repair not win"],"affirm":"Choosing repair over winning, making your partner feel heard, ends the spiral faster than landing your point.","relearn":"Choose repair over winning a tired fight; making your partner feel heard ends the spiral.","persona":"any","source":"win or okay"},
  {"id":"uk-1133","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: a calming repair move, or a move that escalates a tired moment?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Sorry, that was the exhaustion, not you.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You ALWAYS make this about you.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Can we pause and talk rested?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Forget it, you never listen anyway.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Thank you for taking the night feed.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"I'm done, I'm not talking to you.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"repair","label":"Calming repair","valence":"pos"},{"id":"escalate","label":"Escalates it","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"repair","b":"escalate","c":"repair","d":"escalate","e":"repair","f":"escalate"},"relearn":"Apologising, pausing and appreciating calm a tired moment; 'you always', 'forget it' and stonewalling escalate it.","persona":"any","source":"repair vs escalate"},
  {"id":"uk-1128","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each opener: a soft start that lands, or a harsh start that backfires?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm struggling, can we sort this together?\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You never help with anything.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Could we find a calm ten minutes?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"What is the matter with you?\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"I felt a bit alone today.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"This is all your fault, as usual.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"soft","label":"Soft start","valence":"pos"},{"id":"harsh","label":"Harsh start","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"soft","b":"harsh","c":"soft","d":"harsh","e":"soft","f":"harsh"},"relearn":"'I'm struggling', 'a calm ten minutes', 'I felt alone' are soft starts; 'you never', 'what's wrong with you', 'your fault' backfire.","persona":"any","source":"soft vs harsh"},
  {"id":"uk-1129","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: speak up now, or let it wait for a calmer moment?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A quick 'sorry' right after you snap"},{"id":"b","text":"A heavy money-and-baby talk at 3am"},{"id":"c","text":"Noticing the night feed they just did"},{"id":"d","text":"Reopening last week's hurt mid-meltdown"},{"id":"e","text":"A warm 'thank you' as they head to bed"},{"id":"f","text":"Relitigating the chore split at midnight"}],"bins":[{"id":"now","label":"Say it now","valence":"tell"},{"id":"wait","label":"Let it wait","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"now","b":"wait","c":"now","d":"wait","e":"now","f":"wait"},"relearn":"Quick repair and appreciation are worth saying now; heavy or old grievances are kinder held for a calm, rested moment.","persona":"any","source":"now vs wait"},
  {"id":"uk-1130","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each thought: a fair read of a tired partner, or an unfair assumption?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"He's quiet because he's shattered.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Her snap means she despises me.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"He forgot because his brain is fried.\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"She's cold because she's done with me.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"We're both just running on empty.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"He's against me on purpose tonight.\""}],"bins":[{"id":"fair","label":"Fair read","valence":"pos"},{"id":"unfair","label":"Unfair assumption","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"fair","b":"unfair","c":"fair","d":"unfair","e":"fair","f":"unfair"},"relearn":"Reading tiredness behind quiet, forgetting or a snap is fair; jumping to contempt or 'done with me' is an unfair assumption.","persona":"any","source":"fair vs unfair read"},
  {"id":"uk-1131","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each line: it makes your partner feel seen, or it makes them feel dismissed?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That sounds so hard, tell me more.\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Everyone's tired, get over it.\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"How are YOU doing in all this?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"You're making a drama out of nothing.\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"I see how much you carry.\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Can we not do this right now?\""}],"bins":[{"id":"seen","label":"Feels seen","valence":"pos"},{"id":"dismissed","label":"Feels dismissed","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"seen","b":"dismissed","c":"seen","d":"dismissed","e":"seen","f":"dismissed"},"relearn":"Listening, asking how they are and naming what they carry make a partner feel seen; brushing them off dismisses them.","persona":"any","source":"seen vs dismissed"},
  {"id":"uk-1132","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each habit: it protects the tired couple, or it slowly corrodes them?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Repairing fast after a snap"},{"id":"b","text":"The silent treatment for days"},{"id":"c","text":"Naming the tiredness, not blaming"},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping a running score of chores"},{"id":"e","text":"Small daily thank-yous"},{"id":"f","text":"Bringing up every old grievance"}],"bins":[{"id":"protect","label":"Protects the couple","valence":"pos"},{"id":"corrode","label":"Corrodes the couple","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"protect","b":"corrode","c":"protect","d":"corrode","e":"protect","f":"corrode"},"relearn":"Fast repair, naming tiredness and small thanks protect the couple; silent treatment, scorekeeping and dredging old hurts corrode it.","persona":"any","source":"protect vs corrode"},
  {"id":"uk-1134","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each tired complaint to the softer request that gets a better result.","pairs":[{"left":"\"You never get up at night.\"","right":"\"Can we split the night shifts?\""},{"left":"\"You're always on your phone.\"","right":"\"I'd love ten phone-free minutes with you.\""},{"left":"\"You forgot again, typical.\"","right":"\"Can we put the appointments in one place?\""},{"left":"\"You don't care how tired I am.\"","right":"\"I really need a break tomorrow.\""},{"left":"\"Why is it always me?\"","right":"\"Can you own bedtime this week?\""}],"relearn":"Every tired complaint has a softer request that gets a far better result.","persona":"any","source":"complaint to request"},
  {"id":"uk-1135","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each appreciation line to the unseen effort it finally names out loud.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Thanks for the 3am feed.\"","right":"The nights you take so I can sleep"},{"left":"\"You kept us all fed today.\"","right":"The cooking nobody notices"},{"left":"\"You remembered the vaccine date.\"","right":"The mental load of tracking it all"},{"left":"\"You stayed calm when I lost it.\"","right":"Holding steady when I couldn't"},{"left":"\"You made the morning run smooth.\"","right":"The quiet logistics you carry"}],"relearn":"Naming the specific unseen effort, the nights, the tracking, the calm, is what makes appreciation actually land.","persona":"Vikram","source":"name the unseen"},
  {"id":"uk-1136","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each reason a tired talk went badly to the fix for next time.","pairs":[{"left":"It started at 3am, both depleted","right":"Pick a rested moment"},{"left":"It opened with 'you always'","right":"Start soft, with 'I'"},{"left":"One of you was scrolling","right":"Put the phone down and listen"},{"left":"It became about winning","right":"Aim for repair, not a win"},{"left":"Old grievances got dragged in","right":"Stick to the one issue"}],"relearn":"Bad timing, harsh openers, scrolling, point-scoring and dredging old hurts each sink a tired talk; each has a clear fix.","persona":"any","source":"why it went badly"},
  {"id":"uk-1137","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing your partner is doing to the check-in it calls for.","pairs":[{"left":"Gone very quiet all evening","right":"\"You're quiet; rough one today?\""},{"left":"Snapping at small things","right":"\"You seem stretched; how can I help?\""},{"left":"Crying in the bathroom","right":"\"I'm here; you don't have to be okay.\""},{"left":"Doing everything in silence","right":"\"Let me take over; you rest.\""},{"left":"Picking a pointless fight","right":"\"I think we're both just wiped.\""}],"relearn":"Each signal, silence, snapping, tears, over-doing, picking fights, calls for a gentle, specific check-in, not a guess.","persona":"Divya","source":"signal to check-in"},
  {"id":"uk-1138","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each repair moment to the line that mends it fastest.","pairs":[{"left":"You snapped two minutes ago","right":"\"Sorry, that was unfair of me.\""},{"left":"You were cold all evening","right":"\"I've been distant; it's not you.\""},{"left":"You dismissed their feeling","right":"\"I brushed that off; tell me again.\""},{"left":"You forgot something they asked","right":"\"I dropped that; let me fix it now.\""},{"left":"You raised your voice","right":"\"I shouldn't have shouted; I'm sorry.\""}],"relearn":"Each kind of slip, a snap, coldness, dismissal, forgetting, shouting, has a quick honest repair line that mends it fast.","persona":"any","source":"slip to repair line"},
  {"id":"uk-1139","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'we're over' fear to the small thing that quietly proves it wrong.","pairs":[{"left":"\"We never really talk anymore.\"","right":"Five honest minutes after bedtime"},{"left":"\"There's no warmth left.\"","right":"A hand squeeze across the chaos"},{"left":"\"We're just flatmates now.\"","right":"One shared laugh at the mess"},{"left":"\"He doesn't see me.\"","right":"\"How are YOU?\" at the door"},{"left":"\"The fun is gone for good.\"","right":"A two-minute tea together"}],"relearn":"Connection changes shape, not ends; tiny things, five minutes, a squeeze, a laugh, quietly disprove the 'we're over' fear.","persona":"any","source":"fear to small proof"},
  {"id":"uk-1140","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"them first","hook":"You are worn out, but Kavya has clearly had an even worse day and needs you tonight.","setup":"She drops her bag and sinks onto the sofa.","steps":[{"prompt":"Kavya says: \"Today was awful. I don't even know where to start.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm wiped too, but you look done in. Tell me first.\"","then":"She exhales. \"Thank you. It started at the office at nine.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I can't deal with your day. I've had a worse one.\"","then":"Her face falls. \"Okay. Never mind.\""},{"text":"\"Hold on. Let me tell you about mine first, quickly.\"","then":"She waits, and her day sits heavier while she does."},{"text":"\"Just eat something and sleep it off. That helps.\"","then":"She nods, and swallows her day with the dinner."}],"why":"Owning your tiredness and making room for hers first helps her feel seen."},{"prompt":"A bit later, Kavya says: \"Sorry. I know you're tired too.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I am. Can we keep your day to five minutes?\"","then":"She nods, and squeezes her whole day into a few lines."},{"text":"\"It's fine. Just don't get upset about it tonight.\"","then":"She blinks hard and keeps the rest of her day to herself."},{"text":"\"Don't be sorry. I want to hear your day. Mine after.\"","then":"She smiles, damp-eyed. \"Then I'll listen to yours after.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then let's skip it. I'd rather not hear it tonight.\"","then":"She goes quiet, then shrugs. \"Fine. Then tell me yours.\""}],"why":"Welcoming her story, and promising your turn, keeps both of you heard."},{"prompt":"Kavya asks: \"How was your day, really?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Rough. I'm wiped. Can I sit close for a minute?\"","then":"She opens her arm. \"Come here. Tell me when you're ready.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine. Nothing worth saying about it, honestly.\"","then":"She nods, and the talk closes on both of you."},{"text":"\"Worse than yours, but let's not get into it.\"","then":"She stiffens. Now it feels like a contest."},{"text":"\"Long. Let's not do this tonight. Maybe tomorrow.\"","then":"She nods, and you both go to bed unheard."}],"why":"Saying your own tiredness honestly makes your turn as real as hers."}],"relearn":"Owning your own tiredness yet still making room for theirs first is how feeling-seen gets shared, not hoarded."},
  {"id":"uk-1141","cat":"talk-through-tired","type":"sort","hook":"Sort each move: it helps you both repair, or it just helps you win the fight?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Saying 'I might be wrong here too'"},{"id":"b","text":"Listing every point to prove you're right"},{"id":"c","text":"Asking 'what do you need from me?'"},{"id":"d","text":"Bringing up a past win to score points"},{"id":"e","text":"Repeating back what they feel"},{"id":"f","text":"Talking over them to land your point"}],"bins":[{"id":"repair","label":"Helps repair","valence":"pos"},{"id":"win","label":"Just wins","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"repair","b":"win","c":"repair","d":"win","e":"repair","f":"win"},"relearn":"Admitting fault, asking what they need and reflecting feelings repair; point-listing, score-scoring and talking over just chase a win.","persona":"any","source":"repair vs win"},
  {"id":"uk-1170","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"We argue more since the baby, so we must have married the wrong person.\"","myth":{"un":"We argue more since the baby, so we must have married the wrong person.","re":"More friction after a baby is about the new load, not a wrong choice; most couples bicker more while sleep-starved.","why":"Blaming the choice hides the real, fixable cause: exhaustion."},"relearn":"More arguing after a baby points to the load, not a wrong partner; it's common and workable.","persona":"Vikram","source":"not wrong person"},
  {"id":"uk-1171","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My husband seems fine, so the post-baby strain must be only in my head.\"","myth":{"un":"My husband seems fine, so the strain must be only in my head.","re":"Both partners are under load after a baby; one looking 'fine' often just shows it differently, not that it's imagined.","why":"Naming that you're both carrying it stops you blaming yourself."},"relearn":"The post-baby strain isn't in your head; both partners feel it, even when one shows it less.","persona":"Divya","source":"both under load"},
  {"id":"uk-1172","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Couples who still look in love after kids must just have an easier life than us.\"","myth":{"un":"Couples still in love after kids just have an easier life.","re":"The couples who hold on aren't living easier; they practise sharing the load, repairing fast and appreciating each other.","why":"It reframes their bond as skills you can copy, not luck you lack."},"relearn":"Couples who stay close after kids use learnable skills, not an easier life.","persona":"Priya","source":"skills not easier"},
  {"id":"uk-1173","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If I have to work this hard at us, the love must already be gone.\"","myth":{"un":"If I have to work this hard at us, the love must already be gone.","re":"After a baby, effort isn't proof love is gone; it's what keeps a tired couple connected while the load is heavy.","why":"Effort is a sign you're protecting the bond, not losing it."},"relearn":"Working at your bond after a baby protects love; it doesn't mean love is gone.","persona":"any","source":"effort not loss"},
  {"id":"uk-1174","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A strong couple wouldn't feel this distant just a few months in.\"","myth":{"un":"A strong couple wouldn't feel this distant a few months in.","re":"The dip in closeness is steepest in the first year for most couples; distance now is the load, not weak love.","why":"It moves you from 'we're weak' to 'we're early in a known dip'."},"relearn":"Feeling distant in the first months is common; it's the load and the timing, not a weak couple.","persona":"Divya","source":"steepest early"},
  {"id":"uk-1175","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Becoming a father changed nothing for me, so something's wrong with me.\"","myth":{"un":"Becoming a father changed nothing for me, so something's wrong with me.","re":"Identity shifts land at different speeds; feeling it slowly, or mostly through the load, is one normal way, not a defect.","why":"It frees a dad from judging himself by someone else's timeline."},"relearn":"Parenthood reshapes each partner differently; a slower or quieter shift is still normal.","persona":"Vikram","source":"shift varies"},
  {"id":"uk-1176","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once the baby sleeps through the night, we'll just snap back to how we were.\"","myth":{"un":"Once the baby sleeps through, we'll just snap back to how we were.","re":"You don't snap back to the old us; you build a new us. Sleep helps, but the couple grows forward, it doesn't rewind.","why":"Aiming forward beats waiting to return to a couple that has changed."},"relearn":"Parenthood doesn't rewind you to the old couple; you build a new connection forward.","persona":"any","source":"forward not back"},
  {"id":"uk-1177","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"My parents never talked about any of this, so we should just cope quietly too.\"","myth":{"un":"My parents never talked about this, so we should just cope quietly.","re":"Older couples felt the post-baby strain too; they just had no words for it. Naming it now isn't weakness, it's a head start.","why":"Silence didn't make it easier then; it only hid the cause."},"relearn":"The post-baby strain isn't new; naming it instead of suffering quietly gives you a head start.","persona":"Meena","source":"name it now"},
  {"id":"uk-1178","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If we just love the baby enough, the couple part will sort itself out.\"","myth":{"un":"If we just love the baby enough, the couple part will sort itself out.","re":"Loving your child deeply doesn't automatically tend the couple; the bond between you two needs its own small care.","why":"The couple is a separate relationship that also needs feeding."},"relearn":"Loving your baby is not the same as tending your bond; the couple needs its own care.","persona":"Divya","source":"couple needs care"},
  {"id":"uk-1179","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"In a joint family with this much help, we've got no excuse to feel strained.\"","myth":{"un":"With this much family help, we've got no excuse to feel strained.","re":"Help with the baby is real support, yet it can also mean less couple privacy; feeling strained anyway isn't ingratitude.","why":"You can be grateful for help and still need couple time."},"relearn":"Family help and feeling strained can both be true; less privacy is a real cost, not ingratitude.","persona":"any","source":"grateful and strained"},
  {"id":"uk-1180","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Everyone calls this the happiest time, so feeling low must mean I'm a bad parent.\"","myth":{"un":"Everyone calls this the happiest time, so feeling low must mean I'm a bad parent.","re":"You can adore your baby and still feel low, tired and unlike yourself; that mix is common and says nothing about your love.","why":"Holding both feelings is normal, not a verdict on you."},"relearn":"Loving your baby and still feeling low can both be true; it doesn't make you a bad parent.","persona":"Divya","source":"both feelings true"},
  {"id":"uk-1181","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a learnable skill the close-after-kids couples use, or just luck they got?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Sharing the load and saying 'this is ours'"},{"id":"b","text":"Happening to get a calm, easy baby"},{"id":"c","text":"Repairing fast after a tired snap"},{"id":"d","text":"Just having matching personalities"},{"id":"e","text":"Noticing and naming small kindnesses"},{"id":"f","text":"Getting lucky with no money worries"}],"bins":[{"id":"skill","label":"Learnable skill","valence":"pos"},{"id":"luck","label":"Just luck","valence":"neutral"}],"key":{"a":"skill","b":"luck","c":"skill","d":"luck","e":"skill","f":"luck"},"relearn":"Thriving couples share the load, repair fast and appreciate each other; those are skills, not luck.","persona":"any","source":"skill not luck"},
  {"id":"uk-1182","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a thought that fuels the post-baby panic, or one that calms it?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"This is normal and it eases\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"One bad day means we're doomed\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"We can work on this together\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Other couples have it perfect\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"We're under load, not broken\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"If it's hard, the love is gone\""}],"bins":[{"id":"calm","label":"Calms the panic","valence":"pos"},{"id":"fuel","label":"Fuels the panic","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"calm","b":"fuel","c":"calm","d":"fuel","e":"calm","f":"fuel"},"relearn":"'Normal and workable, under load not broken' calms the panic; 'doomed, others are perfect, love is gone' fuels it.","persona":"Divya","source":"calm vs fuel"},
  {"id":"uk-1183","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a fair thing to expect of yourselves this year, or a perfect-parent trap?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Some days you only just cope"},{"id":"b","text":"Always staying calm and patient"},{"id":"c","text":"Asking for help when you're spent"},{"id":"d","text":"Looking serene like the online parents"},{"id":"e","text":"Getting some things wrong and adjusting"},{"id":"f","text":"Never needing a break from the baby"}],"bins":[{"id":"fair","label":"Fair of yourselves","valence":"pos"},{"id":"trap","label":"Perfect-parent trap","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"fair","b":"trap","c":"fair","d":"trap","e":"fair","f":"trap"},"relearn":"Coping, asking for help and getting things wrong are fair; always-calm, serene and never-needing-a-break is a trap.","persona":"any","source":"fair vs trap"},
  {"id":"uk-1184","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: the load talking, or a real sign your couple is in trouble?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Snapping at 3am over nothing"},{"id":"b","text":"Feeling unsafe around your partner"},{"id":"c","text":"Both too tired for much talk"},{"id":"d","text":"Being mocked or belittled daily"},{"id":"e","text":"Missing your old date nights"},{"id":"f","text":"One of you controlling the money"}],"bins":[{"id":"load","label":"The load talking","valence":"pos"},{"id":"trouble","label":"Real trouble, get help","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"load","b":"trouble","c":"load","d":"trouble","e":"load","f":"trouble"},"relearn":"Snapping, tiredness and missing dates are the load; feeling unsafe, daily belittling or money control are not.","persona":"any","source":"load vs trouble"},
  {"id":"uk-1185","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: part of the normal identity shift, or a worry to take to support?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Missing your pre-baby freedom sometimes"},{"id":"b","text":"Not recognising yourself for weeks on end"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling proud and lost at the same time"},{"id":"d","text":"Dreading each day with no relief"},{"id":"e","text":"Mourning your old routine a little"},{"id":"f","text":"Feeling nothing at all for a long time"}],"bins":[{"id":"shift","label":"Normal identity shift","valence":"pos"},{"id":"support","label":"Take to support","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"shift","b":"support","c":"shift","d":"support","e":"shift","f":"support"},"relearn":"Missing freedom and mourning your old routine are normal; weeks of not recognising yourself or feeling nothing need support.","persona":"Divya","source":"shift vs support"},
  {"id":"uk-1186","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a true reading of the online 'perfect parents', or a false one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"It's a curated highlight reel"},{"id":"b","text":"Their life is really that calm"},{"id":"c","text":"They're quietly struggling too"},{"id":"d","text":"You're failing for not matching it"},{"id":"e","text":"Your messy real day is the fair measure"},{"id":"f","text":"They never argue like you do"}],"bins":[{"id":"true","label":"True reading","valence":"pos"},{"id":"false","label":"False reading","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"true","b":"false","c":"true","d":"false","e":"true","f":"false"},"relearn":"The feed is a curated reel and others struggle too; 'their life is calm, you're failing' is the false reading.","persona":"any","source":"reading the feed"},
  {"id":"uk-1187","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a way to meet the big shift as a team, or a way that adds weight?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming it as a normal phase together"},{"id":"b","text":"Keeping score of who's more tired"},{"id":"c","text":"Being patient with each other"},{"id":"d","text":"Each suffering it in silence alone"},{"id":"e","text":"Checking in on how you're both doing"},{"id":"f","text":"Blaming each other for the strain"}],"bins":[{"id":"team","label":"Meets it as a team","valence":"pos"},{"id":"weight","label":"Adds weight","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"team","b":"weight","c":"team","d":"weight","e":"team","f":"weight"},"relearn":"Naming it together, patience and checking in meet the shift as a team; scorekeeping, silence and blame add weight.","persona":"Vikram","source":"team vs weight"},
  {"id":"uk-1188","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a fair belief about the post-baby dip, or a myth that hurts?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"The dip is common, not a verdict"},{"id":"b","text":"A real couple wouldn't dip at all"},{"id":"c","text":"It's the load, not lack of love"},{"id":"d","text":"Struggling means you chose wrong"},{"id":"e","text":"It's workable as a team"},{"id":"f","text":"You just have to endure it forever"}],"bins":[{"id":"fair","label":"Fair belief","valence":"pos"},{"id":"myth","label":"Myth that hurts","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"fair","b":"myth","c":"fair","d":"myth","e":"fair","f":"myth"},"relearn":"Common, load-not-love and workable are fair; 'no real dip, wrong choice, endure forever' are myths that hurt.","persona":"any","source":"fair vs myth"},
  {"id":"uk-1189","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: something a baby naturally changes for a while, or something that needn't change?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"How much sleep you both get"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether you respect each other"},{"id":"c","text":"How spontaneous your evenings are"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether you're a team at all"},{"id":"e","text":"How much spare time you have"},{"id":"f","text":"Whether you treat each other kindly"}],"bins":[{"id":"changes","label":"Naturally changes","valence":"neutral"},{"id":"stays","label":"Needn't change","valence":"pos"}],"key":{"a":"changes","b":"stays","c":"changes","d":"stays","e":"changes","f":"stays"},"relearn":"Sleep, spontaneity and spare time naturally shift for a while; respect, teamwork and kindness needn't.","persona":"any","source":"changes vs stays"},
  {"id":"uk-1190","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a kind thing to tell a struggling new-parent couple, or one that adds shame?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Most couples feel this dip\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"You should be enjoying every moment\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"It's the load, not your love\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"Other couples handle it fine\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"It eases as you find your feet\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"Don't worry, you'll be fine\" and change the subject"}],"bins":[{"id":"kind","label":"Kind and true","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shame","label":"Adds shame","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kind","b":"shame","c":"kind","d":"shame","e":"kind","f":"shame"},"relearn":"'Most feel this, it's the load, it eases' helps; 'enjoy every moment, others cope fine, just stop worrying' shames.","persona":"Priya","source":"kind vs shame"},
  {"id":"uk-1191","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a normal post-baby feeling, or one worth flagging to a clinician?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Crying from tiredness now and then"},{"id":"b","text":"Hopelessness that won't lift for weeks"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling overwhelmed on the hard days"},{"id":"d","text":"Scary thoughts you can't shake"},{"id":"e","text":"Touchy and irritable when sleep-starved"},{"id":"f","text":"No interest in anything for a long stretch"}],"bins":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal feeling","valence":"pos"},{"id":"flag","label":"Flag to a clinician","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"normal","b":"flag","c":"normal","d":"flag","e":"normal","f":"flag"},"relearn":"Tired tears, overwhelm and irritability are normal; weeks of hopelessness, scary thoughts or no interest deserve a clinician.","persona":"Divya","source":"normal vs flag"},
  {"id":"uk-1192","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"who are we now","hook":"You blurt \"I don't even know who we are anymore\" and Sunil goes quiet.","steps":[{"prompt":"The silence stretches. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take the silence as proof he has checked out","then":"You turn away, and the gap between you widens."},{"text":"Say \"Forget I said anything\" and change the subject","then":"Sunil nods, but the scary sentence is still hanging there."},{"text":"Add \"That's normal. Let's figure the new us out together.\"","then":"Sunil looks up, surprised. \"I've been thinking the same thing.\"","best":true},{"text":"Snap \"Say something!\" and stare at him until he answers","then":"Sunil flinches. \"I don't know what to say.\" The tension rises."}],"why":"Calling it a normal, shared phase turns a scary line into a project."},{"prompt":"The next day Sunil says he has felt lost too, but didn't know how to say it. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Thank him, and ask what he misses most about you two","then":"Sunil thinks, then says he misses your Sunday drives.","best":true},{"text":"Say \"So you agree we're failing, then\"","then":"Sunil looks stricken. \"That's not what I meant.\""},{"text":"Say \"Let's not dwell on it. We're fine now, really.\"","then":"Sunil nods, and the feeling goes back underground."},{"text":"Say it's his turn to work out how to fix all of it","then":"Sunil feels handed a job and no idea where to begin."}],"why":"Thanking him and asking what he misses moves the two of you from fear to specifics."},{"prompt":"You want to start building the new us. What do you try first?","options":[{"text":"Plan a big date with a sitter, and rush to make it perfect","then":"The plan is stressful, and the evening feels like a test."},{"text":"Wait until you both feel like your old selves again","then":"The wait drags on, and the old selves don't return by themselves."},{"text":"Pick one small thing you both enjoyed and do it for ten minutes","then":"You take a short walk to the corner shop, holding hands, and it feels like you.","best":true},{"text":"Make a long list of everything that has changed","then":"The list looks scary on paper, and nothing on it gets done."}],"why":"One small shared thing rebuilds the couple, without waiting or rushing."}],"debrief":"Name the 'who are we now' fear as a normal, shared phase, not a verdict to fear.","relearn":"The 'who are we now' feeling is normal; name it as a shared project, and don't read silence as the end."},
  {"id":"uk-1193","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"laughing comes back","hook":"At a family lunch, an aunt remarks that you two used to laugh so much, and it stings.","steps":[{"prompt":"On the way home the remark is still with you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Quietly decide the spark is gone for good","then":"The thought settles in, and your partner feels you drifting."},{"text":"Say it's the tired phase, and plan one light moment together","then":"Your partner smiles. \"Saturday, the old film? Let's do it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Tell each other it's just the tired phase, and drop it","then":"You both agree, but nothing changes, and no laughter is planned."},{"text":"Blame the aunt for saying it and stay angry all evening","then":"You spend the evening on her comment, not on each other."}],"why":"Naming the tired phase, and acting on it, keeps the laughter within reach."},{"prompt":"That weekend the baby does something funny and you both laugh for a second. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Stop laughing quickly and check the time","then":"The laugh vanishes, and the moment is gone before it counted."},{"text":"Think it was just luck and won't last","then":"The small warmth gets brushed away, and you feel wary again."},{"text":"Post the clip for the family to show you are happy","then":"The likes come, but the moment between you two slips by."},{"text":"Notice it out loud: \"There it is. We still laugh.\"","then":"Your partner grins. \"We do, don't we.\" The moment lasts a bit longer.","best":true}],"why":"Noticing a small laugh shows you the closeness is still there."},{"prompt":"A cousin asks how you keep laughing with a newborn. What do you honestly say?","options":[{"text":"Say it comes naturally to a good couple","then":"Your cousin looks worried. She wonders what is wrong with her."},{"text":"Say some days you don't, but small light moments help","then":"Your cousin nods. \"I'll try that. We've forgotten how.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you've given up on laughing for now, honestly","then":"Your cousin looks sad, and the talk ends there."},{"text":"Say the baby makes everything perfect and easy","then":"Your cousin nods politely, and no one says the real thing."}],"why":"Being honest that some days are hard and light moments help is real and helpful."}],"debrief":"An outsider noticing the tired phase isn't proof the spark is gone; name it as temporary.","relearn":"A comment about lost laughter marks the tired phase, not permanent loss; name it and add a light moment."},
  {"id":"uk-1194","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"2am reel","hook":"At 2am during a feed, you scroll a \"thriving new parents\" reel and feel like the only couple barely holding on.","steps":[{"prompt":"The next reel starts to play. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Remind yourself it's a highlight reel and put the phone down","then":"You lower the screen and watch your baby's tiny hand instead.","best":true},{"text":"Conclude that every other couple has it together but you","then":"The feeling settles in, and you sleep badly after the feed."},{"text":"Send the reel to your partner with \"why can't we be like this\"","then":"Your partner wakes to it and feels judged before the day even starts."},{"text":"Search for tips until dawn to fix everything at once","then":"The tips pile up, and you are more tired and more worried by morning."}],"why":"Seeing the reel as curated stops you measuring your night against someone's edit."},{"prompt":"In the morning your partner looks as tired as you feel. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing about the night and start the day","then":"The day starts polite and tense, and you feel a bit alone."},{"text":"Say \"We need to be more like those couples.\"","then":"Your partner hears a list of failings before their first tea."},{"text":"Say \"We're both running on empty. We're not the only ones.\"","then":"Your partner lets out a breath. \"Thank god. I thought it was just me.\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask why your partner isn't more cheerful with the baby","then":"Your partner stiffens. The question sounds like a complaint."}],"why":"Saying you're both running on empty makes the strain a shared load, not a failing."},{"prompt":"A friend says \"we're doing great!\" and then asks how you two are. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"It's hard some days, but we're getting through.\"","then":"Your friend's shoulders drop. \"Same. Thank you for saying it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"Amazing, just like you!\" and smile","then":"You both keep smiling, and each thinks the other has it easy."},{"text":"Say \"Terrible, we're a disaster\" and laugh weakly","then":"Your friend looks alarmed, and now you feel worse about it."},{"text":"Change the topic quickly to her baby's routine","then":"The talk moves on, and the honest part never happens."}],"why":"Being honest and kind about the hard days helps other couples stop pretending."}],"debrief":"Most couples are quietly struggling too; judge your night by your own progress, not a reel.","relearn":"The 'thriving parents' reel is curated; put it down and measure by your own progress."},
  {"id":"uk-1195","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"tired not distant","hook":"After the night feeds, you and Ravi barely speak, and you wonder if you are drifting apart.","steps":[{"prompt":"It is morning and neither of you has spoken. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide the silence means love has quietly died","then":"The thought grows, and you avoid his eyes across the table."},{"text":"Say \"We should talk sometime\" and leave it at that","then":"Ravi agrees, but no time is set, and \"sometime\" doesn't come."},{"text":"Stay quiet and wait for him to speak first","then":"The silence lasts through breakfast, and you both leave feeling alone."},{"text":"Say \"We're tired, not distant. Ten minutes tomorrow?\"","then":"Ravi nods slowly. \"Ten minutes. I'd like that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming tiredness and protecting a small slot fixes the load, not the love."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Ravi hands you a cup of tea without a word. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Assume it's routine and doesn't mean anything","then":"The tea goes cold, and you sit apart with your thoughts."},{"text":"Say thanks, squeeze his hand and read it as care","then":"Ravi smiles. \"You looked like you needed it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"A word would have been nice\"","then":"Ravi's smile fades, and he doesn't hand you tea tomorrow."},{"text":"Wonder what he wants from you in return","then":"You sip warily, and the kindness gets lost in suspicion."}],"why":"Reading a small gesture as care, and saying thanks, builds closeness again."},{"prompt":"Ravi says he has been worrying about the silence too. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"If you'd talked, this wouldn't have happened\"","then":"Ravi goes quiet, and now the worry is about blame."},{"text":"Say the silence isn't a big deal and he shouldn't worry","then":"Ravi nods, but feels his worry was brushed off."},{"text":"Suggest a big fix, like a whole weekend away with a sitter","then":"It sounds nice, but no one can plan it, and the worry stays."},{"text":"Say you're both tired and ask what small thing would help","then":"Ravi thinks. \"Sitting together while the baby sleeps, no phones.\"","best":true}],"why":"Sharing the worry and asking for one small thing keeps it about the two of you."}],"debrief":"Exhausted silence is usually the load, not lost love; name it and protect a small slot.","relearn":"Silence after night feeds is usually tiredness, not drifting; name it and protect a little time."},
  {"id":"uk-1196","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"exhausted thought","hook":"On a brutal day, you think \"maybe we just weren't meant to be parents together.\"","steps":[{"prompt":"The thought will not leave you. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take it as a sign and start pulling away from Diya","then":"Diya notices the distance, and the day turns into a rift."},{"text":"Say it out loud to Diya, as if it were a fact","then":"Diya goes white. Neither of you can take it back."},{"text":"Treat it as an exhausted thought and revisit it when rested","then":"The thought loosens a little once you stop arguing with it.","best":true},{"text":"Tell yourself to just stop thinking it, right now","then":"The thought comes back louder, and you feel worse."}],"why":"A worst-day thought is exhaustion talking, so hold it lightly and revisit it rested."},{"prompt":"That evening Diya sits down beside you and asks what's going on. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Bad day. Gloomy thoughts about us. Rest first?\"","then":"Diya squeezes your hand. \"We can. Tea, then bed.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"Nothing\" and go to the other room","then":"Diya hears the door and wonders what she did."},{"text":"Say \"Maybe this was all a mistake\"","then":"Diya looks stricken, and the night grows longer."},{"text":"Say her mood has been making things harder for you","then":"Diya stiffens, and the day ends in a quarrel about blame."}],"why":"Sharing the bad day, without acting on the thought, keeps you close."},{"prompt":"A few days on, a good morning arrives, and the gloomy thought about you two flickers back. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take it as proof the good morning isn't real","then":"The good morning sours, and you brood over your tea."},{"text":"Bring it up right away and demand answers","then":"Diya stares. \"Now? It was a good morning.\""},{"text":"Notice it, let it pass, and enjoy the morning","then":"The thought fades, and the tea tastes fine.","best":true},{"text":"Start planning what you would do if you split","then":"The plan takes over your day, and the morning is gone."}],"why":"Letting a thought pass without acting on it keeps a good morning from souring."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, you still feel flat and hopeless, even after rested nights. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Talk to Diya and a clinician, or call Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"A clinician listens without judging, and Diya comes along to the next visit.","best":true},{"text":"Hide it from everyone, since dads are meant to cope","then":"The weight stays, and you feel alone in your own home."},{"text":"Decide Diya is the cause, and pull back","then":"The distance grows, and the thoughts feel even truer."},{"text":"Wait for a long holiday to fix it all on its own","then":"The holiday helps for a day, and the thoughts return."}],"why":"Low mood that stays is real and treatable. Reaching out is strength, not weakness."}],"debrief":"A worst-day thought is exhaustion talking. If low mood or hopelessness stays, help is there: Tele-MANAS 14416.","relearn":"Brutal-day doom thoughts aren't verdicts; let them pass and judge the couple when you're rested."},
  {"id":"uk-1197","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Meena","source":"got on with it","hook":"When you mention the strain, your mother-in-law says \"In my day we just got on with it.\"","steps":[{"prompt":"You feel a flash of shame. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide you must be weak for struggling with what she handled","then":"The shame settles, and you say less to everyone."},{"text":"Snap that her generation had it easy compared to now","then":"She stiffens, and the room turns cold."},{"text":"Say \"I'm sure you felt it too. We're just talking about it now.\"","then":"Her face softens. \"I did. We never said it out loud.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"You're right\" and stop mentioning it at home","then":"The silence protects nobody, and the strain stays hidden."}],"why":"Honouring her without swallowing the strain makes room for both of you."},{"prompt":"Later, she offers to take the baby for the afternoon. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say yes warmly and use the time to rest with your partner","then":"She beams. You sleep for an hour, and it makes the evening lighter.","best":true},{"text":"Refuse, so she sees you can manage on your own","then":"You stay on your feet all afternoon, and she is quietly hurt."},{"text":"Accept, then complain the whole time she has the baby","then":"The help comes with friction, and neither of you enjoys it."},{"text":"Accept, and spend the free hours cleaning the house","then":"The house is spotless, and you are even more tired."}],"why":"Accepting help warmly, and using it to rest, honours her offer and your own need."},{"prompt":"Your partner Sanjay saw that his mother's remark hurt you, and wonders whether to speak to her. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Ask him to tell her that her generation was wrong","then":"Sanjay tells her, and she feels attacked. The house goes tense."},{"text":"Say never mind, and don't make trouble","then":"Sanjay lets it go, and you feel more alone with it."},{"text":"Ask him to back you warmly, and be kind to her too","then":"Sanjay nods. \"I can do that. She'll hear it from me.\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask him to stay out of it, so you can handle it alone","then":"Sanjay steps back, though he wanted to help."}],"why":"A warm word from your partner supports you without blaming his mother."},{"prompt":"A neighbour aunty says new mothers shouldn't complain. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"It's okay to say it's hard. It helps us cope.\"","then":"She blinks. \"Maybe you're right. We never said it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Nod and agree that you shouldn't complain at all","then":"You go home carrying the strain and the guilt."},{"text":"Tell her she's old-fashioned, and turn away","then":"She feels dismissed, and you feel bitter."},{"text":"Change the subject to the weather and leave it","then":"The moment passes, and nothing gets said."}],"why":"Saying it is okay to find it hard makes it easier to cope, and to ask for help."}],"debrief":"Older couples felt the strain too with no words for it; naming it now is a strength, not weakness.","relearn":"'We just got on with it' hid the same strain; talking about it now is a strength, not a failing."},
  {"id":"uk-1198","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Priya","source":"not your fate","hook":"A friend says her marriage \"fell apart after kids,\" and you start bracing for the same.","steps":[{"prompt":"Her words follow you home. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Remember one story isn't your fate, and learn what helps couples","then":"You look up a few habits, and the fear turns into a list.","best":true},{"text":"Decide yours is doomed the same way and stop trying","then":"The idea takes root, and you stop putting in effort."},{"text":"Ask her for every detail so you can avoid her mistakes exactly","then":"You get the whole story, and now you scan your own life for signs."},{"text":"Ask your partner to promise it will never happen to you two","then":"Kunal promises, but no one can, and you both feel the strain."}],"why":"One couple's hard ending isn't your forecast, and the skills that help are learnable."},{"prompt":"Later that week, Kunal says something still seems to be sitting between you. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Nothing,\" so you don't worry him","then":"Kunal lets it go, and the fear stays yours alone."},{"text":"Say you're sure you'll be fine, so there's no need to talk","then":"Kunal agrees, and the fear goes underground."},{"text":"Share the fear, and ask what you can each do to stay close","then":"Kunal takes your hand. \"Let's pick one thing each. I'm in.\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask Kunal whether he's secretly unhappy too","then":"Kunal is baffled and hurt, and he has no answer for the fear."}],"why":"Sharing the fear and picking one action each turns it into a team plan."},{"prompt":"A few weeks on, you catch up with the friend again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask how she is doing and listen without comparing","then":"She tears up. \"Nobody asked me that. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say her break-up taught you what not to do","then":"She goes quiet. Her story has become your lesson."},{"text":"Say she should have tried harder for the marriage","then":"She stiffens. She has heard this from everyone."},{"text":"Pull away so her luck won't rub off on you","then":"She feels dropped, and your fear quietly grows."}],"why":"Listening without comparing supports her, and keeps her story from becoming your fear."},{"prompt":"Kunal suggests learning simple habits for couples, like check-ins and quick sorries. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say couples who need habits are already in trouble","then":"The idea gets dropped, and the old fear stays put."},{"text":"Say you'll read about it someday when you have time","then":"Someday doesn't come, and the fear creeps back."},{"text":"Say yes, and try one small habit this week","then":"You both try a two-minute check-in, and it feels easy.","best":true},{"text":"Say habits feel fake, since love should be natural","then":"Kunal shrugs, and the chance to learn together passes."}],"why":"Skills like check-ins and quick sorries are learned, so trying one is a good start."}],"debrief":"One couple's hard ending isn't a forecast for yours; the protective skills are learnable.","relearn":"Another couple's break-up isn't your fate; the protective skills are learnable, so learn."},
  {"id":"uk-1199","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"co-parent to couple","hook":"You realise you have stopped seeing Arjun as your partner, only as a co-parent on shift.","steps":[{"prompt":"It has been weeks like this. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Name it gently, and pick one small thing that's just \"us\"","then":"Arjun looks relieved. \"I miss us too. What's one thing?\"","best":true},{"text":"Assume the lover part is gone now you're co-parents","then":"The idea settles in, and the couple fades by default."},{"text":"Wait until the baby is a lot older, then start rebuilding","then":"The wait has no end date, and the shifts continue."},{"text":"Tell Arjun he only ever talks about the baby now","then":"Arjun feels blamed for something you both slipped into."}],"why":"Naming it gently and protecting one 'us' thing rebuilds the couple alongside the team."},{"prompt":"The next evening, Arjun starts on the baby's nap times again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Join in the nap schedule talk for the whole evening","then":"The evening is efficient, and nothing between you changes."},{"text":"Snap that the baby is all you two ever talk about","then":"Arjun looks hurt, and the evening goes cold."},{"text":"Ask \"Can we have five minutes with no baby talk?\"","then":"Arjun blinks, then smiles. \"Five minutes. Go.\"","best":true},{"text":"Change the subject sharply to something random","then":"Arjun looks confused and takes a while to find footing."}],"why":"A friendly, time-limited ask makes room for the two of you inside the day."},{"prompt":"A rare free hour appears while the baby naps. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Spend part of it on \"us\": a walk, a chat, a hug","then":"Arjun takes your hand on the walk. It feels like before.","best":true},{"text":"Use it to plan next week's baby schedule together","then":"The plan is neat, and the hour is gone."},{"text":"Plan something elaborate to fill the whole hour","then":"The plan takes forty minutes to set up and leaves twenty."},{"text":"Scroll on separate sofas, too tired for anything else","then":"The hour passes quietly, and the distance stays."}],"why":"Using a small part of a free hour for 'us' keeps the couple alive."},{"prompt":"Arjun says he misses being partners, not just parents. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Laugh and say he's being dramatic about it","then":"Arjun smiles thinly, and doesn't bring it up again."},{"text":"Promise a big date night soon to make up for it all","then":"The date keeps slipping, and nothing small happens meanwhile."},{"text":"Say you miss it too, and ask what small thing feels good now","then":"Arjun thinks. \"A hug at the door when I come home.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say that's just how it is once you're co-parents","then":"Arjun accepts it, and the distance starts to feel permanent."}],"why":"Saying you miss it too, and choosing a small comfortable step, keeps closeness at your pace."}],"debrief":"Slipping into pure co-parent mode is common early; name it and protect one small 'us' moment.","relearn":"Becoming all co-parent and no couple is common early; name it and reclaim one small 'us' moment."},
  {"id":"uk-1200","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each post-baby worry to the calmer truth that fits it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"We fight more, so we're failing\"","right":"More friction is the load, not a verdict"},{"left":"\"Happy couples just got lucky\"","right":"They use learnable skills, not luck"},{"left":"\"I've lost who I was\"","right":"The identity shift is common and normal"},{"left":"\"Everyone else has it perfect\"","right":"Most are quietly struggling too"},{"left":"\"It'll be this hard forever\"","right":"It's steepest early, then it eases"}],"relearn":"Friction is load not failure, thriving is skills not luck, the shift is normal, others struggle too, and the dip eases.","persona":"any","source":"worry to truth"},
  {"id":"uk-1201","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each 'this is normal' fact to why it brings relief.","pairs":[{"left":"Two in three couples feel a dip","right":"You're not the broken exception"},{"left":"The strain is steepest in year one","right":"The hardest part has a horizon"},{"left":"Both partners feel changed","right":"Neither of you is the problem"},{"left":"It's workable as a team","right":"There's something you can actually do"},{"left":"Naming it removes the panic","right":"Words turn dread into a plan"}],"relearn":"Knowing the dip is common, time-limited, shared, workable and namable is what turns post-baby dread into calm.","persona":"any","source":"facts that relieve"},
  {"id":"uk-1202","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each part of the identity shift to a gentle, true way to hold it.","pairs":[{"left":"Missing your old freedom","right":"Grief for the old self is valid"},{"left":"Feeling proud yet lost","right":"Two true feelings can sit together"},{"left":"Not feeling like yourself","right":"Common early, and it settles"},{"left":"Fearing you've changed for good","right":"You can carry both selves forward"},{"left":"Guilt for not feeling only joy","right":"Mixed feelings aren't ingratitude"}],"relearn":"Missing freedom, feeling proud-yet-lost and mixed feelings are all normal parts of the parent identity shift.","persona":"Divya","source":"holding the shift"},
  {"id":"uk-1203","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each thriving-couple skill to what it protects against.","pairs":[{"left":"Sharing the load fairly","right":"Resentment building up silently"},{"left":"Repairing fast after a snap","right":"Small hurts piling into distance"},{"left":"Naming small appreciations","right":"Both feeling unseen and taken for granted"},{"left":"Assuming good intent","right":"Tired tone read as contempt"},{"left":"Pausing hard talks for later","right":"3am rows that solve nothing"}],"relearn":"Sharing, fast repair, appreciation, good-intent and well-timed talks are the learnable skills that protect the couple.","persona":"Vikram","source":"skill to shield"},
  {"id":"uk-1204","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each line you might hear to whether it's normal strain or a flag for help.","pairs":[{"left":"\"We're snapping more lately\"","right":"Normal strain, lean on skills"},{"left":"\"I've felt numb for weeks\"","right":"A flag, reach for support"},{"left":"\"We barely get couple time\"","right":"Normal strain, protect a little"},{"left":"\"I have thoughts that scare me\"","right":"A flag, call for help"},{"left":"\"I miss who I used to be\"","right":"Normal shift, hold both selves"}],"relearn":"Snapping, lost couple time and missing your old self are normal; weeks of numbness or scary thoughts are flags for help.","persona":"any","source":"strain or flag"},
  {"id":"uk-1205","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each unhelpful comparison to a fairer measure of your own couple.","pairs":[{"left":"Their calm online photos","right":"Your real, messy, tiring day"},{"left":"How fast they 'bounced back'","right":"Your own pace, which is valid"},{"left":"Their never-arguing image","right":"Your honest repairs after a snap"},{"left":"Their grand date nights","right":"Your small moments that count"},{"left":"Their 'perfect' baby routine","right":"What actually works for your family"}],"relearn":"Judge your couple by your real day, your own pace, honest repairs and small moments, not anyone's polished image.","persona":"any","source":"fairer measure"},
  {"id":"uk-1206","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"match","hook":"Match each early-parent moment to the reassuring reframe that fits.","pairs":[{"left":"You cried in the bathroom again","right":"A release valve, not a breakdown"},{"left":"You forgot your partner's birthday","right":"Sleep-loss, not a love problem"},{"left":"You felt jealous of your child-free friends","right":"Honest, not a sign of regret"},{"left":"You wished for one normal evening","right":"A fair need, not selfish"},{"left":"You doubted you're cut out for this","right":"A tired thought, not the truth"}],"relearn":"Bathroom tears, forgetfulness, envy, wanting a normal evening and self-doubt are common early; none is a verdict.","persona":"Divya","source":"moment to reframe"},
  {"id":"uk-1207","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the dip after a baby is something most couples feel, not a private flaw of yours. Land okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands okay","Most couples, then","Good to hear"],"affirm":"The post-baby dip is something most couples meet, not a private flaw; you're in good, ordinary company.","relearn":"The post-baby dip is common to most couples, not a private flaw of yours.","persona":"any","source":"not a private flaw"},
  {"id":"uk-1208","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you didn't pick the wrong partner, you just hit a hard, shared season. Sit right?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Sits right","A hard season","Not the wrong one"],"affirm":"A hard season after a baby is not the same as a wrong partner; the season passes, the choice stands.","relearn":"A hard post-baby season isn't proof you chose the wrong partner; it's a shared phase that passes.","persona":"Vikram","source":"hard season"},
  {"id":"uk-1209","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: grieving the person you were before is allowed, even while you love your baby. Feel okay?","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["Feels okay","I'm allowed to","Both at once"],"affirm":"You can grieve your old self and love your baby in the same breath; the grief isn't ingratitude.","relearn":"Grieving your pre-baby self is allowed and normal, even while you deeply love your child.","persona":"Divya","source":"grief allowed"},
  {"id":"uk-1210","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you two aren't drifting apart, you're rowing the same hard stretch of river. Ring true?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Rings true","Same stretch","Not drifting"],"affirm":"You're not drifting apart; you're both pulling through the same hard stretch, which is a thing you do together.","relearn":"The tired distance isn't drifting apart; you're both crossing the same hard stretch together.","persona":"any","source":"same river"},
  {"id":"uk-1211","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the goal isn't the old couple back, it's a new us that fits this life. Sit okay?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Sits okay","A new us","Forward not back"],"affirm":"You're not failing to get the old couple back; you're building a new us that fits the life you have now.","relearn":"The aim isn't restoring the old couple but building a new us that fits parenthood.","persona":"any","source":"a new us"},
  {"id":"uk-1212","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: every couple online is showing their best frame, not their 3am. Worth remembering?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Worth it","Just the best frame","Not their 3am"],"affirm":"What you see online is a best frame, never the 3am; comparing your whole night to it was never fair.","relearn":"Other couples show their best frame online, not their hard nights; the comparison was never fair.","persona":"any","source":"best frame"},
  {"id":"uk-1213","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: feeling changed by becoming a parent doesn't mean you lost yourself, just that you grew. Land?","prompt":"How does it land?","options":["It lands","Grew, not lost","Good to frame"],"affirm":"Feeling changed by parenthood is growth, not loss; the self you were is still in there, alongside the new.","relearn":"Feeling changed by parenthood is growth, not losing yourself; you carry both the old and new self.","persona":"Divya","source":"grew not lost"},
  {"id":"uk-1214","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: the strain is something you can work on, not a sentence you just serve. Feel it?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Feel it","Workable","Not a sentence"],"affirm":"The post-baby strain is workable with skills and time, not a fixed sentence you simply have to serve.","relearn":"The strain is workable, not a sentence; skills and time can shift it.","persona":"any","source":"not a sentence"},
  {"id":"uk-1215","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: both of you are carrying this, even if you each show the weight differently. Ring true?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Rings true","Both of us","Just shown differently"],"affirm":"You're both carrying the load, even when one shows it loudly and the other goes quiet; neither is the problem.","relearn":"Both partners carry the post-baby load, even when they show the weight differently.","persona":"any","source":"both carrying"},
  {"id":"uk-1216","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: a hard first year doesn't predict a hard forever; the dip is steepest now. Sit right?","prompt":"How does that sit?","options":["Sits right","Steepest now","Not forever"],"affirm":"A hard first year is the steep part of a known dip, not a forecast of forever; the slope eases.","relearn":"A hard first year is the steepest part of the dip, not a prediction of forever.","persona":"Priya","source":"not forever"},
  {"id":"uk-1217","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: needing help with this doesn't mean you're failing at it, it means you're human. Land okay?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Lands okay","Just human","Not failing"],"affirm":"Reaching for help with the post-baby strain is a human, sensible move, not a sign you're failing at parenthood.","relearn":"Needing help through the post-baby strain is human and sensible, not failing.","persona":"Divya","source":"just human"},
  {"id":"uk-1218","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that turn a normal hard patch into a doom story.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"This is hard right now, and that's expected\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"If it's this hard, we must be broken\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"We can get through this as a team\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"It's too late for us to fix this\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"We're tired, not finished\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"We must be broken\" and \"too late to fix this\" are doom stories; a hard patch is the load, not a verdict.","relearn":"Catch the doom lines: a hard patch means you're under load, not broken or too late to fix.","persona":"any","source":"doom story lines"},
  {"id":"uk-1219","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two myths hiding among the truths about the post-baby dip.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Most couples feel a dip after a first child","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"The happy couples just got an easy baby","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"The strain is the load, not a lack of love","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"A real couple wouldn't struggle at all","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"It's steepest early, then it eases","trick":false}],"why":"\"Just got an easy baby\" and \"a real couple wouldn't struggle\" are myths; thriving is skills and the dip is common.","relearn":"Spot the myths: thriving is learnable skill not an easy baby, and struggling doesn't mean you're not a real couple.","persona":"Priya","source":"myths among truths"},
  {"id":"uk-1220","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two thoughts that quietly compare you to a fake perfect couple.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Our messy day is the real measure\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Everyone else has this figured out\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Most couples are struggling quietly too\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"We're the only ones barely coping\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Their feed is a highlight reel\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"Everyone else has it figured out\" and \"we're the only ones\" are unfair comparisons to a curated image.","relearn":"Catch the comparison thoughts: 'everyone else copes, we're the only ones' measures you against a fake reel.","persona":"any","source":"compare thoughts"},
  {"id":"uk-1221","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two ideas that confuse normal strain with something being truly wrong.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Snapping when sleep-starved is normal","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Feeling unsafe is just part of new-parent stress","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Missing couple time is part of adjusting","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Being controlled is normal for tired couples","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Out-of-sync days settle as you adapt","trick":false}],"why":"\"Feeling unsafe\" and \"being controlled\" are not normal strain; control and fear are abuse, not the load.","relearn":"Don't file fear or control under normal strain; snapping and missing time adjust, but control needs help.","persona":"any","source":"strain vs wrong"},
  {"id":"uk-1222","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two things that say 'reach for support', not just 'ride out the tiredness'.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Crying from exhaustion some evenings","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Hopelessness that hasn't lifted in weeks","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling frazzled on the worst days","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Thoughts about harm that frighten you","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Short fuse when you're running on no sleep","trick":false}],"why":"Weeks-long hopelessness and frightening thoughts are flags for support, not tiredness to simply ride out.","relearn":"Tired tears and a short fuse pass; weeks of hopelessness or frightening thoughts mean reach for support.","persona":"Divya","source":"ride out or support"},
  {"id":"uk-1223","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two reactions that make the post-baby shift heavier instead of lighter.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Naming it as a normal phase together","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Each blaming the other for the strain","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Being patient with yourselves","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Bottling it all up and going silent","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Checking in on how you're both doing","trick":false}],"why":"Blaming each other and bottling it up in silence both add weight; naming, patience and check-ins lighten it.","relearn":"Avoid blame and bottled silence; naming the phase, patience and checking in are what lighten the shift.","persona":"Vikram","source":"heavier or lighter"},
  {"id":"uk-1224","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two pressures that pile guilt onto an already-tired new parent.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"It's okay to find this hard\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You should cherish every single moment\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"Resting isn't failing\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"A good parent never feels this way\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Asking for help is allowed\"","trick":false}],"why":"\"Cherish every moment\" and \"a good parent never feels this way\" pile guilt; both deepen an exhausted parent's shame.","relearn":"Catch the guilt-pilers: 'cherish every moment' and 'a good parent never feels this' shame a tired parent.","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt pressures"},
  {"id":"uk-1225","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"buried not lost","hook":"Since the baby came, evenings with your partner Zoya have been all feeds and logistics.","setup":"Tonight you are folding the laundry together in silence.","steps":[{"prompt":"Zoya says: \"I feel like we've completely lost each other.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yeah... maybe this is just how it is with kids.\"","then":"She looks down. \"I was hoping you'd say something else.\""},{"text":"\"Don't say that. We're fine. It's only tiredness.\"","then":"She nods, and the fear goes back inside."},{"text":"\"Then what do you want me to do about it, exactly?\"","then":"She flinches. It sounds like a complaint, not a worry."},{"text":"\"We're not lost. We're buried in load. Let's dig out.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Buried. Yes. That feels truer.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming it as buried under load, not lost, gives you a shared next step."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Zoya says: \"I miss talking about things that aren't the baby.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We can talk about other things once the baby is older.\"","then":"She nods, and the wait feels long."},{"text":"\"Me too. One no-baby topic a day, even five minutes?\"","then":"She grins. \"Today is films. I'll start.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"There's nothing else to talk about right now.\"","then":"She looks stricken, and you both fall silent."},{"text":"\"Then you pick a topic and I'll listen to you, fine.\"","then":"She sighs. It's up to her again."}],"why":"A small no-baby topic each day rebuilds the talk you miss, without waiting."},{"prompt":"One night, Zoya says: \"I'm scared I'm the only one who feels this.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe you're overthinking it a bit, honestly.\"","then":"She pulls back. The fear feels foolish now."},{"text":"\"Everyone feels it. Don't make it a big thing.\"","then":"She nods, and says no more."},{"text":"\"If you're scared, talk to your friends about it.\"","then":"She looks hurt. She wanted to talk to you."},{"text":"\"You're not. I feel it too. I just didn't say.\"","then":"She takes your hand. \"Thank you. I needed that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying you feel it too tells her she isn't alone."},{"prompt":"A week later, Zoya says: \"I felt closer to you tonight than I have in a while.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good. So now we can go back to how we were.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"I don't think we go back.\""},{"text":"\"Me too. Let's keep these small moments, no pressure.\"","then":"She smiles. \"No pressure. Just more of tonight.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Finally. Let's not waste it. Let's make plans.\"","then":"She stiffens. Plans sound like pressure, and the moment cools."},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about it. We might jinx it all.\"","then":"She nods, and the warmth stays unspoken."}],"why":"Welcoming small moments, without pressure, lets closeness grow at your pace."}],"relearn":"Naming it as 'buried under load, not lost' and offering to dig out together replaces dread with a shared next step."},
  {"id":"uk-1226","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"different speeds","hook":"Your friend Imran became a dad a month ago, and he seems unsettled.","setup":"You catch him alone by the gate after a long night.","steps":[{"prompt":"Imran says: \"I don't feel transformed like they say. Am I doing this wrong?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It lands at different speeds. Slow isn't less love.\"","then":"He lets out a long breath. \"So it's not just me. I was scared to say it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yeah. You're probably just not the fatherly type.\"","then":"He goes quiet. \"Right. Maybe not.\""},{"text":"\"Don't be silly. Every dad feels a rush at once.\"","then":"He nods, and hides that he didn't."},{"text":"\"Just fake it until it comes. Nobody will notice.\"","then":"He nods, and the feeling stays hidden."}],"why":"Saying the shift lands at different speeds frees him from someone else's timeline."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Imran says: \"My wife bonded so fast. I feel behind.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's mums for you. Let her handle most of it, really.\"","then":"He shrugs, and the gap between them widens."},{"text":"\"You're the provider. Earning is your way of bonding.\"","then":"He nods, and stays on the edge of it all."},{"text":"\"Bonding grows with doing. Own one routine, like the bath.\"","then":"He grins. \"The evening bath. Mine, start to finish.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Compare less. You'll be fine. It sorts itself out.\"","then":"He nods, and nothing about the evenings changes."}],"why":"Owning a whole routine, as an equal parent, grows the bond, and earning doesn't replace care."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Imran says: \"I picked her up and she stopped crying. I felt something.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's real. Keep showing up. You're an equal parent.\"","then":"He beams. \"Equal. I like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"See? It comes on its own. Now you can relax.\"","then":"He nods, and quietly eases off."},{"text":"\"Good. Now feel that every time, and it gets easy.\"","then":"He nods, then worries when the next cry brings nothing."},{"text":"\"Nice. Your wife must be relieved you finally got there.\"","then":"He winces at \"finally\", and the moment shrinks."}],"why":"Noticing the real moment, and staying involved, builds on it."},{"prompt":"A month on, Imran asks: \"Should I tell my wife I've felt behind and slow to bond?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No. Keep it to yourself. Dads don't complain.\"","then":"He nods, and carries it quietly."},{"text":"\"Tell her only once you feel better about it.\"","then":"He waits. The feeling doesn't fade by itself."},{"text":"\"Yes. She would want to know. Say it plainly.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Tonight, then. Plainly.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Tell her, but blame the lack of sleep for it.\"","then":"He does, and the real thing stays unsaid."}],"why":"Telling his wife plainly shares the feeling, so the two of them can face it together."}],"relearn":"Telling him the identity shift comes at different speeds frees him from judging himself by someone else's timeline."},
  {"id":"uk-1227","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"miss who i was","hook":"Your sister Nandini, a new mum, feels like a different person.","setup":"She whispers it in the kitchen, checking no one is near.","steps":[{"prompt":"Nandini whispers: \"I love her, but I miss who I was. I feel awful for it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Missing your old self isn't ingratitude. Both are real.\"","then":"Her eyes fill. \"Thank you. I thought only I felt it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You shouldn't think like that. Just focus on the baby.\"","then":"She nods, and swallows it back down."},{"text":"\"Don't tell anyone that. They'll think you're ungrateful.\"","then":"She nods, and locks it away again."},{"text":"\"Everyone says these are the best days. Enjoy them.\"","then":"She forces a smile. The guilt gets heavier."}],"why":"Loving her baby and grieving her old self can both be true, and it's never ingratitude."},{"prompt":"A few days later, Nandini says: \"I used to have my work and my friends. Now it's all feeds.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's only temporary. It will pass before you know it.\"","then":"She nods, but doesn't feel heard."},{"text":"\"Your baby is your work now. It should be enough.\"","then":"She goes quiet. It isn't enough, and she feels worse."},{"text":"\"That's a real loss. What's one piece of you to bring back?\"","then":"She thinks. \"Coffee with Meenal. Just an hour.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Other women manage. You'll get used to it soon enough.\"","then":"She nods, and the comparison stings."}],"why":"Naming the loss, and bringing back one small piece of herself, keeps her whole."},{"prompt":"Later, Nandini says: \"I feel guilty even asking for an hour to myself.\"","options":[{"text":"\"An hour for you isn't selfish. Let's plan it.\"","then":"She lets out a breath. \"Thursday, then. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then don't ask. The baby needs you more right now.\"","then":"She nods, and drops the idea."},{"text":"\"Ask your mother-in-law. Don't burden me with it.\"","then":"She winces. She wanted your support, not a redirect."},{"text":"\"Only if the house is clean and the baby fed first.\"","then":"She sighs. The hour vanishes under conditions."}],"why":"Protecting an hour for herself keeps her steadier, and it isn't selfish."},{"prompt":"A week later, Nandini says: \"Some days I feel numb, and it scares me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Everyone feels numb sometimes. Just ignore it and carry on.\"","then":"She nods, and the numbness settles in."},{"text":"\"Don't say numb. Say tired. It sounds less worrying.\"","then":"She nods, and hides it from everyone."},{"text":"\"I'm glad you told me. See a clinician or call Tele-MANAS 14416.\"","then":"Her breath shakes. \"Okay. I'll call. Will you sit with me?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You have a healthy baby, so you have no reason for that.\"","then":"She goes quiet, and the shame lands on top."}],"why":"Numbness after birth is real, treatable and never her fault, so telling someone is a strong step."},{"prompt":"Weeks on, Nandini says: \"Some days are better. But some are still very heavy.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's real. Shall we see a clinician together this week?\"","then":"She squeezes your hand. \"I'd like that. I'm ready.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then you must be doing something wrong, somehow.\"","then":"She flinches. Now she feels she is failing at getting better too."},{"text":"\"Keep going. Heavy days pass if you stay busy.\"","then":"She nods, and fills the days. The heaviness stays."},{"text":"\"At least you have good days. Many mums don't.\"","then":"She looks down, ashamed of wanting more."}],"why":"Heavy days that stay call for support, and you can go along with her."}],"relearn":"Naming that she can love her baby and grieve her old self at once lifts the guilt off a normal feeling."},
  {"id":"uk-1228","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"Priya","source":"barely surviving","hook":"Your friend Sonal, a new mum, has been scrolling other parents' posts all week.","setup":"She holds her phone out across the table.","steps":[{"prompt":"Sonal says: \"Everyone looks like they're thriving. We're barely surviving.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Hmm. Maybe they really are just better at this.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Yeah. That's what I thought.\""},{"text":"\"That's a highlight reel. Most of us are surviving too.\"","then":"She laughs shakily. \"Most of us? You too? That's a relief.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ha. Their real lives must be a mess behind that.\"","then":"She half laughs. It feels mean, and the envy stays."},{"text":"\"Just stop following them. Then you'll feel fine again.\"","then":"She nods. It sounds simpler than it is."}],"why":"Seeing it's a highlight reel, and that most parents struggle too, lifts an unfair comparison."},{"prompt":"Later, Sonal says: \"I compared our bad night to her perfect Sunday. I hated myself.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then don't look at Sundays. Look at weekdays only.\"","then":"She frowns. It doesn't fix the comparing."},{"text":"\"You're right to feel bad. You should do better than that.\"","then":"She goes quiet. The shame gets heavier."},{"text":"\"She's probably faking it. Message her and call it out.\"","then":"She hesitates. It would only start a fight."},{"text":"\"That's unfair to you. Compare with your own last week.\"","then":"She thinks. \"Last week was worse. Okay. We're better.\"","best":true}],"why":"Comparing with your own past shows your real progress, not someone's best day."},{"prompt":"Sonal asks: \"Would it help to tell people how it really is?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Post it publicly so everyone knows how it really is.\"","then":"She hesitates. It sounds exposed and exhausting."},{"text":"\"With one or two you trust. It often lets them say it back.\"","then":"She nods. \"Okay. I'll start with my cousin.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No. Keep the hard parts to yourself. It's safer that way.\"","then":"She nods, and the loneliness stays."},{"text":"\"Only tell them the nice parts. That's kinder to everyone.\"","then":"She nods, and the feed stays glossy for everyone."}],"why":"Telling one or two people you trust makes it easier for them to be honest too."}],"relearn":"Reminding her the feed is curated and most couples are quietly struggling lifts the unfair comparison weight."},
  {"id":"uk-1229","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"new us not old","hook":"Your partner Rahul has been flicking through old photos from before the baby.","setup":"The baby has just gone down and the flat is quiet.","steps":[{"prompt":"Rahul says: \"What if we never get back to how we were?\"","options":[{"text":"\"We won't go back. We'll grow into a new us.\"","then":"He almost smiles. \"A new us. I can work with that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't know... maybe the old us is just gone.\"","then":"He looks away. \"That's what I was afraid of.\""},{"text":"\"Of course we will. Nothing has really changed.\"","then":"He nods, but doesn't believe it."},{"text":"\"Don't be dramatic. It's only a passing phase, really.\"","then":"He goes quiet, and the worry goes underground."}],"why":"Framing it as building a new us, not failing to return, points you forward."},{"prompt":"The next day, Rahul asks: \"If we can't go back, what do we want now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just co-parents, I suppose. That's what we are now.\"","then":"His face falls. \"Is that all?\""},{"text":"\"Same as before, only more tired all the time.\"","then":"He gives a weak smile. It doesn't help."},{"text":"\"Different, and ours. Let's each name one thing to keep.\"","then":"He thinks. \"Sunday mornings. And you laughing at my jokes.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I don't know. You decide, and I'll go along.\"","then":"He shrugs. It's a lot to decide alone."}],"why":"Naming what each of you wants to keep starts the new us on real things."},{"prompt":"Later, Rahul says: \"I keep missing the old us anyway.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's okay. We can miss it and still build the new one.\"","then":"He nods slowly. \"Both. Yes. I can do both.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then stop missing it. It won't do us any good at all.\"","then":"He swallows the feeling, and it stays."},{"text":"\"Then we should just try hard to go back to it.\"","then":"He tries. It feels forced, and the old us can't be recreated."},{"text":"\"Missing it means that we're failing at all of this.\"","then":"He pales. Now the missing feels like a fault."}],"why":"Missing the old us is normal, and you can grieve it while building the new one."},{"prompt":"That weekend, Rahul asks: \"Do you think it's normal to feel this?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm not sure. Maybe we shouldn't ask anyone at all.\"","then":"He nods, and the doubt stays."},{"text":"\"We're already failing, so no. It isn't normal.\"","then":"He sinks. The hope goes out of him."},{"text":"\"Very normal. And workable. Let's try one small thing.\"","then":"He nods. \"One small thing. A walk tomorrow?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't overthink it. It will fix itself in time.\"","then":"He nods, and waits for it to fix itself."}],"why":"Knowing it's normal and workable, and trying one small step, replaces dread with action."}],"relearn":"Framing it as building a new us, not failing to return, points the couple forward instead of mourning the past."},
  {"id":"uk-1230","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"decline the shame","hook":"You and your partner have had a rough few months, and word has reached your uncle.","setup":"Over tea, he shakes his head at you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your uncle says: \"A good couple wouldn't be struggling like this.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Most couples dip after a baby. We're not a bad couple.\"","then":"He blinks. \"Hm. I hadn't thought of it that way.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. We should be handling this much better.\"","then":"He nods, satisfied, and you sit there carrying the shame."},{"text":"\"Uncle, keep your nose out of it. Nobody asked you.\"","then":"He bristles. \"I was only trying to help.\""},{"text":"\"We're fine, Uncle. There's nothing to worry about.\"","then":"He nods, unconvinced, and the subject stays raw."}],"why":"Holding that the dip is common lets you decline a relative's shame without a fight."},{"prompt":"Later, your uncle says: \"In my time, nobody spoke about such things.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then maybe we should keep quiet about it too.\"","then":"He nods, and you take the silence home."},{"text":"\"Your time was different. You wouldn't understand.\"","then":"He stiffens. The warmth in the room drops."},{"text":"\"I'm sure it was hard for you too. We talk to ease it.\"","then":"He looks thoughtful. \"Maybe it was. We never said.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fair enough. I'll stop telling you about anything.\"","then":"He shrugs. A door closes a little."}],"why":"Honouring his time while explaining yours keeps the talk kind and honest."},{"prompt":"Later that night, your partner Alka asks: \"What did your uncle say? You looked upset.\"","options":[{"text":"\"He said we're a bad couple. I don't believe it.\"","then":"She exhales. \"Good. Neither do I. Let's talk about us.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing. Forget it. It's not worth repeating.\"","then":"She nods, and wonders what was said."},{"text":"\"Just family noise. Anyway, you've been so cross lately.\"","then":"Her face falls. Now the row is about her."},{"text":"\"He's right. We're failing. I've been thinking so too.\"","then":"She goes pale. The dip has become a verdict."}],"why":"Sharing what he said, and what you think, keeps the two of you on one side."},{"prompt":"A month later, your aunt asks: \"I hear you two are still struggling. Is it true?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, everything is perfect. We're both very happy indeed.\"","then":"She smiles, unconvinced. You feel like a fraud."},{"text":"\"Yes. We're a mess. I don't know what we'll do.\"","then":"She frowns, worried, and starts offering advice you didn't ask for."},{"text":"\"It's been hard, like for most new parents. The details are ours.\"","then":"She nods. \"Fair enough. Ask if you need help.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Why does everyone keep asking us about our marriage?\"","then":"She flinches. \"I only cared.\""}],"why":"Being honest and steady, without shame, lets family help instead of judge."}],"relearn":"Holding that the dip is common, not a verdict, lets you decline a relative's shame instead of absorbing it."},
  {"id":"uk-1231","cat":"the-big-shift","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"not tonight","hook":"It is a brutal, sleepless night, and your partner Nikhil is at breaking point.","setup":"He sits on the edge of the bed in the dark.","steps":[{"prompt":"Nikhil says: \"I keep thinking we made a mistake.\"","options":[{"text":"\"That's exhaustion talking. Let's not decide tonight.\"","then":"He rubs his face. \"Okay. Yes. Tonight isn't the night.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe you're right. Maybe we did rush into this.\"","then":"He goes still. The doubt gets heavier."},{"text":"\"Don't you dare say that! Take it back right now.\"","then":"He flinches. The thought goes quiet, but not away."},{"text":"\"Then say what you actually want. Tell me now.\"","then":"He stares at his hands. He has no answer at this hour."}],"why":"Naming it as exhaustion and pausing any decision keeps a bad night from becoming a rift."},{"prompt":"In the morning, Nikhil says: \"About last night. I'm sorry I said that.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You scared me. Don't ever say things like that.\"","then":"He nods, and looks away, unsure it's safe to say such things."},{"text":"\"It's fine. Forget it. Let's pretend it never happened.\"","then":"He nods, and the doubt stays unspoken."},{"text":"\"I'm glad you told me. Let's revisit it when we've slept.\"","then":"He nods. \"Thank you. I didn't want to hide it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I've been thinking it too, actually. Maybe we did.\"","then":"He looks at you, shaken. The doubt now has company."}],"why":"Thanking him for telling you keeps it safe to say hard things, and you can revisit it rested."},{"prompt":"A week later, Nikhil hesitates, then says: \"The thought is back. I feel flat and hopeless, and it scares me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'm glad you said. See a clinician or call Tele-MANAS 14416.\"","then":"His shoulders drop. \"Okay. I'll call. Come with me?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just try to think about something else. That usually works.\"","then":"He tries. The thought is back by evening."},{"text":"\"It means something. You aren't sure about us, are you?\"","then":"He goes pale. The fear turns into a fight."},{"text":"\"Every dad thinks that at times. Just cope and carry on.\"","then":"He nods, and carries it alone."}],"why":"Low mood and hopelessness that return are real and treatable, and asking for help is never a failing."}],"relearn":"Naming a worst-night doubt as exhaustion, and choosing not to decide while depleted, keeps a bad night from becoming a rift."},
  {"id":"uk-1260","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"phone-free ten","hook":"You and Pooja sit side by side every night, each scrolling on your own phone.","steps":[{"prompt":"You want one habit that sticks. What do you try?","options":[{"text":"Promise a long heart-to-heart this coming weekend","then":"The weekend fills up, and the big talk keeps slipping."},{"text":"Ten phone-free minutes each night, just to talk","then":"Pooja slides her phone under a cushion. \"Ten minutes. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"Send her funny reels, hoping she talks back","then":"She laughs and sends one back, but you stay parallel."},{"text":"Decide you're too tired and keep scrolling","then":"The distance widens with nobody choosing it."}],"why":"A tiny repeatable window works where a postponed big talk doesn't."},{"prompt":"A few nights on, you put your phone down but Pooja is still on hers. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Take the phone gently out of her hand","then":"Pooja snatches it back. \"Don't do that.\""},{"text":"Sigh loudly and pick your own phone back up","then":"You scroll in stereo, and the habit fizzles."},{"text":"Tell her she seems to care more about the phone than you","then":"Pooja bristles, and the evening is lost to a quarrel."},{"text":"Say \"Ten minutes with me? Then scroll all you like.\"","then":"Pooja smiles and turns her phone face down. \"Fair.\"","best":true}],"why":"A friendly, time-limited ask invites her in, without grabbing or blaming."},{"prompt":"Pooja says she misses just laughing together, not only planning. What do you suggest?","options":[{"text":"Say laughing can wait until the baby is older","then":"Pooja nods, but the evenings feel like shifts, not company."},{"text":"Add one light thing, like a funny story or a song","then":"Pooja laughs. \"Okay, my turn to pick the song.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say logistics matter most for now","then":"Pooja goes quiet. The plans get made, and the joy waits."},{"text":"Block out an hour a night just for fun talk","then":"The hour lasts two nights, and then it feels like homework."}],"why":"A small light moment inside the routine keeps warmth alive, and you can actually keep it up."}],"debrief":"Ten phone-free minutes sticks where a postponed grand talk never lands.","relearn":"A short phone-free moment each evening beats a long catch-up you keep postponing."},
  {"id":"uk-1261","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"tea ritual","hook":"There is never any free time as new parents, and you and Dev want closeness built into the day.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you try?","options":[{"text":"Make morning chai a two-minute \"us\" ritual, no logistics","then":"Dev grins over his cup. \"Two minutes. No lists.\" It feels good.","best":true},{"text":"Wait until you can afford a sitter and a night out","then":"The sitter plan keeps slipping, and the wait stretches on."},{"text":"Plan one big anniversary weekend to make up for it","then":"The weekend is lovely to plan, but it is months away."},{"text":"Do chores side by side and count it as time together","then":"You get the chores done, but no one asks how the other is."}],"why":"A tiny ritual tied to something you already do survives a busy day."},{"prompt":"One morning the baby wakes early, and every spare minute you and Dev had is gone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Skip time together, and promise double tomorrow","then":"Tomorrow has its own chaos, and the double never happens."},{"text":"Decide closeness just doesn't fit this stage of life","then":"Closeness is shelved, and the mornings are all logistics. Dev misses it, though he hasn't said so."},{"text":"Take one minute anyway: a look, a hug, a kind line","then":"Dev squeezes your hand. It was over in a minute, and it counted.","best":true},{"text":"Save it for bedtime, when you are both wrecked","then":"At bedtime you are both too flat to talk, and it feels forced."}],"why":"Shrinking closeness to one minute, not dropping it, keeps it alive on hard days."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, Dev says he misses feeling close, and wants it to feel natural, not forced. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Pick one daily moment, and add surprises like a note","then":"Dev smiles. \"A note in my bag. I'd like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"Drop routines and wait for natural moments to appear","then":"The moments don't appear, and weeks pass without any."},{"text":"Add more scheduled time so it feels serious","then":"The calendar fills, and the warmth gets squeezed out."},{"text":"Say natural moments are a luxury you can't afford now","then":"Dev feels his idea was brushed off, and stops raising it."}],"why":"One steady daily moment plus small surprises stays warm without becoming a chore."}],"debrief":"Hooking connection onto the morning chai makes it sustainable; grand plans wait forever.","relearn":"A tiny fixed ritual, like chai with no logistics, protects connection better than waiting for free time."},
  {"id":"uk-1262","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"which tool","hook":"Nisha snapped at you at 2am over a bottle, and the morning feels awkward.","steps":[{"prompt":"The snap still hangs in the air. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Give her the silent treatment so she learns","then":"The silence spreads through the flat, and Nisha feels punished."},{"text":"Keep a tally of who has snapped more this week","then":"Nisha hears the count, and the tension goes up, not down."},{"text":"Wait politely for Nisha to apologise before you speak","then":"She waits too. The breakfast is polite and cold."},{"text":"Say \"Last night was the tiredness, not us.\"","then":"Nisha's eyes fill. \"I'm sorry. I hated snapping at you.\"","best":true}],"why":"A quick, kind repair treats the tiredness as the problem, not each other."},{"prompt":"A few days later Nisha snaps again over something small. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Snap right back, at the same volume as her","then":"The volume climbs, and now there are two tired people shouting."},{"text":"Take a breath and say \"Tired snap. Water for both?\"","then":"Nisha lets out a shaky laugh and drinks. The moment passes.","best":true},{"text":"Tell her calmly that her tone was unacceptable","then":"Nisha goes quiet, corrected but not repaired."},{"text":"Say she has been like this ever since the baby","then":"Nisha feels judged, and she turns her face away."}],"why":"Assuming a tired snap is tiredness lets both of you calm down."},{"prompt":"You want a tool for tired snaps that you both can use. What do you agree?","options":[{"text":"A points chart of who has snapped and how often","then":"The chart makes every snap a scoreboard entry."},{"text":"A rule that nobody talks after eleven at night","then":"Silence after eleven helps, but the morning snaps stay unaddressed."},{"text":"A promise that neither of you will ever snap again","then":"The first snap breaks the promise, and you both feel worse."},{"text":"A shared phrase, and a fast sorry from either of you","then":"Nisha nods. \"Okay. We say 'tired snap', and we say sorry.\"","best":true}],"why":"A shared phrase and a fast sorry give you a tool that works for both of you."}],"debrief":"A tired snap is fixed by quick repair, not silent treatment or a scorecard.","relearn":"Match the moment to the tool: a tired snap needs quick repair, not a scorecard or silence."},
  {"id":"uk-1263","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"strain or worse","hook":"Your friend Rukhsar says her partner checks her phone and decides who she can see.","steps":[{"prompt":"She looks worried and a little ashamed. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say every couple struggles and she should just share more","then":"Rukhsar nods and says nothing more. She feels even more alone."},{"text":"Say she has to leave him right now, tonight","then":"Rukhsar looks frightened and stops answering your messages."},{"text":"Say \"That's control, not stress. It isn't your fault.\"","then":"Rukhsar's eyes fill. \"I thought I was just being oversensitive.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll talk to her partner yourself, and soon","then":"Rukhsar panics and begs you not to. She fears it will make things worse."}],"why":"Naming control and saying it isn't her fault believes her, without pushing her."},{"prompt":"Days later, Rukhsar messages late at night. She wonders if she is overreacting. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"You're not overreacting. I believe you. Your pace.\"","then":"Rukhsar replies with a long message, then a shaky \"thank you\".","best":true},{"text":"Say maybe he is just stressed and it will pass","then":"Rukhsar agrees, and her fear goes back into hiding."},{"text":"Say she should have left him a long time ago","then":"Rukhsar feels judged, and her replies get shorter."},{"text":"Say it's private, so you two shouldn't discuss it again","then":"Rukhsar goes quiet, and she carries it alone again."}],"why":"Believing her and respecting her pace tells her the doubt isn't the truth."},{"prompt":"A week later Rukhsar writes again: he now keeps her phone at night. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say she should confront him tonight and demand her phone back","then":"Rukhsar goes pale. She knows how he might react."},{"text":"Say to wait and see if things calm down on their own","then":"Nothing calms, and weeks go by with no support."},{"text":"Offer to help her reach Women Helpline 181 or a trusted person","then":"Rukhsar breathes out. \"I'd like that. I don't know where to start.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you'll tell her family everything, right away","then":"Rukhsar begs you to wait. She fears their reaction, and his."}],"why":"Helping her reach 181 or someone she trusts finds her support, and keeps her in charge of her choices."}],"debrief":"Controlling who she sees is control, not the dip. It is never her fault, and Women Helpline 181 can help.","relearn":"Tiredness and a lopsided load are workable; control, threats or fear are abuse and route to help."},
  {"id":"uk-1264","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"low mood tool","hook":"You have felt flat and hopeless for weeks, and Karan suggests date nights to cheer you up.","steps":[{"prompt":"You know this is deeper than tired. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Agree to the date nights and hope they lift it","then":"You go out and smile, and the heaviness follows you home."},{"text":"Say \"It's deeper than tired. I'd like to talk to someone.\"","then":"Karan's face softens. \"Okay. Let's find someone. I'll come with you.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say it's only baby blues and you will just push through","then":"Weeks of pushing on leave you emptier, though you keep smiling for Karan."},{"text":"Say you feel flat but ask him to give you time","then":"Karan gives space, and the weeks pass with no one to talk to."}],"why":"Weeks of hopelessness need real support, and asking for it is never a failing."},{"prompt":"One night Karan finds you awake and teary, and asks how he can help. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing can be done for how you feel","then":"Karan doesn't know what to do, and neither of you moves."},{"text":"Ask him to tell no one, not even a doctor","then":"The secret stays, and so does the weight on you."},{"text":"Say you will manage alone, since he has enough to carry","then":"Karan looks helpless, and you carry it by yourself."},{"text":"Ask him to mind the baby while you call Tele-MANAS 14416","then":"Karan takes the baby. You make the call, and someone finally listens.","best":true}],"why":"Getting help for low mood is a practical step, and a partner can make it easy."},{"prompt":"Weeks later a rough day hits and you think \"I'm a bad mother for feeling this.\" What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Push the thought away and put on a bright smile for the whole family","then":"The smile holds all day, and you cry alone in the bathroom."},{"text":"Tell yourself it isn't your fault, and tell someone you trust","then":"Karan listens and holds your hand. The thought loosens a little.","best":true},{"text":"Decide you should just be grateful, not sad","then":"Gratitude piles on top of the sadness, and both feel heavier."},{"text":"Stay silent so that nobody worries about you","then":"The silence grows, and the thought gets louder."}],"why":"Low mood after birth is real and treatable. It is never your fault."}],"debrief":"Weeks of hopelessness aren't a date-night problem. It is never your fault, and a clinician or Tele-MANAS 14416 can help.","relearn":"For persistent low mood the right tool is help: a clinician or Tele-MANAS, not a check-in."},
  {"id":"uk-1265","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"joint family privacy","hook":"Your in-laws help a lot, but you and Leena never get a private minute.","steps":[{"prompt":"You want couple time without hurting anyone. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest a nightly walk after dinner, as \"our wind-down\"","then":"Leena smiles. \"Our wind-down. I love that.\" Everyone nods.","best":true},{"text":"Announce that everyone must give you two some space","then":"Her parents look stung, and the friction lasts all week."},{"text":"Go quiet and stop asking, to keep the peace at home","then":"The peace holds, and you two stay without any private time."},{"text":"Whisper complaints about them in your room at night","then":"The whispers feel like a bond, but nothing changes for you."}],"why":"A warmly framed ritual carves out couple time without pushing anyone away."},{"prompt":"One evening your mother-in-law gently asks how you and Leena are really doing. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"We're fine, just tired\" and smile","then":"She lets it drop, but the gap stays unspoken."},{"text":"Say you need more space from all of them","then":"She looks hurt, and the tension reaches the dinner table."},{"text":"Thank her for the help, and say you need time to reconnect","then":"She nods. \"Of course. I should have thought of it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say all is well, and promise more family evenings","then":"You spend your evenings in the living room, more drained than before."}],"why":"Thanking her and being honest about your need shows respect and makes room for privacy."},{"prompt":"A relative walks into your room without knocking, again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Ask warmly if everyone can knock first, and thank them","then":"They laugh, a bit embarrassed. \"Of course, beta.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing and hope it stops by itself","then":"It happens again, and you feel a little more watched."},{"text":"Fit a lock and say nothing about it to anyone","then":"The lock is noticed, and the family feels shut out."},{"text":"Complain about it in the big family WhatsApp group","then":"The group goes stiff, and the family feels publicly blamed."}],"why":"A warm request to knock keeps your privacy and your relationships intact."}],"debrief":"A warmly framed nightly walk carves out couple time even in a joint household.","relearn":"In a joint family, couple privacy can be carved out warmly, like a walk or a shut door, without rejecting help."},
  {"id":"uk-1266","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"appreciation habit","hook":"You want Neha to feel seen after weeks of broken nights, but a plain \"good job\" feels empty.","steps":[{"prompt":"It is morning and Neha looks wrecked. What do you say?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Good job with everything lately.\"","then":"Neha smiles politely, and the words slide past without landing."},{"text":"Say \"You settled her so calmly at 3am. I saw that.\"","then":"Neha's eyes soften. \"You noticed? I thought it just disappeared.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say nothing until a real wave of gratitude comes","then":"The wave doesn't come between feeds, and the morning passes unspoken."},{"text":"Say you feel lucky to have her","then":"Neha smiles, but she still doesn't know what you noticed."}],"why":"Naming one specific thing shows her you really saw it."},{"prompt":"A few days on, you have thirty seconds before leaving for work. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"Thanks for everything\" while grabbing your keys","then":"Neha says \"Sure,\" and the words fade before you reach the door."},{"text":"Text a heart emoji and hope she gets it","then":"Neha sends a heart back, unsure what it was for."},{"text":"Name one thing she did yesterday, then go","then":"Neha grins. \"Thirty seconds was enough.\"","best":true},{"text":"Save your thanks for a big gesture on your anniversary","then":"The anniversary is months away, and the small moments pass unmarked."}],"why":"One specific line fits into thirty seconds, so the habit survives busy days."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, on a hard evening, Neha admits she sometimes feels invisible in all this. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you notice everything and she knows it","then":"Neha says \"I don't, actually,\" and the room cools."},{"text":"Point out what you did this week too","then":"Neha goes quiet. It has turned into a tally."},{"text":"Name two specific things she did this week, and mean it","then":"Neha listens, then says quietly, \"You really do see it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say she is just tired and it will pass","then":"Neha nods, but the feeling of being unseen stays."}],"why":"Real examples answer \"do you see me\" better than a promise."},{"prompt":"Neha's birthday is coming, and you want more than a card. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Buy an expensive gift and skip the words","then":"The gift is lovely, but she wonders whether you see what she does."},{"text":"Post a photo online praising her as amazing","then":"Friends comment hearts, and she still hasn't heard it from you."},{"text":"Say the usual \"you're the best\" and hug her","then":"She hugs back, warm but a little hollow."},{"text":"Write down four things she did this year","then":"She reads the list twice, and keeps it in her drawer.","best":true}],"why":"A written list of specifics is thanks she can keep, not just hear once."}],"debrief":"Appreciation is a practised tool, like one specific 'you did X', not a grand feeling you wait for.","relearn":"Appreciation is a tool you practise: name one specific thing your partner did daily, not a trait you wait to feel."},
  {"id":"uk-1267","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"the weekly huddle","hook":"Every quiet moment turns into a chore debate, and you want logistics out of couple time.","steps":[{"prompt":"At bedtime Ishaan starts on tomorrow's pickups and bills again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Sort it all out right now, as usual","then":"An hour later the diary is sorted and the evening is gone."},{"text":"Say you are too tired and switch off the light","then":"Ishaan goes quiet, and the same questions are waiting tomorrow."},{"text":"Suggest a 15-minute weekly huddle","then":"Ishaan thinks. \"Fifteen minutes, then we're done with it? Try it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Start a shared notes app for all the chores","then":"The note fills up, but bedtime questions carry on regardless."}],"why":"A fixed slot gives planning its own home, so bedtime stays free."},{"prompt":"A week later, a tender moment turns into a chore debate again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Settle the chore quickly so the mood can come back","then":"The chore takes forty minutes, and the mood doesn't return."},{"text":"Say \"Let's park this for a set slot\" and stay close","then":"Ishaan jots \"bills\" on his hand and leans back into your shoulder.","best":true},{"text":"Say chores must never be discussed at night","then":"Ishaan feels shut down, and the chore stays unsettled."},{"text":"Let it slide and hope he drops it","then":"He raises it again in ten minutes, and the moment is gone."}],"why":"Parking it for a set slot protects the moment and still gets the chore handled."},{"prompt":"Ishaan says the two of you have turned into business partners. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree, and give up planning ahead","then":"The chores flood back into bedtime by the weekend."},{"text":"Say romance needs a tidy diary first","then":"He shrugs. It sounds like one more rule."},{"text":"Say a short slot frees your evenings for just you two","then":"He smiles. \"So a short slot is what buys us the tender time.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say he can't complain when you do most of the planning","then":"Ishaan stiffens, and the talk turns into a scorecard."}],"why":"A short slot is what buys you the unhurried couple time."}],"debrief":"A 15-minute weekly huddle gives logistics their own slot, so couple time isn't swallowed by chore-talk.","relearn":"A short weekly logistics 'huddle' keeps planning out of bedtime and frees couple time for connection."},
  {"id":"uk-1268","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"tool to moment","relearn":"Each toolkit move fits a moment: repair a snap, thank effort, huddle on logistics, check in nightly, route trouble to help.","hook":"Match each new-parent tool to the moment it's made for.","pairs":[{"left":"A quick 'sorry, that was the tiredness'","right":"After a tired snap"},{"left":"One specific thank-you","right":"When your partner went unnoticed"},{"left":"The weekly logistics huddle","right":"When planning hijacks quiet moments"},{"left":"The nightly check-in","right":"To stay connected through the chaos"},{"left":"A helpline like Tele-MANAS 14416","right":"When low mood lingers for weeks"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1269","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"route the trouble","relearn":"Troubles route differently: low mood to wellbeing help, load to equal-parenting, control to Respect at Home, recovery to a clinician.","hook":"Match each post-baby trouble to where it routes.","pairs":[{"left":"Hopeless, numb for weeks","right":"Tele-MANAS 14416 or a clinician"},{"left":"One parent carries the whole load","right":"Equal Parents, the sharing node"},{"left":"A partner who controls and frightens you","right":"Respect at Home and 181"},{"left":"Lingering discomfort after birth","right":"A clinician, not silent endurance"},{"left":"Snappy, out of sync, exhausted","right":"The couple toolkit and quick repair"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1270","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"tool vs trap","relearn":"Healthy tools (repair, appreciation, soft start, check-in, shared planning) each have a damaging trap they replace.","hook":"Match each healthy tool to the damaging habit it replaces.","pairs":[{"left":"Quick repair after a snap","right":"Days of cold silence"},{"left":"A specific thank-you","right":"Keeping a ledger of who did more"},{"left":"A soft 'I'm struggling, can we talk?'","right":"A harsh 'you never help'"},{"left":"The nightly check-in","right":"Drifting past each other in silence"},{"left":"Sharing the planning end-to-end","right":"Helping only when asked"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1271","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"who does what","relearn":"A clear toolkit names each move and its job so connection becomes deliberate, not accidental.","hook":"Match each part of the couple toolkit to what it does.","pairs":[{"left":"Protected couple time","right":"Keeps the partnership alive, not just the team"},{"left":"Daily appreciation","right":"Makes each partner feel seen"},{"left":"Fast repair","right":"Stops a tired snap becoming a grudge"},{"left":"The help routes","right":"For strain bigger than the two of you"},{"left":"A hug or shared chai","right":"Rebuilds closeness without a grand gesture"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1272","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"Vikram","source":"father owns it","relearn":"Owning a domain end-to-end, not waiting to be assigned, is how a father shares load, not 'helps'.","hook":"Match each domain to what 'owning it' looks like.","pairs":[{"left":"The night feeds","right":"You take your turns without being asked"},{"left":"The vaccination schedule","right":"You know the dates and book them yourself"},{"left":"The nappy supplies","right":"You notice they're low and restock"},{"left":"The bath routine","right":"You run it start to finish, not 'assist'"},{"left":"The bedtime wind-down","right":"You lead it some nights as your job"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1273","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"match","persona":"any","source":"green or red","relearn":"Tell green-flag couple tools from red-flag traps: each healthy move has a damaging twin to avoid.","hook":"Match each healthy after-kids habit to its damaging look-alike.","pairs":[{"left":"Naming the tiredness out loud","right":"Blaming your partner for the mood"},{"left":"Pausing a hard talk till morning","right":"Stonewalling and refusing to talk at all"},{"left":"Asking 'how are YOU?'","right":"Only ever asking about the baby"},{"left":"A gentle, honest opener","right":"A sarcastic dig dressed as a joke"},{"left":"Reaching for help early","right":"Insisting you must cope alone"}]},
  {"id":"uk-1274","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"start the check-in","hook":"You want to start a daily check-in but feel awkward going first.","setup":"You wait until the baby is down and your partner is folding clothes.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"You've been hovering. What is it?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Oh, nothing. I was just thinking.\"","then":"They shrug and keep folding. The moment closes."},{"text":"\"We should probably talk more or something.\"","then":"They smile vaguely. \"Sure, sometime.\" Nothing is set."},{"text":"\"Can we try two minutes each night? I'll start.\"","then":"They pause, then nod. \"Two minutes. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone says couples drift after a baby.\"","then":"They look worried. \"So are we drifting?\""}],"why":"A warm, doable invite you start yourself is easy to say yes to."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"I'm exhausted. I don't even know how we'd find time to talk.\"","options":[{"text":"\"We'll find time when things settle down.\"","then":"They nod, and the settling down stays out of sight."},{"text":"\"Two minutes tonight. How was your day, really?\"","then":"They smile faintly. \"Hard. But that was easy to say.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's talk about the baby's schedule. That's what matters.\"","then":"It turns into a logistics slot, and nothing about the two of you comes up."},{"text":"\"I'll write some questions and you just answer.\"","then":"They feel like they are taking a test."}],"why":"A tiny, concrete slot beats hoping the time appears."},{"prompt":"A week on, your partner goes quiet at dinner and says: \"I miss us.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's just this stage. We'll be fine.\"","then":"They nod, but the ache stays with them."},{"text":"\"Then you start the talking for once.\"","then":"They flinch. \"I didn't know you were waiting on me.\""},{"text":"\"Let's plan a proper date night next month.\"","then":"They smile, but the ache is still there tonight."},{"text":"\"I miss us too. Two minutes tonight, and I'll start.\"","then":"Their eyes fill. \"Yes. Please.\"","best":true}],"why":"Answering with a small step you'll begin tonight shows you mean it."}],"relearn":"You can launch the daily check-in yourself with a warm, doable invite rather than waiting for your partner to start."},
  {"id":"uk-1275","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"ask for the walk","hook":"You want to ask your mother-in-law to mind the baby for a short evening walk without seeming ungrateful.","setup":"She is folding baby clothes in the living room.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law says: \"You look pale, beta. Sit, sit. I'll manage.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thanks, Ma. We're fine here. Just rest and don't worry.\"","then":"She nods, and another evening goes by indoors."},{"text":"\"We need you to take the baby so we can get out.\"","then":"Her smile drops. \"So I'm just the babysitter now.\""},{"text":"\"Ma, your help means everything. Could you hold her a while?\"","then":"She beams. \"A while? Take an hour. Go.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll get my partner to ask you about it later on, Ma.\"","then":"She looks puzzled. \"Why not tell me yourself?\""}],"why":"Thanking her first and asking for something small honours her help."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your mother-in-law says: \"The baby only settles with me, you know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right, Ma. We'll stay close to home.\"","then":"She looks pleased, and the idea of a walk fades again."},{"text":"\"Then we'll go when she's asleep and not mention it.\"","then":"She finds out anyway, and feels shut out."},{"text":"\"She loves you. Some evenings we'd still like to try.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Try, then. Call me if she cries.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"That's exactly why we need a proper break from her, Ma.\"","then":"She goes quiet, hurt, and turns back to the clothes."}],"why":"Agreeing with what's true, then asking again gently, keeps her on your side."},{"prompt":"At a family gathering, your mother-in-law tells a cousin: \"Young couples today all want to go out and leave the baby.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Ma, it isn't like that at all. Please don't say it again.\"","then":"She goes cold, and the gathering feels awkward."},{"text":"\"Sorry, Ma. We'd never leave her. We'll stay in.\"","then":"She nods, but you both feel the loss."},{"text":"\"We only want a little break from the baby, Ma. That's all.\"","then":"The cousin raises an eyebrow, and she looks hurt."},{"text":"\"Twenty minutes, with her grandmother. That's a gift.\"","then":"She softens. \"Her grandmother, yes.\" The cousin nods.","best":true}],"why":"Naming her as the trusted person turns a jab into a compliment."}],"relearn":"You can ask in-laws for a little couple time gratefully, honouring their help while protecting the bond."},
  {"id":"uk-1276","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"repair fast","hook":"You snapped at your partner over nothing this morning and now want to repair it.","setup":"Your partner is at the sink when you get home.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says without turning around: \"Hi. You were pretty sharp this morning.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, I was tired, so it's understandable, isn't it.\"","then":"They put the cup down slowly. \"Understandable to you.\""},{"text":"\"I'm sorry. That was the exhaustion talking, not how I see you.\"","then":"They turn around. \"Thank you. It did hurt.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You were snappy too, if we're keeping count.\"","then":"They turn, eyes sharp. \"So we're keeping count now?\""},{"text":"\"It was nothing. Let's not make it a thing tonight.\"","then":"They nod, but the sharpness sits in the room all evening."}],"why":"A short, clean sorry that blames the tiredness, not them, mends it fastest."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"Yesterday you said I never finish anything. It stung.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I only said it because I was completely exhausted.\"","then":"They nod, but the hurt is exactly where it was."},{"text":"\"That was unfair. I'm sorry. You do finish things.\"","then":"Their face loosens. \"Thanks. I needed to hear that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're too sensitive right now. That's all it is.\"","then":"They look away. \"Right now? It feels like every day.\""},{"text":"\"It was one sentence. Do we have to go over it again?\"","then":"They go quiet. \"One sentence to you. It stayed with me.\""}],"why":"Owning the words plainly, without excuses, lets them feel heard."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says over tea: \"We had a bad row last night. I didn't like it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's over now. Let's not dwell on it.\"","then":"They shrug, but the unsaid part stays between you."},{"text":"\"You started it, though, with the feeding thing.\"","then":"They put the tea down. \"Here we go again.\""},{"text":"\"Me neither. It was exhaustion. Can we start fresh?\"","then":"They exhale. \"Yes. Fresh. I'm sorry too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll do the next two night feeds to make up for it.\"","then":"They nod, though what they wanted was a word, not a rota."}],"why":"A quick shared restart that names the tiredness beats replaying who started it."}],"relearn":"A fast, specific repair that names the tiredness, not the person, mends a snap better than defending it."},
  {"id":"uk-1277","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"soft start","hook":"You are overwhelmed by the load and need to raise it without a fight.","setup":"You wait until the baby is asleep and your partner puts down the phone.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"You've been quiet all evening. Everything okay?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You never do anything around here.\"","then":"They stiffen. \"That's not true. I did the bottles.\""},{"text":"\"I'm sinking a bit. Can we look at it this weekend?\"","then":"They pause. \"Yes. I'd rather know. Saturday?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'm fine. Just tired. Let's leave it.\"","then":"They shrug and pick up the phone. The evening ends."},{"text":"\"Everyone else's partner seems to manage more than this.\"","then":"They bristle. \"So I'm the problem?\""}],"why":"A soft opening names your own struggle and asks for teamwork."},{"prompt":"On Saturday, your partner says: \"You look worn out. What's been the hardest part?\"","options":[{"text":"\"All of it. You should just know by now.\"","then":"They frown. \"I'm not a mind reader.\""},{"text":"\"Nights and daily planning. Can we split them?\"","then":"They nod slowly. \"That's specific. I can work with that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nothing is hard. I'm only moaning, so just ignore me.\"","then":"They pause. \"Still, I'll try to pick up more.\""},{"text":"\"Let's make a chart of who does what, hour by hour.\"","then":"The chart turns into a scoreboard, and neither of you enjoys it."}],"why":"Naming one or two concrete things gives them something they can take on."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says: \"I tried doing more this week. Did you even notice?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's still not that much, is it.\"","then":"Their face falls. \"I thought I was helping.\""},{"text":"\"Well, I did every single night for weeks.\"","then":"They go quiet. The thanks got lost in the tally."},{"text":"\"I did notice. Thank you. It helped me sleep.\"","then":"They smile, tired. \"Then I'll try for a bit more.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"It's what you should have been doing anyway.\"","then":"They shrug, deflated, and stop offering."}],"why":"Thanking the effort keeps the change going, where scoring it stops it."}],"relearn":"A soft start, like 'I'm struggling. Can we figure this out?', opens a hard talk where a harsh 'you never' shuts it down."},
  {"id":"uk-1278","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"name not nag","hook":"The load has tilted onto you and you want your partner to own more, without it landing as a scold.","setup":"You sit beside your partner after the evening feed.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"You look upset. What's going on?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You need to finally step up as a parent.\"","then":"They stiffen. \"Finally? I've been trying.\""},{"text":"\"Nothing. I'll just do it all myself.\"","then":"They nod, unsure whether to push, and the pile stays with you."},{"text":"\"The load's tilted onto me. Can we redraw who owns what?\"","then":"They blink, then nod. \"Okay. Show me what's on your side.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"My friend's partner does far more than you do.\"","then":"They frown. \"Comparing us? Great.\""}],"why":"Naming a lopsided load as a shared problem invites teamwork, not defence."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"Honestly, I'm not sure what you want from me.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just watch the baby more when I ask you to.\"","then":"They agree, and you still end up asking every time."},{"text":"\"If you cared, you would see it.\"","then":"They stiffen. \"That's unfair.\""},{"text":"\"I'd like you to own bedtimes, start to finish.\"","then":"They take a breath. \"Bedtimes, start to finish. Done.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's split everything exactly fifty-fifty, hour by hour.\"","then":"They agree, but the tally soon feels like a contract."}],"why":"Handing over one whole area ends the asking and the tracking."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I got today's feed wrong. I feel like I'm not good at this.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Next time just ask me first. I'll do it right.\"","then":"They stop trying things on their own."},{"text":"\"Yes, that was a bit careless, honestly.\"","then":"They go silent and stop volunteering."},{"text":"\"You're learning it. Let's work out a fix together.\"","then":"Their shoulders drop. \"Okay. Show me how you do it?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No worries. I'll take over from here.\"","then":"The load slides back onto you within the week."}],"why":"Backing the learning, not taking over, lets them own it for good."},{"prompt":"A month later, your partner says: \"Can we look at who does what? Things have shifted.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's fine. Let's not tinker with what works.\"","then":"They nod, though something has gone unsaid."},{"text":"\"Yes. Let's redraw it. What feels heavy for you?\"","then":"They exhale. \"Mornings, actually. Thank you for asking.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Only if I get first pick of what to drop.\"","then":"They frown, and the review turns into a bargain."},{"text":"\"You're just trying to get out of your share.\"","then":"They flinch. \"That's not fair.\""}],"why":"Reviewing together keeps the split fair as life changes."}],"relearn":"Naming the shared problem, like 'the load feels lopsided', invites teamwork; 'step up' shaming invites defensiveness."},
  {"id":"uk-1279","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"ask how I am","hook":"Everyone asks about the baby and never about you, and you want your partner to see you too.","setup":"Your partner sits with you after the visitors leave.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"The baby was such a hit today. Everyone loved her.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's fine. Nobody really needs to worry about me at all.\"","then":"They nod, relieved, and go back to folding her blanket."},{"text":"\"She was. Could you ask how I am sometimes, too?\"","then":"They look up. \"I'm sorry. Yes. How are you?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone forgets about me, and now you do too, it seems.\"","then":"They look hurt. \"That's not fair. I'm here.\""},{"text":"\"I'll tell my mother how I feel instead of you.\"","then":"They look stung. \"Your mother? Why not me?\""}],"why":"A plain, warm request tells your partner exactly what you need."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner asks: \"How are you doing? Really?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine. Just a bit tired.\"","then":"They accept it, and the honest answer stays inside."},{"text":"\"Tired and a bit lost. I miss feeling like myself.\"","then":"They sit closer. \"I didn't know. Tell me more.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Honestly it's been rough, and you're no help.\"","then":"They flinch. \"I asked because I want to help.\""},{"text":"\"Better than the baby is, I think.\"","then":"They smile, and the talk swings back to the baby."}],"why":"Answering honestly, without blame, lets them meet you where you are."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says: \"I can see you're struggling. I don't know how to fix it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Forget it then. I'll just cope by myself, as usual.\"","then":"They step back, and you carry it alone again."},{"text":"\"It's you who should be figuring this out, not me.\"","then":"They go quiet, unsure where to even start."},{"text":"\"You don't have to fix it. Listening helps, and an evening off.\"","then":"They nod. \"Listening I can do. Saturday evening is yours.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just buy me something nice to cheer me up.\"","then":"A gift arrives, and the real feelings stay unsaid."}],"why":"Listening and small, real relief matter more than a fix or a gift."}],"relearn":"You can gently ask your partner to check on you, not only the baby. 'Ask how I'm doing too' is a fair request."},
  {"id":"uk-1280","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"no clock on closeness","hook":"Your partner seems anxious that intimacy has not returned, and you want to take the pressure off.","setup":"They turn toward you in the dark, sounding nervous.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says quietly: \"I know it's been a while. I'm sorry.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's been a while now, so we should get back to it.\"","then":"They go stiff. \"So there's a deadline?\""},{"text":"\"There's no rush and no clock. We'll get there together.\"","then":"Their shoulders loosen. \"Thank you. I was scared to say it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Don't be sorry. I'm just a bit lonely lately.\"","then":"They wince, and guilt settles on top of the worry."},{"text":"\"Let's not talk about this. It's awkward for us both.\"","then":"They nod, but the worry stays with them, unspoken."}],"why":"Taking the clock away is what makes closeness feel safe again."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"My body still doesn't feel like mine yet.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I miss how we were, though.\"","then":"They look away. \"I know. That makes it harder.\""},{"text":"\"That makes sense. Take all the time you need.\"","then":"They lean against you. \"I just needed you to say that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe a romantic date night would fix things.\"","then":"They smile politely, but the pressure has quietly returned."},{"text":"\"Most people recover by now. Maybe ask around.\"","then":"They feel like the odd one out."}],"why":"Recovery has no universal timeline, and saying so takes the pressure off."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"It still hurts sometimes. I don't know if that's normal.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It will pass by itself. Just give it time.\"","then":"They nod, but the worry stays with them."},{"text":"\"Maybe we should just try and see.\"","then":"They tense. \"I'm not ready to just try.\""},{"text":"\"Let's ask your doctor. It's common and treatable.\"","then":"They breathe out. \"Okay. I'll book it this week.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Let's search online for what helps.\"","then":"The pages give twelve opinions, and they end up more anxious."}],"why":"Ongoing discomfort deserves a doctor, and there is still no clock on closeness."},{"prompt":"Some weeks later, your partner says: \"I think I'm ready to try being close again, but I'm nervous.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Great. It's been a long time.\"","then":"They tense. \"Please don't make it a big thing.\""},{"text":"\"Let's book a hotel and make it special.\"","then":"The plan adds pressure, and they feel the clock return."},{"text":"\"We'll go slowly, and either of us can stop.\"","then":"They relax. \"Okay. Slowly. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Tonight, then, while the baby's asleep.\"","then":"They hesitate. \"Tonight? I said I'm nervous.\""}],"why":"Going slowly, with a stop for either of you, keeps closeness mutual."}],"relearn":"You can reassure your partner there's no clock on physical closeness: readiness-led, no pressure, whenever it's right for both."},
  {"id":"uk-1281","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"thank the small","hook":"Your partner quietly handled the night feeds so you could sleep, and you want them to feel seen.","setup":"You come into the kitchen after your first proper sleep in weeks.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"Morning. You look better.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Thanks for, you know, helping out and stuff.\"","then":"They smile, but the thanks didn't land on anything real."},{"text":"\"I slept so well. The house was so quiet.\"","then":"They nod, and the effort behind that quiet goes unnoticed."},{"text":"\"You took every feed so I'd rest. Thank you.\"","then":"They look up, surprised. \"You noticed. Thank you for saying it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Finally, a proper night. I needed that.\"","then":"They nod, but wonder if you saw who made it happen."}],"why":"Naming what they did makes thanks feel real, not general."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner mentions: \"I finally fixed the bottle steriliser.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Great. What's next on the list?\"","then":"They shrug. The moment was over before it began."},{"text":"\"Nice. I did the bath and the shopping, so.\"","then":"They go quiet, and the thanks turns into a tally."},{"text":"\"I saw it working. Thanks for handling that.\"","then":"They grin. \"It's small, but thanks for seeing it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Oh, thanks. You're a star, really.\"","then":"They smile, but being called a star isn't the same as being noticed."}],"why":"A specific thank-you tells them their small effort registered."},{"prompt":"Weeks later, after a draining stretch, your partner says: \"Some days I wonder if any of it registers.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Of course it does. You know I appreciate you.\"","then":"They nod, but \"you know\" isn't the same as being told."},{"text":"\"Well, I do things too.\"","then":"They stiffen. It was never a competition."},{"text":"\"It does. This week I noticed the feeds.\"","then":"Their face softens. \"Okay. I needed that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're just tired. It'll feel better soon.\"","then":"They shrug, and the question stays where it was."}],"why":"Real examples land where \"you know I appreciate you\" doesn't."}],"relearn":"Naming a small specific thing your partner did makes appreciation real and keeps the bond warm under load."},
  {"id":"uk-1282","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"pause till calm","hook":"You are both fighting at 3am over feeding, and you want to pause it well, not just walk off.","setup":"The baby has finally gone quiet, but neither of you is.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner snaps: \"You always do the bottle wrong. Just move.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Forget it. I'm done talking about this.\"","then":"They watch you leave, and the fight stays open."},{"text":"\"We're wrecked. Let's pause and talk over chai.\"","then":"They exhale. \"Yes. Tomorrow. I'm too tired to be fair.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Fine. You do it your way then.\"","then":"They take the bottle stiffly, and the argument stays open."},{"text":"\"You're the one who wanted it done this way!\"","then":"Voices rise, and the baby starts crying again."}],"why":"Pausing with a named time isn't avoiding it. It's a way to fix it."},{"prompt":"Over tea, your partner says: \"We should talk about last night, or maybe just forget it.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Forget it. It was 3am. It doesn't count.\"","then":"They nod, and the real issue of who does the bottle is left alone."},{"text":"\"Let's talk now. What was hardest about the night?\"","then":"They rub their eyes. \"Honestly, feeling like I can't get it right.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Right, so who is to blame for last night?\"","then":"They cross their arms, and the blaming begins again."},{"text":"\"You said the bottle thing. You apologise first.\"","then":"They refuse, and you both wait for the other to move."}],"why":"Talking once you're both calmer turns a 3am fight into a real conversation."},{"prompt":"A week later at 2am, it flares again and your partner says: \"Here we go. Another night fight.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then stop starting it.\"","then":"Voices rise, and the baby wakes."},{"text":"\"Let's settle it now, however long it takes.\"","then":"You keep going until 4am. Nothing sticks, and you are both wrecked."},{"text":"\"Let's pause it now and set a time.\"","then":"They nod. \"Chai after breakfast. And I'll go first.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I can't do this. I'm sleeping on the sofa.\"","then":"They are left alone with the baby and the argument."}],"why":"Naming a pause and setting a time beats pushing on or walking off."}],"relearn":"Pausing a 3am argument for a calmer moment is a timing skill, not avoidance. You name it and set a time."},
  {"id":"uk-1283","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Meena","source":"suggest help together","hook":"You sense the two of you are stuck in a loop you cannot fix alone.","setup":"After another quiet dinner, you sit opposite your partner.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"We had the same fight again. I don't know what else to do.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You clearly need to fix whatever's wrong with you.\"","then":"They go rigid. \"Wrong with me?\""},{"text":"\"Let's just try harder to stop fighting.\"","then":"They nod, but there's no new way to do it."},{"text":"\"We keep circling this. A counsellor might help us.\"","then":"They pause. \"Both of us. Okay. That feels less scary.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe our families can help us sort it out.\"","then":"They wince. Now the whole family would know."}],"why":"Framing help as a team step blames neither of you."},{"prompt":"On the weekend, your partner asks: \"Do you think we're in real trouble?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe. I honestly don't know.\"","then":"They look frightened, and the conversation stops there."},{"text":"\"Only if you keep being like this.\"","then":"They flinch. The words hang in the room."},{"text":"\"No. We're under strain, and outside help could support us.\"","then":"They nod. \"Under strain. Not broken. I can hold that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"No, we're fine. We just need more sleep.\"","then":"They nod, though the loop is still there."}],"why":"Saying it's strain, not failure, makes asking for help feel safe."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"If we did want help, how would we even find someone? I don't want everyone to know.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's ask my sister. She knows everyone.\"","then":"Your sister is kind, but now she knows."},{"text":"\"We can leave it a few more months.\"","then":"Months later, the same fight is still on repeat."},{"text":"\"You look for someone. I'm too busy.\"","then":"They search alone and feel the weight fall on them."},{"text":"\"We can ask our doctor for a name, privately.\"","then":"They relax a little. \"Together, and private. Yes.\"","best":true}],"why":"Choosing a counsellor together and privately keeps it a team step."}],"relearn":"Suggesting you talk to someone together frames help as a shared step, not a verdict on either partner."},
  {"id":"uk-1284","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"protect the ritual","hook":"Your nightly check-in is slipping as life gets busier, and you want to protect it before it fades.","setup":"It is nine at night, and you both reach for your phones.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"We skipped our ten minutes again, didn't we?\"","options":[{"text":"\"I guess we're too busy for that check-in thing now.\"","then":"They nod, and one more small habit slips away."},{"text":"\"Not much happened anyway. We can just skip it.\"","then":"They shrug, and skipped nights start to feel normal."},{"text":"\"We did. I miss it. Can we guard it again?\"","then":"They smile. \"I miss it too. Tonight, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"We should do it properly on Sunday for an hour.\"","then":"Sunday arrives, the hour doesn't, and the nights stay empty."}],"why":"Saying you miss it out loud protects a tool that works."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says: \"I want to say something real, but I'm too tired to think.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Save it for when we're both fresh.\"","then":"Fresh never comes, and the words stay unsaid."},{"text":"\"I'm too tired to listen. Tell me tomorrow.\"","then":"Tomorrow, they say nothing at all."},{"text":"\"Then just one sentence. Say it, and we'll go from there.\"","then":"They breathe, then say it. It takes ten seconds and eases something.","best":true},{"text":"\"Text it to me. I'll read it later on.\"","then":"The text arrives, but a message isn't the same as being heard."}],"why":"Shrinking it to one sentence keeps the door open on the tiredest nights."},{"prompt":"Your friend says over chai: \"You two still do that check-in thing? Seems like a lot.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Not really. We let it go a while back.\"","then":"Your friend nods, and you realise how much you miss it."},{"text":"\"You wouldn't get it. You don't have kids.\"","then":"Your friend goes quiet, hurt, and the chai cools."},{"text":"\"It's a lot of work, honestly. Maybe we'll stop.\"","then":"Saying it out loud makes stopping feel a little more settled."},{"text":"\"When we do it, it's my best ten minutes.\"","then":"Your friend smiles. \"Maybe I should try that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying why it matters reminds you both it is worth guarding."}],"relearn":"Protecting a tiny ritual out loud, like 'let's keep our ten minutes', keeps a working tool from quietly lapsing."},
  {"id":"uk-1285","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"open door to her help","hook":"Your partner has seemed heavier than tired for weeks, and you want to open the door to help.","setup":"You sit beside them on the bed after another early wake-up.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I'm fine. I'm just tired. I told you.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Everyone's tired. You'll snap out of it.\"","then":"They turn away. \"Then why don't I feel like everyone?\""},{"text":"\"This feels heavier than tired. Can we talk to someone?\"","then":"They go still, then nod. \"Yes. I've been scared to say it.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay. But tell me if it gets worse.\"","then":"They nod, and the hard days go unmentioned."},{"text":"\"I'll book you an appointment. You can't go on like this.\"","then":"They feel decided for, not heard, and go quiet."}],"why":"Naming your worry gently and offering to go together invites help without pushing."},{"prompt":"Later that week, your partner says: \"I cry for no reason now. What's wrong with me?\"","options":[{"text":"\"You've got so much to be grateful for.\"","then":"They nod, and now guilt sits on top of the tears."},{"text":"\"Try to get out for a walk each day.\"","then":"They try. The tears come anyway, and now they feel they failed at that too."},{"text":"\"Nothing is wrong with you. It's real and treatable.\"","then":"They cry, but it's softer. \"Thank you for saying that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe don't tell anyone. They'll only worry.\"","then":"They agree, and the loneliness deepens."}],"why":"Saying it is real and treatable removes the shame that keeps people quiet."},{"prompt":"A few days on, your partner asks: \"If I did need to talk to someone, who would we call? I don't want to make a fuss.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's wait until the six-month check-up.\"","then":"The weeks pass, and the tears do not."},{"text":"\"Let's ask the neighbours who's been through it.\"","then":"Neighbour talk spreads, and they wish they had kept it private."},{"text":"\"I'll call and explain everything on your behalf.\"","then":"They feel relief, then shame that they couldn't do it themselves."},{"text":"\"Our doctor, or Tele-MANAS 14416. It's not a fuss.\"","then":"They exhale. \"Okay. Let's call together.\"","best":true}],"why":"A doctor or Tele-MANAS 14416 meets it as treatable, and it is never their fault."},{"prompt":"A few weeks later, your partner says: \"Some days I feel almost like myself. Maybe it's passing.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Great. Then we don't need to bother anyone.\"","then":"The bad days return, and no one is asked."},{"text":"\"See? I told you it would pass.\"","then":"They nod, and feel foolish for the hard days."},{"text":"\"Good days matter. Let's still see the doctor.\"","then":"They nod slowly. \"Okay. I do still have the hard ones.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then let's stay quiet about it all now.\"","then":"The silence closes, and they carry the bad days alone."}],"why":"Good days are welcome, and keeping support in place protects them."}],"relearn":"Gently naming worry and offering to find help together opens the door when a partner's low mood seems beyond tired."},
  {"id":"uk-1286","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"own a domain","hook":"You want to genuinely take load off your partner, not just wait to be handed tasks.","setup":"Your partner is writing yet another list on the fridge door.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"The doctor visits, the vaccines, the supplies. I track everything.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just tell me exactly what to do and I'll do it.\"","then":"They sigh. They would still be the manager, with one more employee."},{"text":"\"Put it all in the family chat. I'll pitch in.\"","then":"The chat fills with messages, and they still track it all."},{"text":"\"Let me own doctor visits and supplies, end to end.\"","then":"They stare, then smile. \"All of it? Okay. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I already do a lot around here, you know.\"","then":"They stop talking. The list stays on the fridge."}],"why":"Owning a whole area, from remembering to doing, removes the tracking."},{"prompt":"A week later, the baby has a fever and your partner asks: \"Should we call the doctor now?\"","options":[{"text":"\"What do you think we should do?\"","then":"They sigh. They're back to deciding everything."},{"text":"\"Let me know when it's booked and I'll drive.\"","then":"They book it, plan it, and you drive."},{"text":"\"I've got it. I'll call and take her.\"","then":"Their shoulders drop. They watch you dial.","best":true},{"text":"\"Maybe wait until morning and see how she is.\"","then":"The fever climbs, and they lie awake worrying alone."}],"why":"Taking the call end to end, not waiting to be asked, is what owning means."},{"prompt":"A month later, your partner says: \"I still track every doctor visit in my head. I can't switch off.\"","options":[{"text":"\"I'll try to remember. Just remind me when I forget.\"","then":"They'd still be the reminder, only now with extra checking."},{"text":"\"Why don't you trust me with this?\"","then":"They frown. \"It's not trust. My head just won't stop.\""},{"text":"\"It's really not that hard to remember.\"","then":"They stiffen. Remembering was never the hard part."},{"text":"\"From now on the reminders and records are mine. Rest.\"","then":"Their breath catches. \"Okay. I'll try to switch off.\"","best":true}],"why":"Owning the tracking, not only the tasks, lets them truly put it down."},{"prompt":"Your neighbour says: \"Such a helpful dad! Your partner is lucky.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Ha, thank you. I try my best.\"","then":"The neighbour beams, and the idea of \"helping\" stays."},{"text":"\"Yes, I'm one of the good ones.\"","then":"The neighbour laughs, and your partner rolls their eyes."},{"text":"\"Tell my partner that. They never say it.\"","then":"There's an awkward silence, and your partner hears it."},{"text":"\"It's not helping. She's my child too.\"","then":"The neighbour blinks, then smiles. \"True. Quite right.\"","best":true}],"why":"Parenting your own child isn't helping, and saying so quietly changes the story."}],"relearn":"Offering to own a whole domain, not 'tell me what to do', shifts a father from helper to equal sharer."},
  {"id":"uk-1287","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"decline the comparison","hook":"Your partner keeps comparing you both to a \"perfect\" couple online, and you want to set a kinder frame.","setup":"They scroll through photos on the sofa, then sigh.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"Look at them. They seem to do it all so easily.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe we just aren't as good at this as they are.\"","then":"They nod slowly, and the mood sinks."},{"text":"\"They're probably faking. Nobody is that happy.\"","then":"They frown. You've swapped envy for a sneer, and the sting stays."},{"text":"\"Their posts are a highlight reel. Let's measure us by us.\"","then":"They lower the phone. \"Okay. Us by us.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Then let's copy exactly what they do.\"","then":"You spend the weekend copying a schedule, and neither of you enjoys it."}],"why":"A highlight reel is a few curated seconds, not the whole of anyone's life."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner says: \"Our home is a mess next to theirs. I feel like we're failing.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Let's tidy everything tonight, even if we're wrecked.\"","then":"You clean until midnight and wake even more tired."},{"text":"\"Just stop looking at their account, then.\"","then":"They shrug. They'll stop, but the feeling stays."},{"text":"\"The mess is us living. We're under load, not failing.\"","then":"They laugh, a little wetly. \"Under load. I like that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. We should do better.\"","then":"They nod, and the bar for \"enough\" moves up again."}],"why":"Under load is not the same as failing, and saying so takes the sting out."},{"prompt":"At a family function your cousin says: \"You must be so tired. My baby sleeps all night.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Good for you. We're clearly not managing.\"","then":"Your cousin looks awkward, and you both feel smaller."},{"text":"\"You're lying. No baby sleeps all night.\"","then":"Your cousin's face falls, and the mood chills."},{"text":"\"We are tired. Every family finds its own way.\"","then":"Your cousin nods. \"True. Some nights are rough here too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Tell us your secret. We must be doing it wrong.\"","then":"Your cousin lists tips, and you leave feeling behind."}],"why":"Naming your own pace calmly protects you both from comparison."}],"relearn":"Naming that comparison-to-others hurts, and choosing your own pace, is a tool that protects the couple from highlight-reel pressure."},
  {"id":"uk-1288","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"role-play","persona":"any","source":"small reconnect","hook":"You miss your partner, but a big night out is impossible, so you want to suggest something small.","setup":"The baby has just gone down, and your partner slumps on the sofa.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner sighs: \"We haven't had a proper date in months.\"","options":[{"text":"\"There's no point until we can do a proper date.\"","then":"They nod, and the months of nothing stretch on."},{"text":"\"Let's book a nice restaurant for next month.\"","then":"They smile, but next month is a long way from tonight."},{"text":"\"No big night, but ten quiet minutes with tea?\"","then":"They look up. \"Tea, just us. Yes, I'd love that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I know. Everyone says this stage is like that.\"","then":"They shrug, and the missing stays unspoken between you."}],"why":"Ten quiet minutes now beat waiting for a grand date that never comes."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"I don't even know what we'd talk about anymore.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then maybe we've just drifted apart.\"","then":"They look stricken, and now you have both gone cold."},{"text":"\"Let's plan a big conversation this weekend.\"","then":"The weekend fills up, and the big talk never happens."},{"text":"\"Let's just sit and scroll side by side.\"","then":"You sit together, each alone with a screen."},{"text":"\"Not the baby. Tell me one thing on your mind.\"","then":"They pause, surprised. \"Okay. Actually, there is something.\"","best":true}],"why":"A small, simple question restarts the talk without any pressure."},{"prompt":"A week on, your partner asks: \"Do small things, like tea together, even count for much?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Probably not. Let's not bother with them.\"","then":"They shrug, and the little bridge disappears."},{"text":"\"Only if we do them perfectly, every single night.\"","then":"Tea turns into a duty, and by Thursday you both dread it."},{"text":"\"Small counts. It keeps the thread between us.\"","then":"They smile. \"The thread. Okay. Tea tonight, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Next month I'll organise something huge.\"","then":"They smile, but tonight you both sit in silence."}],"why":"Small, regular moments keep the bond alive better than rare big ones."}],"relearn":"Suggesting a small reconnect now, like shared tea or a short sit, beats holding out for a grand date that never comes."},
  {"id":"uk-1289","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"keep tool or drop","relearn":"Sort working couple tools from damaging traps: check-ins, specific thanks and quick repair keep; silent treatment, scorekeeping and stonewalling go.","hook":"Sort each habit: a tool that keeps a couple strong, or a trap to drop.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A nightly two-minute check-in"},{"id":"b","text":"Giving the silent treatment for days"},{"id":"c","text":"One specific thank-you each day"},{"id":"d","text":"Keeping a resentment scorecard"},{"id":"e","text":"A fast 'sorry, that was the tiredness'"},{"id":"f","text":"Stonewalling and refusing to talk"}],"bins":[{"id":"keep","label":"Keep: a tool","valence":"pos"},{"id":"drop","label":"Drop: a trap","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"keep","b":"drop","c":"keep","d":"drop","e":"keep","f":"drop"}},
  {"id":"uk-1290","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"workable or help","relearn":"Exhaustion and a lopsided load are workable with tools; persistent low mood and control need outside help.","hook":"Sort each: workable with couple tools, or reach for outside help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Snappy and out of sync from no sleep"},{"id":"b","text":"Weeks of numbness and hopelessness"},{"id":"c","text":"The load feels lopsided lately"},{"id":"d","text":"A partner who controls and frightens you"},{"id":"e","text":"You keep missing each other"},{"id":"f","text":"Frightening thoughts after the birth"}],"bins":[{"id":"work","label":"Workable with tools","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Reach for help","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"work","b":"help","c":"work","d":"help","e":"work","f":"help"}},
  {"id":"uk-1291","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"Vikram","source":"owning or helping","relearn":"Sort 'owning a domain' from 'just helping when asked': owning means tracking and doing it yourself end-to-end.","hook":"Sort each move: a father genuinely owning the load, or just 'helping out' when asked?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tracking the vaccine dates and booking them yourself"},{"id":"b","text":"Changing a nappy only when handed the baby"},{"id":"c","text":"Noticing supplies are low and restocking"},{"id":"d","text":"Waiting to be told what needs doing"},{"id":"e","text":"Running bath-time start to finish as your job"},{"id":"f","text":"Calling it 'babysitting' your own child"}],"bins":[{"id":"own","label":"Owning it","valence":"pos"},{"id":"help","label":"Just helping","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"own","b":"help","c":"own","d":"help","e":"own","f":"help"}},
  {"id":"uk-1292","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"sustainable or not","relearn":"Sort sustainable small moves from grand one-off gestures: consistent tiny habits beat dramatic fixes that fade.","hook":"Sort each move: a small sustainable tool, or a grand gesture that fades fast?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A two-minute nightly check-in"},{"id":"b","text":"One expensive holiday to 'fix everything'"},{"id":"c","text":"A daily specific thank-you"},{"id":"d","text":"A single grand anniversary surprise, then nothing"},{"id":"e","text":"A short shared chai each morning"},{"id":"f","text":"A dramatic promise to 'change everything overnight'"}],"bins":[{"id":"small","label":"Small & sustainable","valence":"pos"},{"id":"grand","label":"Grand & fading","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"small","b":"grand","c":"small","d":"grand","e":"small","f":"grand"}},
  {"id":"uk-1293","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"sort","persona":"any","source":"right tool for moment","relearn":"Sort which tool fits which moment: repair for a snap, a huddle for logistics, help for low mood, a check-in for drift.","hook":"Sort each moment to the right tool to reach for.","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A tired snap last night → a quick repair"},{"id":"b","text":"Planning hijacks every quiet moment → a weekly huddle"},{"id":"c","text":"Weeks of hopelessness → reach for help"},{"id":"d","text":"Drifting past each other → a nightly check-in"},{"id":"e","text":"Partner went unseen → a specific thank-you"},{"id":"f","text":"Control and fear at home → Respect at Home help"}],"bins":[{"id":"couple","label":"A couple tool","valence":"pos"},{"id":"helproute","label":"A help route","valence":"uhoh"}],"key":{"a":"couple","b":"couple","c":"helproute","d":"couple","e":"couple","f":"helproute"}},
  {"id":"uk-1294","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"tools are traits myth","relearn":"Repair and appreciation are skills you practise, not personality traits you either have or lack.","hook":"\"Some couples are just naturally good at this; we're not the appreciating, repairing type.\"","myth":{"un":"Some couples are just naturally good at this; we're not the appreciating, repairing type.","re":"Repair and appreciation are skills any couple can practise and get better at, not traits you're born with.","why":"The couples who thrive use learnable tools, not luck or personality."}},
  {"id":"uk-1295","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"no time myth","relearn":"You don't need free time for connection; tiny tools ride on what you already do, like the morning chai.","hook":"\"We have zero free time as new parents, so connection just has to wait.\"","myth":{"un":"We have zero free time as new parents, so connection just has to wait.","re":"Connection rides on tiny habits you already do, like two minutes over morning chai, so it never has to wait.","why":"Small sustainable moves beat waiting for free time that never comes."}},
  {"id":"uk-1296","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"asking help fails myth","relearn":"Reaching for couples or wellbeing help is using a tool early, not admitting your relationship failed.","hook":"\"If we need a counsellor or a helpline, it means our relationship has already failed.\"","myth":{"un":"If we need a counsellor or a helpline, it means our relationship has already failed.","re":"Reaching for help is a tool strong couples use early, not proof anything has failed.","why":"Help used early protects the bond; it's a sign of care, not collapse."}},
  {"id":"uk-1297","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"Vikram","source":"grand gesture myth","relearn":"Consistent small moves rebuild a couple after kids; one grand gesture can't carry the bond alone.","hook":"\"One big romantic holiday will fix everything that's gone flat between us.\"","myth":{"un":"One big romantic holiday will fix everything that's gone flat between us.","re":"A grand trip is lovely, but it's the small daily tools, check-ins, thanks, repair, that actually keep a couple close.","why":"Consistent beats dramatic; the bond is rebuilt in tiny repeated moments."}},
  {"id":"uk-1298","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"more sharing fixes all myth","relearn":"Sharing the load helps ordinary strain, but control or abuse needs Respect at Home and 181, not 'more sharing'.","hook":"\"Whatever the problem is after a baby, more chore-sharing and patience will fix it.\"","myth":{"un":"Whatever the problem is after a baby, more chore-sharing and patience will fix it.","re":"Sharing helps ordinary strain, but if a partner controls, threatens or frightens you, that needs Respect at Home, not more sharing.","why":"The post-baby phase never excuses control or violence; that routes to Women Helpline 181."}},
  {"id":"uk-1299","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"endure quietly myth","relearn":"A hard post-baby stretch is workable with named tools and help, not something to silently endure.","hook":"\"A good couple just quietly endures the hard baby years without complaining.\"","myth":{"un":"A good couple just quietly endures the hard baby years without complaining.","re":"You don't endure it silently; you use tools, share the load, repair, and get help when needed.","why":"It's workable as a team, not a sentence to suffer through alone."}},
  {"id":"uk-1300","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"strike-rewrite","persona":"any","source":"check-in is soft myth","relearn":"A daily check-in is a practical protective habit, not soft or pointless; it catches small strains early.","hook":"\"A daily 'how are you really' check-in is soft, pointless stuff; real life is too busy for it.\"","myth":{"un":"A daily 'how are you really' check-in is soft, pointless stuff; real life is too busy for it.","re":"A two-minute check-in is a practical tool that catches strain early and keeps a busy couple connected.","why":"Small consistent connection sustains the bond better than waiting for a crisis."}},
  {"id":"uk-1301","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"which tool feels yours","relearn":"Picking one small tool to start with, like a check-in, a thank-you or a repair line, is enough; you don't need them all at once.","hook":"Lensy: you don't need the whole toolkit at once. Which one small tool would you try first?","prompt":"What feels doable for you?","options":["A nightly check-in","One daily thank-you","A fast repair after a snap","A weekly logistics huddle"],"affirm":"Starting with one small tool you'll actually keep beats reaching for all of them and dropping every one."},
  {"id":"uk-1302","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"know the help routes","relearn":"Knowing the help routes exist for low mood, fair load or control is part of the toolkit, even if you never need them.","hook":"Lensy: knowing where help lives is part of the kit. Does it steady you to know the routes are there?","prompt":"How does that land?","options":["Yes, steadier","Good to know","One less worry"],"affirm":"Knowing low mood routes to Tele-MANAS, control to Respect at Home, and recovery to a clinician means you're never stuck alone."},
  {"id":"uk-1303","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"Vikram","source":"owning feels right","relearn":"Choosing to own a whole domain, not wait to be asked, is the move that turns helping into true sharing.","hook":"Lensy: owning one whole job, end to end, is yours to choose. Which would you take on fully?","prompt":"What could you own?","options":["The doctor visits","The bedtime routine","The supplies and stock","The night feeds"],"affirm":"Taking one domain start-to-finish, tracking and doing it without being asked, is real sharing, not babysitting."},
  {"id":"uk-1304","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"repair over perfect","relearn":"Aiming to repair fast, not to never snap, takes the pressure off and keeps the bond resilient under exhaustion.","hook":"Lensy: nobody snaps zero times on no sleep. Does aiming to repair fast feel kinder than aiming for perfect?","prompt":"What's your take?","options":["Kinder, yes","More realistic","A relief"],"affirm":"Fast repair after a snap matters more than never snapping; that's a tool you can actually keep under exhaustion."},
  {"id":"uk-1305","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"Divya","source":"help is strength","relearn":"Reaching for help when the weight is real is a strong, caring move, not a personal failing.","hook":"Lensy: reaching for help when the weight is real is strength, not failure. Can you hold that?","prompt":"How does it sit?","options":["I can hold that","Strength, yes","I'll remember it"],"affirm":"Asking for help, for the couple or for your own low mood, is a strong, caring choice; you don't have to be sure or alone."},
  {"id":"uk-1306","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"protect couple time","relearn":"Deciding couple time is worth protecting, even in a packed joint household, is the first step to actually carving it out.","hook":"Lensy: couple time is worth defending, even when the house is full. Is it worth protecting for you two?","prompt":"What do you feel?","options":["Worth it","Yes, ours to keep","I'll carve it out"],"affirm":"Protected couple time can be carved out even in a joint family, and choosing to defend it is what makes it real."},
  {"id":"uk-1307","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"reflect","persona":"any","source":"consistent over grand","relearn":"Trusting small consistent moves over grand gestures frees you to connect now instead of waiting for a perfect plan.","hook":"Lensy: small and steady, done often, beats one grand gesture. Does that take the pressure off?","prompt":"How does that feel?","options":["Less pressure","Steady wins","I can do small"],"affirm":"Small consistent tools you'll actually keep rebuild a couple after kids; the grand gesture isn't the thing that holds you."},
  {"id":"uk-1308","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot misapplied tool","relearn":"Some 'tools' are traps: the silent treatment and a resentment scorecard damage the bond, not protect it.","hook":"Lensy: three keep a couple strong; spot the two that secretly damage the bond.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A nightly two-minute check-in","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"The silent treatment to make a point","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"One specific thank-you each day","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"A private scorecard of who did more","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A quick 'sorry, that was the tiredness'","trick":false}],"why":"The silent treatment and a secret scorecard quietly damage the bond; the check-in, thanks and repair are the real tools."},
  {"id":"uk-1309","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot wrong tool for low mood","relearn":"For persistent low mood the right tool is help; date nights and 'cheer up' advice are the wrong tools to catch.","hook":"Lensy: your partner's had weeks of hopelessness. Spot the two wrong-tool responses.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Gently suggest a clinician or Tele-MANAS","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Tell them a date night will fix it","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Offer to find someone to talk to, together","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Say everyone's tired; they'll get over it","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Take load off them while they get support","trick":false}],"why":"Persistent low mood needs help, not a date night or 'you'll get over it'; those misuse a couple tool."},
  {"id":"uk-1310","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot control mislabelled","relearn":"Control, threats and isolation are abuse routing to Respect at Home, not 'post-baby stress' to share through.","hook":"Lensy: spot the two wrongly waved off as 'just post-baby stress'.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Snapping after a sleepless night","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"A partner controlling who you can see","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Feeling out of sync and overwhelmed","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Threats that frighten you into silence","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A lopsided load you can renegotiate","trick":false}],"why":"Controlling who you see and frightening threats are abuse, not strain; they route to Respect at Home and 181, not 'share more'."},
  {"id":"uk-1311","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"Vikram","source":"spot helping-not-owning","relearn":"Owning means tracking and doing it yourself; 'only when asked' and 'babysitting' are the helper traps.","hook":"Lensy: spot the two that are 'helping when asked', not owning the load.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Booking the baby's check-ups yourself","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Changing a nappy only when handed the baby","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Tracking supplies and restocking","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Calling minding your own child 'babysitting'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Running bath-time start to finish","trick":false}],"why":"Acting only when handed the baby, and 'babysitting' your own child, are helper traps; owning means doing it yourself."},
  {"id":"uk-1312","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot fake reconnect","relearn":"Real reconnecting is small, mutual and pressure-free; a guilt-trip or a deadline on intimacy is the trap.","hook":"Lensy: spot the two 'reconnect' moves that secretly add pressure.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A shared chai and ten quiet minutes","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Guilt-tripping a partner who isn't ready","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A hug with no expectation attached","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Saying intimacy is 'overdue' by now","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Naming you miss them, gently","trick":false}],"why":"Guilt-tripping and 'it's overdue' put a clock on closeness; real reconnecting is small, mutual and never owed."},
  {"id":"uk-1313","cat":"tools-and-help","type":"spot","persona":"any","source":"spot grand-not-sustainable","relearn":"Sustainable connection is small and repeated; one-off grand gestures with nothing after are the trap to catch.","hook":"Lensy: spot the two moves that look romantic but won't sustain the couple.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A short shared chai every morning","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"One huge surprise, then back to silence","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"A daily two-minute check-in","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"A grand promise to 'change everything overnight'","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"A specific thank-you most days","trick":false}],"why":"A one-off grand surprise and an overnight-change promise fade fast; the small repeated habits are what actually sustain a couple."},
  {"id":"uk-1350","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A good mother has no needs of her own.\"","myth":{"un":"A good mother has no needs of her own.","re":"You're a person and a parent at once; meeting some of your own needs keeps you steady enough to give.","why":"You can't pour from an empty cup."},"relearn":"A good parent still has needs; meeting some of your own keeps you steady enough to keep giving.","persona":"Divya","source":"needs are allowed"},
  {"id":"uk-1351","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Wanting an evening with your old friends means you love the baby less.\"","myth":{"un":"Wanting an evening with your old friends means you love the baby less.","re":"Missing your friends isn't a measure of your love; reconnecting with them brings a refreshed you back home.","why":"Your world can hold both."},"relearn":"Wanting your friends doesn't mean you love the baby less; it brings a refreshed you back home.","persona":"Divya","source":"friends still matter"},
  {"id":"uk-1352","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Resting while the baby sleeps is lazy.\"","myth":{"un":"Resting while the baby sleeps is lazy.","re":"Rest isn't laziness; it's how a depleted parent recovers enough to keep going, and the laundry can wait.","why":"Recovery is real work too."},"relearn":"Resting when the baby sleeps isn't lazy; it's how a depleted parent recovers enough to keep going.","persona":"any","source":"rest is not lazy"},
  {"id":"uk-1353","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Asking your partner for help means you're not coping.\"","myth":{"un":"Asking your partner for help means you're not coping.","re":"Asking for help is a skill, not a failure; raising a child was never meant to be carried alone.","why":"Sharing the load is the plan, not a backup."},"relearn":"Asking for help isn't failing to cope; a child was never meant to be carried alone.","persona":"Divya","source":"asking is strength"},
  {"id":"uk-1354","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Once you're a parent, your own dreams are over.\"","myth":{"un":"Once you're a parent, your own dreams are over.","re":"Your dreams may slow or shift shape, but they don't have to vanish; keeping one alive models a whole life.","why":"Your child watches what a full life looks like."},"relearn":"Becoming a parent doesn't end your dreams; keeping one alive shows your child a whole life.","persona":"Divya","source":"dreams continue"},
  {"id":"uk-1355","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A father doesn't need a break; he's not the one who gave birth.\"","myth":{"un":"A father doesn't need a break; he's not the one who gave birth.","re":"Both parents run low on sleep and self; a father needs his own breaks and identity protected too.","why":"Depletion isn't only the birthing parent's."},"relearn":"Fathers run low too; both parents need their own breaks and a self protected, not just one.","persona":"Vikram","source":"fathers deplete too"},
  {"id":"uk-1356","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"If you ever feel resentful or sad after a baby, you're an ungrateful parent.\"","myth":{"un":"If you ever feel resentful or sad after a baby, you're an ungrateful parent.","re":"Hard feelings can sit right beside deep love; they're a sign of depletion, not a verdict on your gratitude.","why":"Two feelings can be true at once."},"relearn":"Sad or resentful feelings can sit beside love; they signal depletion, not a lack of gratitude.","persona":"Priya","source":"feelings aren't a verdict"},
  {"id":"uk-1357","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"You should be able to cherish every single moment of this.\"","myth":{"un":"You should be able to cherish every single moment of this.","re":"No one enjoys every moment of anything; the pressure to 'cherish it all' only adds guilt on top of the tiredness.","why":"Some moments are just hard, and that's okay."},"relearn":"You don't have to cherish every moment; the pressure to enjoy it all only adds guilt to the tiredness.","persona":"Divya","source":"not every moment"},
  {"id":"uk-1358","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Showering, eating well, a walk. Those can all wait now.\"","myth":{"un":"Showering, eating well, a walk. Those can all wait now.","re":"The basics aren't extras you earn later; a fed, washed, moved-a-little parent has more to give the baby.","why":"Your wellbeing is part of the baby's."},"relearn":"Basics like food, a wash and a short walk aren't extras; a cared-for parent has more to give.","persona":"any","source":"basics aren't extras"},
  {"id":"uk-1359","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"A strong parent just pushes through low mood that won't lift.\"","myth":{"un":"A strong parent just pushes through low mood that won't lift.","re":"Low mood that lingers after birth is real and treatable; reaching for support is strength, not weakness.","why":"Help works; call Tele-MANAS 14416."},"relearn":"Persistent low mood after birth is real and treatable; reaching for support is strength, not weakness.","persona":"Divya","source":"pushing through isn't strength"},
  {"id":"uk-1360","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"strike-rewrite","hook":"\"Whatever's left of you after the baby's cared for is all you deserve.\"","myth":{"un":"Whatever's left of you after the baby's cared for is all you deserve.","re":"You're not a leftover; a parent's wellbeing isn't a luxury saved for last but part of what keeps the family well.","why":"You belong in your own care plan."},"relearn":"You're not a leftover; your wellbeing isn't last in line, it's part of keeping the family well.","persona":"Divya","source":"not a leftover"},
  {"id":"uk-1361","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: refuels a tired parent, or quietly drains them further?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"A real, no-strings hour to themselves"},{"id":"b","text":"\"Rest later, finish the chores first\""},{"id":"c","text":"Someone asking how THEY are doing"},{"id":"d","text":"Guilt every time they sit down"},{"id":"e","text":"A friend covering so they can sleep"},{"id":"f","text":"\"Cherish every moment\" pep talks"}],"bins":[{"id":"refuel","label":"Refuels them","valence":"pos"},{"id":"drain","label":"Drains them","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"refuel","b":"drain","c":"refuel","d":"drain","e":"refuel","f":"drain"},"relearn":"Real rest, being asked about, and cover to sleep refuel a parent; chore-guilt and cheery pressure drain them.","persona":"any","source":"refuel or drain"},
  {"id":"uk-1362","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: normal adjustment after a baby, or a sign to reach for help?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Tired and snappy, but still enjoying moments"},{"id":"b","text":"Low and numb for weeks, nothing feels good"},{"id":"c","text":"Missing your old free time"},{"id":"d","text":"Frightening thoughts that won't go away"},{"id":"e","text":"Out of sync with your partner some days"},{"id":"f","text":"Hopelessness that doesn't lift"}],"bins":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal adjustment","valence":"neutral"},{"id":"help","label":"Reach for help","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"normal","b":"help","c":"normal","d":"help","e":"normal","f":"help"},"relearn":"Tired, snappy, out of sync is normal; weeks of numbness, frightening thoughts or hopelessness mean reach for help.","persona":"any","source":"normal vs needs help"},
  {"id":"uk-1363","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: keeps a parent a whole person, or shrinks them to only 'the baby's parent'?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Holding onto one hobby that's just yours"},{"id":"b","text":"Dropping every friend you had"},{"id":"c","text":"Ten honest minutes with a friend"},{"id":"d","text":"No name left but 'baby's mum/dad'"},{"id":"e","text":"Keeping a small bit of your work or study"},{"id":"f","text":"Guilt at any thought that isn't the baby"}],"bins":[{"id":"whole","label":"Stays whole","valence":"pos"},{"id":"shrinks","label":"Shrinks them","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"whole","b":"shrinks","c":"whole","d":"shrinks","e":"whole","f":"shrinks"},"relearn":"A hobby, a friend, a bit of your work keep you whole; dropping everyone and guilt at any other thought shrink you.","persona":"Divya","source":"whole vs shrunk"},
  {"id":"uk-1364","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: this supports a depleted partner, or piles more on them?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Taking the baby so they can nap"},{"id":"b","text":"\"You look exhausted, do more then\""},{"id":"c","text":"Cooking so they don't have to think"},{"id":"d","text":"Listing all they still haven't done"},{"id":"e","text":"\"How are YOU, really?\""},{"id":"f","text":"Comparing them to a tidier parent online"}],"bins":[{"id":"supports","label":"Supports them","valence":"pos"},{"id":"piles","label":"Piles on","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"supports","b":"piles","c":"supports","d":"piles","e":"supports","f":"piles"},"relearn":"Taking the baby, cooking and asking how they are support a partner; chore-lists and comparisons pile on.","persona":"Vikram","source":"support or pile on"},
  {"id":"uk-1365","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: a kind way to talk to yourself as a new parent, or a cruel one?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm learning, and that's enough today\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Everyone copes better than me\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"I'm allowed to find this hard\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"I'm failing at the one thing that matters\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Resting now helps me parent later\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"My needs don't count anymore\""}],"bins":[{"id":"kind","label":"Kind to yourself","valence":"pos"},{"id":"cruel","label":"Cruel to yourself","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"kind","b":"cruel","c":"kind","d":"cruel","e":"kind","f":"cruel"},"relearn":"Kind self-talk allows the hard and the rest; cruel self-talk compares, calls you failing, and erases your needs.","persona":"Divya","source":"kind vs cruel self-talk"},
  {"id":"uk-1366","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: this protects YOUR identity through parenthood, or hands it all away?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Saying yes to one thing you love each week"},{"id":"b","text":"Cancelling everything that was ever yours"},{"id":"c","text":"Telling your partner what you miss"},{"id":"d","text":"Waiting for permission to ever rest"},{"id":"e","text":"Naming a goal that's still your own"},{"id":"f","text":"Believing wanting anything is selfish"}],"bins":[{"id":"protect","label":"Protects you","valence":"pos"},{"id":"surrender","label":"Hands it away","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"protect","b":"surrender","c":"protect","d":"surrender","e":"protect","f":"surrender"},"relearn":"One loved thing weekly, naming what you miss and your own goal protect you; cancelling all and 'wanting is selfish' don't.","persona":"Divya","source":"protect or surrender identity"},
  {"id":"uk-1367","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: who is this moment really checking on, the baby, or the parent?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Is she feeding well?\""},{"id":"b","text":"\"Are you sleeping at all?\""},{"id":"c","text":"\"Has he put on weight?\""},{"id":"d","text":"\"How are YOU holding up?\""},{"id":"e","text":"\"Is the baby's cough better?\""},{"id":"f","text":"\"What do you need today?\""}],"bins":[{"id":"baby","label":"Checks the baby","valence":"neutral"},{"id":"parent","label":"Checks the parent","valence":"tell"}],"key":{"a":"baby","b":"parent","c":"baby","d":"parent","e":"baby","f":"parent"},"relearn":"Most questions land on the baby; the parent often goes unseen, so 'how are YOU?' is the one that's missing.","persona":"Priya","source":"who gets asked about"},
  {"id":"uk-1368","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"sort","hook":"Sort: real room for yourself, or just more pressure dressed up?","items":[{"id":"a","text":"Swapping an off-duty shift with your partner"},{"id":"b","text":"\"Do your hobby AND all the chores\""},{"id":"c","text":"Accepting a relative's offer to mind the baby"},{"id":"d","text":"\"Self-care means waking earlier to fit it in\""},{"id":"e","text":"Lowering the bar on a spotless house"},{"id":"f","text":"\"Relax, once nothing's left undone\""}],"bins":[{"id":"healthy","label":"Real room for you","valence":"pos"},{"id":"pressure","label":"Pressure in disguise","valence":"neg"}],"key":{"a":"healthy","b":"pressure","c":"healthy","d":"pressure","e":"healthy","f":"pressure"},"relearn":"Off-duty swaps, help and a lower bar make real room; 'do it all anyway' is pressure in disguise.","persona":"any","source":"room vs disguised pressure"},
  {"id":"uk-1369","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"name the need","hook":"You've not had ten minutes alone in weeks and you're fraying.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner comes home from work cheerful, and you feel close to snapping. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Keep going and hope they notice you're spent","then":"They chat about their day, and you swallow how wrung out you are."},{"text":"Snap at them for not noticing sooner","then":"They look bewildered and hurt, and the evening turns tense."},{"text":"Say plainly you need a break, and pick a time","then":"They put their bag down. \"Thank you for telling me. Saturday morning?\"","best":true},{"text":"Say \"I'm fine\" and plan to rest later","then":"Later never arrives, and the night feeds erase it."}],"why":"Naming the need is what lets it actually get met."},{"prompt":"Two weeks later, you are running on empty again. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Assume you should be coping by now","then":"You keep quiet, and the fraying builds."},{"text":"Hint loudly by sighing and slamming cupboards","then":"They can't tell what is wrong, and get defensive."},{"text":"Say what you need and ask for a regular slot","then":"They nod. \"A weekly slot makes sense. Which day works?\"","best":true},{"text":"Ask a relative to nudge your partner for you","then":"They feel ambushed, and the advice arrives with judgement."}],"why":"Asking for a regular slot keeps the need from building up again."},{"prompt":"Your partner comes home late when you'd hoped for a rest. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing and try harder to manage alone","then":"You get through the night, and resentment builds a little."},{"text":"Tell them they never care about your needs","then":"They defend themselves, and the real request gets lost."},{"text":"Say never mind and cancel the rest yourself","then":"The break quietly disappears again."},{"text":"Say you're disappointed, then agree a time together","then":"They wince, then nod. \"You're right. Tomorrow at six, no excuses.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying so and agreeing a new time keeps your need met, without blame."}],"debrief":"Name the need and arrange the break; waiting to be noticed only deepens the strain.","relearn":"Say plainly you need a break and arrange one; don't wait to be noticed."},
  {"id":"uk-1370","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"reclaim a self","hook":"Your partner says they don't even know who they are anymore.","steps":[{"prompt":"They say it quietly while folding the baby's clothes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"You're a parent now. That's who you are\"","then":"They nod, and feel more alone in the loss."},{"text":"Joke that they're still the same old person","then":"They laugh thinly, and the feeling goes back inside."},{"text":"Listen, take it seriously, and ask what they miss","then":"They put the clothes down. \"Everything, honestly. Thank you for asking.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say they're just tired and will feel like themselves soon","then":"They nod, but the loss stays unspoken."}],"why":"Taking the words seriously lets them feel seen, and that's the first step."},{"prompt":"A few days later, they mention how much they used to love dancing. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say it's hard to fit dancing in now","then":"They nod, and the memory goes back in the drawer."},{"text":"Say maybe when the baby is older","then":"The baby's age becomes the reason, and the dancing keeps waiting."},{"text":"Suggest they dance around the kitchen with the baby","then":"They smile, but it isn't the same as dancing for themselves."},{"text":"Ask about it and offer a night out to dance","then":"They blush. \"Really? I'd forgotten how much I wanted that.\"","best":true}],"why":"Offering a real evening turns a wistful memory into a plan."},{"prompt":"An old friend calls to invite them out, and they hesitate. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll manage if they really want to go","then":"They sense your reluctance, and decline."},{"text":"Remind them the baby needs them tonight","then":"They cancel, and the friend hears the flatness in their voice."},{"text":"Encourage them to go, and have the baby yourself","then":"They laugh in surprise. \"Okay. I'm going. Thank you.\"","best":true},{"text":"Tell them to ask their mother to babysit","then":"Arranging that feels like too much, and they stay in."}],"why":"Having the baby yourself is what lets them step back into themselves."}],"debrief":"Take 'I don't know who I am' seriously and help them reclaim a piece; don't erase it.","relearn":"When a partner grieves their old self, listen and help them reclaim a piece, not erase it."},
  {"id":"uk-1371","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"guilt-free hour","hook":"You feel guilty doing anything besides baby tasks, even when there is time.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner has the baby, and you have a full hour. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Fill it with extra chores to feel you earned it","then":"The hour is gone, and you're more tired than before."},{"text":"Hover by the baby monitor for the whole hour","then":"You rest little, and the hour blurs."},{"text":"Spend it scrolling other parents' perfect posts","then":"The hour vanishes, and you come back feeling behind."},{"text":"Use it for something that refuels you","then":"You read in the sunlight. You return calmer, and the hour has not hurt anyone.","best":true}],"why":"A refuelled parent returns more present, so rest is an investment."},{"prompt":"The next day, your partner says you should rest more often. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you couldn't possibly. There's too much to do","then":"They shrug, and the offer fades."},{"text":"Say you'll rest once everything is done","then":"Everything is never done, so rest keeps getting pushed back."},{"text":"Say thank you, and plan the next hour together","then":"They smile. \"Good. Every Thursday, then.\"","best":true},{"text":"Wave it off as kind but unnecessary","then":"They stop offering, thinking you don't want it."}],"why":"Accepting the offer and naming a time makes rest a routine, not a rarity."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your mother-in-law finds you sitting with tea and calls it a waste when there's so much to do. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell yourself good mothers don't sit with tea","then":"You gulp the tea and stand up, and the calm goes with it."},{"text":"Say she doesn't understand modern parenting","then":"She goes silent, hurt, and the talk closes."},{"text":"Say it's what keeps you going, and stay put","then":"She sniffs, but offers to hold the baby a while.","best":true},{"text":"Agree with her, and fill the time with chores","then":"You are more tired and less yourself."}],"why":"Holding your ground calmly shows that rest helps the whole household."}],"debrief":"Spend a rare free hour refuelling; chores-to-earn-it rob you of the rest.","relearn":"Use a free hour to refuel without guilt; don't 'earn' it with endless chores."},
  {"id":"uk-1372","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"cover so they can return","hook":"You notice your partner has stopped doing everything they used to love.","steps":[{"prompt":"They wistfully mention their old choir. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Tell them to just make time somehow","then":"They nod, and add \"find time\" to a list with no free hours."},{"text":"Say choirs meet at awkward hours anyway","then":"They agree, and let the idea go."},{"text":"Suggest they hum at home instead","then":"They smile politely, and the choir stays a memory."},{"text":"Offer to cover, so they can pick it back up","then":"They look up, hopeful. \"Would you? Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"Concrete cover gives them a real door back to themselves."},{"prompt":"A friend from the choir messages to ask if they're coming back. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Offer to help if they sort out the arrangements","then":"They tally what needs arranging, and decide it's too much."},{"text":"Cover the baby but leave dinner for them to prep first","then":"They cook first, then arrive at practice out of breath and late."},{"text":"Suggest they wait until next term","then":"Next term arrives with new reasons to wait."},{"text":"Cover Wednesdays fully: dinner, bath and bedtime","then":"They read the message again, and start typing a yes.","best":true}],"why":"Owning the whole evening removes the arranging that keeps them home."},{"prompt":"After a hard week, they say they're too tired to bother with the choir. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say fine, and that there's always next week","then":"Next week has its own list, and the choir slips away."},{"text":"Point out how much effort this has already taken","then":"Their guilt grows, and they stay home."},{"text":"Push them to go, since they've been so keen","then":"They go tense and resentful, and enjoy none of it."},{"text":"Ask what would make it possible, and cover that too","then":"They think. \"A nap first, actually.\" You give them one.","best":true}],"why":"Meeting the exhaustion with more cover keeps their own life within reach."}],"debrief":"Offer concrete cover so they can reclaim something they love; don't just say 'make time'.","relearn":"Cover the load so a partner can reclaim something they love; don't just say 'make time'."},
  {"id":"uk-1373","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"reach for support","hook":"Weeks in, you feel persistently low, flat and tearful, not just tired.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner asks how you're doing, and \"fine\" is on your tongue. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide parents shouldn't feel this way and hide it","then":"The flatness gets heavier, and no one knows why you're quiet."},{"text":"Put it down to lack of sleep and wait","then":"Weeks pass, and the sleep improves, but the flatness doesn't."},{"text":"Tell them it feels heavier than tired","then":"They put down the phone. \"Thank you for telling me. What can I do?\"","best":true},{"text":"Post about it online to see if others feel it too","then":"Strangers reply kindly, but nobody who knows you does."}],"why":"Telling someone close is the first step, and it is never your fault."},{"prompt":"A week later, your partner gently suggests seeing a doctor. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Insist you're fine and change the subject","then":"They let it go, but they worry, and so do you."},{"text":"Say you'll go once things calm down","then":"Things don't calm down, and the appointment never gets booked."},{"text":"Agree, and ask them to come with you","then":"They smile with relief. \"Of course. I'll book it.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say it's silly to bother a doctor over sadness","then":"They nod, unsure, and the idea fades."}],"why":"Saying yes, with company, makes the first step feel possible."},{"prompt":"It is a hard night and the hopelessness feels heavy. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Wait for morning and see how you feel","then":"Morning comes, and the heaviness is still there."},{"text":"Stay awake scrolling online for answers","then":"Hours pass, and the heaviness is heavier."},{"text":"Tell yourself others have it worse","then":"The thought lightens nothing, and you feel more alone."},{"text":"Call Tele-MANAS 14416 and tell your partner","then":"A calm voice listens. Your partner sits beside you, and the night feels less alone.","best":true}],"why":"Reaching support, like Tele-MANAS 14416, meets a treatable thing with help."}],"debrief":"Lasting low mood after birth is real and treatable. Reach for support instead of hiding it.","relearn":"Treat lasting low mood as real and reach for support; don't hide it in silence."},
  {"id":"uk-1374","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"hold the rest boundary","hook":"After months of broken sleep, you've planned your first evening off since the baby came.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says you're spoiling yourself for planning an evening off. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Cancel the evening to avoid the disapproval","then":"Your aunt is pleased, and you spend the evening resentful."},{"text":"Argue that she is old-fashioned and rude","then":"Voices rise, and your evening off is overshadowed by the row."},{"text":"Say calmly that rest helps you, and you're going","then":"Your aunt sniffs, but says nothing more.","best":true},{"text":"Go, but feel guilty and cut it short","then":"You come home early, and you've rested no more than before."}],"why":"Holding the boundary calmly protects your rest without a fight."},{"prompt":"A week later, your aunt tells the family you want to \"go out and leave the baby\". What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say nothing and let the story spread","then":"By dinner, everyone has an opinion on your evening."},{"text":"Confront her in front of everyone","then":"The room goes quiet, and the gap between you widens."},{"text":"Promise not to go out in the evenings again","then":"You get peace, and lose your evenings."},{"text":"Tell your partner and ask them to back you","then":"They tell the family plainly that you planned it together.","best":true}],"why":"Your partner's backing shows the family it's a shared decision."},{"prompt":"Your partner suggests an evening off this month. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say maybe, once the relatives stop talking","then":"The relatives never stop, so the date never comes."},{"text":"Say no, to avoid another fuss","then":"There is no fuss, and no rest either."},{"text":"Say yes but keep it secret from the family","then":"It stays a secret, and every mention makes you tense."},{"text":"Say yes, and pick a date so it's fixed","then":"You mark it on the calendar. It's yours.","best":true}],"why":"Fixing a date turns a boundary into a habit that lasts."}],"debrief":"Hold the boundary calmly. Your rest helps you parent; don't cancel for approval.","relearn":"Hold the boundary on your rest; don't cancel it for someone's approval."},
  {"id":"uk-1375","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"ask to be asked","hook":"You realise everyone has been asking about the baby, and no one has asked about you.","steps":[{"prompt":"A cousin visits and gushes over the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Decide your feelings don't matter next to the baby's","then":"The unseen feeling sits heavier, and no one knows."},{"text":"Smile and talk only about the baby","then":"The cousin leaves happy, and you feel more invisible."},{"text":"Tell your cousin: \"I could use being asked too\"","then":"Your cousin pauses. \"I'm sorry. How are you, really?\"","best":true},{"text":"Complain about everyone in the family group chat","then":"The chat turns awkward, and nobody quite knows how to reply."}],"why":"Saying plainly what you need lets someone meet it."},{"prompt":"That evening, your partner asks about the baby's feeds. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer only about the baby, as you always do","then":"The talk stays on the baby, and you feel unseen again."},{"text":"Say \"Never mind\" and walk away","then":"They call after you, confused about what they did."},{"text":"Answer, then say you'd like to be asked about you too","then":"They blink. \"You're right. How are you today?\"","best":true},{"text":"Snap that nobody ever asks about you","then":"They're stung. \"I do ask.\""}],"why":"Adding one plain sentence turns the question into a chance to be seen."},{"prompt":"A week later, a friend asks how the baby is, then how you are. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say \"I'm great\" to keep it short","then":"The friend nods, and the honest answer goes unsaid."},{"text":"Turn it back to the baby straight away","then":"The friend follows your lead, and the chance passes."},{"text":"Answer honestly about how you are","then":"The friend goes quiet, then says, \"Thank you for telling me.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say you should be happy, so you've no right to complain","then":"The friend frowns. \"Of course you can,\" but you have already changed the subject."}],"why":"Answering honestly lets people show you the care they're offering."},{"prompt":"A relative asks how the baby is for the fifth time this week. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer curtly and walk away","then":"She looks bewildered, and the moment passes."},{"text":"Answer brightly and leave it there","then":"She smiles, and you're unseen once more."},{"text":"Snap that no one ever asks about you","then":"She flinches, and the talk closes."},{"text":"Answer, and add that you're okay but tired","then":"She pauses. \"Oh, beta. Sit. Tell me.\"","best":true}],"why":"A short honest add-on makes it easy for people to ask about you."}],"debrief":"Say plainly you'd like to be asked about too; don't rank yourself last.","relearn":"Name that you'd like to be asked about too; don't rank yourself last."},
  {"id":"uk-1376","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"hear the ache","hook":"Your wife jokes that she \"used to have a personality\", and you can hear the ache in it.","steps":[{"prompt":"She laughs as she says it, over the dishes. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Laugh it off and move on quickly","then":"She laughs with you, and the ache goes back inside."},{"text":"Tell her she's still the same person","then":"She smiles tightly. It sounds like reassurance without listening."},{"text":"Take it seriously and ask what she misses","then":"She stops washing. \"Honestly? Painting. And my friends.\"","best":true},{"text":"Joke back that you still like her anyway","then":"She smiles, but nothing has been said."}],"why":"Answering the ache with a real question shows you heard it."},{"prompt":"A few days later, she mentions how much she misses her old friends. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say she can see them once things calm down","then":"Things don't calm down, and the friends drift."},{"text":"Suggest she invite them over and cook for them","then":"She'd host and clean, so it becomes more work than rest."},{"text":"Tell her she should make more effort","then":"She stiffens, hurt."},{"text":"Offer to plan an evening out for her with them","then":"She stares. \"You'd do that? Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"Planning it for her removes the effort that keeps her home."},{"prompt":"Her friends invite her out, and she says she feels guilty leaving you with the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say fine, go if you must","then":"She hears the reluctance and stays home."},{"text":"Say go, then message her every half hour","then":"She keeps checking her phone and enjoys it less."},{"text":"Say she needn't go if she's unsure","then":"She takes the out, and the invitation lapses."},{"text":"Say you'd love her to go. Tonight is hers","then":"She laughs, teary. \"Okay. I'm going.\"","best":true}],"why":"Making the evening hers with no strings answers the ache with action."}],"debrief":"Hear the ache behind the joke and act on it; don't just laugh it off.","relearn":"Hear the ache behind a joke about a lost self and act; don't laugh it off."},
  {"id":"uk-1377","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"any","source":"take the rest","hook":"The baby was up all night, and you are running on almost no sleep.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt offers to hold the baby while you sleep. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Refuse, sure that resting means you're not coping","then":"You stay up, and the tiredness deepens by the afternoon."},{"text":"Accept, but lie awake listening for the baby","then":"The nap is broken, and you feel no fresher."},{"text":"Take the nap and let her have the baby","then":"You sleep two solid hours and wake up clearer.","best":true},{"text":"Accept, but do the ironing before you lie down","then":"The nap shrinks to ten minutes, and you wake groggy."}],"why":"Taking the offered rest banks real recovery for later."},{"prompt":"Your neighbour remarks that you look exhausted and asks how you're coping. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you're coping fine, thanks","then":"She nods, and the truth stays with you."},{"text":"Joke that you'll sleep when the child is eighteen","then":"She laughs and moves on. Nothing changes."},{"text":"Say honestly that you're worn out and could use help","then":"She nods. \"Then I'll drop off some food tomorrow.\"","best":true},{"text":"Say she should mind her own business","then":"She leaves offended, and the next offer of help is less likely."}],"why":"Being honest about being tired lets help find its way to you."},{"prompt":"Your partner will be at work all day tomorrow and asks if you'll manage. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Say you'll manage because you can't keep asking","then":"You struggle through, and the fatigue deepens."},{"text":"Say you'll nap only if the baby sleeps first","then":"The baby doesn't, and neither do you."},{"text":"Ask your partner to decide for you","then":"They guess, and the plan doesn't match what you need."},{"text":"Ask your aunt for a nap slot, and say when","then":"Your aunt agrees. \"Two o'clock, then. Come, I'll take her.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking for rest, with a set time, keeps recovery going."}],"debrief":"Take the offered rest; refusing it to look strong only drains you further.","relearn":"Take offered rest; refusing it to look strong only burns you out."},
  {"id":"uk-1378","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Divya","source":"highlight reel","hook":"You catch yourself comparing your messy days to a glowing parent online.","steps":[{"prompt":"It's 2am during a feed, and you're scrolling a picture-perfect nursery. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Conclude you're simply worse at this than everyone","then":"The scrolling continues, and the feeling of failing grows."},{"text":"Scroll on, looking for tips to copy","then":"By dawn you have twelve new to-dos and no sleep."},{"text":"Message the parent to ask how she does it","then":"She replies with a smiley, and you feel just as far away."},{"text":"Call it a highlight reel and put the phone down","then":"The room is quiet, and your chest loosens a little.","best":true}],"why":"Seeing a highlight reel for what it is stops the comparison."},{"prompt":"A relative praises a friend's baby who sleeps ten hours. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Agree that you must be doing something wrong","then":"The relative nods, and you leave lunch feeling small."},{"text":"Ask for the friend's exact routine and copy it","then":"The routine doesn't fit your baby, and you feel like failing again."},{"text":"Say your baby is just a difficult sleeper","then":"The word sticks, and now everyone treats her as a problem."},{"text":"Smile, and remember every family's nights differ","then":"You sip your tea, and the comment loses its bite.","best":true}],"why":"Remembering how different every family's nights are keeps comparison in check."},{"prompt":"A friend messages that some days she feels like a fraud. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Reply \"You're doing great!\" and leave it there","then":"She sends a heart, and the honest moment passes."},{"text":"Say you feel fine and she's overthinking","then":"She apologises for bothering you."},{"text":"Send her a list of tips to feel more confident","then":"She thanks you, but now feels like a project to fix."},{"text":"Tell her about your own messy days too","then":"She replies with three laughing faces and \"Me too. Thank you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Sharing the real day breaks the highlight-reel spell for both of you."}],"debrief":"Treat a picture-perfect social media feed as a highlight reel; don't read it as proof you're failing.","relearn":"See the perfect parent online as a highlight reel, not proof you're failing."},
  {"id":"uk-1379","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"branch","persona":"Vikram","source":"you haven't disappeared","hook":"Your partner whispers that they sometimes feel they've \"disappeared\".","steps":[{"prompt":"They say it in the dark, after the baby has finally slept. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Get defensive and list what you've been doing","then":"They stop talking, and the honest moment closes."},{"text":"Say they haven't disappeared, only that they're tired","then":"They turn over, unheard."},{"text":"Squeeze their hand and say nothing","then":"It's warm, but they still don't know what you think."},{"text":"Say you see them, and ask what would help","then":"They turn toward you. \"Some time that's mine, maybe. I don't know yet.\"","best":true}],"why":"Being seen, and asked, pulls them back from feeling invisible."},{"prompt":"A guest asks your partner only about the baby. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Answer for your partner and move on","then":"Your partner is spoken over, and stays quiet."},{"text":"Say nothing, since guests always ask about babies","then":"They look at you, hoping you'd say something."},{"text":"Tease them that everyone loves the baby more","then":"The joke lands hard, and they smile thinly."},{"text":"Ask your partner something about themselves in front of everyone","then":"They light up, and the room turns to listen.","best":true}],"why":"Showing others how to ask makes your partner visible again."},{"prompt":"Your partner says they need something that's just theirs, but don't know what. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Suggest a big holiday to find themselves again","then":"It's a nice idea, but the days at home stay the same."},{"text":"Suggest they figure it out when the baby is older","then":"They nod, and the wish goes quiet again."},{"text":"Give them a long list of hobbies to try","then":"The list becomes another task, and they feel more tired."},{"text":"Help them choose one thing, and protect a regular time","then":"They pick pottery, and you both mark Thursday in the diary.","best":true}],"why":"Protecting a regular time makes their own space real, not just a wish."}],"debrief":"Tell them you see them and protect space that's theirs; don't get defensive.","relearn":"Tell a fading partner you see them and protect space that's theirs; don't get defensive."},
  {"id":"uk-1380","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each worn-out thought to the self-kindness it calls for.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I've achieved nothing today\"","right":"You kept a small human alive"},{"left":"\"I should be enjoying every bit\"","right":"Survival days are fine"},{"left":"\"I'm so behind on everything\"","right":"Lower the bar; the list can wait"},{"left":"\"I can't ask for more help\"","right":"Asking is a skill, not a failure"},{"left":"\"I don't recognise myself\"","right":"Reclaim one small piece that's yours"}],"relearn":"Worn-out thoughts call for kindness: you kept a human alive, survival days are fine, lower the bar, ask, reclaim.","persona":"Divya","source":"thought to kindness"},
  {"id":"uk-1381","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each depleted sign to the support it points to.","pairs":[{"left":"Snapping over tiny things","right":"A real, uninterrupted break"},{"left":"Feeling invisible behind the pram","right":"Someone asking how you are"},{"left":"Low and flat for weeks","right":"Support like Tele-MANAS 14416"},{"left":"Lost touch with old friends","right":"One reconnecting message sent"},{"left":"Can't remember the last hobby","right":"A protected hour to pick one up"}],"relearn":"Snapping needs a break; invisibility needs asking; lasting low mood needs help; lost friends and hobbies need a small return.","persona":"Divya","source":"sign to support"},
  {"id":"uk-1382","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each thing a whole parent keeps to why it matters.","pairs":[{"left":"A name beyond 'baby's mum/dad'","right":"You're a person, not only a role"},{"left":"Ten minutes that are only yours","right":"Refuels you to give the rest"},{"left":"One friendship still tended","right":"Keeps you from disappearing"},{"left":"A goal that's still your own","right":"Models a full life for your child"},{"left":"Permission to find this hard","right":"Stops guilt from grinding you down"}],"relearn":"A name, your ten minutes, a friendship, a goal and permission to struggle each keep a parent a whole person.","persona":"Divya","source":"keep and why"},
  {"id":"uk-1383","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each unhelpful line to the supportive one it should become.","pairs":[{"left":"\"You can rest once it's all done\"","right":"\"Go rest now, the rest can wait\""},{"left":"\"Cherish every moment\"","right":"\"This is hard; I've got you\""},{"left":"\"How's the baby?\"","right":"\"How are YOU doing, really?\""},{"left":"\"You're just a mum now\"","right":"\"You're still you, and I see you\""},{"left":"\"Other parents manage fine\"","right":"\"You're doing more than you can see\""}],"relearn":"Swap 'rest when it's done', 'cherish it all', 'how's the baby' and 'just a mum' for rest now, I've got you, how are YOU, you're still you.","persona":"any","source":"swap the line"},
  {"id":"uk-1384","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each way to protect a partner's self to a moment it fits.","pairs":[{"left":"Cover so they nap","right":"They've not slept properly in days"},{"left":"Send them out with friends","right":"They've not left the house in weeks"},{"left":"Ask how they are first","right":"Everyone's only asked about the baby"},{"left":"Defend their hobby time","right":"A relative calls it spoiling themselves"},{"left":"Encourage support","right":"They've been low and flat for weeks"}],"relearn":"Cover for a nap, send them out, ask first, defend their time, encourage support. Each fits a different worn-out moment.","persona":"Vikram","source":"protect and when"},
  {"id":"uk-1385","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each parent's quiet truth to the kind reply it deserves.","pairs":[{"left":"\"I miss who I used to be\"","right":"\"That self is still in there\""},{"left":"\"I feel guilty resting\"","right":"\"Rest helps you parent\""},{"left":"\"No one asks about me\"","right":"\"I'm asking. How are you?\""},{"left":"\"I'm scared I'm not enough\"","right":"\"You're under load, not failing\""},{"left":"\"I can't keep going like this\"","right":"\"Then we change it, together\""}],"relearn":"Met with kindness: a missed self gets room, guilt gets permission, the unseen get asked, fear gets reassured.","persona":"Divya","source":"truth to kind reply"},
  {"id":"uk-1386","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each small reclaiming act to the part of you it feeds.","pairs":[{"left":"Texting an old friend back","right":"Your friendships"},{"left":"Reading two pages at night","right":"Your own mind"},{"left":"A short walk without the pram","right":"Your body and breath"},{"left":"Saving for a course someday","right":"Your future self"},{"left":"Cooking a dish you love","right":"Your everyday joy"}],"relearn":"Small acts feed real parts of you: a text, two pages, a walk, a saved-for plan and a loved dish keep the whole person alive.","persona":"Divya","source":"act to part of you"},
  {"id":"uk-1387","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"match","hook":"Match each myth about new parents to the truth that frees it.","pairs":[{"left":"\"Good parents have no needs\"","right":"Needs met keep you steady to give"},{"left":"\"Resting is lazy\"","right":"Recovery is real work too"},{"left":"\"Asking help means failing\"","right":"No one was meant to do this alone"},{"left":"\"Your dreams are over\"","right":"They shift shape, they don't vanish"},{"left":"\"Sad feelings mean ingratitude\"","right":"Love and hard feelings can both be true"}],"relearn":"Each myth has a truth: needs steady you, rest is work, asking isn't failing, dreams shift not end, love and ache coexist.","persona":"any","source":"myth to truth"},
  {"id":"uk-1388","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: you can love your baby with everything and still be your own whole person. Sit right?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Sits right","Both at once","Still me"],"affirm":"Loving your child fully and staying a whole person aren't opposites; keeping a self is part of parenting well.","relearn":"You can love your baby fully and stay a whole person; keeping a self is part of parenting well.","persona":"Divya","source":"love fully, stay whole"},
  {"id":"uk-1389","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: resting when you're empty isn't quitting on your family. It's how you keep showing up. Agree?","prompt":"Where do you land?","options":["Agree","Rest helps","Not quitting"],"affirm":"Rest isn't abandoning anyone; a refuelled parent has more patience and presence to bring back to the baby.","relearn":"Resting when you're empty isn't quitting; a refuelled parent brings back more patience and presence.","persona":"any","source":"rest is showing up"},
  {"id":"uk-1390","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: 'I used to be someone else' is something so many new parents quietly feel. Does that land?","prompt":"How does it sit?","options":["It lands","I've felt it","Good to hear"],"affirm":"Grieving the person you were is common and valid; naming it is how you start to hold the old and new you together.","relearn":"Grieving who you were is common and valid; naming it helps you hold the old and new you together.","persona":"Divya","source":"grieving the old self"},
  {"id":"uk-1391","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: your wellbeing isn't a treat for after the to-do list. It's part of the baby's wellbeing. Feel true?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Feels true","Part of it","Worth keeping"],"affirm":"A cared-for parent is steadier, warmer and more present; looking after you is looking after your child too.","relearn":"Your wellbeing is part of the baby's; a cared-for parent is steadier, warmer and more present.","persona":"Divya","source":"your wellbeing counts"},
  {"id":"uk-1392","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: there's no medal for doing all of it alone and running yourself into the ground. Sit right?","prompt":"Where do you land?","options":["Sits right","No medal","Ask for help"],"affirm":"Pride in doing it all alone usually costs you the most; sharing and asking aren't failures, they're how it stays sustainable.","relearn":"There's no medal for doing it all alone; sharing and asking are how parenting stays sustainable.","persona":"Vikram","source":"no medal for alone"},
  {"id":"uk-1393","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: on the hard days, you can be a tired, struggling parent AND a good one at the same time. True?","prompt":"How does it sit?","options":["True","Both at once","Needed that"],"affirm":"Struggling doesn't cancel good; the tired, doubting days are part of devoted parenting, not evidence against it.","relearn":"You can be a struggling parent and a good one at once; hard days don't cancel your devotion.","persona":"Divya","source":"struggling and good"},
  {"id":"uk-1394","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"reflect","hook":"Lensy: keeping one small thing that's just yours doesn't take anything away from your child. Agree?","prompt":"What do you think?","options":["Agree","Mine still","No harm"],"affirm":"One small thing that's yours doesn't shortchange the baby; it keeps you a person they'll one day be proud to know.","relearn":"Keeping one small thing that's yours doesn't shortchange your child; it keeps you a whole person.","persona":"Divya","source":"one small thing yours"},
  {"id":"uk-1395","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two lines that quietly tell a new parent they don't matter. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"Rest when you can, you've earned it\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You'll have time for yourself later, not now\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"How are YOU coping, honestly?\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"A good mum doesn't need a break\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Let me take the baby so you can sleep\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Your time comes later' and 'a good mum needs no break' both teach a parent their needs don't count, the red flags.","relearn":"Lines that push a parent's needs to 'later' or call needing a break a failing tell them they don't matter.","persona":"any","source":"you don't matter lines"},
  {"id":"uk-1396","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two replies that dismiss a parent's lost-self feeling. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"That makes sense; let's get you a piece of it back\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"You're a parent now, forget all that\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I hear you; you're still you to me\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Stop being dramatic, everyone manages\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"What do you miss most?\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Forget all that' and 'stop being dramatic' dismiss a real grief instead of meeting it, the red flags.","relearn":"Replies that say 'forget who you were' or 'stop being dramatic' dismiss a parent's grief.","persona":"Vikram","source":"dismissing the lost self"},
  {"id":"uk-1397","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two signs this is more than tiredness and needs help. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Snappy after a broken night, better by lunch","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Numb and joyless for weeks on end","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Missing your old lie-ins","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Frightening thoughts that won't leave","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Some days feeling out of sync with your partner","trick":false}],"why":"Weeks of numbness and persistent frightening thoughts are signs to reach for help, not normal tiredness, the red flags.","relearn":"Lasting numbness or frightening thoughts go past tiredness and mean reach for help, like Tele-MANAS 14416.","persona":"Divya","source":"past tiredness signs"},
  {"id":"uk-1398","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two comments that pile guilt on a new mother. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"You're doing brilliantly, take a breather\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"In my day we never complained or rested\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"What can I take off your plate today?\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"Real mothers cherish every second\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Go nap, I'll watch the baby\"","trick":false}],"why":"'We never rested' and 'real mothers cherish every second' shame a tired parent for being human, the red flags.","relearn":"Comments that brag 'we never rested' or demand you 'cherish every second' pile guilt on an already tired parent.","persona":"Priya","source":"guilt-piling comments"},
  {"id":"uk-1399","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two ways a partner makes it harder, not easier. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"Asks how she's really doing, then listens","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Calls his own day with the baby 'babysitting'","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Takes the night feeds without being asked","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"Says she should be grateful she stays home","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Books her an evening out with friends","trick":false}],"why":"Calling his own parenting 'babysitting' and demanding gratitude both add load, the red flags.","relearn":"A partner who calls his own care 'babysitting' or demands gratitude makes it harder.","persona":"Vikram","source":"harder not easier"},
  {"id":"uk-1400","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two things a parent says that mean step in now. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"\"I'm shattered but we had a sweet morning\"","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"\"Nothing feels good and hasn't for weeks\"","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"\"I really miss a full night's sleep\"","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"\"They'd be better off without me\"","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"\"Some days drag, but we get through\"","trick":false}],"why":"'Nothing has felt good for weeks' and 'better off without me' are distress signals, the red flags.","relearn":"'Nothing feels good for weeks' and 'better off without me' are distress signals. Step in and get help.","persona":"Divya","source":"step-in signals"},
  {"id":"uk-1401","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"spot","hook":"Spot the two myths hiding among real self-care truths. Tap the red flags.","scene":[{"id":"a","text":"A short walk can lift a heavy day","trick":false},{"id":"b","text":"Self-care is selfish when you're a parent","trick":true},{"id":"c","text":"Asking for help is a strength","trick":false},{"id":"d","text":"You must earn rest by finishing everything","trick":true},{"id":"e","text":"Your wellbeing is part of the baby's","trick":false}],"why":"'Self-care is selfish' and 'earn rest by finishing everything' are the myths that keep parents depleted, the red flags.","relearn":"'Self-care is selfish' and 'earn rest first' are myths; real self-care needs no earning and isn't selfish.","persona":"any","source":"myths among truths"},
  {"id":"uk-1402","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"i need to rest first","hook":"Your mother-in-law keeps adding tasks while you're clearly running on empty.","setup":"You've just put the baby down after a rough night.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your mother-in-law says: \"Since you're up, can you chop the vegetables and fold the washing?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, I'll get to it. It's fine.\"","then":"She nods, pleased, and adds one more task."},{"text":"\"You always pile more onto me, don't you.\"","then":"She stiffens. \"I only asked for two small things.\""},{"text":"\"I want to help, but I'm spent. I need to rest.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Hm. You do look pale. Go, lie down.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll do it, but please don't ask again today.\"","then":"She sighs, and the chill lasts until dinner."}],"why":"Saying you're spent, kindly, protects your rest without a fight."},{"prompt":"The next afternoon, your mother-in-law sees you lying down and says: \"Rest, now? I never rested at your age.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine, you're right. I'll get up now.\"","then":"You get up, and the exhaustion comes with a bill for tomorrow."},{"text":"\"That was your time. This is mine.\"","then":"She goes silent, and the door shuts hard."},{"text":"\"Ask my partner to help. I'm lying down.\"","then":"Your partner is caught, unsure what you've agreed."},{"text":"\"I know you carried a lot, Ma. I need an hour.\"","then":"She sighs. \"One hour, then. I'll manage.\"","best":true}],"why":"Respecting what she carried while holding your need keeps the peace."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"Ma says you're not helping enough.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then tell Ma to stop asking me for things.\"","then":"They stiffen, caught between the two of you."},{"text":"\"Maybe she's right. I'll try even harder.\"","then":"You try, and the fatigue deepens."},{"text":"\"I'm exhausted. Can we ask Ma to share chores?\"","then":"They nod slowly. \"Okay. Let's talk to her together.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ma is unfair. I won't speak to her.\"","then":"Silence sits in the house for days."}],"why":"Bringing your partner in makes the load a shared problem."},{"prompt":"A week later, your mother-in-law says: \"You look so tired. Should I take her for an hour?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, it's fine. I can do it myself.\"","then":"She shrugs, and the offer isn't repeated."},{"text":"\"Only if you promise not to feed her sweets.\"","then":"She stiffens, and the offer feels like a test."},{"text":"\"Yes please, Ma. An hour would help me so much.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Good. Go and sleep.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Are you sure? I don't want to be a bother.\"","then":"She says she's sure, but the moment cools."}],"why":"Saying yes plainly turns a passing offer into real rest."}],"relearn":"Naming that you're spent and need rest first protects you without a fight, instead of silently taking on more."},
  {"id":"uk-1403","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"ask about me too","hook":"A friend asks how the baby is, and you'd love to be asked about you too.","setup":"Your friend calls while the baby naps.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your friend asks: \"How's the baby? Is she sleeping?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She's great. That's all that matters, really.\"","then":"Your friend chats about her, and the call ends with you unseen."},{"text":"\"I'm fine. Don't worry about me at all.\"","then":"She says \"Good!\" and moves on."},{"text":"\"She's wonderful. I'm exhausted, and could use a question too.\"","then":"Your friend pauses. \"Oh. Of course. How are you?\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone forgets I exist these days.\"","then":"Your friend is taken aback, and pulls back."}],"why":"Warm honesty invites a friend to see the person behind the parent."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your friend messages: \"Thinking of you. How are you?\"","options":[{"text":"\"All good, thanks. Hope you're well!\"","then":"She replies with a thumbs-up, and the thread ends."},{"text":"\"Busy. Will talk soon.\"","then":"Soon doesn't come."},{"text":"\"Honestly, tired and a bit lost. A call would help.\"","then":"She rings within minutes. \"Tell me everything.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"How's your job? I miss hearing about normal things.\"","then":"She chats about work, and your feelings stay out of it."}],"why":"Naming the feeling and one small request turns a text into support."},{"prompt":"Your friend visits with coffee and asks: \"So, what would help most?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Nothing. I'm fine, honestly.\"","then":"She nods and leaves the coffee, and you stay tired."},{"text":"\"Some advice on how other mums cope.\"","then":"She gives tips, and the list makes you feel behind."},{"text":"\"Tell me about your life. I'll just listen.\"","then":"You listen for an hour, and feel more depleted."},{"text":"\"An hour to sleep. Could you hold the baby?\"","then":"She smiles. \"Go. I've got her.\"","best":true}],"why":"Asking for one concrete thing lets a friend actually help."}],"relearn":"Saying you'd like to be asked about too lets a friend see the person behind the parent, not just the baby."},
  {"id":"uk-1404","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"be you tonight","hook":"Your wife has lost herself in the baby's days, and you want her to take a real evening for herself.","setup":"She refolds the same baby vests, looking far away.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your wife says: \"I can't remember the last time I saw my friends.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Maybe go out once you've sorted things at home.\"","then":"She nods, and the list at home has no end."},{"text":"\"Why don't you invite them here instead?\"","then":"She would host and tidy, so it's more work than a break."},{"text":"\"I know. Life changes after a baby.\"","then":"She smiles sadly, and the missing stays."},{"text":"\"Go out with your friends tonight. I've got the baby.\"","then":"She looks up, stunned. \"Tonight? Really?\"","best":true}],"why":"An evening with no conditions hands her back a piece of herself."},{"prompt":"Your wife says: \"Even if I went out, what if she cries and you can't settle her?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She'll be fine. Babies cry.\"","then":"She hears no plan, and stays worried."},{"text":"\"I'll call you if there's a problem.\"","then":"She goes, but keeps her phone in her lap all evening."},{"text":"\"I'll work it out. She's my daughter too.\"","then":"She laughs a little. \"Okay. That's fair.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Just leave detailed notes on everything.\"","then":"She spends an hour on notes, and the evening is half gone."}],"why":"Trusting yourself with your own child means she can actually go."},{"prompt":"One Friday, she comes back from seeing friends, glowing, and asks: \"Is the kitchen a mess?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Yes. I couldn't do everything. Sorry.\"","then":"She starts tidying at once, and the glow fades."},{"text":"\"A bit. Perhaps clean it tomorrow?\"","then":"She sighs, and the evening turns back into a to-do list."},{"text":"\"It's fine. Tonight was yours. How was it?\"","then":"She sits down and tells you everything, glowing.","best":true},{"text":"\"It's a wreck. Why do I even bother?\"","then":"She flinches, wondering if she should have gone."}],"why":"Asking about her evening, not the chores, keeps the break hers."},{"prompt":"A week later, she says: \"I want to go out again, but I feel bad asking.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Okay, but not this week. I'm tired.\"","then":"The wish waits, and gets quieter."},{"text":"\"Only if you've done everything first.\"","then":"She agrees, and the list wins."},{"text":"\"Sure, but you owe me the same night next week.\"","then":"It turns her rest into a debt, and she feels she must pay it back."},{"text":"\"You don't have to ask. Pick a night.\"","then":"She smiles. \"Friday?\" \"Friday.\"","best":true}],"why":"Making the offer standing removes the guilt of asking."},{"prompt":"Her friend messages you: \"Thank you for last Friday. She has been so happy.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Happy to help her out. It was no trouble.\"","then":"Your wife hears it later, and her evening starts to sound like a favour."},{"text":"\"Tell her not to make a habit of it.\"","then":"Your wife hears about it, and feels awkward."},{"text":"\"I did it all, and she never noticed.\"","then":"Your wife hears about that, too, and the warmth cools."},{"text":"\"She's happier, and so am I. It should be regular.\"","then":"The friend replies with three hearts.","best":true}],"why":"Treating it as regular, shared parenting, not a favour, keeps it going."}],"relearn":"A no-strings evening that says 'be you for a few hours' hands a partner back a piece of herself."},
  {"id":"uk-1405","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"just ask for rest","hook":"You're drowning and your partner hasn't noticed, so instead of hoping, you'll ask.","setup":"Your partner comes in from work and puts the keys down.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"Hi. How was your day?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Fine, don't worry about me. I'll manage.\"","then":"They nod, relieved, and you are still on empty."},{"text":"\"It was the same. You'd know if you were here.\"","then":"They stiffen, and the evening starts on a sour note."},{"text":"\"I'm on empty. Can you take her for two hours?\"","then":"They set their bag down. \"Yes. Go and lie down.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Long. I'm sure yours was longer, though.\"","then":"They agree eagerly, and you never mention what you need."}],"why":"Asking plainly, with a time, gets you the rest instead of hoping for it."},{"prompt":"The next evening, your partner says: \"I've got a late work call tonight, and I'm wiped out too.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Forget it. I'll just do it all myself.\"","then":"They open their mouth, close it, and the night is yours alone."},{"text":"\"Your work is always more important.\"","then":"They flinch. \"That's not fair.\""},{"text":"\"I get that. Can you take her after the call?\"","then":"They think. \"Yes. Eight to ten. I'll set an alarm.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Okay, no problem. I'll rest when she does.\"","then":"She doesn't sleep, and neither do you."}],"why":"Keeping the ask, and moving the time, respects them both."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I feel like I'm always the last to know you're struggling.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You should just have noticed.\"","then":"They shrink. \"I'm not a mind reader.\""},{"text":"\"It's fine. I hate making a fuss.\"","then":"They nod, and you both keep guessing."},{"text":"\"I know. I'll tell you sooner. You can ask me too.\"","then":"They breathe out. \"Deal. Ask me, too.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You're right. It's all your fault, isn't it.\"","then":"Blame drops between you, and the ask is lost."}],"why":"Agreeing to tell and to ask makes noticing a shared habit."},{"prompt":"Two weeks on, your partner asks: \"Anything you need this week?\"","options":[{"text":"\"No, I'm fine. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"They nod, and you're back to hoping."},{"text":"\"You should just know what I need by now.\"","then":"They freeze. \"Tell me, and I'll do it.\""},{"text":"\"Whatever you can spare, really.\"","then":"They spare a little, and you feel unsupported."},{"text":"\"Yes. Thursday evening and Saturday morning.\"","then":"They write it down. \"Thursday and Saturday. Done.\"","best":true}],"why":"Naming exact times turns a general offer into real rest."}],"relearn":"Asking plainly for the rest you need gets it met, where hoping to be noticed usually leaves you carrying it alone."},
  {"id":"uk-1406","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Vikram","source":"self-time isn't selfish","hook":"Your partner says they feel guilty taking any time for themselves, and you want to ease that.","setup":"They put their shoes back by the door, hesitating.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I want to go for a walk, but I feel selfish.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Well, we all have to make sacrifices now, I suppose.\"","then":"They nod, and their shoes stay by the door."},{"text":"\"Go if you want, but don't be long.\"","then":"They hover at the door, eyes already on the clock."},{"text":"\"You deserve it. You've earned it by doing so much.\"","then":"They smile, but now rest is something to be earned."},{"text":"\"It isn't selfish. You come back better for all of us.\"","then":"They pause, then lace up. \"Okay. Thank you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Time for yourself isn't selfish, and saying so lifts the guilt."},{"prompt":"Your partner asks: \"What if the baby needs me and I'm not there?\"","options":[{"text":"\"She'll be fine. Stop fretting.\"","then":"They tense, and worry all the way."},{"text":"\"Then maybe stay in and rest at home.\"","then":"They stay, and the guilt stays too."},{"text":"\"I've got her. If it's serious, I'll call.\"","then":"Their shoulders drop. \"Okay. I trust you.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Take her with you so you won't worry.\"","then":"They walk with the pram, and it isn't a break."}],"why":"Showing you can handle it is what makes their break possible."},{"prompt":"The next weekend, your partner returns from a walk, cheeks pink: \"That was lovely. Sorry it took so long.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Right, now can you do the bath?\"","then":"They nod, and the walk drains away into work."},{"text":"\"You were gone ages. I nearly rang you.\"","then":"They frown, and their pleasure dims."},{"text":"\"No need to be sorry. Same time next week?\"","then":"They smile. \"Yes. Same time.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You look better. Perhaps you needed that.\"","then":"They stiffen. It sounds like a diagnosis."}],"why":"Welcoming them back warmly, and offering it again, makes it a habit."},{"prompt":"Your mother says: \"A parent's place is at home.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right, Ma. We'll stay in more.\"","then":"Your partner's walks quietly end."},{"text":"\"Ma, that's old-fashioned.\"","then":"She frowns, and the tension lasts through dinner."},{"text":"\"Talk to my partner, not me.\"","then":"Your partner faces the criticism alone."},{"text":"\"Ma, rested parents are better for everyone.\"","then":"She hesitates. \"Well. They did come back smiling.\"","best":true}],"why":"Backing your partner in front of family shows it's a shared choice."}],"relearn":"Reframing self-time as something the whole family gains from lifts the guilt that keeps a parent depleted."},
  {"id":"uk-1407","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"both can be true","hook":"A relative's cheerful advice about new parenthood stings because you're struggling.","setup":"You are rocking the baby when your aunt sits down beside you.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"Enjoy every moment, beta. It goes so fast.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I should be more grateful.\"","then":"She pats your hand, and the guilt lands heavier."},{"text":"\"Easy for you to say. You had help.\"","then":"Her smile drops. \"I had it hard too.\""},{"text":"\"I love this baby deeply, and some days are hard.\"","then":"She pauses. \"Hm. I suppose that's true.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Yes, it's magical. I'm so lucky.\"","then":"She beams, and the truth stays hidden."}],"why":"Love and hard days can both be true, and saying so removes the guilt."},{"prompt":"Your aunt looks at you: \"You seem tired, beta. But parents today have it all. A healthy baby, a good home.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I'm being silly and ungrateful.\"","then":"She nods, and you swallow your feelings."},{"text":"\"I know, and I'm grateful. I'm also exhausted.\"","then":"She sighs. \"Hm. Grateful and tired. I remember.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll try to focus only on the good, Aunty.\"","then":"She approves, and you feel a little more alone."},{"text":"\"That doesn't make it any easier for me.\"","then":"She flinches. \"I didn't mean to hurt you.\""}],"why":"Being thankful and tired at once is honest, not ungrateful."},{"prompt":"Your aunt says: \"In our time, we never complained.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then you were braver than me.\"","then":"She nods, and you feel small."},{"text":"\"Times are different. You don't get it.\"","then":"She goes stiff."},{"text":"\"I hear you. Saying it helps me.\"","then":"She looks away, then nods. \"Hm. I never had anyone to tell.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Never mind. Forget I said anything.\"","then":"She changes the topic, and you feel unheard."}],"why":"Explaining that honesty isn't complaining lets her hear you."},{"prompt":"Later, your cousin sits down beside you and says: \"You look worn out. Everything alright?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just a little tired. Nothing serious.\"","then":"She nods, and nothing shifts."},{"text":"\"I'm fine. The baby is perfect.\"","then":"She smiles, and the mask holds."},{"text":"\"Honestly, some days are hard. Saying it helps.\"","then":"She squeezes your arm. \"Thank you. Tell me more.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"You should see the state of the house.\"","then":"She laughs, but the joke steers away."}],"why":"Saying the honest thing to one safe person invites real support."}],"relearn":"Naming that love and hard days coexist answers the 'cherish it all' pressure without taking on extra guilt."},
  {"id":"uk-1408","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"let's get support","hook":"Your partner has been low and flat for weeks, and you want to gently point toward help.","setup":"You sit beside them on the sofa after another silent evening.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I don't know what's wrong with me. I feel nothing.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You just need to snap out of it and stay positive.\"","then":"They look away, and the numbness deepens."},{"text":"\"Maybe it's the weather. Let's go out for a day.\"","then":"They agree, but the flatness follows them."},{"text":"\"This is bigger than tiredness. Let's find support.\"","then":"They breathe out shakily. \"Together. Okay.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Everyone feels like this. It will pass.\"","then":"They nod, but the fear stays."}],"why":"Naming it gently and going together treats it as real and treatable, and never their fault."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"If I told anyone, they'd think I'm failing as a parent.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Then don't tell them everything.\"","then":"They hold back, and the help gets thinner."},{"text":"\"Asking for help isn't failing. Not your fault.\"","then":"Their eyes fill. \"Thank you. I needed to hear that.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Nobody will think that. Don't worry about it.\"","then":"They nod, but the fear stays unspoken."},{"text":"\"Let's wait and see if you feel better.\"","then":"Time passes, and they do not feel better."}],"why":"Saying help is not failing removes the shame that stops people asking."},{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"I can't tell if it's baby blues or something more.\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's just baby blues. It always passes.\"","then":"They nod, though it has already been weeks."},{"text":"\"Let's search the symptoms tonight.\"","then":"The results frighten them and settle nothing."},{"text":"\"Weeks of this deserve a doctor. Try Tele-MANAS 14416.\"","then":"They nod. \"Okay. We can call it tomorrow.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"Ask your mother what she thinks.\"","then":"Her answer is \"we all felt sad\", and nothing moves."}],"why":"Weeks of low mood deserve a doctor, and Tele-MANAS 14416 can guide the first step."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"Part of me wants help. Picking up the phone feels huge.\"","options":[{"text":"\"Just call. It will take ten minutes.\"","then":"They feel rushed, and put the phone down."},{"text":"\"I'll call and explain everything for you.\"","then":"They feel replaced, and are not heard."},{"text":"\"You needn't call at all if it feels too much.\"","then":"They put it off, relieved, and the flatness continues."},{"text":"\"I'll sit with you while you call. You say when.\"","then":"They squeeze your hand. \"That would help. Thank you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Sitting beside them, at their pace, makes the first call possible."}],"relearn":"Naming persistent low mood gently and offering to seek support together meets a treatable thing with care, not dismissal."},
  {"id":"uk-1409","cat":"you-still-matter","type":"role-play","persona":"Divya","source":"protect my hour","hook":"You want a weekly hour for the one hobby that still feels like you, and you'll ask your partner to hold it.","setup":"You wait until the baby is asleep and your partner sits down.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your partner says: \"You've been quiet. Anything on your mind?\"","options":[{"text":"\"It's fine. I'll just give up my hobby for now.\"","then":"They nod, but a few days later they ask what the hobby is."},{"text":"\"Never mind. You wouldn't get it.\"","then":"They shrug, but a few days later they ask again."},{"text":"\"Can we protect one hour a week that's just mine?\"","then":"They lean in. \"Yes. Tell me when.\"","best":true},{"text":"\"I'll go back to it when the baby is older.\"","then":"Years feel long, but they ask again a few days later."}],"why":"Asking plainly for a protected hour tells them exactly what would help."},{"prompt":"A few days later, your partner says: \"I want to help with your hobby, but our week is so full. When would it fit?\"","options":[{"text":"\"Whenever you're free. I'll fit in.\"","then":"They pick Thursday for you, and you nod, unsure it's what you wanted."},{"text":"\"You figure it out. You're better at schedules.\"","then":"They set Thursday, and your hour becomes one more job for them."},{"text":"\"Forget it. It's too much to ask.\"","then":"They let it drop, but a week later suggest Thursday anyway."},{"text":"\"Thursday evening, after the bath. I'll plan around it.\"","then":"They write it on the calendar. \"Thursday. Done.\"","best":true}],"why":"Picking a real time makes the hour something they can hold."},{"prompt":"The first Thursday arrives, and your partner says: \"Maybe skip tonight? She's fussy.\"","options":[{"text":"\"You're right. I'll skip tonight.\"","then":"The hour is lost, and the next one is easier to lose."},{"text":"\"You never let me have anything.\"","then":"They stiffen, hurt."},{"text":"\"Okay, but I'll take twice as long next time.\"","then":"They agree, and the ledger begins."},{"text":"\"She'll settle. I'm going. It's my hour.\"","then":"They nod, and you go. The house holds.","best":true}],"why":"Keeping the hour on the hardest evening shows it is a promise, not a treat."},{"prompt":"At a family lunch, a relative asks: \"Still going to that hobby?\" and your partner glances at you.","options":[{"text":"\"Not really. It's too busy right now.\"","then":"They nod, and your partner looks puzzled."},{"text":"\"Ask my partner. It was their idea.\"","then":"Your partner is left holding an idea that was yours."},{"text":"\"Just now and then. I don't want a fuss.\"","then":"The relative nods, and the hour shrinks in everyone's mind."},{"text":"\"Yes, every Thursday. It keeps me feeling like a person.\"","then":"Your partner smiles. The relative says, \"Good for you.\"","best":true}],"why":"Saying it out loud helps the family treat your hour as normal."}],"relearn":"Asking your partner to protect a weekly hour for yourself keeps a piece of your own identity alive through parenthood."},
];

